INSERT INTO question_translations (id, question_id, lang_id, question_text, explanation) VALUES
  ('ad78c0d9-8efe-4dff-a618-c3c09e0d8daf','7cc423e2-c5f3-438c-94a5-90a7e7b28f98',1,'What is apomixis in flowering plants?','Apomixis is a special mechanism where seeds are produced without fertilization. It is a form of asexual reproduction that mimics sexual reproduction, found in some species of Asteraceae and grasses.'),
  ('a8e34884-38ae-4237-ab42-a86ddb8f8619','02d99edf-89d0-4f6e-b833-31125315fd9a',1,'Which of the following families are mentioned as having apomictic species?','The text mentions that some species of Asteraceae and grasses have evolved apomixis as a special mechanism to produce seeds without fertilization.'),
  ('77bbbe78-70c0-458a-8d79-8a2f4980007a','8b6aec5f-df1e-48bc-afbf-80cefeef0939',1,'What is polyembryony?','Polyembryony refers to the occurrence of more than one embryo in a seed. This happens in some species where nucellar cells surrounding the embryo sac develop into embryos.'),
  ('611acb58-c9e5-47e2-b491-90c9c40c9190','d9577e74-e038-4613-9eeb-0e4814379d6b',1,'In which fruit is polyembryony commonly observed according to the text?','The text specifically mentions that in orange seeds, multiple embryos of different sizes and shapes can be observed when squeezed. This is an example of polyembryony.'),
  ('73baa56b-1578-4c96-bd39-2047e87285e4','6b35e6a6-19db-4424-ae8e-3d586c504b18',1,'What is the genetic nature of apomictic embryos?','Apomictic embryos are formed without fertilization from diploid cells, so they are genetically identical to the parent plant. Therefore, they can be called clones.'),
  ('da9258c2-409f-4e91-bbed-be6638eb91a0','543c32c4-268a-4d51-a285-39d1486b59ff',1,'Why is apomixis important in hybrid seed industry?','If hybrids are made into apomicts, there is no segregation of characters in the hybrid progeny. Farmers can keep using hybrid seeds year after year without buying new ones, making it economically beneficial.'),
  ('7f59462b-dae7-48db-b7c2-93390155170d','0e6ce8e0-7269-4b5f-8d26-588c689d2b68',1,'What happens when seeds collected from hybrids are sown without apomixis?','When seeds from hybrids are sown, the plants in the progeny will segregate and do not maintain hybrid characters. This is why hybrid seeds must be produced every year.'),
  ('5b27a03c-c4dc-40c8-9b1b-b1e8ce290022','624a3296-02c3-4a07-a424-9a4135eb4921',1,'Which cells develop into embryos in polyembryonic species like Citrus and Mango?','In many Citrus and Mango varieties, some of the nucellar cells surrounding the embryo sac start dividing, protrude into the embryo sac, and develop into embryos, resulting in polyembryony.'),
  ('5c5ce025-284e-4f9c-8a7f-f7d8c7b924de','588d6567-d901-409a-9741-59a95a90b50e',1,'What is the main problem with hybrid seed production mentioned in the text?','The text states that production of hybrid seeds is costly and hence the cost of hybrid seeds becomes too expensive for the farmers. This is a major problem in hybrid cultivation.'),
  ('b0f7193a-e5a2-4ef9-b4c2-a1f7d2a1d290','55f06f4c-af79-4628-84ce-0cbdafa73fe9',1,'How does the diploid egg cell form in some apomictic species?','In some apomictic species, the diploid egg cell is formed without reduction division (meiosis) and develops into the embryo without fertilization.'),
  ('183b32ec-299c-42b0-88ab-c97264649492','e0606738-230e-40fc-a08c-e0a9ec4fb605',1,'What is the ploidy level of the primary endosperm nucleus (PEN) formed during double fertilization?','The primary endosperm nucleus (PEN) is formed by the fusion of one male gamete with two polar nuclei, resulting in a triploid (3n) nucleus. This process is called triple fusion.'),
  ('6bf2cf7a-1780-4caa-b70b-da564393ede7','53ac33ff-aa3b-42b2-b717-44a02f480816',1,'Which of the following is a parthenocarpic fruit mentioned in the text?','Banana is mentioned as an example of a fruit that develops without fertilization, making it a parthenocarpic fruit.'),
  ('df6eded1-db45-4040-9323-cfd4f9016c63','933f131d-24eb-41c7-9146-a7115815b583',1,'What happens to the central cell after triple fusion?','After triple fusion, the central cell becomes the primary endosperm cell (PEC) and develops into the endosperm tissue, which provides nutrition to the developing embryo.'),
  ('6f6f1b7d-36c7-42d3-8349-874c50200561','5b7138a7-261f-4840-9187-984511d51f1b',1,'What is the function of the endosperm in a seed?','The endosperm tissue is filled with reserve food materials and is used for the nutrition of the developing embryo. It may be completely consumed or persist in the mature seed.'),
  ('36bedcb1-be25-4e04-b213-8cda2e30dabe','3e09d394-2299-4303-9252-761f42781224',1,'What is the oldest viable seed mentioned in the text?','The oldest viable seed mentioned is that of a lupine, Lupinus arcticus, excavated from Arctic Tundra, which germinated after an estimated 10,000 years of dormancy.'),
  ('532175c3-4541-46e5-9db6-38b6e4ff3054','5c108b48-cfea-4ab3-a7ca-aecd3fa31a42',1,'In which part of an angiosperm flower does the male gametophyte develop?','The male gametophyte develops inside the anther. The anther contains microsporangia where pollen grains (male gametophytes) are formed through microsporogenesis.'),
  ('9a7bc3c6-f587-42ff-832c-1cb4015abdef','a00539e1-7af1-4711-9416-3c784f4e245f',1,'Which type of cell division occurs during microsporogenesis and megasporogenesis?','Meiosis occurs during both microsporogenesis and megasporogenesis. This reduction division produces haploid microspores and megaspores from diploid mother cells.'),
  ('c945fa7a-849c-48ce-b0b1-4c94cc6beda8','e6949f04-a6b7-42fd-8fbe-4594155069e5',1,'What is the correct developmental sequence: Sporogenous tissue, Pollen mother cell, Microspore tetrad, Pollen grain, Male gametes?','The correct sequence is: Sporogenous tissue differentiates into pollen mother cells (PMC), which undergo meiosis to form microspore tetrads. Individual microspores develop into pollen grains, and the generative cell divides to form male gametes.'),
  ('5159a2a0-e42e-4260-8ac9-bda35f6d511d','197d5537-c86f-44cf-93f4-d6fa245d7de0',1,'Which structure connects the ovule to the placenta in a typical angiosperm ovule?','The funicle is the stalk that attaches the ovule to the placenta. The hilum is the junction point between the ovule and funicle.'),
  ('f1920ade-de43-4726-83f1-5b7ac8ffda6b','98f47b8c-f255-42b6-b6ce-6b1f4e7ccce3',1,'What is meant by monosporic development of the female gametophyte?','Monosporic development refers to the formation of the embryo sac from only one functional megaspore, while the other three megaspores degenerate. This is the most common type in flowering plants.'),
  ('649bb6ac-7ad3-4e62-b0d8-fb383df52c72','586c2f78-afb8-4263-b51f-7596d214cea1',1,'How many cells and nuclei are present in a mature angiosperm embryo sac?','A mature angiosperm embryo sac is 7-celled and 8-nucleate. It contains the egg apparatus (2 synergids + 1 egg cell), 3 antipodals, and 1 central cell with 2 polar nuclei.'),
  ('7c6b37c7-7575-413c-87e2-b3bea136b499','edcdd84e-5ea9-4e3e-8d9a-e7a11523a2ff',1,'Which statement about chasmogamous and cleistogamous flowers is correct?','Cleistogamous flowers do not open at all, so cross-pollination cannot occur in them. They are invariably autogamous. Chasmogamous flowers open and expose their reproductive parts, allowing cross-pollination.'),
  ('09fc3e24-7e7a-4321-8506-86e4b049683d','0b1ce5ff-1a0d-4fa9-a5c4-e28dd519ccb4',1,'Which is a strategy evolved to prevent self-pollination in flowers?','Dichogamy (non-synchronization of pollen release and stigma receptivity) is a major outbreeding device. Either pollen is released before stigma becomes receptive, or stigma becomes receptive before pollen release.'),
  ('77f643d2-e73e-4688-96ae-6380e6d98c70','2e545e93-236f-4df9-a7c8-4c644337861e',1,'What is self-incompatibility?','Self-incompatibility is a genetic mechanism that prevents self-pollen from fertilizing the ovules by inhibiting pollen germination or pollen tube growth in the pistil. This promotes cross-pollination and genetic diversity.'),
  ('58601735-5b66-45b7-96dd-6faadf4ae1ec','2e1e4498-f529-42c2-807a-ad1a134f5728',1,'What is the function of the filiform apparatus in the synergid?','The filiform apparatus is a special cellular thickening present at the micropylar tip of synergids. It plays an important role in guiding the pollen tube into the synergid, ensuring proper fertilization.'),
  ('3d52817d-da63-40a1-b178-e4bd1719610d','72fb4e5e-2f79-40f5-a12b-eabf2adb072b',1,'Where does the pollen tube enter the ovule?','After reaching the ovary, the pollen tube enters the ovule through the micropyle, which is a small opening in the integuments.'),
  ('29aab10c-12bc-4255-af47-eddb24d5742f','d732ff56-f61e-425e-a0ca-0442838a2021',1,'What happens to the two male gametes after they are discharged into the synergid?','One male gamete moves towards the egg cell and fuses with its nucleus (syngamy), while the other moves towards the two polar nuclei and fuses with them (triple fusion). This is called double fertilization.'),
  ('c068c3e1-cc7d-4fb0-9abd-fc0b97586f3a','36b02a2a-4f61-467a-8e3f-82099a8bf945',1,'What is emasculation in plant breeding?','Emasculation is the removal of anthers from the flower bud before the anther dehisces using a pair of forceps. This is done to prevent self-pollination in bisexual flowers during artificial hybridization.'),
  ('09c56ea5-8782-45f4-a430-7ca82ba163e5','984ca61e-379f-4f35-8394-df43cbbc17c3',1,'Why is bagging done after emasculation?','After emasculation, flowers are covered with a bag (usually made of butter paper) to prevent contamination of the stigma with unwanted pollen from other sources.'),
  ('6aff93b5-510f-43c1-b9f5-bfbaaa972210','7b8e95b7-c62e-4bcf-a47f-7e1c2a792cc2',1,'What is the purpose of artificial hybridization in crop improvement?','Artificial hybridization is used to combine desirable characters from different species and genera to produce commercially superior varieties. This is one of the major approaches of crop improvement programs.'),
  ('39c524ea-99d8-420b-a170-c8fb417da3a0','817e1fb3-4c1d-4bff-89d3-83b0402764f3',1,'Which of the following events occurs first in the pollen-pistil interaction sequence?','The sequence of pollen-pistil interaction begins with the germination of the pollen grain on the stigma to produce a pollen tube. This is followed by growth through the style, reaching the ovary, entering the ovule, and finally discharging male gametes.'),
  ('c055c00b-1031-4bd5-bdf4-e315359df933','7f4733c2-130a-4b5c-a17c-1cd0c745afd2',1,'Which of the following is the primary function of a flower in angiosperms?','Flowers are the sites of sexual reproduction in angiosperms. They contain the male reproductive structures (androecium/stamens) and female reproductive structures (gynoecium/pistil) that produce gametes and facilitate fertilization.'),
  ('6db37781-2f7a-4ae4-a84a-138845cd0475','a0c82835-7210-4e11-8efe-9b8adc4fcfb6',1,'Which two parts of a flower contain the most important units of sexual reproduction?','The stamen (androecium) represents the male reproductive organ producing pollen grains, while the pistil (gynoecium) represents the female reproductive organ containing the ovule and embryo sac. These are the two most important units for sexual reproduction.'),
  ('a9f53464-f33f-4e54-af2c-e51f9462988c','b3d23635-0bbc-4dab-8cfc-7cd8dd9f470e',1,'Flowers are valued by human beings for all of the following EXCEPT:','Flowers are objects of aesthetic, ornamental, social, religious and cultural value. They are used as symbols for conveying human feelings such as love, affection, happiness, grief, and mourning. Industrial chemical production is not mentioned as a primary value.'),
  ('fcac85af-9bf3-49bd-8d4e-f87dd02b5ba3','f07decd8-13aa-4776-99c3-819095a1e191',1,'What does floriculture refer to?','Floriculture refers to the cultivation of flowers for ornamental and commercial purposes. It involves growing flowers for gardens, landscaping, and the floral industry including cut flowers and potted plants.'),
  ('07729112-bfff-4ed6-9b40-7d0019eca366','2a6502ce-c649-4d5e-b2a0-e6b37aed8e8d',1,'In the diagrammatic representation of L.S. of a flower, which structure is located at the topmost position?','In the longitudinal section (L.S.) of a flower, the stigma is located at the topmost position. It is the receptive surface of the pistil that receives pollen grains during pollination.'),
  ('2ef4aeb4-35cd-4532-ac2e-0fa49bef7625','78298bdf-4d3a-4650-a2c5-c9df93d9f834',1,'What initiates before the actual flower is seen on a plant?','Much before the actual flower is seen on a plant, the decision that the plant is going to flower has taken place. Several hormonal and structural changes are initiated which lead to the differentiation and development of the floral primordium.'),
  ('a74ec442-95e7-4428-98fc-7a487da88dc0','f9490ffb-4279-4110-9b1e-649ebae222cd',1,'Which statement correctly describes the androecium and gynoecium?','The androecium consists of a whorl of stamens representing the male reproductive organ, while the gynoecium represents the female reproductive organ of the flower. These are the essential reproductive structures.'),
  ('ec5e56cb-334b-4c75-a874-28bf3643f4ff','6e71d455-61dd-435d-b0f3-1a5692b3066c',1,'Which of the following is NOT a part of the pistil?','The pistil (gynoecium) consists of three parts: the stigma, style, and ovary. The anther is part of the stamen (androecium), which is the male reproductive structure, not the pistil.'),
  ('5593eea4-4a9e-4a0b-9f81-9fb155e51fcf','a649e93b-b856-4984-b50a-ace0d4bee8d0',1,'What is the function of the filament in a stamen?','The filament is the long and slender stalk of the stamen that supports the anther. Its proximal end is attached to the thalamus or petal, positioning the anther appropriately for pollen dispersal.'),
  ('1424eb94-04b6-4e38-ac86-266fed978b13','ab5cbb69-c67d-4a81-8c76-620809bbb478',1,'The diversity of structures of inflorescences, flowers and floral parts shows:','The diversity of structures of inflorescences, flowers and floral parts shows an amazing range of adaptations to ensure formation of the end products of sexual reproduction, which are the fruits and seeds.'),
  ('b6cdbc61-604d-4968-b035-14d180574262','e0ef844c-0533-4c71-8b40-f8818db9afe3',1,'Flowers are used as symbols for conveying which of the following human feelings?','Flowers have always been used as symbols for conveying important human feelings such as love, affection, happiness, grief, mourning, etc. They serve multiple emotional and cultural purposes.'),
  ('1c89ee69-bb50-4c1d-af59-e0780355cd11','21aa6cb9-8182-4a2c-b489-12032b31eaa7',1,'In the L.S. diagram of a flower, the ovary is located at which position?','In the longitudinal section (L.S.) of a flower, the ovary is located at the base of the flower. It is the enlarged basal portion of the pistil that contains the ovules.'),
  ('b00cd473-f0ed-4f65-ad97-dd0da09b9187','eef1470d-220c-4456-a174-fc05f0b27f4e',1,'Which structure in the flower produces the male gametophyte?','The anther is the terminal bilobed structure of the stamen that produces pollen grains. The pollen grains represent the male gametophytes in flowering plants.'),
  ('dd4f1ba4-ee4a-4604-a69d-c4a87a573d5b','30ccb432-da95-4730-9568-b404f4c7a76c',1,'What leads to the differentiation and development of the floral primordium?','Several hormonal and structural changes are initiated which lead to the differentiation and further development of the floral primordium. These changes prepare the plant for flowering.'),
  ('58675dd1-2f86-4a5a-b92f-59c8aed6b7f0','e06bc5c1-89ab-47cc-aff4-b6e52970b405',1,'Which of the following are the accessory (non-essential) parts of a flower?','Petals and sepals are the accessory or non-essential parts of a flower. They do not directly participate in reproduction but serve protective and attractive functions. Stamens and pistils are the essential reproductive parts.'),
  ('f4670a2d-5946-4eee-b5b0-919611b5932f','e7d62934-5a94-4f41-87f2-b20718a0c7e5',1,'What are inflorescences?','Inflorescences are formed which bear the floral buds and then the flowers. They are arrangements of flowers on the floral axis of a plant.'),
  ('1074dfa9-4dec-4754-9316-1c629e150d6c','fdf0d78e-5b97-40a8-8948-e38fd4bf2b99',1,'To a biologist, flowers are considered as:','To a biologist, flowers are morphological and embryological marvels and the sites of sexual reproduction. They represent complex and highly specialized structures evolved for reproductive success.'),
  ('ff831613-82b7-48fa-bf75-6660992b7d38','63243128-c8ed-4fe6-bf45-3db2033d2be7',1,'A typical stamen consists of which two main parts?','A typical stamen consists of two parts: the long and slender stalk called the filament, and the terminal generally bilobed structure called the anther. The anther produces pollen grains.'),
  ('5cf42759-0e66-4916-b3c1-94b1ce2c99bd','a3d30565-cc86-4a3c-9195-54fec792cdfa',1,'Why is sexual reproduction important for species survival?','Sexual mode of reproduction enables creation of new variants, so that survival advantage is enhanced. This genetic variation helps species adapt to changing environments and avoid extinction.'),
  ('6a29b18e-2451-447c-99ca-9e938f8e1407','281c63e2-c96a-4931-89e8-70433bb9f720',1,'For complete autogamy in a normal flower, what is required?','In a normal flower which opens and exposes the anthers and the stigma, complete autogamy requires synchrony in pollen release and stigma receptivity, and also the anthers and the stigma should lie close to each other.'),
  ('cff678d4-d619-45ef-be72-9d535f73961b','70f25cd7-e84f-4486-aa95-874e09d59814',1,'Which structure connects the stigma to the ovary in the pistil?','The style is the elongated slender part of the pistil that connects the stigma to the ovary. It provides the pathway for pollen tubes to grow from the stigma to the ovary.'),
  ('f4d067c2-1df6-4ecd-8cbd-208e6f4e65bd','027af50a-6da1-4f1d-9cbc-00da03e49190',1,'What happens after the floral primordium develops?','After the floral primordium develops, inflorescences are formed which bear the floral buds and then the flowers. This is part of the normal developmental sequence leading to flowering.'),
  ('0dcf7129-960c-4179-8e16-8e47bf8fb509','3947567b-1cd0-430e-97f0-c8a199f90eaa',1,'What is the main unit discussed in the uploaded textbook page?','The page clearly states ''UNIT VI REPRODUCTION'' at the top, indicating the main unit is about reproduction.'),
  ('7c3ef037-1667-4469-83af-4e9f4b859b97','70519adc-c717-4fb4-be95-13604b1f64de',1,'How many chapters are listed under Unit VI in the given page?','The page lists three chapters: Chapter 1 (Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants), Chapter 2 (Human Reproduction), and Chapter 3 (Reproductive Health).'),
  ('98449846-ccd0-4deb-b400-b792037f4237','fbcf43e1-1632-4aa4-8baf-b41d1f36c7a1',1,'Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a chapter in Unit VI?','The three chapters mentioned are Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants, Human Reproduction, and Reproductive Health. Plant Physiology is not listed.'),
  ('7b900dbf-312d-4dec-a67c-94dd20666626','d41d5330-fc8e-401e-bd48-9d624f91c5e6',1,'According to the text, what happens to individual organisms without fail?','The text states ''While individual organisms die without fail, species continue to live through millions of years.'''),
  ('a17a6328-9010-4827-9700-887d911cc1bf','e5d2ea0b-9ba3-418a-a4a9-ad9e8930b653',1,'What does the text describe as a vital process for species survival?','The text explicitly states ''Reproduction becomes a vital process without which species cannot survive for long.'''),
  ('86e4d908-f5ec-4c3f-8271-98ac50836c47','46ae09ac-7a8a-4532-a789-eb2d7f1a559e',1,'How can an individual leave its progeny according to the text?','The text states ''Each individual leaves its progeny by asexual or sexual means.'''),
  ('765a0a89-a793-4150-9091-ca15359f2365','94af99f8-f5e2-4bdb-a6cc-7cd3c8ae3045',1,'What advantage does sexual reproduction provide according to the text?','The text states ''Sexual mode of reproduction enables creation of new variants, so that survival advantage is enhanced.'''),
  ('5a51ed8f-92c0-4c1f-b8c2-f8dec029b9ca','7396030d-fd6f-43fe-8fa3-0406e3f4b289',1,'What is the purpose of using flowering plants and humans as examples in this unit?','The text states the unit explains reproductive processes in flowering plants and humans ''as easy to relate representative examples.'''),
  ('21dd003e-f2ad-4ace-94f4-ca091b213702','4bd25944-aa11-46df-8300-05906349a4e8',1,'Which of the following is the most common type of abiotic pollination among flowering plants?','Wind pollination is more common amongst abiotic pollinations compared to water pollination, which is quite rare in flowering plants.'),
  ('66dd0fc7-6dda-4a44-9e62-06cc64c13804','bc1a8da5-2e9f-448b-be93-bcd37b5e10e6',1,'What characteristic must pollen grains have for successful wind pollination?','Wind pollination requires that pollen grains are light and non-sticky so that they can be transported in wind currents.'),
  ('bca643a9-fbc4-45db-b6f8-5a0284573f2c','1fb53d64-411d-4b2f-95f6-581e397c709a',1,'Which structure in wind-pollinated flowers is often described as large and feathery?','Wind-pollinated flowers often possess large, often-feathery stigma to easily trap air-borne pollen grains.'),
  ('c35a1f4a-5f9a-406a-9858-762ddbf16667','d15dd518-264c-4722-b5f4-93753d118028',1,'How many ovules are typically found in each ovary of wind-pollinated flowers?','Wind-pollinated flowers often have a single ovule in each ovary.'),
  ('20f3bf56-db9c-438c-9953-50ddfad65bb0','487e5719-21ef-4ea8-aaac-a7ad6ef7f254',1,'What is the familiar example given in the text for wind-pollinated plants with numerous flowers in an inflorescence?','The corn cob is given as a familiar example where the ears are the stigma and style which wave in the wind to trap pollen grains.'),
  ('63be2683-b33a-443b-8fe7-6edfbe034ec8','dba4d71e-d6c5-4a28-8f73-fea3c6dfdb0d',1,'Approximately how many genera of flowering plants are known to use water pollination?','Pollination by water is limited to about 30 genera, mostly monocotyledons.'),
  ('aaa740c5-b4f9-44c7-a864-0936751f834f','3796b953-15b1-4280-be13-30a0565af5a5',1,'Which of the following is NOT a water-pollinated plant mentioned in the text?','Water hyacinth is mentioned as an aquatic plant whose flowers emerge above water and are pollinated by insects or wind, not by water.'),
  ('a1e47441-653c-454c-b2fd-d8e35f1eb2ae','8b91bc28-3d12-405e-9972-727b6cc407eb',1,'In Vallisneria, how do the female flowers reach the water surface for pollination?','In Vallisneria, the female flower reaches the surface of water by the long stalk.'),
  ('958d9b56-8f77-4f75-ab04-5f45bcd76f9b','25003b77-a7a7-45da-a669-3625b51aa23e',1,'How are pollen grains of Vallisneria carried to the female flowers?','In Vallisneria, male flowers or pollen grains are released onto the surface of water and carried passively by water currents.'),
  ('c83a960f-573f-45a3-8187-5ce1e1bad226','c925c1be-78eb-4366-95c8-1568334dd957',1,'What is the shape of pollen grains in many water-pollinated species like seagrasses?','Pollen grains in many water-pollinated species are long, ribbon-like and carried passively inside the water.'),
  ('25135a78-73d6-49cd-b54f-4e76c7cbaeaa','91680910-5daa-4c14-add0-040c9f251afd',1,'How are pollen grains protected from wetting in most water-pollinated species?','In most water-pollinated species, pollen grains are protected from wetting by a mucilaginous covering.'),
  ('f968e440-8043-419d-aed1-8afb08b0ec0d','e0d291fb-2b8d-492a-8620-4a85bdcfe52e',1,'Which group of plants commonly uses water as a regular mode of transport for male gametes?','Water is a regular mode of transport for male gametes among lower plant groups such as algae, bryophytes and pteridophytes.'),
  ('5ca0ec50-7860-4769-bbf7-fe3a194e4522','8b0a2448-9572-48f7-ac64-d5dc374a3697',1,'Why are wind and water pollinated flowers typically not very colourful?','Both wind and water pollinated flowers are not very colourful and do not produce nectar because they do not need to attract animal pollinators.'),
  ('1fa619b0-c9e1-4752-9400-25b00d3c129e','6ac6a58c-87fa-4337-a786-c27db4d5c82f',1,'Which of the following aquatic plants has flowers that emerge above water level and are pollinated by insects or wind?','Water hyacinth and water lily have flowers that emerge above the level of water and are pollinated by insects or wind.'),
  ('d42b0b32-3a9b-46ac-85dd-738a95be8273','8f5371aa-38ac-4f8f-bfcf-7971b574ecb4',1,'In seagrasses, where do the female flowers remain during pollination?','In seagrasses, female flowers remain submerged in water and pollen grains are released inside the water.'),
  ('c91754a7-1a04-4b07-b1ed-e375e88e5109','7b3dbd0f-79c1-4560-8e66-c7fadb0a5188',1,'What limits the distribution of some bryophytes and pteridophytes according to the text?','The distribution of some bryophytes and pteridophytes is limited because of the need for water for transport of male gametes and fertilisation.'),
  ('bbc0ddaf-4ed6-46c7-b20a-30b258c2d223','e7a5e986-fe0a-43e2-b22e-8d45c3394158',1,'Which marine plant is mentioned as an example of water-pollinated species?','Zostera is mentioned as a marine sea-grass that is water-pollinated.'),
  ('eefd2189-f252-4d84-94ae-563010c053c4','124acd37-17ab-4951-bdb7-e749a92e27a2',1,'What type of plants are most water-pollinated flowering plants?','Water pollination in flowering plants is limited to about 30 genera, mostly monocotyledons.'),
  ('d0e04b1f-8571-4568-bf06-069ccab06e8f','58ab5639-f841-493a-bace-c4c5252810f2',1,'What happens to male flowers or pollen grains in Vallisneria?','In Vallisneria, male flowers or pollen grains are released onto the surface of water.'),
  ('dfe07ceb-14d7-4cbf-8759-4742cfc37468','0a7e7379-667e-438a-b1d1-05773bff8449',1,'Which of the following is a characteristic of wind-pollinated flowers mentioned in the text?','Wind-pollinated flowers often have numerous flowers packed into an inflorescence, as seen in the corn cob example.'),
  ('2799f270-3f65-4e4d-b09f-4d1a4d1a49fc','1d41a4e4-4edd-4ecf-aed1-807ab02d0d2a',1,'Which part of the ovule hardens to form the protective seed coat?','The integuments of the ovule harden to form tough protective seed coats.'),
  ('8777a04a-de06-46db-ad94-903ec5258dbe','2e254cb3-a4ab-4d92-baa2-4de8bdd327a5',1,'What is the function of the micropyle that remains as a small pore in the seed coat?','The micropyle remains as a small pore in the seed coat and facilitates entry of oxygen and water into the seed during germination.'),
  ('811930ea-9377-416f-abf1-6628456ce273','3cffa970-0bcc-461b-b970-3232d514c4b2',1,'Which of the following is an example of a non-albuminous seed?','Non-albuminous seeds have no residual endosperm as it is completely consumed during embryo development. Examples include pea and groundnut.'),
  ('cfed5b67-da0c-4dca-a3f8-b8e31f35fddb','5ef21962-91e3-4078-95ed-222a00007279',1,'What is the term for the residual persistent nucellus found in some seeds like black pepper and beet?','In some seeds such as black pepper and beet, remnants of nucellus are persistent. This residual persistent nucellus is called perisperm.'),
  ('c9ce984f-8946-4d7d-80c5-a90ac6980b18','0ab55525-f3a5-492d-b476-b360fcea34f1',1,'Which of the following fruits is NOT a false fruit?','Apple, strawberry, and cashew are false fruits where the thalamus contributes to fruit formation. Mango develops only from the ovary and is a true fruit.'),
  ('7192bc94-6ae0-4c9b-bcb7-9dabdb90f6d1','2f118e85-cb05-4de3-8644-dc6a8536d128',1,'What is the term for fruits that develop without fertilisation?','Parthenocarpic fruits develop without fertilisation. Banana is one such example.'),
  ('555c01e5-8a00-4810-9d08-3155edfa74bf','ea940067-73ac-48ac-a895-f76ba3b8e85e',1,'What is the wall of the fruit called that develops from the wall of the ovary?','The wall of the ovary develops into the wall of the fruit called pericarp.'),
  ('fc9f7843-6236-40f7-a719-78b50b0beccb','fd81f361-df3b-4b1f-ac9a-87bc2bf4c26a',1,'Which seed holds the record for the oldest viable seed, estimated to be 10,000 years old?','The oldest viable seed is that of Lupinus arcticus excavated from Arctic Tundra, which germinated after an estimated 10,000 years of dormancy.'),
  ('63759521-b370-4527-a167-62eb65e72ec8','9a4ef930-1a95-4b2f-8f64-dc610f062002',1,'What is the typical moisture content of a mature seed by mass?','As the seed matures, its water content is reduced and seeds become relatively dry with 10-15 percent moisture by mass.'),
  ('0ca0b781-f89a-4896-82da-bfeb9eac8d38','d520504a-a3ee-44a8-8068-938386822a02',1,'Which of the following is NOT an advantage of seeds to angiosperms?','Seeds offer several advantages including independence from water, dispersal strategies, protection, and genetic variation. However, seeds do NOT germinate immediately; they enter dormancy which is crucial for storage.'),
  ('31773bd0-c374-4888-9f85-597a162ea97e','5260d37d-27fe-40b5-8627-68a806c3c730',1,'In which year was Panchanan Maheshwari born?','Panchanan Maheshwari was born in November 1904 in Jaipur, Rajasthan.'),
  ('b54a62b9-caf8-4e23-b949-55307a858816','60a756e8-d587-48d2-b96d-5ab67b3ac54f',1,'Where was Panchanan Maheshwari born?','Panchanan Maheshwari was born in Jaipur, Rajasthan.'),
  ('abde51cb-4ada-4657-9f5c-c407e8cde9da','95e70222-2ef7-400d-b340-62d62bf47f73',1,'Which city did Maheshwari move to for higher education?','He moved to Allahabad for higher education where he obtained his D.Sc.'),
  ('b8ce1bd1-ac42-436e-ba9e-ef2f90943a9a','8541d466-0103-40dc-8914-1eba8968da2b',1,'Who inspired Maheshwari to develop interest in Botany?','He was inspired by Dr. W. Dudgeon, an American missionary teacher, to develop interest in Botany and especially morphology.'),
  ('18de4910-4d09-4e0f-bfec-53f6308077b2','f2d998bd-946b-49c6-abc4-0c9129a4e78e',1,'What was Dr. W. Dudgeon''s profession?','Dr. W. Dudgeon was an American missionary teacher who inspired Maheshwari.'),
  ('00cd7029-ea05-4dd6-b8e9-7a5b2629bdc8','2db5e2fc-1695-46b5-b611-254130bb072a',1,'Which field did Maheshwari work on and popularise?','He worked on embryological aspects and popularised the use of embryological characters in taxonomy.'),
  ('526a7470-4c5c-4573-9d39-001e276c8d64','21e88022-a521-47b9-ac4d-cd6ba7d9d16a',1,'Which university did Maheshwari establish the Department of Botany at?','He established the Department of Botany, University of Delhi as an important centre of research in embryology and tissue culture.'),
  ('6b5cb457-08c0-449f-907b-51c147d97d19','a55afd2d-af2a-4f32-8125-f17160e26ab2',1,'What did Maheshwari emphasise the need for?','He emphasised the need for initiation of work on artificial culture of immature embryos.'),
  ('09932d0c-2348-4805-87e0-0aec65e1e7f9','17e1eb40-8721-468a-9c65-764d3f20f264',1,'Which prestigious fellowship was Maheshwari honoured with?','He was honoured with fellowship of Royal Society of London (FRS).'),
  ('c8b41895-8db6-417f-be16-892463daa46c','57d6dc9a-369b-4a19-aa14-a69d68e298dc',1,'In which year were the first NCERT Biology textbooks published under Maheshwari''s leadership?','He made a significant contribution to school education by his leadership in bringing out the very first textbooks of Biology for Higher Secondary Schools published by NCERT in 1964.'),
  ('287649c8-94eb-4be8-a9de-a989133661ed','1941a406-d00f-447c-9c6f-fe7f8303120a',1,'What was the main contribution of Maheshwari''s work on test tube fertilisation?','His work on test tube fertilisation and intra-ovarian pollination won worldwide recognition.'),
  ('1bab018f-35c5-4b8b-ab2d-5aaf2d678452','4f94ad96-aa42-4f50-9d75-548d59c502d8',1,'What type of teacher was Dr. W. Dudgeon?','Dr. W. Dudgeon was an American missionary teacher who inspired Maheshwari''s interest in Botany.'),
  ('a7d40860-e855-4c04-9af4-59843d5245a0','0e7e4e20-5aae-47af-910b-cb485136ed7b',1,'What did Maheshwari''s teacher say that encouraged him?','His teacher once expressed that if his student progresses ahead of him, it will give him a great satisfaction. These words encouraged Panchanan.'),
  ('f9099b0e-f14e-4eab-96a8-a173078dcb25','641f410e-8c5e-4cf0-8e4f-8ca945428e6f',1,'What has tissue culture become today according to the text?','These days, tissue culture has become a landmark in science.'),
  ('e115509d-1a24-4666-8709-f45e85c1ea5a','36e6574b-e3b1-4385-b304-93df4d09a1fa',1,'Which of the following is NOT mentioned as an institution that honoured Maheshwari?','He was honoured with fellowship of Royal Society of London (FRS), Indian National Science Academy and several other institutions of excellence. Indian Academy of Sciences is not mentioned.'),
  ('f5fd0512-bed1-4ee5-9ca9-e25a8a6ca388','3b944d2f-9fe9-4ff1-90a8-c035c61e5398',1,'What are the two main parts of a typical stamen?','A typical stamen consists of two parts: the long slender stalk called the filament and the terminal bilobed structure called the anther.'),
  ('414a0ebb-c9c8-49ed-b061-b4e04877f417','f250e882-36a3-48cd-b9f6-1e41f0430c1b',1,'How many theca are present in a typical angiosperm anther?','A typical angiosperm anther is bilobed with each lobe having two theca, making it dithecous. The two theca are separated by a longitudinal groove.'),
  ('df9c4828-96f7-4b19-8c31-e0fa2753072e','ba935f80-0ef1-4a38-bcd6-f03282fd2860',1,'What is the term for an anther that has two theca?','An anther with two theca is called dithecous. Each lobe of the bilobed anther contains one theca, and together they form two theca.'),
  ('f9ba85d3-e696-48ed-b285-18687b65cec4','bf1dfd55-9cf7-42c8-8b4b-9567f92053b4',1,'How many microsporangia are present in a transverse section of a typical anther?','The anther is a four-sided (tetragonal) structure consisting of four microsporangia located at the corners, with two in each lobe.'),
  ('71a68e38-d524-483d-bd2c-a1b34ea22929','b724d0f0-361e-47d9-9925-ad4c7cab2984',1,'What do microsporangia develop into?','The microsporangia develop further and become pollen sacs. They extend longitudinally all through the length of an anther and are packed with pollen grains.'),
  ('b04075e7-96c6-4bd9-a8b8-6720c1289f5f','a7845bd9-8019-4101-9cbb-90a8b67ded73',1,'Which is the innermost wall layer of a microsporangium?','The innermost wall layer is the tapetum. It nourishes the developing pollen grains. The outer three layers (epidermis, endothecium, middle layers) perform protection and help in dehiscence.'),
  ('33e0d833-da0a-46f0-9408-8226242cfffb','e2235a89-0ab6-45e1-b403-92d0037dfbcd',1,'What is the function of the tapetum in the microsporangium?','The tapetum nourishes the developing pollen grains. Cells of the tapetum possess dense cytoplasm and generally have more than one nucleus.'),
  ('971a7ea5-4882-4ad9-b7de-899b5272c653','af4a1493-b09f-4bcb-986e-281cff9312a2',1,'What occupies the center of each microsporangium when the anther is young?','When the anther is young, a group of compactly arranged homogenous cells called the sporogenous tissue occupies the centre of each microsporangium.'),
  ('3dc357a9-dbe6-4fb1-9d4e-ce6aef98d0ef','f1804492-2196-4d8c-b47d-e16bd31fa707',1,'What is the process called when sporogenous tissue cells undergo meiotic divisions to form microspore tetrads?','Microsporogenesis is the process of formation of microspores from a pollen mother cell through meiosis. The microspores are arranged in a cluster of four cells called the microspore tetrad.'),
  ('f168a070-bbd0-43ca-aaca-990a55e0000c','ffb3101c-27f5-4244-ad9e-b2342b1305c7',1,'What is the ploidy level of cells in a microspore tetrad?','The cells of the tetrad are haploid (n) because they are formed through meiotic division of the diploid sporogenous tissue cells. Meiosis reduces the chromosome number by half.'),
  ('b3a01a75-e52f-4b65-be12-ff13a8465761','cf88ae41-096a-4b67-8a07-8636f19f8cca',1,'What is the process of formation of megaspores from the megaspore mother cell called?','Megasporogenesis is the process of formation of megaspores from the megaspore mother cell (MMC) through meiotic division.'),
  ('10332c7b-589a-4584-976b-0e1683aae75f','54fd6092-46f6-4651-80c4-03b62be78ea6',1,'Where is the megaspore mother cell (MMC) located in the ovule?','The megaspore mother cell (MMC) is located in the micropylar region of the nucellus. It is a large cell containing dense cytoplasm and a prominent nucleus.'),
  ('ca0591b2-bb47-4405-90b6-70201cae2bb7','020861a6-8f30-49a5-beba-7b2d15ad71c9',1,'What is the method of embryo sac formation from a single megaspore termed?','Monosporic development is the method where only one functional megaspore develops into the female gametophyte (embryo sac), while the other three degenerate.'),
  ('9f063603-1da2-4ecb-9809-61ff2b7cc845','ee4297ad-7bc9-4ea9-ba75-e77be918931c',1,'How many nuclei are present in the mature embryo sac after the 8-nucleate stage?','After the 8-nucleate stage, cell walls are laid down. Six of the eight nuclei are surrounded by cell walls and organized into cells, while the remaining two nuclei (polar nuclei) remain in the central cell. Thus, the mature embryo sac is 8-nucleate but 7-celled.'),
  ('a736330f-c320-44c8-9262-4e26e0e4f667','d95d9d0d-e765-41e6-9766-210845300912',1,'Which cells are grouped together at the micropylar end of the embryo sac?','The egg apparatus consists of three cells grouped at the micropylar end: two synergids and one egg cell. The synergids have special cellular thickenings called filiform apparatus.'),
  ('b993bd09-3a08-4a19-82c1-2334490a0146','77a620ba-66bf-487a-a5e9-5e15a5982cb3',1,'How many cells are at the chalazal end of the mature embryo sac?','Three cells are located at the chalazal end of the embryo sac and are called antipodals. These cells eventually degenerate.'),
  ('aabe80c3-d7c4-465e-9e20-4f3409cd4818','8fcbf56b-3055-47c7-a0c6-8d8b2440bb3a',1,'What is the function of the filiform apparatus present in synergids?','The filiform apparatus consists of special cellular thickenings at the micropylar tip of synergids and plays an important role in guiding the pollen tubes into the synergid.'),
  ('d34e8697-0e61-4ae2-bbb7-6ce7198f1a2f','7bdf253c-c61f-414d-81a0-f09019b24789',1,'How many cells does a typical angiosperm embryo sac contain at maturity?','A typical angiosperm embryo sac at maturity is 7-celled though it is 8-nucleate. The cells are: 2 synergids, 1 egg cell, 3 antipodals, and 1 central cell (containing 2 polar nuclei).'),
  ('5a6a18ed-53c8-4a97-a74a-bde405947a60','a1d0fd66-1f35-402d-aef0-58c60e6fe5eb',1,'What is the ploidy level of the megaspore mother cell (MMC)?','The megaspore mother cell (MMC) is diploid (2n) and undergoes meiosis to produce four haploid megaspores.'),
  ('70d69391-4852-436c-ae24-f847b076d1cf','0588f770-e5ea-42f5-8a4c-536e6833a3b6',1,'Which of the following statements about the 8-nucleate embryo sac is correct?','The mitotic divisions in embryo sac formation are strictly free nuclear, meaning nuclear divisions are not followed immediately by cell wall formation. After the 8-nucleate stage, cell walls are laid down.'),
  ('a4c9ef44-daf4-495f-b3d0-ac16865ce5e9','cfee8007-1a70-4bfd-b650-18ed5a9aeb91',1,'What happens to the three non-functional megaspores in monosporic development?','In monosporic development, one of the four megaspores is functional while the other three degenerate and do not participate in further development.'),
  ('6f29e078-75e6-45e3-8c07-5623eb9a395c','6e098b4a-2ed0-4f32-87ef-babb34c9c6cc',1,'What is the ploidy of the functional megaspore?','The functional megaspore is haploid (n) because it is formed by meiotic division of the diploid megaspore mother cell.'),
  ('56b0a503-8efd-404d-bbe7-1f6b1d05d39b','e4496db9-2f4d-4e8f-8503-484afe4a505e',1,'Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of wind-pollinated flowers?','Wind-pollinated flowers have light non-sticky pollen, well-exposed stamens, and large feathery stigmas to trap airborne pollen. They do NOT produce bright colours or nectar since they do not need to attract pollinators.'),
  ('1743a35b-d65a-4545-b806-8f9d4c74695f','2246f16e-4264-4860-aeab-33c6727564ea',1,'What structure in corn represents the stigma and style that wave in the wind to trap pollen grains?','The corn cob or ears represent the stigma and style which wave in the wind to trap pollen grains. Wind-pollinated flowers often have numerous flowers packed into an inflorescence with a single ovule in each ovary.'),
  ('f576dca9-35dc-4b1c-977a-6c335028312a','55185179-efc0-4066-83ae-1c886c5eb1c7',1,'How many genera of flowering plants are known to use water pollination?','Water pollination is quite rare in flowering plants and is limited to about 30 genera, mostly monocotyledons. This is in contrast to lower plant groups where water is a regular mode of transport for male gametes.'),
  ('2bcb7306-e59e-4fdb-9bd1-80c9df93f14b','a475c2da-3b02-4aa0-a2da-c1d1e08ec12d',1,'Which of the following plant groups regularly uses water as a mode of transport for male gametes?','Water is a regular mode of transport for male gametes among lower plant groups such as algae, bryophytes, and pteridophytes. It is believed that the distribution of some bryophytes and pteridophytes is limited because of the need for water for transport of male gametes and fertilisation.'),
  ('18ca2e95-845f-4814-9ea6-3ea0bc4c524a','008281af-a8d1-42e6-bfbf-51ff0a4c4fc0',1,'In Vallisneria, how do the male flowers or pollen grains reach the female flowers?','In Vallisneria, the female flowers reach the surface of water by a long stalk, and the male flowers or pollen grains are released onto the surface of water. They are carried passively by water currents, and some eventually reach the female flowers and the stigma.'),
  ('92888924-3c3d-4b7f-9f4a-5ffea4817584','f645102d-fd4b-4d47-aca5-9856379ef906',1,'What is a characteristic feature of pollen grains in seagrasses and other submerged water-pollinated plants?','In seagrasses and other water-pollinated plants where female flowers remain submerged, the pollen grains are released inside the water. The pollen grains in many such species are long, ribbon-like and are carried passively inside the water to reach the stigma.'),
  ('34711f8d-1d9a-4371-b55b-740c2394b34f','e4189517-65a2-4469-9366-ea879b77d35f',1,'Why are pollen grains in most water-pollinated species protected by a mucilaginous covering?','In most water-pollinated species, pollen grains are protected from wetting by a mucilaginous covering. This protective layer prevents the pollen from being damaged by water when it is transported through water to reach the stigma.'),
  ('ceff8327-1771-4d38-a70d-d8fb7e8682f1','0b4faf69-ae85-4f06-871d-6363ca33beba',1,'Which of the following aquatic plants does NOT use water for pollination?','Not all aquatic plants use water for pollination. In a majority of aquatic plants such as water hyacinth and water lily, the flowers emerge above the level of water and are pollinated by insects or wind as in most land plants. Vallisneria, Hydrilla, and Zostera use water pollination.'),
  ('c8829de7-1609-4827-b645-f31dc1b54c8b','cf477651-b972-4e68-9111-9f929decb0d1',1,'Why are wind and water pollinated flowers generally not colourful and do not produce nectar?','Both wind and water pollinated flowers are not very colourful and do not produce nectar because they do not need to attract biotic pollinators such as insects or animals. Wind and water are abiotic agents that do not require visual or chemical attraction.'),
  ('4f4e3d26-4fdd-4cdf-845e-a0b5796128c5','04402150-759e-4e7c-ae73-3d3324511157',1,'Wind-pollinated flowers often have how many ovules in each ovary?','Wind-pollinated flowers often have a single ovule in each ovary and numerous flowers packed into an inflorescence. This is an adaptation to maximise the chance of successful pollination given the random and uncertain nature of wind pollination.'),
  ('8df65f23-5f11-4667-a63c-cdc48a4e8874','33e21db5-ead8-4e2a-94b3-3ee949083805',1,'What is the advantage of having numerous flowers packed into a compact inflorescence in wind-pollinated plants?','Wind-pollinated flowers often have numerous flowers packed into an inflorescence to increase the chances of trapping airborne pollen grains. The corn cob is a familiar example where the ears represent the stigma and style that wave in the wind to trap pollen.'),
  ('fff06ac3-de8d-4b71-be55-e717c2789be7','28a50f62-6d55-46d6-b2a4-10cbc29bd6e7',1,'What limits the distribution of some bryophytes and pteridophytes according to the text?','It is believed, particularly for some bryophytes and pteridophytes, that their distribution is limited because of the need for water for the transport of male gametes and fertilisation. This dependence on water restricts them to moist habitats.'),
  ('131ca6ae-24ed-4436-80da-c04d3179da26','877daa1c-cbce-46a3-b192-d1289b2752c5',1,'Why do wind-pollinated flowers possess well-exposed stamens?','Wind-pollinated flowers possess well-exposed stamens so that the pollens are easily dispersed into wind currents. This exposure ensures maximum release of pollen into the air for effective wind dispersal.'),
  ('f8399300-17d6-4ff2-ad0c-a1cf4d00f827','74a3f749-87f8-4ab4-9c9a-c69150eae358',1,'Which of the following are examples of freshwater plants that use water pollination?','Some examples of water-pollinated plants are Vallisneria and Hydrilla which grow in fresh water. Several marine sea-grasses such as Zostera also use water pollination. Rose, jasmine, wheat, rice, mango, and papaya do not use water pollination.'),
  ('adcc8678-4e4b-44ff-9dfd-29f5530618e0','6dba1e8b-e38c-423c-89b7-073aaf1abfcd',1,'What are the required characteristics of pollen grains for effective wind pollination?','Wind pollination requires that the pollen grains are light and non-sticky so that they can be transported in wind currents. Heavy or sticky pollen would fall to the ground rather than being carried by the wind.'),
  ('d6d09a8e-b90d-451c-9a34-7fb51cdd4641','7661c52a-262e-44b7-8437-6dbaaf8871a3',1,'What is the ploidy level of the zygote formed after syngamy during double fertilisation?','The zygote is diploid (2n) because it is formed by the fusion of one haploid male gamete (n) with the haploid egg cell (n).'),
  ('42f930a0-03cb-4ef7-bf4f-57a80ca6f1c8','5f0ec000-0404-4862-9ddb-b01fc7914769',1,'What is the ploidy level of the primary endosperm nucleus (PEN) formed after triple fusion?','The primary endosperm nucleus is triploid (3n) because it is formed by the fusion of one haploid male gamete (n) with two polar nuclei (n + n).'),
  ('9ad3199b-039b-4d93-a65b-c801b26fb776','b4cea48e-ca96-45fb-8fad-91392a21a3c2',1,'Which two fusion events together constitute double fertilisation in flowering plants?','Double fertilisation involves two fusion events: syngamy (fusion of male gamete with egg cell to form zygote) and triple fusion (fusion of male gamete with two polar nuclei to form PEN).'),
  ('9b0116d4-b115-454c-b182-8b0619faf2d3','cd117744-1d07-4ce9-8f77-934127763462',1,'What is the primary endosperm cell (PEC) derived from?','The primary endosperm cell (PEC) develops from the primary endosperm nucleus (PEN) which is formed after triple fusion. The PEC then develops into the endosperm.'),
  ('f68af7e4-047c-450d-8251-a6385700ba50','50aa85d0-a400-4976-bbe5-c41e0b5472ce',1,'Which structure develops into the embryo after fertilisation?','The zygote develops into the embryo. The primary endosperm nucleus develops into the endosperm, and the synergids degenerate after fertilisation.'),
  ('753c28c4-0a99-4d0d-879c-8fe07023e1bc','132a51cb-0883-4c45-a266-ecbd7b5707de',1,'What is the function of the endosperm in seed development?','The endosperm provides nutrition to the developing embryo. It is rich in reserve food materials that support embryo growth.'),
  ('52a426d3-da46-4dfc-9826-58bee0791a95','b2d185f8-2d80-4956-8399-78cf8b241ff0',1,'Which of the following is a characteristic of the free-nuclear endosperm stage?','In free-nuclear endosperm, the primary endosperm nucleus undergoes successive nuclear divisions without immediate cell wall formation, resulting in multiple free nuclei.'),
  ('b35a728f-901b-45c3-a929-395840008f3c','2bc23895-779b-4e09-8ddc-b04ce5c47c4d',1,'What is the coconut water in tender coconut composed of?','Coconut water is free-nuclear endosperm containing thousands of nuclei. The surrounding white kernel is the cellular endosperm.'),
  ('0e9305b3-f578-4f70-91f4-369b40c3e5e1','931faf4e-dd7f-4c58-946f-bcf835f919fb',1,'Which part of the embryo is located above the level of cotyledons in a dicot?','The epicotyl is the portion of the embryonal axis above the level of cotyledons and terminates with the plumule or stem tip.'),
  ('2dc998eb-43b1-409e-9cec-e1e787b7486f','3df17bac-0841-443c-9142-d7d3cf7aec9e',1,'What does the hypocotyl terminate into at its lower end?','The hypocotyl is the cylindrical portion below the level of cotyledons and terminates at its lower end in the radicle or root tip, which is covered by a root cap.'),
  ('193a4ffd-f582-49b0-aa44-13ae4bd87f33','5afc34e5-463e-47d9-a702-741d98169b18',1,'Which structure in a grass embryo is equivalent to the cotyledon?','In grasses, the cotyledon is called the scutellum. It is situated towards one side (lateral) of the embryonal axis.'),
  ('593fa285-85e0-4496-a6a7-bbd2dccac680','d9323125-e2a3-4f51-a29f-a37ff830503a',1,'What is the coleorrhiza in a grass embryo?','The coleorrhiza is an undifferentiated sheath that encloses the radicle and root cap at the lower end of the grass embryo.'),
  ('f499cc61-8140-4605-929c-3a6b428ad16b','67353e37-9f6e-4bb9-b7aa-3fd7b446039d',1,'Which structure encloses the shoot apex and leaf primordia in a grass embryo?','The coleoptile is a hollow foliar structure that encloses the shoot apex and a few leaf primordia in the grass embryo.'),
  ('e3a05f6a-03ca-495b-b658-f406db8d3d83','a03c5ba1-98c6-47c2-a577-302e9d0001f5',1,'Why does the zygote typically divide only after some endosperm has formed?','The zygote divides only after some endosperm is formed as an adaptation to provide assured nutrition to the developing embryo.'),
  ('4ee52082-fa85-4b88-97dd-8d470c10a521','7e7b873d-2cdb-47f8-8293-feb7f4ae1904',1,'Which of the following stages is NOT part of the normal dicot embryo development sequence?','The normal sequence of dicot embryo development is: proembryo → globular embryo → heart-shaped embryo → mature embryo. Triangular embryo is not a standard stage.'),
  ('8b35226c-b449-41e6-ac1c-fb1d1f97f992','12f2cb08-0e44-4b19-b548-95901bb28753',1,'What is the coleoptile in a grass embryo?','The coleoptile is a hollow foliar structure that encloses the shoot apex and leaf primordia above the level of scutellum attachment.'),
  ('dcd83188-be00-42c7-801c-0dc2a4f8b1fa','8fa27cad-adbd-4912-b08e-16366fce24a8',1,'Which part of the embryonal axis in grasses has the shoot apex?','In grasses, the epicotyl (portion above the scutellum) contains the shoot apex and leaf primordia enclosed in the coleoptile.'),
  ('1cf52090-0cba-4656-bcb2-08b5672de391','a94a6c52-46fa-4a26-b2aa-0f20ed37e596',1,'What is the first stage of endosperm development after the primary endosperm nucleus is formed?','After the primary endosperm nucleus is formed, it undergoes successive nuclear divisions to form free-nuclear endosperm before cell walls are laid down.'),
  ('d1028384-d744-4bcc-ba41-89f77eb1c2ea','2e92cad4-4e54-4110-bbdb-cd9e5963ea4f',1,'Which of the following seeds has persistent endosperm?','Castor and coconut are examples of seeds with persistent endosperm. Pea, groundnut, and beans are non-albuminous seeds where endosperm is completely consumed.'),
  ('d8ae6731-ff33-4671-ab5c-3365fbb76385','f8297e75-c78a-4ffe-b5e7-d940e17d5741',1,'What is the term for the process where the primary endosperm nucleus divides repeatedly to form endosperm tissue?','Endosperm development is the process where the primary endosperm cell/nucleus divides repeatedly to form the triploid endosperm tissue that nourishes the embryo.'),
  ('859f2e46-9712-4ddd-b41b-323fd22c10c4','a735aed2-5387-42ce-a7b0-e5f5b9d383ca',1,'Which post-fertilisation event precedes embryo development in flowering plants?','Endosperm development always precedes embryo development. The primary endosperm cell divides repeatedly to form triploid endosperm tissue that provides nutrition to the developing embryo.'),
  ('8faa25e1-3d36-4c62-90b6-d24e2021b5ef','da2295d9-ff46-4b33-8337-791a810bfcfb',1,'What is the ploidy level of the primary endosperm nucleus (PEN) formed after triple fusion?','The primary endosperm nucleus is triploid (3n) because it is formed by the fusion of one male gamete (n) with two polar nuclei (n + n) located in the central cell of the embryo sac.'),
  ('4a1f0bc1-ecf7-4d36-bc74-6a985e1c6ef7','2a87f30f-2f47-462a-9171-e3b3d138577a',1,'What is the stage of endosperm development called when nuclear divisions occur without immediate cell wall formation?','Free-nuclear endosperm is the stage where the primary endosperm nucleus undergoes successive nuclear divisions without cell wall formation. This stage is seen in tender coconut water which contains thousands of free nuclei.'),
  ('397cdc01-0763-4df9-adb2-c1697a8d50e4','12fd0ff8-540a-4989-a031-6954e66d4dbc',1,'Which part of the coconut represents free-nuclear endosperm?','The coconut water from tender coconut is free-nuclear endosperm made up of thousands of nuclei. The surrounding white kernel is the cellular endosperm.'),
  ('b6e2dad1-d66f-43fd-a209-f2c47565171c','bd07fa58-8e0f-4388-89ec-083536d58dc4',1,'What is the function of the endosperm tissue in developing seeds?','The endosperm tissue cells are filled with reserve food materials and are used for the nutrition of the developing embryo.'),
  ('debd89b0-bacc-40b6-a5d6-5dc84925b8f9','ab394baf-c029-44b0-8a53-d2ae3081c397',1,'In which seeds is the endosperm completely consumed before seed maturation?','In non-albuminous seeds like pea and groundnut, the endosperm is completely consumed by the developing embryo before seed maturation. Albuminous seeds like castor and coconut retain endosperm.'),
  ('d3d8d770-ff49-41f8-b38c-f5ae4450a0c2','72f890e7-3c0c-43d2-ab7f-1b8077d273ae',1,'What is the term for seeds that retain a part of endosperm as it is not completely used up during embryo development?','Albuminous seeds retain a part of endosperm as it is not completely used up during embryo development. Examples include wheat, maize, barley, and castor.'),
  ('6f9902b2-53ac-40cc-816a-af599c5ea706','b07ee67b-a153-491d-849e-ef9b95f7661e',1,'What is the residual persistent nucellus in some seeds called?','In some seeds such as black pepper and beet, remnants of nucellus are persistent. This residual persistent nucellus is called perisperm.'),
  ('7c2f7123-658e-4060-aa9a-3fa82acf5d81','a22cacbe-323d-4e11-a9ff-ca1ad329e45e',1,'Where does the embryo develop within the embryo sac?','The embryo develops at the micropylar end of the embryo sac where the zygote is situated.'),
  ('be117bb3-abfd-45d4-8964-a497a23507c7','77ba4fbb-3478-4028-960a-6361f79686a7',1,'Why does the zygote divide only after certain amount of endosperm is formed?','Most zygotes divide only after certain amount of endosperm is formed. This is an adaptation to provide assured nutrition to the developing embryo.'),
  ('7a25994c-48f2-4bd7-961e-871aa6754c33','10ff07d0-2351-49d3-9a35-4e670911c83c',1,'Which of the following is NOT a stage in embryo development?','The developing embryo passes through stages such as proembryo, globular, and heart-shaped stages before maturation. Spherical embryo is not a standard stage mentioned.'),
  ('614abb5e-7940-4225-aa07-3eb2b59692c2','9d254989-a847-4543-b73b-271611865da7',1,'What is the portion of the embryonal axis above the level of cotyledons called?','The portion of embryonal axis above the level of cotyledons is the epicotyl, which terminates with the plumule or stem tip.'),
  ('660a2270-9f84-47fd-a544-e1c32b2994e5','d62c26bf-99bd-42f8-a07d-782a5f44b8f9',1,'What terminates at the lower end of the hypocotyl in a dicot embryo?','The cylindrical portion below the level of cotyledons is hypocotyl that terminates at its lower end in the radicle or root tip. The root tip is covered with a root cap.'),
  ('4676c620-ac89-4a49-b9ed-b8cb02f13027','43077785-0c50-4a0b-92cd-068ad085409e',1,'What is the cotyledon called in the grass family?','In the grass family, the cotyledon is called scutellum that is situated towards one side (lateral) of the embryonal axis.'),
  ('e5ffd676-fd79-41ea-9a4b-45914f505118','364180af-7486-494f-9bea-a88ed7ccebc2',1,'What is the undifferentiated sheath enclosing the radicle and root cap in monocot embryos called?','In monocot embryos, the radicle and root cap are enclosed in an undifferentiated sheath called coleorrhiza.'),
  ('4db8bc36-e567-4b4a-8db8-85df8998fec7','6124bcd9-95cf-4cb0-80ce-e70601958f03',1,'What is the hollow foliar structure enclosing the shoot apex and leaf primordia in monocot embryos?','The epicotyl has a shoot apex and a few leaf primordia enclosed in a hollow foliar structure called the coleoptile.'),
  ('3f9f0875-36db-4e56-9448-b57fad53ae90','df11de74-0e0b-438b-9114-b49b6d07f463',1,'What is the seed described as in angiosperms?','In angiosperms, the seed is the final product of sexual reproduction and is often described as a fertilised ovule.'),
  ('9b915fc3-45e1-4529-a1ef-9e6137784bc9','1e6e12c7-e810-4440-8d50-83e08f117c0e',1,'What are the three main components of a typical seed?','A seed typically consists of seed coat(s), cotyledon(s), and an embryo axis.'),
  ('cb2b103c-b713-4c68-92ee-52f9a72c0244','dbde3ebe-f383-4e9f-9c57-9a72122f53ba',1,'What happens to the integuments of ovules as seeds mature?','Integuments of ovules harden as tough protective seed coats as the seed matures. The micropyle remains as a small pore in the seed coat.'),
  ('942fa32c-cb07-45f4-9192-d33542ddb679','c6d79820-7f3f-4ad8-85d0-f5fa9435baa2',1,'What is the function of the micropyle remaining as a small pore in the seed coat?','The micropyle remains as a small pore in the seed coat. This facilitates entry of oxygen and water into the seed during germination.'),
  ('9b70dba6-cc60-4473-ac68-d0406eb89f24','7a9d6b3c-a6c1-4306-8af7-83f11ccda8b3',1,'What is the typical moisture content of mature seeds?','As the seed matures, its water content is reduced and seeds become relatively dry (10-15 percent moisture by mass).'),
  ('fa13e486-ce4d-452d-8cbe-35424aeec5b3','f72f12a2-7af5-4e9b-8152-f7a74597c411',1,'What state may the embryo enter when metabolic activity slows down in mature seeds?','The embryo may enter a state of inactivity called dormancy, or if favourable conditions are available, they germinate.'),
  ('965e1cf3-0327-46e7-b2ec-31ada9450330','865e64cc-f1db-4a7b-9204-dab6ae12929b',1,'What develops from the ovary after fertilisation?','As ovules mature into seeds, the ovary develops into a fruit. The wall of the ovary develops into the wall of fruit called pericarp.'),
  ('28f1857f-b9b6-453a-a1a9-7310f91b04dd','f354ead0-fb7e-4fb6-8c2e-e5d123602f84',1,'What is the wall of the fruit called?','The wall of the ovary develops into the wall of fruit called pericarp.'),
  ('479429da-81f6-449e-8104-2b8259f0226d','21cdf4df-ba05-4ae2-8e5a-64452f793395',1,'Which of the following is an example of a false fruit?','In apple, strawberry, and cashew, the thalamus also contributes to fruit formation. Such fruits are called false fruits.'),
  ('a54092f2-13d0-4296-bd92-08704e36a75e','905529c3-acce-4999-bba3-e015df511c90',1,'What are fruits that develop only from the ovary called?','Most fruits develop only from the ovary and are called true fruits. False fruits involve other floral parts like thalamus.'),
  ('6401e1da-d730-4379-8c46-f8d08eaab64e','9522d2eb-e872-474e-aec3-42f8c46b6355',1,'What is parthenocarpy?','Parthenocarpy is the development of fruits without fertilisation. Banana is one such example. Parthenocarpy can be induced through application of growth hormones.'),
  ('880d5a47-bcfa-497c-b214-f18c1146afa2','9b15ef7f-84e1-4db5-88f0-c2067dc6386f',1,'Why are seeds considered advantageous for angiosperms?','Seeds have better adaptive strategies for dispersal to new habitats and help the species to colonise in other areas. They also have food reserves and protective coats.'),
  ('a7a14993-35a2-4783-a804-1c65ef79ff11','735c9f0d-4a48-4f69-829e-07de2f9bc495',1,'What is the estimated age of the oldest viable seed of Lupinus arcticus found in Arctic Tundra?','The oldest viable seed is that of Lupinus arcticus excavated from Arctic Tundra, which germinated after an estimated record of 10,000 years of dormancy.'),
  ('43e9a6c2-254a-4dd3-8a5a-cc111178f0ca','2426e4b5-2592-45ad-a511-c7ae2ffc1f24',1,'Which of the following is a recent record of a very old viable seed?','A recent record of 2000 years old viable seed is of the date palm, Phoenix dactylifera discovered during archaeological excavation at King Herod''s palace near the Dead Sea.'),
  ('e45644f3-53ff-497e-b186-27158d922f20','1789d9de-a42c-42a8-a34e-81ea63d901a9',1,'What is the primary mechanism that brings male and female gametes together in flowering plants?','Pollination is the mechanism that transfers pollen grains from the anther to the stigma of a pistil, bringing the non-motile male and female gametes together for fertilization.'),
  ('6d12d6db-49b2-4188-b9fe-b3cd4e367f74','568cafc9-90bc-4cbe-a670-8c4c0bca6c96',1,'Which of the following is NOT a type of pollination based on the source of pollen?','Based on the source of pollen, pollination is divided into three types: Autogamy (same flower), Geitonogamy (different flower of same plant), and Xenogamy (different plant). Anemogamy is wind pollination based on the agent, not the source.'),
  ('13173371-205d-4a77-9e33-a2fe077c7f0e','b8db9351-0d54-40bb-afce-77b8b34c4244',1,'In which type of pollination does pollen transfer occur within the same flower?','Autogamy is the transfer of pollen grains from the anther to the stigma of the same flower. It requires synchrony in pollen release and stigma receptivity, and close proximity of anthers and stigma.'),
  ('33f57876-f39b-465a-b613-b4c21b5e5d90','ecabe44f-0eee-44ed-b80b-b6bbe2fdbca9',1,'Which type of flowers do not open at all and are invariably autogamous?','Cleistogamous flowers do not open at all. In such flowers, the anthers and stigma lie close to each other, and when anthers dehisce in the flower buds, pollen grains come in contact with the stigma to effect pollination.'),
  ('07f8edc2-1d2b-4a1b-825f-9911f6e9329f','dc327243-a140-40b3-ab58-a896d9943a6c',1,'Geitonogamy is genetically similar to which type of pollination?','Although geitonogamy is functionally cross-pollination involving a pollinating agent, genetically it is similar to autogamy since the pollen grains come from the same plant.'),
  ('7b894788-06c5-4940-a12f-164fba97bc70','20a6bb61-a371-4280-b984-39be606ea017',1,'Which is the only type of pollination that brings genetically different types of pollen grains to the stigma?','Xenogamy is the transfer of pollen grains from the anther to the stigma of a different plant. This is the only type of pollination that brings genetically different types of pollen grains to the stigma during pollination.'),
  ('2462b842-b0d4-4d21-a275-0afaf8ed1ffc','602fb18a-b344-493b-ab71-87e2ccd1dc42',1,'Which abiotic agents are used by plants for pollination?','Plants use two abiotic agents (wind and water) and one biotic agent (animals) to achieve pollination. Majority of plants use biotic agents for pollination.'),
  ('1dba993e-816f-49c1-83c6-89777c22688c','74f70571-cd7b-4b61-9eaa-7913e51d6c95',1,'Why are wind and water pollinated flowers not very colourful and do not produce nectar?','Wind and water pollinated flowers do not need to attract pollinators since their pollen transfer is mediated by abiotic agents. Colour and nectar are adaptations to attract biotic pollinators like insects and animals.'),
  ('1813922d-331c-4ff0-aa4a-6423339301a8','8aa81e55-c73d-4576-b653-6b7daeba16dd',1,'Which of the following plants produce both chasmogamous and cleistogamous flowers?','According to the text, some plants such as Viola (common pansy), Oxalis, and Commelina produce two types of flowers - chasmogamous flowers with exposed anthers and stigma, and cleistogamous flowers which do not open at all.'),
  ('e744e5a3-e608-4e68-a535-c65ceb2139d7','3215adfb-8798-4d86-8b01-95636f80a379',1,'What is the defining characteristic of cleistogamous flowers?','The text states that cleistogamous flowers do not open at all. In such flowers, the anthers and stigma lie close to each other, and when anthers dehisce in the flower buds, pollen grains come in contact with the stigma to effect pollination.'),
  ('3704a43e-4686-4291-a12f-9f79d35309a3','9ecb7268-e000-46ee-a497-c9f95d8eb722',1,'Why are cleistogamous flowers invariably autogamous?','The text clearly states that cleistogamous flowers are invariably autogamous as there is no chance of cross-pollen landing on the stigma since the flowers never open.'),
  ('aaf75c61-d479-42e3-a7dd-48dacce8033e','c72d639c-c8a4-424e-8895-10eab7b1b00a',1,'What is an advantage of cleistogamous flowers mentioned in the text?','The text mentions that cleistogamous flowers produce assured seed-set even in the absence of pollinators, which is a significant advantage for reproduction.'),
  ('a3337e57-c12d-4b6d-9816-43729aaaba5c','1684cc87-da1a-42ef-b8ae-a588869267a3',1,'What is geitonogamy?','Geitonogamy is defined as transfer of pollen grains from the anther to the stigma of another flower of the same plant. Although it is functionally cross-pollination involving a pollinating agent, genetically it is similar to autogamy since the pollen grains come from the same plant.'),
  ('3c000ee1-026c-41b0-b1c5-ebfcc77ffd5f','a3161b9d-705d-4eee-95fc-913294772e7f',1,'Which type of pollination brings genetically different types of pollen grains to the stigma?','Xenogamy is defined as transfer of pollen grains from anther to the stigma of a different plant. This is the only type of pollination which during pollination brings genetically different types of pollen grains to the stigma, promoting genetic diversity.'),
  ('aebe0dee-61fb-43c5-a200-ca675c9dd894','a8c5a4b6-5053-4205-9d4a-b795ec87e31d',1,'How many types of pollination agents are mentioned in the text?','The text mentions that plants use two abiotic agents (wind and water) and one biotic agent (animals) to achieve pollination, making a total of three types of pollination agents.'),
  ('d2418db8-dd6f-4a83-a706-0f98b977038b','225e3d3f-a478-49ff-824b-f2da45094242',1,'Which agents of pollination are abiotic according to the text?','The text explicitly states that plants use two abiotic agents - wind and water - and one biotic agent (animals) for pollination.'),
  ('7aa0dcbb-2d89-46ab-8e38-c4bb2034f05b','48d6e166-3f7f-4f6c-872f-bd685e4bf466',1,'What proportion of plants use abiotic agents for pollination?','The text states that majority of plants use biotic agents for pollination, and only a small proportion of plants use abiotic agents like wind and water.'),
  ('87d283fe-8e93-4abf-84d9-61badedffbe9','994669ce-6162-4b35-a62c-ff617d719076',1,'Why do wind and water pollinated flowers produce enormous amounts of pollen?','The text explains that pollen grains coming in contact with the stigma is a chance factor in both wind and water pollination. To compensate for these uncertainties and associated loss of pollen grains, the flowers produce enormous amount of pollen when compared to the number of ovules available.'),
  ('00fb8a33-6040-4984-8af2-36559734ce3f','57780ae7-b564-48b0-9d67-acaaa5ddb4ad',1,'Is geitonogamy genetically similar to autogamy or xenogamy?','The text states that although geitonogamy is functionally cross-pollination involving a pollinating agent, genetically it is similar to autogamy since the pollen grains come from the same plant.'),
  ('0a80a1a9-f4f4-44ce-bf70-fa2bb1a4c0c0','4d4cc9f3-1880-4f0c-9650-b71bbd066127',1,'What is the relationship between chasmogamous flowers and other species'' flowers?','The text states that chasmogamous flowers are similar to flowers of other species with exposed anthers and stigma, unlike cleistogamous flowers which do not open at all.'),
  ('f4d7953f-193f-46c8-9260-595df8227a80','7f122678-b75e-4e60-afec-8c2194eceb15',1,'Which statement about cleistogamy is true based on the text?','The text explicitly mentions that cleistogamous flowers produce assured seed-set even in the absence of pollinators, making it a reliable reproductive strategy.'),
  ('fdc1de88-2f74-4ded-a714-a571169b3a29','dfa7fa6f-e20e-4599-b2b0-7a3ed1f663b2',1,'What happens when anthers dehisce in cleistogamous flower buds?','The text states that when anthers dehisce in the flower buds of cleistogamous flowers, pollen grains come in contact with the stigma to effect pollination, since the anthers and stigma lie close to each other.'),
  ('de09befd-4811-4e53-a91e-b0a463aad097','35a8fe8a-99b0-4189-ad92-0183d5ca6b26',1,'Which type of pollination is functionally cross-pollination but genetically similar to self-pollination?','Geitonogamy is functionally cross-pollination because it involves transfer between different flowers, but genetically it is similar to autogamy (self-pollination) because the pollen comes from the same plant.'),
  ('6a3a396c-d83a-40ea-a887-4d1b13ded3fe','b423b1a8-420d-41aa-965d-a9df1fcdf7fa',1,'What is the main difference between chasmogamous and cleistogamous flowers?','The text clearly differentiates chasmogamous flowers as having exposed anthers and stigma (similar to other species), while cleistogamous flowers do not open at all.'),
  ('bed73abb-08ec-40d7-8b19-b4cbb741e694','20a3b212-3ff2-46d1-999a-b956a1391ada',1,'Which of the following is NOT mentioned as an abiotic pollination agent in the text?','The text mentions wind and water as abiotic agents, and animals as the biotic agent. Therefore, animals is NOT an abiotic pollination agent.'),
  ('11bca77d-af69-4172-8b8c-890896c5d660','b0c5d297-5aba-469f-bf33-6ee80ecd2148',1,'What is the genetic consequence of xenogamy compared to geitonogamy?','The text states that xenogamy is the only type of pollination that brings genetically different types of pollen grains, while geitonogamy is genetically similar to autogamy since pollen comes from the same plant.'),
  ('ffb0300c-8d6e-424d-b421-8a862cd5b110','22579532-41e2-430b-9bac-da029480a590',1,'In which type of flowers do anthers and stigma lie close to each other?','The text specifically mentions that in cleistogamous flowers, the anthers and stigma lie close to each other, enabling self-pollination when anthers dehisce in the flower buds.'),
  ('24ca08bf-4419-42ab-ba5c-327b4ed94aa3','0dffcc97-d9a0-4426-8726-5b5de2a2c521',1,'Which pollination type is described as ''functionally cross-pollination involving a pollinating agent''?','The text explicitly describes geitonogamy as functionally cross-pollination involving a pollinating agent, even though genetically it is similar to autogamy.'),
  ('45bbd3b8-47f5-4b32-81e5-6705459821b6','0e5d7d88-861c-436a-b1db-aedaff81de68',1,'After compatible pollination, where does the pollen grain germinate to produce a pollen tube?','Following compatible pollination, the pollen grain germinates on the stigma to produce a pollen tube through one of the germ pores.'),
  ('53786564-d692-4b0d-af32-a2dd16d8cc12','19d00ba7-8b1a-4688-b36a-53604b61bf33',1,'What structure does the pollen tube enter after reaching the ovary?','Pollen tube, after reaching the ovary, enters the ovule through the micropyle.'),
  ('bd6b1a30-9739-4e2e-aa46-0df4000026ed','63d8b94e-a047-428d-b18c-e4c7ae470331',1,'Which structure guides the entry of the pollen tube into the synergid?','The filiform apparatus present at the micropylar part of the synergids guides the entry of pollen tube.'),
  ('ac0cc379-061c-4fe9-8c11-716c25403074','f2f4b8f2-9f1f-4edd-a8e1-167cd87d3f11',1,'What are all events from pollen deposition on stigma until pollen tubes enter the ovule collectively called?','All events from pollen deposition on the stigma until pollen tubes enter the ovule are together referred to as pollen-pistil interaction.'),
  ('d7f89805-96e2-442e-bd7a-c4735bd82919','aa06ab9c-e272-42b7-9866-a2f63a11ba06',1,'In plants shedding pollen at two-celled stage, when does the generative cell divide to form two male gametes?','In plants shedding pollen at two-celled condition, the generative cell divides and forms the two male gametes during the growth of pollen tube in the stigma.'),
  ('fb4e12ca-91ec-42f2-a0fd-ad24ba8b87ac','730d07bc-b59e-4cb7-9747-39517ece7ea4',1,'What is the purpose of bagging in artificial hybridization?','Emasculated flowers are covered with a bag to prevent contamination of its stigma with unwanted pollen. This process is called bagging.'),
  ('91bfbb21-96c2-4ce8-a690-5658739346d4','9ed2ec47-25c2-4e5e-9a73-c058ad420deb',1,'What is emasculation in plant breeding?','Emasculation is the removal of anthers from the flower bud before the anther dehisces using a pair of forceps.'),
  ('8f0b3653-218b-4301-9c89-8989a7e290e3','c3fd22dd-0dc6-4c51-abd5-6f5088864249',1,'Which flowers do NOT require emasculation in artificial hybridization?','If the female parent produces unisexual flowers, there is no need for emasculation.'),
  ('0a58d97d-d88c-4024-a8ab-49dcfc95147a','50fddf8f-09a8-48ea-96c5-d460342d092c',1,'What concentration of sugar solution is recommended for studying pollen germination?','You can study pollen germination by dusting pollen on a glass slide containing a drop of sugar solution (about 10 per cent).'),
  ('129a9344-7a0b-4be7-8e01-17b998ebff3b','779afa65-c736-423d-8894-94da58436dc1',1,'How long should you wait after dusting pollen on sugar solution before observing under microscope?','After about 15-30 minutes, observe the slide under the low power lens of the microscope.'),
  ('f3642f32-e650-48eb-ae74-37cea2885962','85b55741-b14b-484a-9b28-c44f60306b01',1,'What is the main goal of artificial hybridization in crop improvement?','A breeder is interested in crossing different species to combine desirable characters to produce commercially superior varieties.'),
  ('cc35b436-48fe-411f-8a4f-58adbdf089ca','301296d1-ea63-49ff-a014-60ee26ae0ff3',1,'Which of these flowers is NOT mentioned for studying pollen germination?','Flowers mentioned for studying pollen germination include pea, chickpea, Crotalaria, balsam and Vinca. Rose is not mentioned.'),
  ('cdcea985-fe49-4fa9-85d6-75623291580b','a0fc46ef-db9d-4019-aece-693fc166b937',1,'What material is generally used for making bags in the bagging technique?','Emasculated flowers are covered with a bag generally made up of butter paper.'),
  ('871146f0-4a31-4cb8-82d0-011015fae7a9','ba6af5ab-8973-415b-ba2b-83aed11f2301',1,'When is the desired pollen dusted on the stigma of a bagged flower?','When the stigma of bagged flower attains receptivity, mature pollen grains collected from anthers of the male parent are dusted on the stigma.'),
  ('9eee7ff4-b6a1-4f44-bccf-68a86a671e0d','6cea96d5-8adc-4ebb-9a90-eb069cb459db',1,'What happens to flowers after dusting with desired pollen in bagging technique?','After dusting the desired pollen on the stigma, the flowers are rebagged and the fruits allowed to develop.'),
  ('ac6119b0-7ca5-4b7e-8f4f-a0ae52259b21','19364294-cb4a-441d-97df-515aa243cede',1,'Which part of a dicot embryo terminates with the plumule or stem tip?','The epicotyl is the portion of the embryonal axis above the level of cotyledons and it terminates with the plumule or stem tip.'),
  ('2a778ea7-e593-45e8-b37f-b246721b3e4b','25141c80-e0f6-4f20-a200-a9c6ec5e8853',1,'In monocotyledonous embryos, what is the cotyledon called in the grass family?','In the grass family, the cotyledon is called scutellum and it is situated towards one side (lateral) of the embryonal axis.'),
  ('99c35c8d-da14-4ed7-a73c-f33598194bdb','db7f3d66-9d02-4b1a-bd63-47616b74796b',1,'What structure encloses the radicle and root cap in monocot embryos?','In monocot embryos, the radicle and root cap are enclosed in an undifferentiated sheath called coleorrhiza.'),
  ('911dfbb5-52fa-4512-977c-29b3dfcca35b','b7d106b8-7f84-4576-92e9-7a0d840981ef',1,'The shoot apex and leaf primordia in monocot embryos are enclosed in which structure?','The epicotyl has a shoot apex and a few leaf primordia enclosed in a hollow foliar structure called the coleoptile.'),
  ('0e505fa0-ecf4-411d-a1b8-d2b70a60e100','1fc40858-ae2b-412c-b94a-b9776149eed3',1,'In angiosperms, the seed is often described as:','In angiosperms, the seed is the final product of sexual reproduction and is often described as a fertilised ovule.'),
  ('cffa0d30-fddb-4347-bd29-df94f7f2c7c4','0fead2e6-2f53-48fd-ae2f-a25e9f07a2f8',1,'Which of the following is a non-albuminous seed?','Non-albuminous seeds have no residual endosperm as it is completely consumed during embryo development. Examples include pea and groundnut.'),
  ('fce4c974-8d1c-40b1-bbb0-2379f55b95dd','d5aba158-4058-426b-bbbe-b786858df85e',1,'Albuminous seeds retain endosperm because:','Albuminous seeds retain a part of endosperm as it is not completely used up during embryo development. Examples include wheat, maize, barley, and castor.'),
  ('db3b24d0-50f4-4b35-94d9-5c9f4385dca3','d01f20ea-c291-41a7-86e6-c604a32476cf',1,'Perisperm refers to the persistent residual:','In some seeds such as black pepper and beet, remnants of nucellus are persistent. This residual, persistent nucellus is called perisperm.'),
  ('32c63e14-ff4f-4743-a35f-08cd57540896','33e87668-c89d-4d71-a180-ad474403bb14',1,'The seed coat is formed from the hardening of:','Integuments of ovules harden as tough protective seed coats. The micropyle remains as a small pore in the seed coat.'),
  ('7ba75b32-882b-4902-9644-9308f6885b59','ce3d96ea-384c-4013-bdda-1ec73cbaf32f',1,'What is the function of the micropyle in a mature seed?','The micropyle remains as a small pore in the seed coat. This facilitates entry of oxygen and water into the seed during germination.'),
  ('e709f48d-c0d8-4e14-8d90-b8861eb9d386','f0f0e1a8-841a-44a3-a1f3-d8a426dc685b',1,'As the seed matures, its water content is reduced to approximately:','As the seed matures, its water content is reduced and seeds become relatively dry with 10-15% moisture by mass.'),
  ('0f984627-25cb-434d-8791-0499e2d74db0','ea78a1cf-9d0f-43ad-ac9c-8a253538edc8',1,'The state of inactivity that the embryo may enter during seed maturation is called:','The embryo may enter a state of inactivity called dormancy, or if favourable conditions are available, they germinate.'),
  ('85ec3000-c0e3-44cf-b41f-c254ccd9380d','3320f344-0934-41ff-9112-dfa4c87d52af',1,'The pericarp is developed from the:','The wall of the ovary develops into the wall of fruit called pericarp.'),
  ('1899a014-314f-4a13-b6be-42a062918c5a','adf9e3da-76a7-4e74-b34f-98f3a91cbfee',1,'Which of the following is a false fruit?','In apple, strawberry, and cashew, the thalamus also contributes to fruit formation. Such fruits are called false fruits.'),
  ('93e5b20b-44b2-4a3f-b2eb-354696980c7f','1cbe6d77-b4dd-471a-bb7d-7d13a5c5c2a8',1,'Parthenocarpic fruits are those that develop:','Parthenocarpic fruits develop without fertilisation. Banana is one such example. Parthenocarpy can be induced through the application of growth hormones.'),
  ('0e07a763-33b4-4be8-b18b-870d9ab53a3d','4cb50a01-c1a4-4e6a-ba8d-bda58c6eaac1',1,'True fruits are those that develop:','Most fruits develop only from the ovary and are called true fruits.'),
  ('bf08b93b-6bf4-46c7-9d99-6d2fe2ae486f','cb0b5cab-49bd-4360-aceb-bb743416feb0',1,'For seed germination, which of the following conditions is NOT required?','If favourable conditions are available such as adequate moisture, oxygen and suitable temperature, seeds germinate. Direct sunlight is not mentioned as a requirement.'),
  ('471d7594-7551-43b9-a733-ea1c6a9d7964','35b8f377-398c-4ffd-acae-7ab6531bfe4a',1,'Which of the following is a dry fruit?','Fruits may be fleshy as in guava, orange, mango, or may be dry as in groundnut and mustard.'),
  ('3b3a83a2-c21e-468f-b961-39302ea214e2','8c84fa31-6184-4f30-93c5-46e58148a117',1,'A seed typically consists of all EXCEPT:','A seed typically consists of seed coat(s), cotyledon(s) and an embryo axis. Tapetum is a wall layer of the microsporangium in the anther, not a seed component.'),
  ('c9cb57e9-9f9d-417d-990a-556671ad1558','6d291983-017c-453e-a504-425bb6c51871',1,'In legumes, cotyledons are generally thick and swollen due to:','The cotyledons of the embryo are simple structures, generally thick and swollen due to storage of food reserves as in legumes.'),
  ('f07c3199-4376-4e10-9560-3846dffaf70c','c5852067-d949-4364-a95f-cde87d7df2c4',1,'As ovules mature into seeds, what develops simultaneously?','As ovules mature into seeds, the ovary develops into a fruit. The transformation of ovules into seeds and ovary into fruit proceeds simultaneously.'),
  ('a0821e28-8b6c-4f71-a8c5-d31508c7fd4a','61ec491e-f0da-455c-be8e-8531d7876de3',1,'In grass embryos, the scutellum is situated:','In the grass family, the cotyledon called scutellum is situated towards one side (lateral) of the embryonal axis.'),
  ('430f3311-1575-4cd1-974a-f40198442be0','b6446341-9f7c-4ae5-b675-a26ab92a71ea',1,'In monocot embryos, the portion of the embryonal axis above the level of attachment of scutellum is called:','The portion of the embryonal axis above the level of attachment of scutellum is the epicotyl. Epicotyl has a shoot apex and a few leaf primordia enclosed in the coleoptile.'),
  ('aab0b716-8f5b-46a9-9fe5-d1c12724abb2','f41efcb4-ebbb-4e4e-b17f-11289886683a',1,'The hypocotyl in a dicot embryo terminates at its lower end in the:','The cylindrical portion below the level of cotyledons is hypocotyl that terminates at its lower end in the radicle or root tip. The root tip is covered with a root cap.'),
  ('e3ea696a-5d45-4c8d-9db3-1f3031e89910','df94a22a-4ba4-41aa-896c-d16cee06697a',1,'Black pepper seeds contain persistent remnants of:','In some seeds such as black pepper and beet, remnants of nucellus are persistent. This residual, persistent nucellus is the perisperm.'),
  ('2519e83f-489e-4f96-b983-103b1d4936d6','57577e93-ca49-43d1-8ff2-89932e02fcdf',1,'Parthenocarpy can be induced through:','Parthenocarpy can be induced through the application of growth hormones and such fruits are seedless.'),
  ('19f57e5a-de93-48a8-a39b-aa4d553bc7d5','28654121-bb4e-44b9-bcc5-a4eac30ebfce',1,'According to Figure 1.15, which structure is shown as a small pore in the seed coat?','Figure 1.15 shows the micropyle as a small pore in the seed coat. The micropyle facilitates entry of oxygen and water into the seed during germination.'),
  ('6c7f3ef5-7534-4c20-94f1-c68449ce8024','60b6685b-7dce-4200-9830-d29d98f26a7c',1,'Which part of the flower contributes to the formation of a false fruit in apple?','In apple, strawberry, and cashew, the thalamus also contributes to fruit formation. Such fruits are called false fruits.'),
  ('89d33f05-8b6b-4816-9459-e630ab2bdee5','52a1b860-7984-4d51-aa04-88eba51fca26',1,'During seed maturation, the general metabolic activity of the embryo:','As the seed matures, its water content is reduced and the general metabolic activity of the embryo slows down. The embryo may enter a state of inactivity called dormancy.'),
  ('3c5ab08a-3f16-4773-ba57-758a8f9a654b','94233e94-3895-4d9d-999f-6ddef0ee1ffe',1,'The textbook suggests soaking seeds overnight in water to observe:','The textbook suggests soaking seeds in water overnight, then splitting the seeds to observe the various parts of the embryo and the seed.'),
  ('51e7d145-6a03-4838-9206-3394bc0a65cb','eb65bc9c-96f9-4b71-aa44-e55746b1b57f',1,'What is one of the primary advantages of seeds in angiosperms regarding pollination and fertilization?','Seeds offer several advantages to angiosperms. Since reproductive processes such as pollination and fertilisation are independent of water, seed formation is more dependable.'),
  ('d808bba7-c061-4739-b0d5-a6bf05378f4d','0e766fc7-b3a0-4ae5-85b3-ae183fad927d',1,'How do seeds help angiosperms colonize new habitats?','Seeds have better adaptive strategies for dispersal to new habitats and help the species to colonise in other areas.'),
  ('d51dea04-a787-496f-96fe-9e19169aae87','f14d7f3c-d3f6-41c6-85ba-cebc5b320be8',1,'Why are young seedlings nourished by seeds until they can perform photosynthesis?','As they have sufficient food reserves, young seedlings are nourished until they are capable of photosynthesis on their own.'),
  ('13f67a86-1e33-4c52-ab63-dcc6b3c8a484','eed64e89-88be-41d5-b792-876310f082b1',1,'What is the function of the hard seed coat in angiosperm seeds?','The hard seed coat provides protection to the young embryo.'),
  ('6856b842-aca3-4055-af45-7c91c8752139','43b69d8c-4934-414d-9f12-c99250edc17c',1,'How do seeds contribute to genetic diversity in angiosperms?','Being products of sexual reproduction, they generate new genetic combinations leading to variations.'),
  ('4132ca72-e722-4835-be7a-d259c39ca4f8','d7169056-36d0-4fee-9984-f969ba913a26',1,'Why is the seed considered the basis of agriculture?','Seed is the basis of our agriculture. Dehydration and dormancy of mature seeds are crucial for storage of seeds which can be used as food throughout the year and also to raise crop in the next season.'),
  ('e41b4f7b-4a91-46a6-9117-71710ec3c12c','c8e6b5a2-b831-4438-a3bb-88ff8d38660a',1,'How long can seeds of different species remain viable after dispersal?','How long do the seeds remain alive after they are dispersed? This period again varies greatly. In a few species the seeds lose viability within a few months. Seeds of a large number of species live for several years. Some seeds can remain alive for hundreds of years.'),
  ('1b030121-5fa1-44db-8561-473a5a0525dc','14258a3e-d419-42b6-9dcf-0dca39d615cc',1,'What is the record for the oldest viable seed mentioned in the text?','The oldest is that of a lupine, Lupinus arcticus excavated from Arctic Tundra. The seed germinated and flowered after an estimated record of 10,000 years of dormancy.'),
  ('8fadf120-ffe3-4233-b863-73d91a857306','97dd7daf-6c6a-458a-90ff-c2ff9f260caf',1,'Where was the 2000-year-old viable date palm seed discovered?','A recent record of 2000 years old viable seed is of the date palm, Phoenix dactylifera discovered during the archeological excavation at King Herod''s palace near the Dead Sea.'),
  ('100495f0-62e7-49d8-a550-4562d52a9aad','c1709c40-0521-49ed-9bde-f43ef2536977',1,'Which plant family produces fruits containing thousands of tiny seeds?','Can you think of some plants in which fruits contain very large number of seeds. Orchid fruits are one such category and each fruit contain thousands of tiny seeds.'),
  ('d9d4f9a0-5510-49ca-8233-e037cbeea49d','cffaf751-1ba3-4a46-9718-04a38bdf0f25',1,'Which parasitic species mentioned in the text produce fruits with many tiny seeds similar to orchids?','Similar is the case in fruits of some parasitic species such as Orobanche and Striga.'),
  ('9496f7e8-0148-4042-89d6-146a6dc6a825','0756f3d8-b096-4ca5-a99a-0eed7e70a1b5',1,'What remarkable characteristic is mentioned about the Ficus tree in relation to its seeds?','Have you seen a tiny seed of Ficus? How large is the tree of Ficus developed from that tiny seed. How many billions of seeds does each Ficus tree produce?'),
  ('612cd7bd-a15c-4886-bca3-3b124d11aca7','5f2cb486-8885-4c9a-b619-65f38bb4ad6c',1,'What is apomixis in flowering plants?','Although seeds, in general are the products of fertilisation, a few flowering plants such as some species of Asteraceae and grasses, have evolved a special mechanism, to produce seeds without fertilisation, called apomixis.'),
  ('7c1c1748-676a-450e-b15d-0a065b37a16b','b61f7fa9-ca89-4bf6-bb77-56fbaba00da6',1,'How is apomixis characterized in terms of reproduction?','Thus, apomixis is a form of asexual reproduction that mimics sexual reproduction.'),
  ('e4bb38bc-6f8a-49a0-bb26-8c28322370cb','37af7840-37ea-45b1-8eba-64ee036e0788',1,'In some species, how does the diploid egg cell develop into an embryo without fertilization?','In some species, the diploid egg cell is formed without reduction division and develops into the embryo without fertilisation.'),
  ('025a0558-9675-4501-8837-5b03e64058a0','b0b431cd-10d4-41db-a7e4-3ca3f56299e5',1,'In Citrus and Mango varieties, what happens to the nucellar cells during apomixis?','More often, as in many Citrus and Mango varieties some of the nucellar cells surrounding the embryo sac start dividing, protrude into the embryo sac and develop into the embryos.'),
  ('58782a5f-a791-40cc-ad37-d1979533bdb4','eda3795c-ec5a-4c47-80c9-157fb5debac3',1,'What is polyembryony?','Occurrence of more than one embryo in a seed is referred to as polyembryony.'),
  ('acbee463-d26d-4bcf-b6fa-8742e6171293','2089c90f-bde4-413e-9157-26996d8c7acc',1,'What is one major problem with hybrid seeds that makes them expensive for farmers?','One of the problems of hybrids is that hybrid seeds have to be produced every year. If the seeds collected from hybrids are sown, the plants in the progeny will segregate and do not maintain hybrid characters. Production of hybrid seeds is costly.'),
  ('ac94923c-c37c-4462-bec8-774ccfa079a3','a59aa4e6-10f6-422d-b1a2-1ae50edf9d52',1,'Why would making hybrids into apomicts be beneficial for farmers?','If these hybrids are made into apomicts, there is no segregation of characters in the hybrid progeny. Then the farmers can keep on using the hybrid seeds to raise new crop year after year and he does not have to buy hybrid seeds every year.'),
  ('7d06af89-b788-41f7-8a85-a8f215fcb26d','c33863a4-92c2-48a7-b51b-7499e505b29c',1,'Why is active research being conducted on apomixis in laboratories worldwide?','Because of the importance of apomixis in hybrid seed industry, active research is going on in many laboratories around the world to understand the genetics of apomixis and to transfer apomictic genes into hybrid varieties.'),
  ('22f95132-ec54-43db-ba19-bbc016c2dd67','dcc081a2-bd2c-49ba-a26f-4d1cea2cb373',1,'Which of the following represents the male reproductive organs in a flower?','According to the summary, the androecium consisting of stamens represents the male reproductive organs in the flower, while the gynoecium consisting of pistils represents the female reproductive organs.'),
  ('7ed0727d-56ca-47d5-be52-0d6447e0141a','a14bad99-d358-4531-83a8-db81c6f45779',1,'How many wall layers surround the microsporangium in a typical anther?','The summary states that four wall layers - the epidermis, endothecium, middle layers and the tapetum - surround the microsporangium.'),
  ('e5d0e8d0-1092-46f2-b3c2-681418371df2','f067d270-f812-4853-93d6-fc99e8907508',1,'What is the process called when cells of the sporogenous tissue undergo meiosis to form tetrads of microspores?','The summary clearly states that cells of the sporogenous tissue undergo meiosis in a process called microsporogenesis to form tetrads of microspores.'),
  ('bdc26d11-975f-49a0-a62b-558e7a6a9038','d0badcbf-ad61-40d8-bde1-65b866d0c09c',1,'The outer layer of the pollen grain wall called exine is made up of which resistant material?','According to the summary, the exine is made up of sporopollenin, which is one of the most resistant organic materials known.'),
  ('523cf384-6ae7-4974-bb93-9e3915e8f5c3','38dc72fe-1891-47e7-9616-7bb17befbcd9',1,'At the time of shedding, pollen grains may contain how many cells?','The summary states that pollen grains may have two cells (a vegetative cell and generative cell) or three cells (a vegetative cell and two male gametes) at the time of shedding.'),
  ('a02c15ff-6d19-4c47-ad04-1c61d7399c7a','ad69d60b-a865-47b6-8ef8-20855c5cc7a6',1,'Which part of the ovule serves as the stalk attaching it to the placenta?','The summary mentions that ovules have a stalk called funicle that attaches them to the placenta.'),
  ('e402e90f-09e0-4e0f-bfe2-cc6729c3956b','684fec50-dd4b-4c54-9fa5-5fc85d26142d',1,'What is the nature of the mature embryo sac in angiosperms?','The summary explicitly states that the mature embryo sac is 7-celled and 8-nucleate, with the egg apparatus at the micropylar end, three antipodals at the chalazal end, and a central cell with two polar nuclei.'),
  ('b6888fbd-4a0a-4acc-9347-41ae24363a51','38cd5231-69a9-4322-8e90-a0bc1fc1b7a5',1,'Which of the following is NOT an abiotic pollinating agent mentioned in the summary?','The summary states that pollinating agents are either abiotic (wind and water) or biotic (animals). Therefore, animals are biotic agents, not abiotic.'),
  ('feb2f10c-5694-49d8-be0a-86d449fbfd1d','ff1d0d01-8a4c-46a4-af0e-d971063118e7',1,'What are the two fusion events that occur during double fertilisation in angiosperms?','The summary states that angiosperms exhibit double fertilisation because two fusion events occur: syngamy (fusion of one male gamete with egg cell) and triple fusion (fusion of second male gamete with two polar nuclei).'),
  ('8050f929-3b00-44d6-8737-3fc1228023bf','c4a7e15b-3264-4be8-bcad-ee17448966d9',1,'What is the phenomenon called when seeds are formed without fertilisation in some angiosperms?','The summary states that apomixis is a phenomenon found in some angiosperms, particularly in grasses, which results in the formation of seeds without fertilisation.'),
  ('0e193d52-21a0-437c-b1eb-346c9ed77ce7','d8ddfa99-8aaa-4059-8bad-42524736ba6f',1,'Which tissue develops into the endosperm after double fertilisation?','The summary states that the primary endosperm cell forms the endosperm tissue, while the zygote develops into the embryo.'),
  ('fffc55b3-1810-4105-aa3d-a2a0e6c111e7','d6c36e07-baa3-4d49-8e87-c34773dd68e0',1,'How many cotyledons does a mature dicotyledonous embryo possess?','The summary clearly states that a mature dicotyledonous embryo has two cotyledons and an embryonal axis with epicotyl and hypocotyl.'),
  ('ae8d0c87-42ec-44e1-93b8-89058e76d1c3','6a576c24-5d4a-48e2-b468-5fb6016f2989',1,'What is the phenomenon called when more than one embryo is produced in a seed?','The summary states that some angiosperms produce more than one embryo in their seed, and this phenomenon is called polyembryony.'),
  ('9b71d4b7-ff62-4965-be53-413a23d26d10','d407413f-7617-4efe-8108-13d9794a6a71',1,'Which structure develops into fruit after fertilisation?','The summary states that after fertilisation, the ovary develops into fruit and ovules develop into seeds.'),
  ('78a0a60b-827e-44cf-8710-765514b960e6','a53658cf-d68a-4882-91ad-aba5b3e2285e',1,'What happens to incompatible pollen during pollen-pistil interaction?','The summary states that pollen-pistil interaction involves pollen inhibition when the pollen is incompatible, and promotion of events when the pollen is compatible.'),
  ('bbb9605f-0238-418d-9dad-d220b233f642','805250aa-bc49-490a-bbe4-755a035df8c8',1,'In which part of an angiosperm flower does the development of the male gametophyte take place?','The male gametophyte (pollen grain) develops inside the anther, specifically within the microsporangia (pollen sacs). The anther is the terminal bilobed structure of the stamen that produces pollen grains through microsporogenesis.'),
  ('12d67077-571f-4872-8f32-7ce8b9718209','38f4962f-4590-4bae-8645-7b2bce6b2ffa',1,'Which type of cell division occurs during both microsporogenesis and megasporogenesis?','Both microsporogenesis and megasporogenesis involve meiotic division. In microsporogenesis, the pollen mother cell undergoes meiosis to form microspore tetrads. In megasporogenesis, the megaspore mother cell undergoes meiosis to produce four megaspores. Meiosis reduces the chromosome number from diploid (2n) to haploid (n).'),
  ('83e0b309-44e9-4d5a-8cc7-ad681ee51a62','10026ded-3c4e-4170-8535-2d905b0460c9',1,'Arrange the following in the correct developmental sequence: Pollen grain, sporogenous tissue, microspore tetrad, pollen mother cell, male gametes.','The correct sequence is: Sporogenous tissue (compact cells in centre of microsporangium) → Pollen mother cell (PMC undergoes meiosis) → Microspore tetrad (four haploid cells after meiosis) → Pollen grain (mature male gametophyte after dissociation) → Male gametes (formed by division of generative cell).'),
  ('476ca4b9-67f6-45f7-931a-5eea9c4bb407','e8830bc1-a7eb-44e2-90bd-047dd8db59e7',1,'Which structure in a typical angiosperm ovule connects the ovule to the placenta?','The funicle is the stalk that attaches the ovule to the placenta. The hilum is the junction between the ovule and funicle. The micropyle is the small opening in the integuments, and the chalaza is the basal part of the ovule opposite the micropyle.'),
  ('618b9a54-34dd-4ed5-a61e-0e47e582d47d','2e421f65-3d58-4dda-88b1-c3798573ea08',1,'What is meant by monosporic development of the female gametophyte?','Monosporic development refers to the formation of the embryo sac from a single functional megaspore, while the other three megaspores produced by meiosis degenerate. This is the most common type of embryo sac development in flowering plants.'),
  ('7d78196a-b977-4c81-aaa2-104f0e74490c','61d4ce2c-d902-4a1c-82a7-48e345376276',1,'In a typical mature angiosperm embryo sac, how many cells and nuclei are present?','A typical angiosperm embryo sac is 7-celled and 8-nucleate. It contains: egg apparatus (2 synergids + 1 egg cell = 3 cells with 3 nuclei), 3 antipodal cells at chalazal end (3 cells with 3 nuclei), and 1 large central cell with 2 polar nuclei (1 cell with 2 nuclei). Total = 7 cells and 8 nuclei.'),
  ('3d83da7b-e378-4986-88ea-fd9bd28802c9','fec7a12c-a4ee-4d7b-baa6-0af002af13ed',1,'Which of the following statements about chasmogamous and cleistogamous flowers is correct?','Cleistogamous flowers do not open at all. Their anthers and stigma lie close to each other, and when anthers dehisce in the flower buds, pollen grains come in contact with the stigma to effect pollination. Thus, cleistogamous flowers are invariably autogamous as there is no chance of cross-pollen landing on the stigma. Chasmogamous flowers are open and exposed, similar to flowers of other species.'),
  ('1737b30d-a5e3-45d1-8e09-bbacf53c185a','1a80295a-9067-46e5-987c-731b6c31aeae',1,'Which of the following is NOT a strategy evolved to prevent self-pollination in flowers?','Cleistogamous flowers actually promote self-pollination (autogamy), not prevent it. Strategies to prevent self-pollination include: (1) Dichogamy - pollen release and stigma receptivity are not synchronised, (2) Herkogamy - anther and stigma are placed at different positions, (3) Self-incompatibility - genetic mechanism preventing self-pollen from fertilising ovules, and (4) Production of unisexual flowers.'),
  ('459f4fb7-0678-48d0-9352-6dd61287cb64','193951dd-d940-44d7-a068-cf02107035e4',1,'What is self-incompatibility in flowering plants?','Self-incompatibility is a genetic mechanism that prevents self-pollen (from the same flower or other flowers of the same plant) from fertilising the ovules. It works by inhibiting pollen germination on the stigma or pollen tube growth in the style. This is an important outbreeding device that promotes cross-pollination.'),
  ('4a7dd1f8-e41b-433c-8fd5-7b32520790fc','90dd2e67-3ca4-47fa-a500-c3ebfa85f19a',1,'According to Figure 1.1, which part of the flower is the terminal structure of the pistil that receives pollen grains?','The stigma is the terminal portion of the pistil that serves as the landing platform for pollen grains. It is usually sticky or feathery to trap pollen grains effectively.'),
  ('f9a4ecf3-19cd-409e-a940-08c138d56b3c','3939c49a-63d0-492c-a2cb-13b3811d8ac8',1,'Which floral part shown in Figure 1.1 is described as the long, slender stalk that connects the stigma to the ovary?','The style is the elongated, slender part of the pistil that connects the stigma to the ovary. It provides the pathway through which the pollen tube grows to reach the ovary.'),
  ('b0ebbbef-7843-4069-b2a5-479277deffae','dabf55f7-af56-4c3a-9abe-a702ef7b8a85',1,'In Figure 1.1, which structure represents the male reproductive part of the flower that produces pollen grains?','The anther is the bilobed, terminal structure of the stamen that produces and contains pollen grains. It is the male reproductive part of the flower.'),
  ('6bf87045-60dc-4e45-b770-503a6693e9b4','bae1fc91-b42e-45c2-b6a8-59e26aadd44b',1,'What is the function of the filament in a flower as shown in Figure 1.1?','The filament is the long, slender stalk of the stamen that supports the anther and positions it optimally for pollen dispersal and transfer to the stigma.'),
  ('b4ffe09b-9959-42f1-bbd7-b3188c2a2100','8166c7b5-a026-43ec-87a5-537450abbac8',1,'Which of the following is NOT a labeled part in Figure 1.1 (L.S. of a flower)?','Figure 1.1 labels the following parts: Stigma, Style, Ovary, Anther, Filament, Petal, and Sepal. The nectary is not labeled in this diagram.'),
  ('b9a0bcb3-8f5e-46f1-96f0-4dc27591ddd0','03fd4d8c-0074-461f-b86a-7339597f8c62',1,'What does the term ''floriculture'' refer to, as mentioned on page 4?','Floriculture refers to the cultivation of flowers for ornamental, aesthetic, social, cultural, and commercial purposes. It involves growing flowers for gardens, celebrations, and the floral industry.'),
  ('c7e1e88e-6251-49c2-b973-d15863675fc8','ba6b3343-f628-4c98-a495-6226d69f2f5f',1,'According to the text on page 4, what are the two most important units of sexual reproduction that develop in specific parts of a flower?','The two most important units of sexual reproduction are the male gametophyte (pollen grains) developed in the anther and the female gametophyte (embryo sac within ovules) developed in the ovary.'),
  ('71e3a470-0316-4afc-ac43-0845ee90fbda','7b51965c-74b3-4552-8006-0bf2874113fa',1,'Which part of the flower shown in Figure 1.1 develops into the fruit after fertilization?','The ovary is the basal, bulged part of the pistil that contains ovules. After fertilization, the ovary develops into the fruit and the ovules develop into seeds.'),
  ('598ed4f0-3388-4601-a128-e23b413edb36','df8e7256-4acb-4095-8940-cf615d0f72e4',1,'What is the collective term for all the sepals of a flower as mentioned in the context of Figure 1.1?','The calyx is the collective term for all the sepals of a flower. It is the outermost whorl of floral parts that typically protects the flower bud before it opens.'),
  ('706c402c-c127-402b-8abb-7833097c20a8','105a8102-7905-4e13-8f48-1681cb9665f4',1,'According to Section 1.2 on page 4, which two reproductive structures differentiate and develop in the flower before fertilization?','Section 1.2 states that the male reproductive structure (androecium) and the female reproductive structure (gynoecium) differentiate and develop in the flower before fertilization occurs.'),
  ('ecd76bd5-1084-405d-a617-8fae547ff8e2','c785f5e1-b077-482b-bf9b-dfb100edb224',1,'What is the hard outer layer of the pollen grain wall called?','The hard outer layer of the pollen grain is called the exine. It is made up of sporopollenin, which is one of the most resistant organic materials known.'),
  ('06f29c7e-0e71-4247-bd00-86773843910f','84a8b5cd-d4d3-4b30-8136-f41bec4e356f',1,'Which substance makes the exine of pollen grains highly resistant?','The exine is made up of sporopollenin, which can withstand high temperatures and strong acids and alkalis. No enzyme that degrades sporopollenin is known.'),
  ('a28fc49c-7fa4-4dd8-98e9-28b244c63fd8','d55940e6-0b0a-4aca-8f50-64e368f5659e',1,'What are the prominent apertures on the pollen grain exine called?','The pollen grain exine has prominent apertures called germ pores where sporopollenin is absent. These allow for pollen tube emergence during germination.'),
  ('b42665c3-35ee-4c9e-a549-8664c4260aa3','875ae004-04af-44c9-8f67-76307bc14dcd',1,'What is the inner wall of the pollen grain called?','The inner wall of the pollen grain is called the intine. It is a thin and continuous layer made up of cellulose and pectin.'),
  ('ac9ce96d-9c60-4720-a3c5-3b35736fbdea','6c3c5d49-9585-4187-94fb-ee767b4f8ac0',1,'How many cells does a mature pollen grain contain at the 2-celled stage?','When the pollen grain is mature at the 2-celled stage, it contains two cells: the vegetative cell and the generative cell. The generative cell is small and floats in the cytoplasm of the vegetative cell.'),
  ('0e3df534-4c53-4d30-9d20-f1c8918ea3f6','325e1b90-34d2-4725-a6fe-944d61740f65',1,'In what percentage of angiosperms are pollen grains shed at the 2-celled stage?','In over 60 per cent of angiosperms, pollen grains are shed at the 2-celled stage. In the remaining species, the generative cell divides mitotically to give rise to two male gametes before pollen grains are shed (3-celled stage).'),
  ('fd516e0e-d676-4132-92c9-8766408336a6','cc2cff2e-3ff5-4014-be62-7cae2c7d1f7b',1,'Which plant mentioned in the text causes severe pollen allergies in India?','Parthenium or carrot grass came into India as a contaminant with imported wheat and has become ubiquitous. It causes severe allergies and bronchial afflictions leading to chronic respiratory disorders like asthma and bronchitis.'),
  ('fb99d9f9-f26f-4a51-a2ce-acda5d396e6b','fc82cb4a-3dbe-4173-8700-8213a68f873d',1,'What is the approximate diameter range of pollen grains?','Pollen grains are generally spherical measuring about 25-50 micrometers in diameter. They have a prominent two-layered wall consisting of the exine and intine.'),
  ('d0d827d9-94f9-4b39-a933-961a8d92eed6','08a649c5-8b7e-4646-83e3-40ee40564d48',1,'What is the function of the vegetative cell in a pollen grain?','The vegetative cell is bigger, has abundant food reserve and a large irregularly shaped nucleus. It provides nutrition and support for the generative cell which will form the male gametes.'),
  ('9f332cc7-fc8c-4559-a592-96b0144bb3c3','725bc379-ef99-4a5b-a952-84ec92f260d0',1,'At what temperature can pollen grains be stored for years in liquid nitrogen?','It is possible to store pollen grains of a large number of species for years in liquid nitrogen at -196°C. Such stored pollen can be used as pollen banks, similar to seed banks, in crop breeding programmes.'),
  ('529751be-9ee8-498d-93c8-57d3a3bd8a8e','d859d219-516f-4ca9-9002-d7ec0d71fd3b',1,'What is the shape of the generative cell in a pollen grain?','The generative cell is small, spindle shaped with dense cytoplasm and a nucleus. It floats in the cytoplasm of the vegetative cell and divides to form two male gametes.'),
  ('dd36fad7-aa50-425c-8136-b3bd8886949d','192aabfa-2c75-4c3f-a82b-7f15b31940e6',1,'Which cereal loses pollen viability within 30 minutes of release?','In some cereals such as rice and wheat, pollen grains lose viability within 30 minutes of their release. In contrast, some members of Rosaceae, Leguminoseae and Solanaceae maintain viability for months.'),
  ('889d4425-28bc-4d9a-bd9a-498d30bc30b8','64b7270a-48f6-4c15-bcb0-a2585eff7f7d',1,'What is the cytoplasm of the pollen grain surrounded by?','The cytoplasm of the pollen grain is surrounded by a plasma membrane. This membrane lies beneath the intine layer and encloses the vegetative and generative cells.'),
  ('f248ece2-0ad7-4fe9-a18f-56347b9eb462','b137cac9-3763-4284-bce4-63b6368a6703',1,'What happens to the generative cell in species that shed 3-celled pollen?','In the remaining species (about 40%), the generative cell divides mitotically to give rise to the two male gametes before pollen grains are shed, resulting in a 3-celled pollen grain.'),
  ('5143f495-172d-4d88-bae9-8a73ad4a1901','9df35427-b04d-4f94-967b-694b758c56f3',1,'Why are pollen grains well-preserved as fossils?','Pollen grains are well-preserved as fossils because of the presence of sporopollenin in the exine. Sporopollenin is one of the most resistant organic materials known and can withstand degradation over geological time.'),
  ('00c5a2ab-f4bd-4d16-ba76-535a25824d38','855b2758-47d7-4dbb-83e5-f9d244697a84',1,'What is the nature of the nucleus in the vegetative cell?','The vegetative cell has a large, irregularly shaped nucleus. It is bigger than the generative cell and contains abundant food reserves to support pollen tube growth.'),
  ('0975bb83-627a-4fbb-87b2-9a4eb55edbc4','adb0caaf-41c8-4513-82e7-6d7cb2c336a8',1,'What is the stalk that attaches the ovule to the placenta called?','The funicle is the stalk that attaches the ovule to the placenta.'),
  ('18e21a2e-b23b-4847-9030-21a3246cc038','e89e85d2-dd7d-4146-9592-178061c0697b',1,'What represents the junction between the ovule and the funicle?','Hilum is the region where the body of the ovule fuses with the funicle, representing the junction between ovule and funicle.'),
  ('39d4f07c-2876-417d-806f-8e7e470f5324','75c214b3-6a19-451d-9d1d-db2f99caa9d9',1,'What are the protective envelopes surrounding the ovule called?','Integuments are one or two protective envelopes that encircle the nucellus of the ovule.'),
  ('c94eb72a-0361-467a-9f8a-7206c571f691','e00d9d66-a4b2-442d-b69c-a09225bc48fd',1,'What is the small opening at the tip of the integuments called?','The micropyle is the small opening at the tip of the integuments, organized opposite to the chalaza.'),
  ('b44e9223-8b3f-4aae-abe0-cfb04e439a79','6b69509c-2d6e-4267-9de5-0db0b7b4bd5f',1,'Which part of the ovule represents the basal part opposite the micropylar end?','The chalaza is the basal part of the ovule located opposite to the micropylar end.'),
  ('7652fc37-4f72-464f-8a2a-bb1d36f112b5','68ee0ee9-3f37-4207-8ebc-d9cee25345b0',1,'What is the mass of cells enclosed within the integuments of the ovule?','The nucellus is a mass of cells enclosed within the integuments that contains abundant reserve food materials.'),
  ('a7b07cdb-2c2a-46e7-86d8-f27e7d354819','9b89c369-60b0-4a29-8885-5a6a9eb8a1e2',1,'Where is the embryo sac located within the ovule?','The embryo sac or female gametophyte is located within the nucellus of the ovule.'),
  ('c72ac39a-2ca1-4aeb-ba71-4fe721f239c9','714f11c2-b038-4df3-bd66-d5a28975092a',1,'What is the process of formation of megaspores from the megaspore mother cell called?','Megasporogenesis is the process of formation of megaspores from the megaspore mother cell through meiotic division.'),
  ('faae47a7-e1a0-47ef-8453-7b2d5d87913e','730ce4e7-3e80-4572-8b22-9cab575d846a',1,'Where does the megaspore mother cell (MMC) differentiate in the ovule?','The megaspore mother cell (MMC) differentiates in the micropylar region of the nucellus.'),
  ('f402b2b0-13ba-4032-b1ad-4516cc6d5892','50ba3f72-3bd1-4e35-8aa4-564b661bec7b',1,'What type of cell division does the megaspore mother cell undergo?','The megaspore mother cell undergoes meiotic division to produce four megaspores.'),
  ('fc08c58c-57c6-4664-9acb-e843b7f18a05','b79829a5-d975-4727-bb60-71ab66098325',1,'How many megaspores are produced from one megaspore mother cell?','Meiosis of the megaspore mother cell results in the production of four megaspores.'),
  ('43dfcd90-d238-47f0-a267-c71d4e1f7d6c','002c98d4-baa5-4a4e-9749-db2ed2e87611',1,'What is the method called when only one functional megaspore develops into the embryo sac?','Monosporic development is when only one functional megaspore develops into the female gametophyte (embryo sac) while the other three degenerate.'),
  ('ae6f3361-4a91-4554-bc96-a5428761cb01','00fbf749-29cf-4ed6-b297-2b70903a7e6a',1,'What happens to the three non-functional megaspores in monosporic development?','In monosporic development, three of the megaspores degenerate while only one remains functional.'),
  ('6d564d61-3fdb-4fa2-bc8e-b7a2b3659cec','2c4ee2ba-75b8-473a-901f-09fc5b5a3cc9',1,'What is the female gametophyte of flowering plants commonly called?','The female gametophyte in flowering plants is commonly called the embryo sac.'),
  ('11c1408e-c27c-40fa-85ba-02266ccfecea','ebc2be4b-a4eb-472a-a642-ae256aed2e58',1,'Which of the following is NOT a part of a typical ovule structure?','Stamen is the male reproductive part of the flower, not a part of the ovule structure. The ovule consists of funicle, hilum, integuments, micropyle, chalaza, and nucellus.'),
  ('8ae0e060-4282-4ff8-8261-ea16cce333cf','25192906-2a28-4cea-83da-5e9ef6475d04',1,'What is the ploidy level of the megaspore mother cell?','The megaspore mother cell is diploid (2n) before undergoing meiosis to produce haploid megaspores.'),
  ('1bc02fc5-faf4-4dc6-89ba-869874e3fee3','83d7910b-dec5-4bf3-9045-ad31191beed6',1,'After meiosis, what is the ploidy level of each megaspore?','After meiotic division of the diploid megaspore mother cell, each megaspore is haploid (n).'),
  ('2e553411-36a2-452d-b748-4df16644928e','15e404bb-0d04-4943-a5d8-e1a298a2fadb',1,'Which region of the ovule is the micropyle located?','The micropyle is located at the tip of the integuments, serving as a small opening.'),
  ('9e7fa0d4-dbdb-407f-ace7-5f11af9a78b4','326d2dbe-2124-422c-bbbe-b28c42be17fd',1,'What characteristic feature do cells of the nucellus possess?','Cells of the nucellus have abundant reserve food materials that support the development of the embryo sac.'),
  ('285081bc-37c1-4eb5-8b92-876b13dff047','57f5c067-a207-43f8-aede-60afc8d4d7a5',1,'In a typical angiosperm ovule, how many embryo sacs are generally present?','An ovule generally has a single embryo sac formed from one functional megaspore.'),
  ('ebeda94a-8aed-4220-992e-fcfe890c6b88','aecbf59a-e511-41f4-8c61-8e8bad41d5ad',1,'What is the significance of meiosis in megasporogenesis?','Meiosis in megasporogenesis reduces the chromosome number from diploid (2n) to haploid (n), which is essential for sexual reproduction.'),
  ('66f3c936-f094-4eba-a08c-5c1a11fa3236','85691f03-3bb6-4f67-b58a-85ff8ddb4e33',1,'Which of the following is the innermost wall layer of a microsporangium?','The tapetum is the innermost wall layer of the microsporangium. It nourishes the developing pollen grains and has dense cytoplasm with generally more than one nucleus.'),
  ('68605f21-a8e8-469f-9e49-aa9b56bc841a','db717ca1-b772-42e6-980e-01cc5bd8ea03',1,'What is the function of the outer three wall layers of a microsporangium?','The outer three wall layers (epidermis, endothecium, and middle layers) perform the function of protection and help in dehiscence of the anther to release pollen.'),
  ('2ef05f8a-eba1-4a73-be32-8e3bdca8caca','64f71dd4-7274-4d54-a2c0-b0fbb4fd1c04',1,'Which tissue occupies the center of a young microsporangium?','When the anther is young, a group of compactly arranged homogenous cells called the sporogenous tissue occupies the center of each microsporangium.'),
  ('b17313ff-4403-446f-b8e2-940c30c433c7','01eede00-490f-4696-890d-5c4fa0b8cccc',1,'What is the ploidy level of cells in a microspore tetrad?','The cells of the sporogenous tissue undergo meiotic divisions to form microspore tetrads. Since meiosis reduces chromosome number by half, the microspores in the tetrad are haploid (n).'),
  ('52455bdf-29a8-4f04-ba1c-7040685b7bd1','b08d593c-4f92-4d66-8fc8-cc8e35c7c7cc',1,'What is the process of formation of microspores from a pollen mother cell called?','The process of formation of microspores from a pollen mother cell (PMC) through meiosis is called microsporogenesis.'),
  ('80f75f51-4c05-47df-b252-d6f3d4f730ba','120f166c-a1ff-409a-8e51-e74d803eb712',1,'How many microsporangia are present in a typical bilobed anther?','A typical angiosperm anther is bilobed with each lobe having two theca, making it dithecous. The anther is a four-sided (tetragonal) structure consisting of four microsporangia located at the corners, two in each lobe.'),
  ('9a5285ba-c64f-4bdb-a08c-b86cd16153e4','4852269b-f943-4bb5-8d82-b3ebaa0b1751',1,'What happens to microspores as the anthers mature and dehydrate?','As the anthers mature and dehydrate, the microspores dissociate from each other and develop into pollen grains.'),
  ('7023314e-c6cc-44ed-8b44-53cdd579a475','06179cc9-fbfa-4935-a08d-4dfc4a313694',1,'Which cells of the tapetum possess dense cytoplasm and generally have more than one nucleus?','Cells of the tapetum possess dense cytoplasm and generally have more than one nucleus. This characteristic helps in nourishing the developing pollen grains.'),
  ('ee81851d-6ece-49ae-8354-62ad0120d57f','5e324775-5817-4ed2-87b0-8e99e4a7cb90',1,'What is the arrangement of microspores immediately after their formation through meiosis?','The microspores, as they are formed, are arranged in a cluster of four cells called the microspore tetrad. This arrangement is characteristic of the immediate post-meiotic stage.'),
  ('f6a86def-0a70-46e3-8553-8694503550c4','7bed58c0-5003-439a-a967-5aa6ecd4eb71',1,'What is released when the anther dehisces?','Inside each microsporangium several thousands of microspores or pollen grains are formed that are released with the dehiscence of the anther. The dehiscence allows the release of mature pollen grains for pollination.'),
  ('77a290d5-947c-4198-b919-970df10c9f03','e6a8a08a-6958-4b88-8e94-30fbd56086c8',1,'What type of reproduction do humans exhibit?','Humans are sexually reproducing and viviparous, meaning they produce offspring through sexual reproduction and give birth to live young rather than laying eggs.'),
  ('6b82fe12-1490-4321-bfbd-f2bbb265a259','5c8d3e58-738e-4e94-af5f-13556e8e4f5b',1,'Which of the following is NOT included in the reproductive events in humans?','The reproductive events in humans include gametogenesis (formation of gametes), insemination (transfer of sperms), fertilisation (fusion of gametes), blastocyst formation, implantation, gestation, and parturition. Photosynthesis is a plant process and is not related to human reproduction.'),
  ('41d23d3b-9183-43c4-bc66-59cf3c89bb32','6bca0131-9f43-4f63-aabc-5024910c392f',1,'What is the process of formation of gametes called?','Gametogenesis is the process of formation of gametes. In males, it produces sperms (spermatogenesis), and in females, it produces ova (oogenesis).'),
  ('6505291e-ae95-4f3a-a67c-df5282c70b2c','3ebea35d-7d99-4249-9abb-3858db50bea4',1,'Where are sperms formed in males?','Sperms are formed in the testes through the process of spermatogenesis. The testes are the primary male reproductive organs located in the scrotum.'),
  ('5666dff3-8948-47d5-82e2-1b66fc3875a0','05fdd28b-23f2-4d1f-a782-5cf417d69dc3',1,'Where are ova (eggs) formed in females?','Ova are formed in the ovaries through the process of oogenesis. The ovaries are the primary female reproductive organs that produce female gametes and steroid hormones.'),
  ('40f04dcf-d009-48a9-874e-6ae51050594e','2fa1bc8b-8172-456e-8402-e575b4b92b7c',1,'What is the fusion of male and female gametes called?','Fertilisation is the fusion of male and female gametes (sperm and ovum) leading to the formation of a zygote. This occurs in the ampullary region of the fallopian tube.'),
  ('da8d6b58-dee5-4ff7-a307-ac5c5462e431','05cddaac-a7be-427a-b111-9019e933dd90',1,'After fertilisation, what structure forms and attaches to the uterine wall?','After fertilisation, the zygote undergoes cleavage to form a blastocyst, which then attaches to the uterine wall in a process called implantation.'),
  ('ebb7838f-9c26-4039-a215-011f18169fe2','428e6ac6-8b38-496b-9ad0-7a46446d7b92',1,'At what stage of life do reproductive events begin in humans?','Reproductive events in humans occur after puberty, which is the stage of sexual maturation when the reproductive organs become functional.'),
  ('e12430bf-0bf1-415d-a672-7b661a1734fe','9566603d-e93b-4fd5-b901-550755d72d42',1,'Which statement correctly describes a difference between male and female reproductive events?','There are remarkable differences between male and female reproductive events. Sperm formation continues even in old men, but formation of ovum ceases in women around the age of fifty years (menopause).'),
  ('5f4fdcca-6a57-44fd-8558-450e75f0b27e','2a47c0d4-14e4-4fe0-9ec4-7261674f3d83',1,'Where are the testes situated in the male reproductive system?','The testes are situated outside the abdominal cavity within a pouch called scrotum. The scrotum helps in maintaining the low temperature of the testes (2-2.5°C lower than normal body temperature) necessary for spermatogenesis.'),
  ('b457e1c4-c440-441c-b46c-7d61bba3b3f1','5a277082-9c0c-44d4-a321-dc4dbf223efb',1,'What is the primary function of the scrotum in the male reproductive system?','The scrotum helps in maintaining the low temperature of the testes (2-2.5°C lower than the normal internal body temperature) which is necessary for spermatogenesis.'),
  ('78ce8f56-dd81-417c-98bb-f7d7cf40a516','4a1b093c-34d3-46e6-9347-fc7d7a7981ef',1,'What are the approximate dimensions of each testis in an adult male?','In adults, each testis is oval in shape, with a length of about 4 to 5 cm and a width of about 2 to 3 cm.'),
  ('1055a85b-5b6a-4522-aacc-918a19ee7854','31d5c7f9-66e6-4ab4-a516-c6862addde46',1,'How many compartments called testicular lobules does each testis contain?','Each testis has about 250 compartments called testicular lobules. Each lobule contains one to three highly coiled seminiferous tubules in which sperms are produced.'),
  ('7229587f-9de7-408f-bde6-d60ede1d67cb','bac4dde4-f61c-4241-b639-eb8804a5433f',1,'What are the two types of cells that line the inside of each seminiferous tubule?','Each seminiferous tubule is lined on its inside by two types of cells called male germ cells (spermatogonia) and Sertoli cells. The male germ cells undergo meiotic divisions leading to sperm formation, while Sertoli cells provide nutrition to the germ cells.'),
  ('3d9c164f-8e82-48d5-91f1-86e5ff2fec6c','3c7f0cfc-1d7b-4828-9c2d-29b9356c42c1',1,'What is the function of Sertoli cells in the seminiferous tubules?','Sertoli cells provide nutrition to the germ cells. The male germ cells (spermatogonia) undergo meiotic divisions finally leading to sperm formation, while Sertoli cells nourish these dividing germ cells.'),
  ('cdb92756-5f3f-495e-b681-41e43b96274d','67cb11f8-97e3-482c-8669-0b1a9860255b',1,'Where are Leydig cells located and what do they secrete?','The regions outside the seminiferous tubules called interstitial spaces contain small blood vessels and interstitial cells or Leydig cells. Leydig cells synthesise and secrete testicular hormones called androgens.'),
  ('bf5b278a-2555-4559-948d-b60a1b37583e','f5895d17-c553-4857-9569-9da4dbff898e',1,'Which of the following are included in the male sex accessory ducts?','The male sex accessory ducts include rete testis, vasa efferentia, epididymis, and vas deferens. The seminiferous tubules open into the vasa efferentia through rete testis.'),
  ('30071d09-d686-4b1d-84d3-5858af98d4bb','98bddf08-c837-4ea0-b9a2-891a96ff7fc0',1,'What is the correct pathway of sperm transport from the testis to the outside?','The seminiferous tubules open into vasa efferentia through rete testis. Vasa efferentia open into epididymis. Epididymis leads to vas deferens that ascends to abdomen and loops over urinary bladder. It receives duct from seminal vesicle and opens into urethra as ejaculatory duct.'),
  ('9f4038a3-7f1f-41f8-905e-4a1a82315f43','660bb972-bda3-414a-833d-f0bc22bacfbd',1,'Where is the male reproductive system located in the human body?','The male reproductive system is located in the pelvis region. It includes a pair of testes along with accessory ducts, glands and external genitalia.'),
  ('d5afdb96-9fa9-4b93-af29-2592bd523f47','495e8cad-8a38-4cf7-9ed5-409cd49cdc67',1,'What is the primary function of the scrotum in the male reproductive system?','The scrotum helps in maintaining the low temperature of the testes (2-2.5 degrees C lower than normal body temperature) which is necessary for spermatogenesis.'),
  ('c5eda6d8-7f04-4807-8ac5-d86600422d21','fcaf9ee8-9663-4519-9e64-697a975de9b4',1,'What is the approximate length of an adult human testis?','In adults, each testis is oval in shape with a length of about 4 to 5 cm and a width of about 2 to 3 cm.'),
  ('91afafc8-d899-4a9d-b4a9-837cc81d0669','f0915a6e-f5c3-479e-98a4-86cccb9d57d5',1,'How many testicular lobules are present in each testis approximately?','Each testis has about 250 compartments called testicular lobules. Each lobule contains one to three highly coiled seminiferous tubules where sperms are produced.'),
  ('8c13692e-bb4a-4fa6-8cc9-bc67df2e4072','1317bee9-fb7a-41a6-b368-9f26dce410db',1,'Which two types of cells line the inside of each seminiferous tubule?','Each seminiferous tubule is lined on its inside by two types of cells: male germ cells (spermatogonia) and Sertoli cells. The male germ cells undergo meiotic divisions leading to sperm formation, while Sertoli cells provide nutrition to the germ cells.'),
  ('c5406f81-8746-4445-bc29-557ce697e914','6c2ae044-f1d2-48fe-afd8-32a09dec2884',1,'What is the function of Leydig cells (interstitial cells) in the testis?','Leydig cells (interstitial cells) are found in the interstitial spaces outside the seminiferous tubules. They synthesize and secrete testicular hormones called androgens.'),
  ('8ad15172-f3f9-4fcd-983f-a7fb7944ac11','3a434bca-0633-4c18-9708-3ce62f7bd74d',1,'What is the correct sequence of male sex accessory ducts from testis to urethra?','The seminiferous tubules open into vasa efferentia through rete testis. The vasa efferentia open into epididymis, which leads to vas deferens. The vas deferens receives a duct from seminal vesicle and opens into urethra as the ejaculatory duct.'),
  ('54947afe-5265-416f-82e2-3fdd53a9f56c','21028692-0600-4a59-b1a9-4efe11bda2d2',1,'Which glands contribute secretions that constitute the seminal plasma?','The male accessory glands include paired seminal vesicles, a prostate, and paired bulbourethral glands. Their secretions constitute the seminal plasma which is rich in fructose, calcium, and certain enzymes.'),
  ('52fae149-ab86-4c82-a926-94fcc8602066','ab0864c0-db81-4389-80b8-781d349587d2',1,'Which of the following is NOT a component of the female reproductive system?','The female reproductive system consists of a pair of ovaries, a pair of oviducts, uterus, cervix, vagina, and external genitalia. Seminal vesicles are male accessory glands, not part of the female system.'),
  ('fbf23c49-e46b-46b8-9e38-6db0eb620c6b','9080c311-b17c-43fd-8975-dd1573f3436a',1,'How are the ovaries connected to the pelvic wall and uterus?','Each ovary is connected to the pelvic wall and uterus by ligaments. Each ovary is about 2 to 4 cm in length and is covered by a thin epithelium.'),
  ('d9521270-656c-45f8-b0b4-0cfb11c7be2a','5565e377-a101-4eab-96e3-e5a14d2bf7a1',1,'The ovarian stroma is divided into which two zones?','The ovarian stroma is divided into two zones: a peripheral cortex and an inner medulla. The cortex contains ovarian follicles in different stages of development.'),
  ('6dce00de-4642-4973-b527-be2e872a994e','b1aaaa13-a00f-4e69-b76f-653e9e88f439',1,'Which part of the fallopian tube is funnel-shaped and possesses finger-like projections called fimbriae?','The part of the fallopian tube closer to the ovary is the funnel-shaped infundibulum. The edges of the infundibulum possess finger-like projections called fimbriae, which help in collection of the ovum after ovulation.'),
  ('b212452e-ddbb-47bb-bc34-aad50db918e7','e25c123e-24c9-4c9b-84dc-1d4207ffcc99',1,'Which layer of the uterus undergoes cyclical changes during the menstrual cycle?','The endometrium is the inner glandular layer that lines the uterine cavity. It undergoes cyclical changes during the menstrual cycle, while the myometrium exhibits strong contractions during delivery.'),
  ('22fa7825-6ac0-4bd5-9100-44bad037b730','e9c53e89-fc37-4a24-94a4-a238737c0b0b',1,'Which of the following is NOT part of the female external genitalia?','The female external genitalia include mons pubis, labia majora, labia minora, hymen, and clitoris. The ovary is an internal primary sex organ, not external genitalia.'),
  ('a7ecc9ea-033d-46d1-999c-b2eb4a25c8d3','0ce85430-9da8-4c4d-9658-58f59a57e761',1,'How many mammary lobes are present in each breast of the mammary gland?','The glandular tissue of each breast is divided into 15-20 mammary lobes containing clusters of cells called alveoli. The cells of alveoli secrete milk.')
INSERT INTO question_translations (id, question_id, lang_id, question_text, explanation) VALUES
  ('934de93a-13e3-4a29-a348-27375c910dd3','f861d616-aa7c-455e-8bb7-ff4850c95a0b',1,'Which of the following is NOT part of the female reproductive system?','Seminal vesicles are male accessory glands, not part of the female reproductive system. The female system consists of ovaries, oviducts, uterus, cervix, vagina, and external genitalia.'),
  ('7c1cce6b-9f41-4880-a6ad-f1f20bd3e26a','8c668e12-7763-4887-81bc-11d48b54309f',1,'What is the funnel-shaped part of the fallopian tube closer to the ovary called?','The infundibulum is the funnel-shaped part of the fallopian tube closer to the ovary. Its edges have finger-like projections called fimbriae that help collect the ovum after ovulation.'),
  ('d43beff7-5131-46aa-90df-4befe0f76bce','45f7808c-e496-4950-a5ed-87e66f52e8dc',1,'What is the function of fimbriae in the female reproductive system?','Fimbriae are finger-like projections at the edges of the infundibulum that help in the collection of the ovum after ovulation. They guide the released ovum into the fallopian tube.'),
  ('1ac79791-e24e-4f9c-b01d-bf3e26a49c90','2ce5de2e-858f-45f2-b011-f355a564c636',1,'What is the shape of the uterus in human females?','The uterus is single and its shape is like an inverted pear. It is supported by ligaments attached to the pelvic wall and opens into the vagina through the cervix.'),
  ('af5cc6d3-c7bc-4c4f-a5e5-4f769c131755','470758bb-d536-4acc-a798-e6e6701f89d7',1,'Which layer of the uterus undergoes cyclical changes during the menstrual cycle?','The endometrium is the inner glandular layer that lines the uterine cavity. It undergoes cyclical changes during the menstrual cycle, thickening and then breaking down if fertilization does not occur.'),
  ('ccfd884f-8351-4361-8baf-0ee7f4f8d3db','927be607-512f-40aa-92cd-2cf936bec179',1,'What is the function of the myometrium during delivery?','The myometrium is the middle thick layer of smooth muscle in the uterus. It exhibits strong contractions during delivery of the baby (parturition) to expel the foetus.'),
  ('07a0cb51-db55-4ef4-b191-b396c5db69fa','ed21a880-2f7f-4e04-a719-cd3f95ac43d7',1,'Which of the following is NOT part of the female external genitalia?','The cervix is part of the internal reproductive system (the lower narrow part of the uterus), not external genitalia. External genitalia include mons pubis, labia majora, labia minora, hymen, and clitoris.'),
  ('82c7dd00-6e85-4c00-ba2a-4da2f870ecf6','3024af55-05ca-4346-aefc-131b55a4d8c2',1,'How many mammary lobes are typically present in each breast of a functional mammary gland?','The glandular tissue of each breast is divided into 15-20 mammary lobes containing clusters of cells called alveoli.'),
  ('b784c9a0-ed90-4766-90b7-075e38bf80f3','71542990-2d3f-4242-9328-c6d73cce2f66',1,'What is the function of alveoli cells in the mammary gland?','The cells of alveoli secrete milk, which is stored in the cavities (lumens) of alveoli. The alveoli open into mammary tubules.'),
  ('f3598ee8-70c9-4dae-b6d1-caf270e69327','4bda2af9-4801-4d84-b5ae-fe4f68f65343',1,'What is the correct pathway of milk from alveoli to the nipple?','The alveoli open into mammary tubules. The tubules of each lobe join to form a mammary duct. Several mammary ducts join to form a wider mammary ampulla which is connected to lactiferous duct and opens at the nipple.'),
  ('c1cc0f02-3225-4e72-9152-48127f37d09b','79a53f46-c037-429b-a171-ac40787564a9',1,'What is the process called that produces gametes in testis and ovaries?','The primary sex organs produce gametes by the process called gametogenesis. In testis, spermatogonia produce sperms by spermatogenesis, while in ovaries, oogonia produce ova by oogenesis.'),
  ('e318dd86-da88-4dda-8478-ea9436a983e6','93eac3f3-34a5-4260-af59-5bad99f2a46a',1,'How many chromosomes does each spermatogonium contain?','Each spermatogonium is diploid and contains 46 chromosomes. Some spermatogonia called primary spermatocytes periodically undergo meiosis.'),
  ('a516df98-5e19-4bdc-a66b-1b55c0d76095','5e7aea91-fd75-42db-88de-e878dc5353b6',1,'What is produced when a primary spermatocyte completes the first meiotic division?','A primary spermatocyte completes the first meiotic division (reduction division) leading to formation of two equal, haploid cells called secondary spermatocytes, which have only 23 chromosomes each.'),
  ('4a2601b3-cc61-4d3c-b56c-c23bef8b31e5','f8cd448d-a6f4-4faa-a6c4-758f3fb1a745',1,'How many spermatids are produced from secondary spermatocytes after the second meiotic division?','The secondary spermatocytes undergo the second meiotic division to produce four equal, haploid spermatids. The spermatids are then transformed into spermatozoa by spermiogenesis.'),
  ('8173edcc-057d-499b-be97-61b6fb3c2efa','be19d6f2-d2f8-4aae-abc9-69913363f26f',1,'Which hormone triggers the start of spermatogenesis at puberty?','Spermatogenesis starts at the age of puberty due to significant increase in the secretion of gonadotropin releasing hormone (GnRH). This hypothalamic hormone acts at the anterior pituitary gland to stimulate LH and FSH secretion.'),
  ('63a52552-be58-45df-b040-01fe88db07d3','34ff7892-305f-4ea4-a359-6c595842e2f9',1,'What is the function of Luteinising Hormone (LH) in male reproduction?','LH acts at the Leydig cells and stimulates synthesis and secretion of androgens. Androgens, in turn, stimulate the process of spermatogenesis. FSH acts on the Sertoli cells and stimulates secretion of factors that help in spermatogenesis.'),
  ('827503f0-a430-4e37-af8c-ae76d4c83dc8','0b912ebb-2b40-421b-8316-b769209e4317',1,'What is the process called when cells of the sporogenous tissue undergo meiotic divisions to form microspore tetrads?','Microsporogenesis is the process of formation of microspores from a pollen mother cell (PMC) through meiosis. The sporogenous tissue cells undergo meiotic divisions to form microspore tetrads.'),
  ('101d0290-487e-486c-970d-08a2b83b2bcd','15caf6e6-cfbb-45e8-8359-398c8d7cf5d7',1,'What is the ploidy of the cells in a microspore tetrad?','The microspore tetrad cells are haploid (n) because they are formed through meiosis (reduction division) from diploid pollen mother cells.'),
  ('e352e724-8e01-4e66-b489-376be8b95e00','fb21d92d-8e08-44fa-ab1c-acc640328ee1',1,'What happens to microspores as the anthers mature and dehydrate?','As the anthers mature and dehydrate, the microspores dissociate from each other and develop into pollen grains. This is a key step in pollen grain formation.'),
  ('05a5c511-696f-4d76-afab-99907fe78bd0','326f9174-6a3c-462b-8792-53c459ac69bd',1,'What is the approximate diameter of a typical pollen grain?','Pollen grains are generally spherical measuring about 25-50 micrometers in diameter. This size allows them to be easily dispersed and captured by stigmas.'),
  ('6a630ce9-62bc-4239-ae20-ef3037126e0d','f1afffe9-aa0a-4d57-8034-705599b09129',1,'What is the composition of the hard outer layer (exine) of the pollen grain wall?','The hard outer layer called the exine is made up of sporopollenin, which is one of the most resistant organic materials known. It can withstand high temperatures and strong acids and alkali.'),
  ('b6f9e476-7f43-485b-900c-12b2458f2c57','451bfb04-7360-4b42-8f23-43290f08fa1c',1,'Why are pollen grains well-preserved as fossils?','Pollen grains are well-preserved as fossils because of the presence of sporopollenin in the exine. Sporopollenin is highly resistant to degradation and no enzyme that degrades it is known.'),
  ('1b1d8e63-d3d3-45da-856a-04fef9e05f86','ec9ba954-43f5-42ed-aece-1c9a51944a76',1,'What are the two cells present in a mature pollen grain at the 2-celled stage?','When the pollen grain is mature at the 2-celled stage, it contains a vegetative cell (bigger, with abundant food reserve and large irregularly shaped nucleus) and a generative cell (small, spindle-shaped with dense cytoplasm).'),
  ('2eb92a2e-a2e4-44e3-b19c-4c0c27415781','9fcf70e8-a66f-4663-a5f2-4330c3d3c668',1,'In what percentage of angiosperms are pollen grains shed at the 2-celled stage?','In over 60 percent of angiosperms, pollen grains are shed at the 2-celled stage (containing vegetative cell and generative cell). In the remaining species, the generative cell divides to form two male gametes before shedding.'),
  ('32ef3d60-3f26-4e27-abf3-9cef673b048a','e25dbb1a-ddfc-4ca7-b09e-500fcd8fa16a',1,'What is the function of the germ pores on the pollen grain exine?','The germ pores are apertures on the exine where sporopollenin is absent. They serve as the sites through which the pollen tube emerges during pollen germination on the stigma.'),
  ('4ff6c44c-3db6-406d-8366-f9a6aad4a0b9','b6453262-6bfc-4770-a7fd-9d781a50247e',1,'During copulation, where is semen released in the female reproductive system?','During copulation (coitus), semen is released by the penis into the vagina. This process is called insemination. The sperms then swim through the cervix and uterus to reach the fallopian tube.'),
  ('7cbaa8b6-8a0c-4651-a5bd-2a2ea4da36a8','d1823627-12e0-44d2-8915-507ade1fa6ba',1,'Which region of the fallopian tube is the site of fertilisation?','Fertilisation takes place in the ampullary region of the fallopian tube. The motile sperms swim through the cervix and uterus to reach this specific region where the ovum is also transported.'),
  ('3f99ef9a-324a-486c-a15a-18ba0920aec9','ec3aa114-6ffb-488b-9a88-1eeba3e1ec21',1,'What is the process called when a sperm fuses with an ovum?','The process of fusion of a sperm with an ovum is called fertilisation. This results in the formation of a diploid zygote.'),
  ('8f8e06be-07fe-434c-a35c-ca558ce1db73','e0d511c3-06f0-453e-9a57-a942fbad91bb',1,'Which layer of the ovum does the sperm first contact during fertilisation?','During fertilisation, a sperm comes in contact with the zona pellucida layer of the ovum. This contact induces changes in the membrane that block the entry of additional sperms, ensuring only one sperm fertilises the ovum.'),
  ('17768d61-80fe-4d19-abf5-2cc7bd0ce783','d3668531-f128-4c6c-87c8-7630aa0cb257',1,'What ensures that only one sperm can fertilise an ovum?','When a sperm contacts the zona pellucida, it induces changes in the membrane that block the entry of additional sperms. This mechanism ensures monospermy, where only one sperm fertilises the ovum.'),
  ('aa4202c2-cf07-4fa8-af47-07499bb71068','3f0769b6-c34b-4748-98de-d79e6671c671',1,'Which structure of the sperm contains enzymes that help penetrate the ovum?','The acrosome is a cap-like structure at the anterior portion of the sperm head. It is filled with enzymes that help the sperm enter into the cytoplasm of the ovum through the zona pellucida and plasma membrane.'),
  ('6017aef1-2377-4dfb-be9b-4177b03919a0','6e8bf244-7f1c-4956-a1cc-23b43654abc8',1,'Why do not all copulations result in fertilisation?','Fertilisation can only occur if the ovum and sperms are transported simultaneously to the ampullary region of the fallopian tube. Since timing may not always coincide, not all copulations lead to fertilisation.'),
  ('0317e4d6-998e-4eea-b2d7-e1724c6c8574','bd544ad6-6d22-46c2-832d-4461fc5b1cd8',1,'What is the outer layer of cells in the blastocyst called?','The blastomeres in the blastocyst are arranged into an outer layer called trophoblast and an inner group of cells attached to trophoblast called the inner cell mass. The trophoblast later forms chorionic villi for placenta formation.'),
  ('3825bc28-c180-4a45-b8a8-ebd6e42a02a5','e4f468a9-f71d-44e2-9ad0-ad406af014da',1,'What is the term for the attachment of the blastocyst to the uterine endometrium?','Implantation is the process where the blastocyst becomes embedded in the endometrium of the uterus. After attachment, uterine cells divide rapidly and cover the blastocyst, leading to pregnancy.'),
  ('545833fe-4db1-482f-a625-f29e881025e2','361f7569-9b4b-403a-9cce-f1e383cdd80b',1,'Which cells provide nutrition to the developing germ cells in the seminiferous tubules?','Sertoli cells are found inside the seminiferous tubules and provide nutrition to the dividing male germ cells (spermatogonia). They also help in the release of sperms through spermiation.'),
  ('f86952ae-6254-44bd-9058-346f8c953dc1','d6512cd9-9be1-4368-a9d7-d4a9e65f6a8d',1,'What is the structure that connects the placenta to the embryo?','The placenta is connected to the embryo through an umbilical cord. This cord helps in the transport of oxygen, nutrients, and waste materials between the embryo and the mother.'),
  ('31ba5255-31a9-42fc-bd24-98c2ea41047d','aeeb9f32-9761-4aef-978a-69099b967e80',1,'Which hormone is produced by the placenta during pregnancy?','The placenta acts as an endocrine tissue and produces several hormones including human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), human placental lactogen (hPL), estrogens, and progestogens. hCG maintains the corpus luteum in early pregnancy.'),
  ('4bb5058a-0d5a-481c-ab4b-e7080c39ef62','07585d2d-7893-4cca-924a-002d64bb5717',1,'What is the first sign of a growing foetus that can be detected after one month of pregnancy?','After one month of pregnancy, the embryo''s heart is formed. The first sign of a growing foetus may be noticed by listening to the heart sound carefully through a stethoscope.'),
  ('49c8a913-4191-4681-b227-042eacb6b5d5','102120b0-cba9-455d-87bd-a7d95f3d8e37',1,'What is colostrum?','Colostrum is the milk produced during the initial few days of lactation. It contains several antibodies absolutely essential to develop resistance for newborn babies.'),
  ('528998fe-24c0-47ab-b803-501ca98f3931','e972098a-94ec-41c9-bc90-b286ff820c9a',1,'Which hormone triggers stronger uterine contractions during parturition?','Oxytocin is released from the maternal pituitary during parturition. It acts on the uterine muscle and causes stronger uterine contractions, which in turn stimulates further secretion of oxytocin in a positive feedback loop.'),
  ('2c3c436a-8ac9-49dd-82b1-a03b89f65fb4','ba933d7d-ff88-43c2-a415-9f89efc88330',1,'After implantation, what finger-like projections appear on the trophoblast?','After implantation, finger-like projections called chorionic villi appear on the trophoblast. These villi are surrounded by uterine tissue and maternal blood, and together they form the placenta.'),
  ('5d597d1e-1230-410f-9585-2ffb2ac89e11','301e1c0b-0e9e-4787-88a2-cf3dd5935331',1,'What structural and functional unit is formed between the developing embryo and maternal body?','The chorionic villi and uterine tissue become interdigitated with each other and jointly form the placenta, which is a structural and functional unit between the developing embryo and maternal body.'),
  ('f62e29df-9c5b-4848-9a41-21deabad0837','59ffe2bc-8c6a-4e59-943b-9df9f154cd24',1,'Which of the following substances does the placenta facilitate supply of to the embryo?','The placenta facilitates the supply of oxygen and nutrients to the embryo and also helps in the removal of carbon dioxide and excretory/waste materials produced by the embryo.'),
  ('afc2c20e-ae32-40b0-93ab-3e999b5e7c4a','cd1badb4-2ef1-4e4e-af8c-0e87d2842b8a',1,'What connects the placenta to the embryo for transport of substances?','The placenta is connected to the embryo through an umbilical cord which helps in the transport of substances to and from the embryo.'),
  ('6377d0c7-81ac-4847-ac00-2c5999b021b9','90985e6e-8d2d-4b2d-85ef-8734e990d3ea',1,'Which hormone is produced by the placenta during pregnancy?','The placenta acts as an endocrine tissue and produces several hormones like human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), human placental lactogen (hPL), estrogens, and progestogens.'),
  ('6953ece5-c8ec-4a8f-b387-9b017601dfbc','b9cbae9c-89cd-4349-b5b5-acecc3da7a41',1,'Which hormone is secreted by the ovary in the later phase of pregnancy?','In the later phase of pregnancy, a hormone called relaxin is secreted by the ovary. hCG and hPL are produced by the placenta, not the ovary.'),
  ('d4ea6643-af76-409a-8ec2-60ea50ee795b','03c28444-0a89-45ac-ba65-d6203cd4ac44',1,'Which hormones are produced in women ONLY during pregnancy?','hCG, hPL and relaxin are produced in women only during pregnancy. Estrogens and progestogens are also produced during normal menstrual cycles.'),
  ('fe4c257d-9c04-46f7-90b0-aa80bc1a85e1','6c0a571a-3139-4df6-9cd5-4a35579f3fb4',1,'What happens to the levels of estrogens, progestogens, cortisol, prolactin and thyroxine during pregnancy?','During pregnancy, the levels of hormones like estrogens, progestogens, cortisol, prolactin, thyroxine, etc., are increased severalfolds in the maternal blood.'),
  ('774b3683-0d82-4b75-93ba-3aa06951605f','8660ffca-5981-4d9f-805e-6ffc6cc8301f',1,'What are the three germ layers that give rise to all tissues in adults?','The inner cell mass differentiates into an outer layer called ectoderm and an inner layer called endoderm. A mesoderm soon appears between the ectoderm and endoderm. These three layers give rise to all tissues (organs) in adults.'),
  ('01ca8cf1-9d9b-46d2-8d76-8714b64be649','742f48b9-742c-4f64-aa6a-83011d63b78f',1,'What cells in the inner cell mass have the potency to give rise to all tissues and organs?','The inner cell mass contains certain cells called stem cells which have the potency to give rise to all the tissues and organs.'),
  ('1469cc21-fb0d-46eb-844f-b3db3dd8fa9e','650d7381-f262-496a-a233-c74203eb311f',1,'By the end of which month is the embryo''s heart formed?','After one month of pregnancy, the embryo''s heart is formed. The first sign of growing foetus may be noticed by listening to the heart sound carefully through the stethoscope.'),
  ('b025cf0b-191d-4b48-b181-8fc6507657ff','d212f42a-55f8-4595-bf86-7ae0ac63ffc6',1,'By the end of the second month of pregnancy, what develops in the foetus?','By the end of the second month of pregnancy, the foetus develops limbs and digits.'),
  ('f4ae09db-1b51-4ae4-9aba-9bd7d2249332','8891123f-248b-4341-9f69-c4c6f060ae23',1,'By the end of 12 weeks (first trimester), which major organ systems are formed?','By the end of 12 weeks (first trimester), most of the major organ systems are formed, for example, the limbs and external genital organs are well-developed.'),
  ('aaa86677-6a61-42b1-b127-58f72d7eeba6','9ab8035f-aa75-4b0e-ab12-d6ce0ba28d70',1,'During which month are the first movements of the foetus and appearance of hair on the head usually observed?','The first movements of the foetus and appearance of hair on the head are usually observed during the fifth month of pregnancy.'),
  ('4f96f5f8-21d9-4c27-a56b-0d23aaf9f16e','63c8b792-a406-4b84-b538-294a8a5bd2be',1,'By the end of about 24 weeks (end of second trimester), what developments occur in the foetus?','By the end of about 24 weeks (end of second trimester), the body is covered with fine hair, eye-lids separate, and eyelashes are formed.'),
  ('b05ba378-3c3a-4bd0-bd9a-84a105a107f3','18327e29-74a3-4a78-b298-932740785b08',1,'How long does human pregnancy last?','The human pregnancy lasts 9 months. By the end of nine months of pregnancy, the foetus is fully developed and is ready for delivery.'),
  ('0cde47fe-53a0-4ccc-8f4d-c1c8cf86d2e2','a47e7798-995f-43f3-b228-27d3e9330bd7',1,'What is the average duration of human pregnancy called?','The average duration of human pregnancy is about 9 months which is called the gestation period.'),
  ('33885cd0-4ac1-4387-9240-22a39c6bfd12','89367900-4602-45f4-b71a-c86294f6ad28',1,'What causes the expulsion of the foetus at the end of pregnancy?','Vigorous contraction of the uterus at the end of pregnancy causes expulsion/delivery of the foetus. This process is called parturition.'),
  ('7de639cf-91e7-4ed1-8d5e-c020cd471246','aba3f7de-9ec6-4285-b2cd-228124b47070',1,'What is the process of delivery of the foetus called?','The process of delivery of the foetus (childbirth) is called parturition.'),
  ('fc283a2a-f43c-4b43-97b5-758a2437a403','b17850df-9599-4f39-8302-4ab3ddee784a',1,'What induces mild uterine contractions at the end of pregnancy?','The signals for parturition originate from the fully developed foetus and the placenta which induce mild uterine contractions called foetal ejection reflex.'),
  ('002472be-f2b8-4c03-8df8-f5b2597aeaec','535ce146-d191-4b37-9a13-cbdcd4b12b8b',1,'What are the mild uterine contractions induced by the foetus and placenta called?','The fully developed foetus and the placenta induce mild uterine contractions called foetal ejection reflex.'),
  ('f0ecc963-86d6-481e-bbb1-a355a8915561','a31623c9-e8fa-461b-b257-2d13b25fb2f2',1,'Which hormone is released from the maternal pituitary during parturition?','The foetal ejection reflex triggers release of oxytocin from the maternal pituitary. Oxytocin acts on the uterine muscle and causes stronger uterine contractions.'),
  ('4eb6db8f-d567-47a7-b52b-c44c7ced97d1','db1f885e-a776-4b83-8794-38c77d54a1ff',1,'What is the effect of oxytocin on uterine muscles during parturition?','Oxytocin acts on the uterine muscle and causes stronger uterine contractions, which in turn stimulates further secretion of oxytocin.'),
  ('146cd8fb-ac40-4159-96b5-9467e90482d9','76f248f0-4185-4aac-9c00-fd7975e8404d',1,'What type of mechanism is involved in the induction of parturition?','Parturition is induced by a complex neuroendocrine mechanism involving signals from the foetus and placenta, and hormones like oxytocin.'),
  ('5888c4aa-5777-4c27-85d4-ed490c24c5c3','985b9739-1946-48d3-91a2-2589b410aa3e',1,'What happens to the placenta soon after the infant is delivered?','Soon after the infant is delivered, the placenta is also expelled out of the uterus.'),
  ('def96986-c4b0-40a8-a17d-233548625a28','e985f18b-5ada-4b95-bba6-972366c62bc9',1,'What do doctors inject to induce delivery artificially?','Doctors inject oxytocin to induce delivery, as oxytocin causes stronger uterine contractions leading to parturition.'),
  ('a0ad07cc-6333-4dd7-9252-05addf1aa1c5','e5802250-d176-4e1f-83b9-de55df3553a7',1,'When do mammary glands start producing milk during pregnancy?','The mammary glands of the female undergo differentiation during pregnancy and starts producing milk towards the end of pregnancy by the process called lactation.'),
  ('f4c808fa-a37d-491d-81b7-0316a68af4ce','82027f8e-6bca-4bec-9023-82eb39bbb218',1,'What is the process called by which mammary glands produce milk?','The mammary glands start producing milk by the process called lactation. This helps the mother in feeding the newborn.'),
  ('0fd53e21-80db-4a08-8f38-bccef6d3bde4','3292d5f0-c66c-40f6-a2c8-139512631064',1,'What is the milk produced during the initial few days of lactation called?','The milk produced during the initial few days of lactation is called colostrum which contains several antibodies absolutely essential to develop resistance for the new-born babies.'),
  ('339d0540-15ac-4f71-8b9d-74a1d53d8931','6feff6f7-7a70-482e-bd8c-725e1ad69601',1,'What does colostrum contain that is essential for new-born babies?','Colostrum contains several antibodies absolutely essential to develop resistance for the new-born babies.'),
  ('da75d092-9a49-43dd-a22e-0a2d271300bc','86807513-add5-4720-ac8e-42ce88ed411f',1,'Why is breast-feeding during the initial period recommended by doctors?','Breast-feeding during the initial period of infant growth is recommended by doctors for bringing up a healthy baby.'),
  ('bb4182fc-82c2-4816-b171-6cbac1c78661','ca999d87-6a1e-4238-8724-94fd71dc6646',1,'Which of the following describes the stimulatory reflex during parturition?','The stimulatory reflex between the uterine contraction and oxytocin secretion continues resulting in stronger and stronger contractions.'),
  ('e3f9232c-7c7e-4d13-a56f-c35d7e15f35f','9fba7807-aeef-48dc-8bbf-888fb4b3fb9c',1,'What is the role of the birth canal during parturition?','The stronger uterine contractions lead to expulsion of the baby out of the uterus through the birth canal.'),
  ('54719f4d-9c3c-41be-bf66-72e9f7c001bd','7127d997-2cf8-4eae-ba25-4fb6b4c1a4a4',1,'What happens to mammary glands during pregnancy?','The mammary glands of the female undergo differentiation during pregnancy and start producing milk towards the end of pregnancy.'),
  ('dafb538a-d999-491c-b4cf-3719a031f569','0f5de32f-2332-4f34-a8b2-1209c7cdd0c7',1,'Which statement about colostrum is correct?','Colostrum contains several antibodies absolutely essential to develop resistance for the new-born babies, making it vital for immunity.'),
  ('0aefda5b-38d0-4d9c-bd77-30fefadafa84','62da5771-21aa-4444-8b30-b5be7e2ecc4e',1,'What is the relationship between uterine contraction and oxytocin during parturition?','The stimulatory reflex between uterine contraction and oxytocin secretion continues, forming a positive feedback loop that results in stronger contractions.'),
  ('1d5371b9-2db7-42a0-8866-143d2afa3ba6','644d522f-064d-4767-8b75-7fa9d0959768',1,'What is the process called when spermatids are transformed into spermatozoa?','Spermiogenesis is the process by which spermatids are transformed into spermatozoa (sperms). This is the final stage of sperm development where the round spermatids undergo structural changes to become motile spermatozoa with head, neck, middle piece and tail.'),
  ('be3263d4-6e44-420e-ab33-15ca0a2174dd','1687cd25-cec1-40a9-a184-a300bbc40f15',1,'Which hormone is released from the hypothalamus to initiate spermatogenesis at puberty?','Gonadotropin releasing hormone (GnRH) is a hypothalamic hormone whose significant increase in secretion at puberty initiates spermatogenesis. GnRH then acts on the anterior pituitary to stimulate LH and FSH secretion.'),
  ('7342abfe-3cca-4bc6-8958-8350f63275d6','3536c3d2-5ae8-4dd9-9684-ed85a5fe7019',1,'Which cells synthesize and secrete androgens in the testis?','Leydig cells, also called interstitial cells, are found in the interstitial spaces outside the seminiferous tubules. They synthesize and secrete testicular hormones called androgens.'),
  ('7ab4cdc2-6c69-4bd9-9451-dc19f8fadc3e','72e1e6b9-0363-4f07-8b88-720a95cba05a',1,'What is the chromosome number in secondary spermatocytes?','A primary spermatocyte completes the first meiotic division (reduction division) leading to formation of two equal, haploid cells called secondary spermatocytes, which have only 23 chromosomes each.'),
  ('c07cf47b-595a-494e-909a-af685bd1de43','d253bc69-8157-4644-814f-31a4c1609e93',1,'Which part of the sperm contains enzymes that help fertilization of the ovum?','The acrosome is a cap-like structure covering the anterior portion of the sperm head. It is filled with enzymes that help fertilization of the ovum by allowing the sperm to penetrate the zona pellucida.'),
  ('14b3af9a-615a-4564-a12e-97c9f0689a4e','775a7264-6e7b-4d3a-b0fd-d2af1cd25765',1,'What is the primary function of mitochondria in the middle piece of sperm?','The middle piece of the sperm possesses numerous mitochondria, which produce energy (ATP) for the movement of tail that facilitates sperm motility essential for fertilization.'),
  ('691e839c-a3e8-4c6e-ab95-70b4fcf74d4c','b2c1841a-8e18-4c82-8686-808c1b096ef5',1,'How many spermatids are produced from one primary spermatocyte after meiosis?','The secondary spermatocytes undergo the second meiotic division to produce four equal, haploid spermatids. Therefore, one primary spermatocyte ultimately yields four spermatids.'),
  ('6277fb6d-cf86-4bb2-b58f-e53ccfb75ee0','1c70f6de-cd82-4494-b651-10c3afb29d81',1,'Which hormone acts on Sertoli cells to stimulate secretion of factors helping spermatogenesis?','FSH (Follicle Stimulating Hormone) acts on the Sertoli cells and stimulates secretion of some factors which help in the process of spermatogenesis. LH acts on Leydig cells to stimulate androgen production.'),
  ('a85131bb-1fc5-47ac-ab5a-b4d3a046f4c6','3268198f-6e68-4042-a21b-c2deeb9fea44',1,'What percentage of ejaculated sperms must have normal shape and size for normal fertility?','For normal fertility, at least 60 percent of sperms must have normal shape and size, and at least 40 percent of them must show vigorous motility.'),
  ('06549088-644a-4710-95c9-83ced4bbdbdd','f84cd871-8c92-4270-9b49-639863a5ef7e',1,'When is oogenesis initiated in females?','Oogenesis is initiated during the embryonic development stage when a couple of million gamete mother cells (oogonia) are formed within each fetal ovary. No more oogonia are formed after birth.'),
  ('cc6cb798-8931-45ab-83a7-f220387319bb','c998a226-d5f3-451d-8da6-fd6f02bebb3d',1,'What is the chromosome number in spermatogonia?','Each spermatogonium is diploid and contains 46 chromosomes. Spermatogonia multiply by mitotic division and some periodically undergo meiosis to form primary spermatocytes.'),
  ('7edbcbe7-61a7-4055-8663-185eb629ff9e','7b3ef2bc-d078-4c32-8be0-892ca5093ce5',1,'What is the male external genitalia called?','The penis is the male external genitalia. It is made up of special tissue that helps in erection of the penis to facilitate insemination. The enlarged end is called glans penis.'),
  ('a7de4b3e-1312-4bd6-b27c-f9fcf84c4fcf','39a390bd-500e-48e3-992c-7d1d8afe3565',1,'Which cells provide nutrition to the dividing germ cells in seminiferous tubules?','Sertoli cells line the inside of seminiferous tubules and provide nutrition to the germ cells. They also help in the release of spermatozoa from the tubules.'),
  ('13efaad7-8a4b-4009-9dac-69f4a31c1711','53605b23-ce3a-4316-bbcd-32da69f860c3',1,'What is the fluid-filled cavity in the tertiary follicle called?','The secondary follicle soon transforms into a tertiary follicle which is characterized by a fluid-filled cavity called antrum. The theca layer is organized into inner theca interna and outer theca externa.'),
  ('6849c117-9a88-492a-84d1-d91f02becb51','f42e9216-2a70-46ac-ab32-88aa784abc72',1,'What is the structure formed when primary oocyte is surrounded by a layer of granulosa cells?','Each primary oocyte gets surrounded by a layer of granulosa cells and is called the primary follicle. Later, more layers of granulosa cells surround it to form secondary follicle.'),
  ('c1764fc3-35c0-4a94-a48b-9b2923da4f2e','e8c7f9c5-5de8-4c55-a829-1e0eba3e68fa',1,'What is the process of formation of mature female gametes called?','The process of formation of a mature female gamete is called oogenesis. It is markedly different from spermatogenesis and is initiated during embryonic development.'),
  ('13d021d1-23db-4bb9-a5b0-e125706b0bd1','1b39f4c6-efbc-43a6-8c9c-58e940faca8a',1,'How many primary follicles are left in each ovary at puberty?','A large number of primary follicles degenerate from birth to puberty. Therefore, at puberty only 60,000-80,000 primary follicles are left in each ovary.'),
  ('7de6de25-02b6-48b0-a73d-9692a8ccf779','b7b1b80d-ee2b-4e4e-8806-e846771a284b',1,'What is the result of the first meiotic division in oogenesis?','In oogenesis, the first meiotic division is unequal, resulting in the formation of a large haploid secondary oocyte and a tiny first polar body. The secondary oocyte retains most of the nutrient-rich cytoplasm.'),
  ('d714caf4-d998-4808-bfc3-f134ab7d0354','f37b1c7d-744c-4922-92fe-476659a1a14c',1,'Which hormone stimulates the synthesis and secretion of androgens?','LH (Luteinizing Hormone) acts at the Leydig cells and stimulates synthesis and secretion of androgens. Androgens in turn stimulate the process of spermatogenesis.'),
  ('51d31fa6-35b5-4613-a70e-201726ac5864','6f251a39-4504-48d5-a7ce-2f6618324fc9',1,'What is the new membrane formed around the secondary oocyte in the Graafian follicle?','The secondary oocyte forms a new membrane called zona pellucida surrounding it. This membrane is important for sperm recognition and fertilization.'),
  ('6e62dc7e-e17e-4ea6-8048-98ad029d622a','1ea9ef52-d10c-4c82-b9ff-1b024f840468',1,'What is the process of release of spermatozoa from seminiferous tubules called?','After spermiogenesis, sperm heads become embedded in the Sertoli cells, and are finally released from the seminiferous tubules by the process called spermiation.'),
  ('728d5d4b-c9e6-43c3-803a-c54aa7659d4e','13c274a4-31f6-444f-85b3-240fb1b7194f',1,'What is the chromosome number in spermatids?','The secondary spermatocytes undergo the second meiotic division to produce four equal, haploid spermatids which have 23 chromosomes each.'),
  ('7ea95ecc-f51a-40e2-8761-2b5726abcd23','63f08501-5bd5-450b-8c90-70e7919bd4c9',1,'Which of the following is NOT a component of seminal plasma?','Secretions of male accessory glands constitute seminal plasma which is rich in fructose, calcium and certain enzymes. Insulin is not a component of seminal plasma.'),
  ('67a849d4-1328-447e-a8c1-a281afcb399b','c344b6d8-1bfd-4260-a80f-548d7fcde58d',1,'What is the structure called when the tertiary follicle changes into mature follicle?','The tertiary follicle further changes into the mature follicle or Graafian follicle. The Graafian follicle ruptures to release the secondary oocyte (ovum) by the process called ovulation.'),
  ('3f5a7ec5-e13d-4c0f-aac0-fe8d201a987c','bf76e53e-3b75-4a66-861c-a26791f8ba15',1,'What is the main difference between spermatogenesis and oogenesis regarding cytoplasm distribution?','In spermatogenesis, meiotic divisions produce four equal spermatids. In oogenesis, the first meiotic division is unequal, producing a large secondary oocyte and a tiny polar body, retaining most cytoplasm in the oocyte.'),
  ('78052b48-bf4b-4811-8ec6-9d388d3effb8','d4817b64-51d9-4434-83c9-82938677ae36',1,'At what stage of embryonic development are oogonia formed within the fetal ovary?','Oogenesis is initiated during the embryonic development stage when a couple of million gamete mother cells (oogonia) are formed within each fetal ovary; no more oogonia are formed and added after birth.'),
  ('00a28e71-743d-4b09-8bb4-602cbb515204','0c6fda8d-a128-4b36-8302-59a2be95e040',1,'What happens to the primary oocytes after they enter prophase-I of meiotic division?','These cells start division and enter into prophase-I of the meiotic division and get temporarily arrested at that stage, called primary oocytes.'),
  ('106068af-db51-49de-bf1e-960a828bb7fc','0a606a7a-38fb-4c8d-8a75-7adbd1032469',1,'Approximately how many primary follicles remain in each ovary at puberty?','A large number of these follicles degenerate during the phase from birth to puberty. Therefore, at puberty only 60,000-80,000 primary follicles are left in each ovary.'),
  ('f24e6cb3-c7bd-4eeb-9da0-7ddeffc2ec3a','058cc7fa-40dd-4f0a-a42d-54d1ae04eb78',1,'What characterizes a tertiary follicle?','The secondary follicle soon transforms into a tertiary follicle which is characterised by a fluid filled cavity called antrum.'),
  ('0b66eb31-3ec5-4614-b511-f1a0cfe33426','a8ab13f0-7cda-41c6-bc55-24618aa1ff48',1,'What is the result of the first meiotic division in oogenesis?','It is an unequal division resulting in the formation of a large haploid secondary oocyte and a tiny first polar body.'),
  ('d7bbcfc6-d2de-48e7-9497-84bd410dc3c9','f4d44b44-afd7-4d73-a8af-51760e02fdb0',1,'What structure surrounds the secondary oocyte in the mature Graafian follicle?','The secondary oocyte forms a new membrane called zona pellucida surrounding it.'),
  ('8c71d397-46d5-46b3-92d6-61d8a099e8bb','eb7b4c1e-fbd5-4c49-82e4-f051ae0e43fd',1,'What is the process called when the Graafian follicle ruptures to release the secondary oocyte?','The Graafian follicle now ruptures to release the secondary oocyte (ovum) from the ovary by the process called ovulation.'),
  ('51d7c768-eb02-4d9b-bd20-1687bb8f00a1','16109e20-f21e-41ec-9079-3cc680f950be',1,'Which of the following is a major difference between spermatogenesis and oogenesis?','Spermatogenesis produces four equal, functional spermatozoa, while oogenesis produces one large functional ovum and polar bodies. This is a major difference between the two processes.'),
  ('c8b2b317-5673-47d5-bc38-6032db17c56f','dac4d212-e7fa-4a03-bf5f-ab9a29caacbe',1,'What are the two zones of the ovarian stroma?','The stroma is divided into two zones – a peripheral cortex and an inner medulla.'),
  ('134e26d0-0d16-40b8-9c1b-cc98bda34885','fbfe5d50-e60e-481b-8c97-71fbc17d0662',1,'What is the chromosome number in a secondary oocyte?','The primary oocyte completes the first meiotic division (reduction division) leading to formation of a haploid secondary oocyte with 23 chromosomes.'),
  ('a853fc55-da31-4b65-ba62-5b1fe749a64a','b23e7ee3-fe4d-4134-accf-8480ff90320d',1,'Which layer of the theca is organized in the tertiary follicle?','The theca layer is organised into an inner theca interna and an outer theca externa in the tertiary follicle.'),
  ('11c0dc87-faaf-44f2-a451-35f2ea2f2be7','89c3ca6d-3276-48a1-b970-d5e57647fcae',1,'What is the fate of the first polar body formed during oogenesis?','Does the first polar body born out of first meiotic division divide further or degenerate? At present we are not very certain about this.'),
  ('e2e97682-edb7-4e6f-891b-03de9ce234d9','a80ccb36-01cc-49e5-bc44-904852e54310',1,'What is the advantage of the secondary oocyte retaining bulk of the nutrient-rich cytoplasm?','The secondary oocyte retains bulk of the nutrient rich cytoplasm of the primary oocyte to provide nutrients for early embryonic development after fertilization.'),
  ('027fc97e-e6de-4114-9a0a-307c80d5dc49','c4cdbcf2-2ae1-468d-9646-7fbb7d36dcc1',1,'Which hormone from the anterior pituitary stimulates the process of spermatogenesis?','LH acts at the Leydig cells and stimulates synthesis and secretion of androgens. Androgens stimulate spermatogenesis. FSH acts on the Sertoli cells and stimulates secretion of factors which help in spermatogenesis.'),
  ('2c67ee41-84e6-4fdd-bfa0-0ba8bcafd45d','ee285fc8-bf45-4c07-9561-fb4af2ea101b',1,'What is the chromosome number in spermatids?','The secondary spermatocytes undergo the second meiotic division to produce four equal, haploid spermatids with 23 chromosomes each.'),
  ('506d90b1-2fc4-48dc-8c84-0fd4bf85e613','ca5ecf41-7195-4ceb-84eb-e115edc428cf',1,'What is the function of the acrosome in a sperm?','The acrosome is filled with enzymes that help fertilisation of the ovum.'),
  ('cad77eca-c094-40fa-b0a5-188e9e12ba16','43137296-c515-45ad-ab30-20f6c9765b4b',1,'What percentage of sperms must have normal shape and size for normal fertility?','For normal fertility, at least 60 per cent sperms must have normal shape and size and at least 40 per cent of them must show vigorous motility.'),
  ('76b1a56f-51cd-4010-9f40-ceb72806a6d6','330d4bbd-9688-4e72-b749-033f095d92e5',1,'What is the role of mitochondria in the middle piece of a sperm?','The middle piece possesses numerous mitochondria, which produce energy for the movement of tail that facilitate sperm motility essential for fertilisation.'),
  ('c67dd512-162f-49d4-8a83-01a826c95cca','bcd6019b-4249-434e-9180-5c95bc25c5e6',1,'What is the process called when spermatids are transformed into spermatozoa?','The spermatids are transformed into spermatozoa (sperms) by the process called spermiogenesis. After spermiogenesis, sperm heads become embedded in the Sertoli cells, and are finally released from the seminiferous tubules by the process called spermiation.'),
  ('f49207cd-7059-4af3-a625-01e713bdd157','833ed401-2997-44c4-81d0-f3ce1a25c545',1,'What triggers the start of spermatogenesis at puberty?','Spermatogenesis starts at the age of puberty due to significant increase in the secretion of gonadotropin releasing hormone (GnRH). This is a hypothalamic hormone.'),
  ('a1c63dee-fe95-40f1-bcc4-12a439837f68','a28ed77e-66c6-473b-b16e-9e0d4de8fe90',1,'What is the reproductive cycle in female primates called?','The reproductive cycle in female primates such as monkeys, apes and human beings is called the menstrual cycle.'),
  ('69b42005-6dc5-4b8e-912a-08e78223bfc7','fe18c353-f561-4b3c-8a85-6be474c2f3e2',1,'What is the first menstruation at puberty called?','The first menstruation that begins at puberty is called menarche.'),
  ('19e4e9ee-31b0-457a-9546-352a80e19075','847d8272-0ca6-44f3-a7ad-e19f5560a059',1,'What is the average interval of the menstrual cycle in human females?','In human females, menstruation is repeated at an average interval of about 28/29 days.'),
  ('2bb7f912-5fe7-444c-8fde-bc8a7af60bbd','6b56c4f2-42f2-48f7-b33c-654ab501b0d1',1,'How many ova are released during the middle of each menstrual cycle?','One ovum is released during the middle of each menstrual cycle.'),
  ('203d025b-ec9b-40fa-a381-485e39fc64ce','3652f665-2796-45e0-8495-f54f3df5c8d7',1,'How long does the menstrual phase typically last?','The menstrual phase where menstrual flow occurs lasts for 3-5 days.'),
  ('5d32dd64-015d-49be-b3ef-9a0a5e753d4d','c116f071-0122-4f48-8633-955958da9778',1,'What causes the menstrual flow?','The menstrual flow results due to breakdown of endometrial lining of the uterus and blood vessels which forms liquid that comes out through vagina.'),
  ('1b6493f3-c79b-4303-b42f-888727940688','5884557b-bd69-476d-aea2-aec31c70fca9',1,'When does menstruation occur?','Menstruation only occurs if the released ovum is not fertilised. Lack of menstruation may be indicative of pregnancy.'),
  ('2d067468-0e02-407a-a5a0-a29f82fadd7d','d3fd0d1d-19f1-4c00-ad7a-6b0186a4b334',1,'Which phase follows the menstrual phase?','The menstrual phase is followed by the follicular phase, during which primary follicles grow to become fully mature Graafian follicle.'),
  ('1198f458-76e1-47bc-b567-54ea86705713','e1cf24b6-f6fa-421e-9cc0-1a04f77cfdb2',1,'Which hormones attain a peak level in the middle of the menstrual cycle?','Both LH (Luteinising Hormone) and FSH (Follicle Stimulating Hormone) attain a peak level in the middle of cycle (about 14th day).'),
  ('9ef65b42-d7d7-4269-9143-80c1da22120a','18658c67-29ed-401e-8474-023280d43d6b',1,'What is the rapid secretion of LH during the mid-cycle called?','Rapid secretion of LH leading to its maximum level during the mid-cycle is called LH surge, which induces rupture of Graafian follicle and release of ovum.'),
  ('9f70bce7-3904-4015-8964-345701fde85c','b3d76710-7c47-4a22-bff8-18013220b8fb',1,'What structure is formed from the remaining parts of the Graafian follicle after ovulation?','After ovulation, the remaining parts of the Graafian follicle transform into the corpus luteum during the luteal phase.'),
  ('68b56691-11b9-46b6-9c3c-75812b262628','aef92542-a022-4ee7-ac8f-56bc6574fa6f',1,'Which hormone is secreted in large amounts by the corpus luteum?','The corpus luteum secretes large amounts of progesterone which is essential for maintenance of the endometrium.'),
  ('430eb802-ed57-4da3-8803-3c3270c312b4','e19c70cf-ddbf-4059-b899-b09d4cd75f3c',1,'What happens to the corpus luteum in the absence of fertilisation?','In the absence of fertilisation, the corpus luteum degenerates, causing disintegration of the endometrium leading to menstruation.'),
  ('b6727d09-b504-4059-93d7-8ea930294ab1','72dc0a6b-c334-45fb-9d3c-acdf6c5784d4',1,'At what age do menstrual cycles typically cease in human females?','In human beings, menstrual cycles cease around 50 years of age, which is termed as menopause.'),
  ('aebde0a4-d7f1-4a5e-a899-fa2308fa092c','dc46e3aa-1d2c-4493-bea9-4a14d26bc9d8',1,'What is the term for the cessation of menstrual cycles around 50 years of age?','The cessation of menstrual cycles around 50 years of age is termed menopause.'),
  ('c2514fdd-68c2-4aa5-8cab-90488b9651ff','844e2562-f377-49bb-9c63-ede50224a069',1,'What is cyclic menstruation an indicator of?','Cyclic menstruation is an indicator of normal reproductive phase and extends between menarche and menopause.'),
  ('4a6ebeed-6020-463e-a757-ca4d0919956e','451e8a23-6116-4b04-9ef9-5f7abba3d786',1,'Which hormones stimulate follicular development and secretion of estrogens during the follicular phase?','The secretion of gonadotropins (LH and FSH) increases gradually during the follicular phase and stimulates follicular development as well as secretion of estrogens by the growing follicles.'),
  ('66bd55e5-e140-475d-b4ab-911ebad06d5f','a7653f31-ca87-4842-9f23-43917c8de31e',1,'What happens to the endometrium during the follicular phase?','During the follicular phase, the endometrium of the uterus regenerates through proliferation.'),
  ('3ffea05c-e8e1-4dbf-8e9c-05decfdd252d','a45a4d37-7354-4875-83d5-dbe4362039d7',1,'What is the function of progesterone secreted by the corpus luteum?','Progesterone is essential for maintenance of the endometrium, which is necessary for implantation of the fertilised ovum and other events of pregnancy.'),
  ('c98f4052-eb8e-48ab-9c94-b7e7f9dccdad','b6c7a4df-2876-4a9d-aa5d-ba22538fa37f',1,'What happens to all events of the menstrual cycle during pregnancy?','During pregnancy, all events of the menstrual cycle stop and there is no menstruation.'),
  ('282087cc-b010-473d-a2df-5b68b4ebc274','6e46183c-2166-4803-8877-6233ecb23c0f',1,'What is the simple definition of reproductive health according to the textbook?','The textbook states that reproductive health simply refers to healthy reproductive organs with normal functions.'),
  ('dc1362e7-64ed-4d5d-b691-6d8683391625','17f4596f-7e76-4482-b4f3-756e8dba2ba9',1,'According to WHO, what does reproductive health mean?','WHO defines reproductive health as total well-being in all aspects of reproduction, including physical, emotional, behavioural and social dimensions.'),
  ('c8233f04-0207-487c-81fd-deb603edd84a','a3f798a9-1402-4ef3-9825-104426bad97c',1,'Which country was among the first to initiate national-level action plans for reproductive health?','India was amongst the first countries in the world to initiate action plans and programmes at a national level to attain total reproductive health as a social goal.'),
  ('704b804e-b450-431b-9393-e04c041493b3','38270dce-78ee-4564-8388-d378610452bc',1,'When were family planning programmes initiated in India?','Family planning programmes were initiated in India in 1951.'),
  ('68b6afc3-01de-469c-9f77-1bedbc824be7','3e263527-ef61-4383-8fb7-c8cda112bd7f',1,'What is the current popular name for improved programmes covering wider reproduction-related areas?','Improved programmes covering wider reproduction-related areas are currently in operation under the popular name ''Reproductive and Child Health Care (RCH) programmes''.'),
  ('c3b273a1-f3a1-4192-a0c6-2d1e1119b50d','8d334852-a2de-4445-a5dd-411d26228c92',1,'What are the major tasks under RCH programmes?','Creating awareness among people about various reproduction related aspects and providing facilities and support for building up a reproductively healthy society are the major tasks under RCH programmes.'),
  ('4e4bccef-702c-4e26-ac7b-16c0eae8b1e0','9c218432-3f7e-46b1-8872-38ebfa2d788d',1,'Which aspects of reproduction are included in the broader perspective of reproductive health?','Reproductive health has a broader perspective and includes physical, emotional, behavioural and social aspects of reproduction.'),
  ('34ee2e2b-7493-4ae2-97e1-fc1cbc32f13f','78bc89bb-6a41-4082-82ed-8da602413b16',1,'What is the significance of maintaining reproductive health in a society?','Reproductive health is significant because it ensures total well-being in all aspects of reproduction, including physical, emotional, behavioural and social well-being.'),
  ('e88a8c25-6945-4bf6-9505-af6b7686a061','27be7dc6-6327-4a91-8e7f-6a442631c60f',1,'Which of the following is NOT a dimension of reproductive health according to WHO?','According to WHO, reproductive health includes physical, emotional, behavioural and social aspects, but not economic aspects.'),
  ('d5603e62-56c7-4ef0-aa79-b4b69490e715','66939bf9-262c-43e6-8bb7-914fd523724b',1,'What does a reproductively healthy society require according to the textbook?','A society with people having physically and functionally normal reproductive organs and normal emotional and behavioural interactions among them in all sex-related aspects might be called reproductively healthy.'),
  ('437df31d-0306-481a-85ca-b7774dd2fdee','3da406b4-af55-45b0-ba8d-e60f7c25c99a',1,'What is the full form of RCH mentioned in the text?','RCH stands for Reproductive and Child Health Care programmes.'),
  ('6c78862b-8f46-456e-a908-66e25eaff082','3cd1c1d4-a792-4b8b-8809-ee14c367eb56',1,'Which of the following is a method to achieve reproductive health mentioned in the text?','Creating awareness among people about various reproduction related aspects is mentioned as a method to achieve reproductive health.'),
  ('9b9edeca-52bb-48ee-8bdf-ec09ab9084e6','4f0a45c0-4c0c-47f2-bfaa-9674732ef66f',1,'What is the popular name for the improved programmes covering wider reproduction-related areas in India?','The text states that improved programmes covering wider reproduction-related areas are currently in operation under the popular name ''Reproductive and Child Health Care (RCH) programmes''.'),
  ('bb990ea4-2311-4f77-bdb1-1a7a9459ae7e','1ac7e574-ef66-489e-b4e1-1a7694a844f9',1,'Which of the following agencies help create awareness about reproduction-related aspects?','The text mentions that with the help of audio-visual and print media, governmental and non-governmental agencies have taken various steps to create awareness among people about reproduction-related aspects.'),
  ('aa997fd8-8afa-4af2-962d-228a825d724d','14df07ec-c622-4071-828e-72cd056372db',1,'Who among the following have a major role in disseminating information about reproduction-related aspects?','The text explicitly states that parents, other close relatives, teachers and friends also have a major role in the dissemination of information about reproduction-related aspects.'),
  ('28bca158-be31-45c6-8647-275faea499b1','00fabf0b-b88f-45c8-ac7c-296fc11186f3',1,'Why should sex education be introduced in schools according to the text?','The text states that introduction of sex education in schools should be encouraged to provide right information to the young so as to discourage children from believing in myths and having misconceptions about sex-related aspects.'),
  ('ef4853a3-de3d-481c-98c7-f96e60486064','8aaef42d-8360-46f3-a177-712f196b3320',1,'What topics should proper information about reproductive health cover?','The text mentions that proper information about reproductive organs, adolescence and related changes, safe and hygienic sexual practices, sexually transmitted diseases (STD), AIDS, etc. would help people lead a reproductively healthy life.'),
  ('a5859751-5cdb-48ef-aaa2-855b655eb505','24b95eea-f0a3-4652-882c-9c3b95ce8283',1,'Which of the following is NOT mentioned as something to educate people about in the marriageable age group?','The text mentions educating people about birth control options, care of pregnant mothers, post-natal care of the mother and child, importance of breast feeding, and equal opportunities for male and female child. Space exploration is not mentioned.'),
  ('ed4083d0-2b5c-4a3e-abe8-27bd91e78b57','1cf4f317-16b9-4af6-bcce-367b5ae7c860',1,'What is the purpose of creating awareness about uncontrolled population growth?','The text states that awareness of problems due to uncontrolled population growth needs to be created to enable people to think and take up necessary steps to prevent them and thereby build up a socially responsible and healthy society.'),
  ('4692904e-b8d5-42a7-90be-bf2bb2e4003f','eee69366-6ae2-4a30-8109-1144c39c2da6',1,'What are the three essential requirements for successful implementation of reproductive health action plans?','The text explicitly states that successful implementation requires strong infrastructural facilities, professional expertise and material support. These are essential to provide medical assistance and care to people.'),
  ('772a922d-a27f-4100-8166-fe4ce4354b44','5cdafae8-ad62-4aac-b0f4-74adc387510a',1,'What is amniocentesis used for according to the text?','The text states that amniocentesis is used to test for the presence of certain genetic disorders such as Down syndrome, haemophilia, sickle-cell anemia, etc., and to determine the survivability of the foetus. The statutory ban is specifically for sex-determination.'),
  ('b6be126d-3988-4155-8829-63d5871b5ef7','481d2c96-5aaf-4828-9805-a0ec0cb18a46',1,'Which institution developed the oral contraceptive ''Saheli''?','The text mentions that ''Saheli'' - a new oral contraceptive for females - was developed by scientists at Central Drug Research Institute (CDRI) in Lucknow, India.'),
  ('bf0eb64f-5c42-4984-8a56-9c62a4e045c5','961320e5-4a52-47b8-ba15-fa401652ec8e',1,'What does the text indicate about improved reproductive health in India?','The text states that better awareness, increased medically assisted deliveries, better post-natal care leading to decreased maternal and infant mortality rates, increased couples with small families, better detection and cure of STDs, and overall increased medical facilities indicate improved reproductive health.'),
  ('b886b860-2bdb-4614-b3c5-312abf53e668','a073d507-bf8d-4bc0-a738-f8232ce22477',1,'What is the statutory ban mentioned in the text regarding amniocentesis?','The text mentions a statutory ban on amniocentesis for sex-determination to legally check the increasing menace of female foeticides. It does not ban amniocentesis for other legitimate medical purposes like genetic disorder testing.'),
  ('582920a9-8d86-4a2a-87b0-30802418e68f','6fa1994a-5277-4e23-a72a-7e28da0d170e',1,'What was the world population in 1900 according to the textbook?','The textbook states that the world population was around 2 billion (2000 million) in 1900.'),
  ('26084d82-e2b8-455b-a5b8-a80591711b9b','814f8b06-ce72-4c31-a43e-9879589f1684',1,'What was the world population in 2011 according to the textbook?','The textbook mentions that the world population reached 7.2 billion in 2011.'),
  ('ae92bcea-bb4a-456a-9678-69b4c27f5ed1','8cde4d3e-bca7-48ce-8da3-990cff22ad2a',1,'What was India''s population at the time of independence?','According to the textbook, India''s population was approximately 350 million at the time of independence.'),
  ('476a7f03-c220-4bce-b055-fa6efe67fd5e','f8d1eb3a-fea4-4c9c-89ac-29a655aa412e',1,'Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a probable reason for population explosion?','The textbook mentions rapid decline in death rate, MMR, IMR and increase in reproducible age as reasons. Increase in literacy rate is not mentioned.'),
  ('68e4efff-f820-497c-9c52-27d63eed3138','3ce43df4-9c98-4462-bf72-0a9e10df3d0c',1,'What was the population growth rate according to the 2011 census report?','The textbook states that according to the 2011 census, the population growth rate was less than 2 percent, i.e., 20/1000/year.'),
  ('06c5d388-3ff2-4ce8-b732-a6aa473bb867','c34d0fdd-89d4-48ad-9041-97f026e3dde6',1,'What could be the consequence of alarming population growth rate?','The textbook warns that such alarming growth rate could lead to absolute scarcity of even basic requirements like food, shelter and clothing.'),
  ('e81a6f08-83c8-4cb7-99f7-ac57a1fbe96a','4ec65133-588f-452b-a776-24e80d9bfaa1',1,'What is the slogan used in advertisements to promote smaller families?','The textbook mentions the slogan ''Hum Do Hamare Do'' (we two, our two) used in advertisements and posters showing a happy couple with two children.'),
  ('53306d7b-393c-44fa-b389-721cd8d5376a','21b2d031-f7d4-4449-a3ad-caace3160812',1,'Which of the following is an ideal characteristic of a contraceptive method?','The textbook states that an ideal contraceptive should be user-friendly, easily available, effective and reversible with no or least side-effects, and should not interfere with sexual drive or desire.'),
  ('a289fa15-6051-402a-9302-3d718a207b2a','e3c76ed8-a0a7-45d5-9d31-299ed73c9a04',1,'Which natural contraceptive method involves avoiding coitus from day 10 to 17 of the menstrual cycle?','Periodic abstinence is the method where couples avoid coitus from day 10 to 17 of the menstrual cycle when ovulation is expected. This period is called the fertile period.'),
  ('b0c33633-c3e3-43ce-baec-56cc17825238','46588504-1b4f-4787-a4fb-504701904769',1,'What is the maximum effective period for lactational amenorrhea as a contraceptive method?','Lactational amenorrhea is effective only up to a maximum period of six months following parturition, as long as the mother fully breast-feeds the child.'),
  ('a441c947-f7e4-4b14-9ca4-48802574e57c','4344a9f4-0372-462a-b477-b2a9b46c0625',1,'Which barrier method is made of thin rubber or latex sheath used to cover the penis?','Male condoms are barriers made of thin rubber or latex sheath that are used to cover the penis before coitus to prevent semen from entering the female reproductive tract.'),
  ('5faed433-4508-4d96-9a4f-85c512a68012','bd69b41c-a3c1-4add-9d61-6ee0d5f536f1',1,'Which of the following is a popular brand of male condom in India?','Nirodh is mentioned in the textbook as a popular brand of condom for males in India.'),
  ('2055b55d-c0a5-4e0c-b8f5-6febfa07f547','59f88e97-bec1-4e71-a0a7-7c6e271c170a',1,'What additional benefit does condom use provide besides contraception?','Use of condoms has increased due to its additional benefit of protecting the user from contracting sexually transmitted infections (STIs) and AIDS.'),
  ('6deac0f5-1428-4fbc-a130-2e574340e9a1','7d1151cf-036f-45c1-ba47-f53173770149',1,'Which barrier method is inserted into the female reproductive tract to cover the cervix during coitus?','Diaphragms, cervical caps and vaults are barriers made of rubber that are inserted into the female reproductive tract to cover the cervix during coitus.'),
  ('900e7206-b4fd-4369-b300-e3812586f5df','3134af85-a420-4daf-948f-f4480a83a7b9',1,'Which type of IUD releases copper ions that suppress sperm motility?','Copper releasing IUDs such as CuT, Cu7, and Multiload 375 release copper ions that suppress sperm motility and the fertilising capacity of sperms.'),
  ('961d72a5-7406-4e58-b05b-0c29123f528c','fde1ad95-9b2f-4cd4-b295-f9f980d49f2a',1,'Which IUD makes the uterus unsuitable for implantation and the cervix hostile to sperms?','Hormone releasing IUDs such as Progestasert and LNG-20 make the uterus unsuitable for implantation and the cervix hostile to the sperms.'),
  ('058f22c7-f2c6-4a54-b3a2-caa0221d754b','6154e0d6-4fdc-4860-9f5f-c217d28b3a9b',1,'What mechanism do IUDs use to prevent conception?','IUDs increase phagocytosis of sperms within the uterus, and copper ions released suppress sperm motility and fertilising capacity.'),
  ('92728a14-32d7-4de2-aaf2-0107209b8a95','35fb8724-358d-44f3-80cd-c80a00bcc2e0',1,'Which contraceptive method is ideal for females who want to delay pregnancy and space children?','IUDs are ideal contraceptives for females who want to delay pregnancy and/or space children, and are one of the most widely accepted methods in India.'),
  ('8cf8f545-5efe-43eb-bffa-9c7c71894832','bc07c6e7-9b0e-46aa-99f0-bfab341550dc',1,'Which of the following is a copper-releasing Intra Uterine Device (IUD)?','CuT is a copper-releasing IUD. Lippes loop is a non-medicated IUD, while Progestasert and LNG-20 are hormone-releasing IUDs. Copper ions released from CuT suppress sperm motility and fertilising capacity.'),
  ('ab617726-8847-439d-9482-3ddb4f672924','64895555-a5bd-4788-a8c4-90bd6a6fce41',1,'How do hormone-releasing IUDs prevent pregnancy?','Hormone-releasing IUDs like Progestasert and LNG-20 make the uterus unsuitable for implantation and the cervix hostile to sperms. This dual action prevents pregnancy effectively.'),
  ('560d84ea-e3f5-49ca-b0d4-5a51ab67e423','dcf0acdf-3b53-4f26-8f8d-0d1ae218f2ba',1,'For how many days must oral contraceptive pills be taken continuously in one cycle?','Oral contraceptive pills must be taken daily for a period of 21 days starting preferably within the first five days of the menstrual cycle. After a gap of 7 days (during which menstruation occurs), the cycle is repeated.'),
  ('f3d7fc47-1831-4617-95ef-97383792f56d','88d4e3e5-7e6f-4bc8-8c0f-5473e79bdc78',1,'What is the unique feature of ''Saheli'' oral contraceptive?','Saheli is a new oral contraceptive developed by scientists at CDRI Lucknow, India. It contains a non-steroidal preparation and is a ''once a week'' pill with very few side effects and high contraceptive value.'),
  ('45fe420a-4483-4545-9a73-cc5564dc45ab','6af80514-d368-4716-904a-909849d99aa6',1,'What is the time limit for using emergency contraceptives after unprotected coitus?','Administration of progestogens or progestogen-estrogen combinations or IUDs within 72 hours of coitus have been found to be very effective as emergency contraceptives. They can be used to avoid possible pregnancy due to rape or casual unprotected intercourse.'),
  ('1b0ccd1c-906a-4ab8-88d9-dbe28ecd870a','5048d82a-b24d-4893-80ac-bfb53ec5c46c',1,'In a vasectomy procedure, which structure is tied and cut?','In vasectomy, a small part of the vas deferens is removed or tied up through a small incision on the scrotum. This is the male sterilisation procedure that blocks gamete transport.'),
  ('d300cccf-202f-4115-80cd-641ccdc8a100','a79b331f-68e6-44b6-aefb-89c09a4ce6eb',1,'Which surgical method is used for female sterilisation?','Tubectomy is the sterilisation procedure in the female where a small part of the fallopian tube is removed or tied up through a small incision in the abdomen or through vagina. It blocks gamete transport and prevents conception.'),
  ('75fb2295-e082-4ca0-99d9-2d62eb5e6208','6f204dbd-6a29-4d8b-9a0d-271e8b23ef6c',1,'What is the main disadvantage of surgical sterilisation methods?','Surgical methods like vasectomy and tubectomy are highly effective in preventing conception, but their reversibility is very poor. Once performed, it is extremely difficult to restore fertility.'),
  ('2a1ad798-a993-4f02-ab90-d443ebf5943c','88b25fc7-d128-4689-881a-342ed0763273',1,'Which of the following is a non-medicated IUD?','Lippes loop is a non-medicated IUD. CuT, Cu7, and Multiload 375 are copper-releasing IUDs. Non-medicated IUDs work primarily by increasing phagocytosis of sperms within the uterus.'),
  ('1de36427-eb34-4a21-939c-fd4bcf77a8e4','1de49635-eb8a-40b1-8275-acd1d64dd574',1,'How do copper-releasing IUDs affect sperm function?','Copper-releasing IUDs like CuT, Cu7, and Multiload 375 release Cu ions that suppress sperm motility and the fertilising capacity of sperms. This prevents fertilisation from occurring.'),
  ('706d0013-fd7a-4083-af2e-5b0fe28d4ef2','da32e285-e364-4e3f-9019-357f6f0648e5',1,'What happens during the 7-day gap in the oral contraceptive pill cycle?','After taking pills for 21 days, there is a gap of 7 days during which menstruation occurs. Then the 21-day pill cycle is repeated in the same pattern until the female desires to prevent conception.'),
  ('bd67795c-708c-4352-9fef-cafd79634a71','89152716-766b-4d17-8ddd-aa6b8249eda2',1,'Which mechanism is NOT involved in the action of oral contraceptive pills?','Oral pills inhibit ovulation and implantation as well as alter the quality of cervical mucus to prevent or retard entry of sperms. They do NOT destroy sperm in the vagina.'),
  ('8356f7b7-57f8-4df4-9d2f-b53643023d52','e81daea4-eeff-450d-8c14-d1c24b0ac972',1,'Progestogens can be administered as contraceptives through which routes?','Progestogens alone or in combination with estrogen can be used by females as oral pills, injections, or implants under the skin. Their mode of action is similar to that of pills but their effective periods are much longer.'),
  ('7eecbf2f-9ed7-4688-a9b2-ba0ffce414ff','3b289057-8f1d-4d7a-8a3c-b7d67b08ac6b',1,'Who are IUDs most suitable for?','IUDs are ideal contraceptives for females who want to delay pregnancy and/or space children. It is one of the most widely accepted methods of contraception in India.'),
  ('6d457d5d-68a7-44da-b07c-9bf9a9230140','268be72e-8e4e-4f27-acbd-18b1f27f138b',1,'What type of preparation does ''Saheli'' contain?','Saheli contains a non-steroidal preparation. This is different from most other oral contraceptives which contain steroidal hormones (progestogens or progestogen-estrogen combinations).'),
  ('770edd08-1df8-4f22-b046-f796e9bc25aa','f2891451-29dc-4794-954d-42ede3064f0d',1,'What is the full form of MTP?','MTP stands for Medical Termination of Pregnancy, which refers to intentional or voluntary termination of pregnancy before full term.'),
  ('3a829206-3212-4956-9e3b-3f2f7e97b463','6ed5a4dc-b622-4b77-9cad-5d27964a69f6',1,'How many MTPs are performed in a year all over the world approximately?','Nearly 45 to 50 million MTPs are performed in a year all over the world, which accounts to 1/5th of the total number of conceived pregnancies in a year.'),
  ('e44903fa-e88d-417b-889c-c8509c647149','f75e6035-ad05-4edd-b3a7-3fea6d1717c1',1,'When was MTP legalised in India?','Government of India legalised MTP in 1971 with some strict conditions to avoid its misuse.'),
  ('25c82b9e-9a5d-417e-9c49-413d9a1a161e','4521c495-32ff-404a-a21c-f31ea5fa2526',1,'What fraction of total conceived pregnancies result in MTPs annually worldwide?','MTPs account to 1/5th of the total number of conceived pregnancies in a year worldwide.'),
  ('02cdc192-de15-4b2b-8c23-75e6df3c4591','cb26f197-7e32-4f24-ac7c-5ba6ade93658',1,'Why are MTPs performed?','MTPs are performed to get rid of unwanted pregnancies either due to casual unprotected intercourse or failure of the contraceptive used during coitus or rapes.'),
  ('927acb2a-691a-4755-b9ba-049374844310','210b18b1-a71f-4b7e-b100-f1062c9e127a',1,'During which trimester are MTPs considered relatively safe?','MTPs are considered relatively safe during the first trimester, i.e., upto 12 weeks of pregnancy. Second trimester abortions are much more riskier.'),
  ('b7202285-b21f-43ed-97f3-9356110aa2f6','b338f141-b178-4e8f-9476-f47c45f1dba3',1,'What is a disturbing trend observed in MTPs?','One disturbing trend observed is that a majority of the MTPs are performed illegally by unqualified quacks which are not only unsafe but could be fatal too.'),
  ('f1e85df8-ea8f-4040-9bfc-cae576b42108','d9c81d41-2a0d-4d46-be80-7a775e3152aa',1,'What is the dangerous misuse of amniocentesis mentioned in the text?','Another dangerous trend is the misuse of amniocentesis to determine the sex of the unborn child. Frequently, if the foetus is found to be female, it is followed by MTP - this is totally against what is ethical.'),
  ('ba893953-2c1d-4107-b485-804cba4f24e9','3f23f374-cdbb-41a4-9256-c12c38e24e33',1,'What is the maximum safe period for MTP in weeks?','MTPs are considered relatively safe during the first trimester, i.e., upto 12 weeks of pregnancy.'),
  ('81451432-c401-4c13-a83b-d9d5ce6aa7e3','0c36d537-2ca4-44d3-bfdb-f273e1c44d05',1,'What are the complications of illegal abortions?','A majority of MTPs are performed illegally by unqualified quacks which are not only unsafe but could be fatal too.'),
  ('c16021dc-a499-40cd-834d-8b4c6489ed2d','5ef25c7b-9c3d-44ef-8d40-3d42f8595ca8',1,'What does the text say about MTPs in cases where continuation of pregnancy is harmful?','MTPs are also essential in certain cases where continuation of the pregnancy could be harmful or even fatal either to the mother or to the foetus or both.'),
  ('168e687d-e78d-4814-b06c-8009b2af1279','99325280-83a5-4c95-aa5e-73ebb214df88',1,'What is the purpose of the strict conditions imposed on MTP in India?','Government of India legalised MTP in 1971 with some strict conditions to avoid its misuse. Such restrictions are all the more important to check indiscriminate and illegal female foeticides.'),
  ('b565e089-f899-4abe-b8ab-84c1c5be02dc','696c710b-934f-4195-9eef-8247b9c1b453',1,'What is the term for spontaneous abortion?','The text mentions that abortions could happen spontaneously too, which refers to natural miscarriage without medical intervention.'),
  ('d5ef9677-7534-42f6-b080-2793d581489d','15f38846-5bda-41e7-b024-646d74008100',1,'What counselling is suggested to avoid unhealthy trends in MTP?','Effective counselling on the need to avoid unprotected coitus and the risk factors involved in illegal abortions as well as providing more health care facilities could reverse the mentioned unhealthy trends.'),
  ('06b1181f-e59c-42b5-b67a-bedd5a3db08b','345ae075-9ee0-473e-be90-df9a903610f2',1,'What is the main reason for legalising MTP according to the text?','MTP is legalised to get rid of unwanted pregnancies and in cases where continuation of pregnancy could be harmful or fatal to the mother or foetus.'),
  ('6f677e0e-b90b-4e39-b950-4fdc14044ac7','8e178f38-e84d-4918-954b-cd25e889c0c3',1,'What is the risk associated with second trimester abortions?','Second trimester abortions are much more riskier compared to first trimester abortions.'),
  ('8d31aa04-454b-463b-b977-19a6542c3f86','3cc62d2a-f047-4597-838e-9ba9c7168d44',1,'What is the relationship between amniocentesis and female foeticide mentioned in the text?','The text mentions that amniocentesis is misused to determine the sex of the unborn child, and if the foetus is found to be female, it is followed by MTP. This is totally against what is ethical.'),
  ('9a999aed-a4f3-4260-86f6-d29b9fcf2155','d6ad9578-30f2-4c0d-8534-0889045ad09e',1,'What are the two main reasons for MTP mentioned in the text?','MTPs are performed to get rid of unwanted pregnancies and in cases where continuation of pregnancy could be harmful or fatal to mother or foetus.'),
  ('9a855284-44d4-4416-a661-15524a9edacb','181d2fb1-aa16-4552-9fa2-b16687913185',1,'What is the ethical concern regarding misuse of amniocentesis?','The misuse of amniocentesis for sex determination leading to female foeticide is totally against what is ethical.'),
  ('3f01d243-d8e9-4895-9a4d-a575a8091bf5','b7050312-4cf6-4bf9-b1e4-f6ff44a139b5',1,'What measures can reverse unhealthy trends in MTP?','Effective counselling on avoiding unprotected coitus, awareness about risk factors in illegal abortions, and providing more healthcare facilities could reverse unhealthy trends.'),
  ('f4b569bb-2920-4473-a6f3-8bab90164856','7a754282-791e-4dc3-884b-77051200a1c6',1,'What are infections transmitted through sexual intercourse collectively called?','Infections or diseases transmitted through sexual intercourse are collectively called sexually transmitted infections (STI) or venereal diseases (VD) or reproductive tract infections (RTI).'),
  ('c6ae0339-a539-4580-a5a7-8e2b957e55dd','f8837733-749b-48e0-b34b-16ae73fb2a00',1,'Which of the following is NOT a common sexually transmitted infection mentioned in the text?','Gonorrhoea, syphilis, genital herpes, chlamydiasis, genital warts, trichomoniasis, hepatitis-B and HIV are mentioned as common STIs. Tuberculosis is not a sexually transmitted infection.'),
  ('881ecf34-d196-4c9c-b8b6-b8f74f90a760','d8b06ee8-14af-4808-87b6-7a34c15682c7',1,'Which STI is described as the most dangerous in recent years?','Among the common STIs, HIV infection is most dangerous and is discussed in detail. It is the most discussed infection in recent years.'),
  ('f281526a-33b2-4b8c-aec3-074fb28e638f','dc6caabf-0896-483a-929a-9ddc5b79bc19',1,'Which of the following STIs can be transmitted through sharing of injection needles?','Some infections like hepatitis-B and HIV can also be transmitted by sharing of injection needles, surgical instruments, transfusion of blood, or from an infected mother to the foetus.'),
  ('72e1994b-7448-43da-adc4-4c2b8d4bb865','2c552927-71b6-4a32-b911-7717d8b60880',1,'Which STIs are completely curable if detected early and treated properly?','Except for hepatitis-B, genital herpes and HIV infections, other diseases are completely curable if detected early and treated properly.'),
  ('90d9c301-4cd7-409d-b6fc-7b6b530e0154','fc828c56-c9e8-4d7b-89c0-8c24ff50b723',1,'What are the early symptoms of most STIs?','Early symptoms of most STIs are minor and include itching, fluid discharge, slight pain, swellings, etc., in the genital region.'),
  ('47aa2a7e-e178-4f00-8e55-ec94f4d94600','a0db95f1-1999-43cd-affd-262ed9e0acfd',1,'Why do infected females often remain undetected for long?','Infected females may often be asymptomatic and hence, may remain undetected for long.'),
  ('77a27ad4-e789-4694-b97f-ce6ec6afc060','58fb004b-142f-4e9b-92ec-8fe1985caa44',1,'What complications can arise from untreated STIs?','Complications of untreated STIs include pelvic inflammatory diseases (PID), abortions, still births, ectopic pregnancies, infertility or even cancer of the reproductive tract.'),
  ('f53a05da-58b8-4e42-bae3-bb61b39f4f5e','774b793e-2fba-4db5-94b7-2ae44b9d3912',1,'Which age group has the highest reported incidence of STIs?','The incidences of STIs are reported to be very high among persons in the age group of 15-24 years.'),
  ('f784cc5e-0926-4f36-b963-bc4388e8320f','6ae625fc-55ee-4e9e-88f1-7da555ac4b1f',1,'Which of the following is NOT a recommended precaution to avoid contracting STIs?','Recommended precautions include avoiding sex with unknown/multiple partners, using condoms, and going to a qualified doctor for early detection. Sharing needles is a mode of transmission, not a precaution.'),
  ('e16c89dc-5399-4c3c-8ed0-c879f1ffd758','4c15a8ce-ffd7-4328-af71-1d19e87af473',1,'What is the definition of infertility?','Infertility is defined as the inability to produce children in spite of unprotected sexual co-habitation.'),
  ('deb2f4cb-516c-4471-9671-c0921083a51a','e3176771-2627-4013-9f1f-99ec191fdd95',1,'Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a cause of infertility?','The reasons for infertility could be physical, congenital, diseases, drugs, immunological or even psychological. Dietary habits are not mentioned as a cause.'),
  ('d6cdfea1-9b37-462f-a298-287ed9a66e8b','2c0770b4-3cba-4d5f-8710-f4be2bcd610f',1,'What is the popular name for the IVF technique?','In vitro fertilisation (IVF) followed by embryo transfer is popularly known as test tube baby programme.'),
  ('342f6559-c69e-4640-9be8-e96efc714b12','972b46c7-9128-4eaf-b7f1-cc2e5037b32a',1,'In ZIFT, where is the zygote or early embryo transferred?','ZIFT (zygote intra fallopian transfer) involves transferring the zygote or early embryos (with upto 8 blastomeres) into the fallopian tube.'),
  ('38e05869-9260-444a-b997-aaa4ec4d8c19','3e05af9e-9e24-485f-9a5a-184f8f11a83c',1,'What does ICSI stand for?','ICSI stands for Intra Cytoplasmic Sperm Injection, a specialised procedure where a sperm is directly injected into the ovum.'),
  ('2cfc046b-c84a-4cb9-9fb5-ff0e56d25b1b','bc66abd7-cdfe-4401-a496-4a77b410d658',1,'What is the full form of GIFT?','GIFT stands for Gamete Intra Fallopian Transfer, where an ovum from a donor is transferred into the fallopian tube of another female.'),
  ('b61d2ad7-cc2d-4783-ae21-c1f1daa331ec','28e31d5d-bd51-4a9e-809a-cd4056cdc2cc',1,'In artificial insemination (AI), where is the semen introduced?','In artificial insemination (AI), the semen is artificially introduced either into the vagina or into the uterus (IUI - intra-uterine insemination).'),
  ('eded1bb5-d396-4cb2-81a1-3adf6b76751d','5d6b1a3f-c04e-4caa-801e-16b385f24431',1,'What is one of the best methods for couples looking for children in India according to the text?','The text mentions that legal adoption of orphaned and destitute children is one of the best methods for couples looking for children, as there are many such children who need care.'),
  ('8980195d-0435-49be-9bb8-a11f8a05a2c1','d365c7cc-1975-4043-9cf4-dea6bb6f7ffb',1,'What is the definition of infertility according to the textbook?','Infertility is defined as the inability to produce children inspite of unprotected sexual co-habitation.'),
  ('234d53f8-9b17-47c2-a422-eff79ca1d2b2','d0ad343e-ac84-4d35-80aa-2531523a7c84',1,'Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a cause of infertility in the textbook?','The textbook mentions physical, congenital, diseases, drugs, immunological and psychological factors as causes of infertility. Nutritional deficiency is not mentioned.'),
  ('e642ea58-89fd-4dde-a658-b3df4189dd5f','a6c6f86c-4548-41cd-b699-b85cbf1d40c1',1,'According to the textbook, who is often blamed for infertility in India?','In India, often the female is blamed for the couple being childless, but more often than not, the problem lies in the male partner.'),
  ('76a9b4f5-9451-48b1-bcaf-57c2be174fa8','d312f952-c6b4-4cc3-a0e8-02c09bb34086',1,'What does IVF stand for in assisted reproductive technology?','IVF stands for In vitro Fertilisation, which means fertilisation outside the body in almost similar conditions as that in the body.'),
  ('3f3b162e-e29d-4d7c-966d-7692b142dd44','20521ad8-df72-4a0f-bb08-bffc9708888e',1,'What is the popular name for the IVF followed by embryo transfer programme?','The method of IVF followed by embryo transfer is popularly known as the test tube baby programme.'),
  ('f9ecac4d-d2d8-4403-8a62-d5b26a41e94a','93ae88ff-162c-4ef4-bc6f-3c62b757ff82',1,'In ZIFT, where is the zygote or early embryo transferred?','In ZIFT (Zygote Intra Fallopian Transfer), the zygote or early embryos with upto 8 blastomeres are transferred into the fallopian tube.'),
  ('605dd47a-fd91-4da2-8a01-fb6b5d3817de','00638e94-fdda-4db4-832c-1d036f40b139',1,'What is the full form of IUT mentioned in the textbook?','IUT stands for Intra Uterine Transfer, where embryos with more than 8 blastomeres are transferred into the uterus.'),
  ('de4c64d5-03c8-4c1e-b669-0dc074319721','1192d3b3-747e-4463-8157-9c34be67360f',1,'What does GIFT stand for in assisted reproductive technology?','GIFT stands for Gamete Intra Fallopian Transfer, where an ovum from a donor is transferred into the fallopian tube of another female.'),
  ('7dcd1dba-37e4-4290-b005-05c39a2e5f68','6bcb2e7f-cbed-42aa-9ef4-905c21a34b7d',1,'What is ICSI in the context of assisted reproductive technology?','ICSI (Intra Cytoplasmic Sperm Injection) is a specialised procedure where a sperm is directly injected into the ovum.'),
  ('5ba5c19d-413f-417d-8974-7818c3e36d5a','981024eb-501d-4efb-9671-0be7372667bc',1,'What is artificial insemination (AI) technique used for?','Artificial insemination (AI) is used to correct infertility cases due to inability of the male partner to inseminate the female or due to very low sperm counts in the ejaculates.'),
  ('552cbbd8-0315-41b7-a63b-e69539004f32','5b310b72-06a1-48f7-ba3b-87b4aa0dce9c',1,'What does IUI stand for?','IUI stands for Intra Uterine Insemination, where semen is artificially introduced into the uterus.'),
  ('7ff169a0-6385-4bfc-9c1d-02b018dce392','2a850304-7af4-4157-a49a-6ee88a3f75a2',1,'Why are assisted reproductive technologies available only in very few centres in India?','These techniques require extremely high precision handling by specialised professionals and expensive instrumentation, making them available only in very few centres.'),
  ('e0bb5422-6125-4151-b5b6-090a6976f558','7844c378-3782-413c-a8d2-98d809d729c0',1,'Which of the following is mentioned as a deterrent in the adoption of assisted reproductive technologies?','Emotional, religious and social factors are mentioned as deterrents in the adoption of these methods.'),
  ('830975be-5906-463f-b8ae-5d0deabd2362','a0860a55-a996-4c8f-b32d-209cd341c9dd',1,'What alternative solution does the textbook suggest for couples looking to have children when ART is not possible?','The textbook mentions that legal adoption is one of the best methods for couples looking for children, as there are many orphaned and destitute children in India.'),
  ('3a98140f-ee98-408d-9f8d-48bfd29c1313','2f2935b7-2586-49ea-ab88-794f1fee88fa',1,'Where are embryos with more than 8 blastomeres transferred according to the textbook?','Embryos with more than 8 blastomeres are transferred into the uterus through IUT (Intra Uterine Transfer).'),
  ('3321d265-bc60-4b04-aae2-75ae8edad2d4','f9436a28-713d-4414-9f96-364d471a0ee0',1,'According to the textbook, what does reproductive health refer to?','Reproductive health refers to a total well-being in all aspects of reproduction, i.e., physical, emotional, behavioural and social.'),
  ('ca0e0613-2586-48f4-9ce8-c957bb347f22','50042885-8235-4833-99ee-cafd44441f60',1,'Which country was the first in the world to initiate action plans at national level for reproductive health?','Our nation (India) was the first nation in the world to initiate various action plans at national level towards attaining a reproductively healthy society.'),
  ('8b0b470e-d655-4117-8d6a-6120ed7c125a','bd44e378-7572-4bda-bc8c-375ad54ff9a4',1,'What is the primary step towards reproductive health according to the textbook?','Counselling and creating awareness among people about reproductive organs, adolescence and associated changes, safe and hygienic sexual practices, sexually transmitted infections (STIs) including AIDS, etc., is the primary step towards reproductive health.'),
  ('9395af39-9db4-4145-b0ea-34e3d836ce2d','2c59da50-ca07-46fd-b8a7-99385a604b3f',1,'Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a problem addressed by Reproductive and Child Health Care programmes?','The textbook mentions menstrual irregularities, pregnancy related aspects, delivery, medical termination of pregnancy, STIs, birth control, infertility, post natal child and maternal management. Common cold is not mentioned.'),
  ('9e67d3b7-2317-46a3-9f47-7ceaf526cb53','aa5e450d-1899-4baf-a83d-7513a698389f',1,'What indicates overall improvement in reproductive health in our country?','An overall improvement in reproductive health has taken place as indicated by reduced maternal and infant mortality rates, early detection and cure of STIs, assistance to infertile couples, etc.'),
  ('e6721adc-b45f-41d1-bb4b-59208838a217','66b4d0b1-b762-4ac2-a122-2cd9b950abfc',1,'What promoted the explosive growth of population according to the textbook?','Improved health facilities and better living conditions promoted an explosive growth of population.'),
  ('2c2a30ae-e19f-469f-84be-41bfa910b589','1bc65be3-5f3b-497f-a236-142c7a4c29d7',1,'Which of the following is NOT listed as a contraceptive option in the textbook?','The textbook lists natural, traditional, barrier, IUDs, pills, injectables, implants and surgical methods as contraceptive options. Radiation therapy is not mentioned.'),
  ('e56b2d78-7a2d-4bcd-a1d0-8679e85b5713','c58f7fab-7f38-4c24-8fbf-05715c75b0d4',1,'Are contraceptives regular requirements for reproductive health?','Though contraceptives are not regular requirements for reproductive health, one is forced to use them to avoid pregnancy or to delay or space pregnancy.'),
  ('8b0ed637-4d13-4399-9fab-935bcec197bc','31a8132b-63d1-4371-bc5e-483f7c675d71',1,'Is medical termination of pregnancy legalised in India?','Medical termination of pregnancy is legalised in our country.'),
  ('7dc18838-75bf-4f76-8ab9-0cd9a29977dd','507278ad-e64d-4183-835a-53fecc412a07',1,'For which reasons is MTP generally performed according to the textbook?','MTP is generally performed to get rid of unwanted pregnancy due to rapes, casual relationship, etc., as also in cases when the continuation of pregnancy could be harmful or even fatal to either the mother, or the foetus or both.'),
  ('f53875b5-867d-40ab-9dfd-542c8d20b532','88f7b3ca-0f32-40a3-b1b4-4f29b777c352',1,'What are infections or diseases transmitted through sexual intercourse called?','Infections or diseases transmitted through sexual intercourse are called Sexually Transmitted Diseases (STIs).'),
  ('3fba2ec2-671c-4072-abc7-882a3ae03b09','5518828e-df28-48e1-81e7-57d9e09616a6',1,'Which of the following is mentioned as a complication of STIs?','Pelvic Inflammatory Diseases (PIDs), still birth, infertility are some of the complications of STIs.'),
  ('4891064c-a169-482a-8902-331f1a083a3f','282f25c0-2d24-49a5-8fa5-3f3ed24c20f7',1,'What facilitates better cure of STIs?','Early detection facilitate better cure of these diseases.'),
  ('98e79dda-d505-43d7-8456-8fd15115bd60','fb513a75-b519-4f95-bba4-8609219e047c',1,'Which of the following is mentioned as a simple precaution to avoid contracting STIs?','Avoiding sexual intercourse with unknown/multiple partners, use of condoms during coitus are some of the simple precautions to avoid contracting STIs.'),
  ('46059b7a-9b64-4aee-8e91-d3cffd5b8daf','b49c39bc-5bcb-4223-ad3a-c97ad00fc89b',1,'How is infertility defined in the textbook?','Inability to conceive or produce children even after 2 years of unprotected sexual cohabitation is called infertility.'),
  ('f71f566a-cb86-4fe7-a568-fdeb445003b5','8d51fc6e-cea0-487e-b7b1-e1b0da670bb6',1,'What is the ''Test Tube Baby'' Programme?','In Vitro fertilisation followed by transfer of embryo into the female genital tract is commonly known as the ''Test Tube Baby'' Programme.'),
  ('ae11a115-41bd-44a0-be16-07e9ffb050b9','476e364f-ef5e-4624-90c5-7da796be8a11',1,'What is the genotypic ratio in the F2 generation of a monohybrid cross between tall (TT) and dwarf (tt) pea plants?','The genotypic ratio in F2 is 1/4 TT : 1/2 Tt : 1/4 tt, which simplifies to 1:2:1. This can be derived from the binomial expansion (1/2T + 1/2t)^2 = 1/4TT + 1/2Tt + 1/4tt.'),
  ('c553b7ed-c1d3-4e02-bf55-585a18a887cf','83eaf70f-c990-47a9-9d3a-849a7cf3349d',1,'What is the phenotypic ratio observed in the F2 generation of Mendel''s monohybrid cross for plant height?','The phenotypic ratio in F2 is 3 tall : 1 dwarf. This is because 3/4 of the plants are tall (1/4 TT + 1/2 Tt) and 1/4 are dwarf (tt). The dominance of T over t masks the heterozygous condition.'),
  ('b75595ab-6048-4f56-9a59-2460cfa9652f','d609aebf-3d50-4889-b9a8-0c411e137bda',1,'In the F2 generation, what fraction of tall plants are homozygous (TT) and what fraction are heterozygous (Tt)?','In F2, 1/4 of all plants are TT (homozygous tall) and 1/2 are Tt (heterozygous tall). Among the 3/4 tall plants, 1/3 are TT and 2/3 are Tt, but the overall population has 1/4 TT and 1/2 Tt.'),
  ('aa18c033-4ab2-45b5-b1c3-980a0d91140f','8a4a0f82-2180-49fc-974b-8f59b4680edf',1,'Why is it impossible to distinguish between TT and Tt plants by external appearance alone?','The allele T (tall) is dominant over t (dwarf). In heterozygous Tt plants, the dominant T allele masks the expression of the recessive t allele, making both TT and Tt plants appear tall externally.'),
  ('b78f06bf-3620-4d9d-8f35-9845d659e277','cfd89b7c-5f60-4a1a-bece-91fcbc4b95a9',1,'What mathematical expression represents the genotypic ratio in a monohybrid cross?','The genotypic ratio is mathematically condensable to the binomial expression (1/2T + 1/2t)^2, which expands to 1/4TT + 1/2Tt + 1/4tt. This represents the equal frequency (1/2 each) of gametes bearing T or t.'),
  ('4f8c618c-9e8e-4d0b-9487-95dc0ad7dd55','537e5712-8479-4a60-9446-015968034e33',1,'When Mendel self-pollinated dwarf F2 plants, what did he observe in subsequent generations?','Mendel found that dwarf F2 plants (genotype tt) continued to generate only dwarf plants in F3 and F4 generations because they are homozygous recessive. The recessive trait breeds true when homozygous.'),
  ('7d5f3ab8-07bb-4f60-9b4e-29e63457fb92','4ed44520-c634-4f1f-acd4-7cd94e826959',1,'What would Mendel have obtained if he self-pollinated a tall F2 plant?','If the tall F2 plant was TT (homozygous), self-pollination would yield all tall plants. If it was Tt (heterozygous), self-pollination would yield a 3:1 phenotypic ratio of tall to dwarf. This is why a test cross is needed to determine the genotype.'),
  ('395c3496-ae4e-47de-a4cc-37923995e54a','aeb68ab2-1089-4c05-803d-909e5d569fef',1,'What is a test cross?','A test cross is defined as crossing an organism showing a dominant phenotype (whose genotype is to be determined) with the recessive parent instead of self-crossing. The progeny ratio reveals whether the test organism is homozygous or heterozygous.'),
  ('3a17fc4d-6bf2-44d7-94d5-f44060481f95','50765360-15f2-4b9b-bb11-cabf09f61e0a',1,'In a test cross where violet flower color (V) is dominant over white (v), what is the purpose of crossing with a white-flowered plant?','The test cross with a recessive (vv) parent helps determine the genotype of the dominant phenotype parent. If all offspring are violet, the parent is VV. If offspring show a 1:1 ratio of violet to white, the parent is Vv.'),
  ('84b5f6ad-82c3-425d-b127-71beeecc2024','851cc822-10db-4e1a-aa62-b6b182540c4e',1,'What is the frequency of gametes bearing gene T in a heterozygous Tt plant?','In a heterozygous Tt plant, gametes are produced in equal frequency: 50% (or 1/2) carry T and 50% (or 1/2) carry t. This is due to the Law of Segregation where alleles separate during gamete formation.'),
  ('76d9b67c-c816-4b5b-a421-c3a615fa4151','395dcc7f-3fc7-42fe-8a59-2b146f537b19',1,'What fraction of random fertilizations in F2 generation lead to the genotype Tt?','From the Punnett square, 1/2 of random fertilizations lead to Tt genotype. This is because there are two ways to get Tt: T from male gamete and t from female gamete, or t from male and T from female, each with probability 1/4, totaling 1/2.'),
  ('9bdaa53c-9cb1-408d-848f-57e6b7ec0224','a951936a-95f0-41ef-b970-1cb837edfca0',1,'What does the term ''dominance'' mean in genetics?','Dominance means that in a heterozygous condition (Tt), one allele (T) masks or suppresses the expression of the other allele (t). The recessive trait is only expressed when homozygous (tt).'),
  ('c41865b4-07f6-4899-994b-f38cf514f755','c8f59115-e63c-4f84-b2ad-30101dba2dae',1,'If a tall F1 plant (Tt) is crossed with a dwarf plant (tt), what is the expected phenotypic ratio in the offspring?','This is a test cross. The Tt plant produces 1/2 T and 1/2 t gametes. The tt plant produces only t gametes. Offspring will be 1/2 Tt (tall) and 1/2 tt (dwarf), giving a 1:1 phenotypic ratio.'),
  ('6f13c358-4af8-4ce1-ae4f-bf5a0efe05d0','396d7930-8b61-4c8a-a496-51382b4fdc6f',1,'What happens to the F1 genotype when true-breeding tall (TT) and dwarf (tt) plants are crossed?','When TT (tall) is crossed with tt (dwarf), all F1 offspring receive one T from the tall parent and one t from the dwarf parent, making all F1 genotype Tt. Phenotypically, all appear tall due to dominance.'),
  ('1774c725-d911-44c1-9395-b6ccb85c9166','8a39847d-6777-4f45-83fe-3344368043e1',1,'Why can genotypic ratios be calculated using mathematical probability but not determined by simply looking at the phenotype?','Mathematical probability can predict genotypic ratios, but simply looking at a dominant phenotype cannot distinguish between homozygous dominant (TT) and heterozygous (Tt) because both produce the same tall appearance. A test cross is required to distinguish them.'),
  ('66f78390-d362-461d-bfac-af04f261d06d','0e13332b-cafb-402f-b715-825f88cc0b26',1,'What is the name of Unit VII in the given textbook?','Unit VII is titled ''Genetics and Evolution'' as stated at the beginning of the unit.'),
  ('27a76bea-ab3d-47ec-8376-91a3e1a985cb','26533c04-2cc9-4af0-ae1f-c63f0226ee73',1,'Which chapter deals with the molecular basis of inheritance?','Chapter 5 is titled ''Molecular Basis of Inheritance'' as listed in the unit contents.'),
  ('d3c338fa-98db-40f4-8b2f-509cbe924ece','36c42fd3-9fdf-482c-95d2-b2a70282f019',1,'Who was the first scientist to systematically study inheritance patterns?','Mendel''s work on inheritance patterns is mentioned as the foundation that gave us an idea of inheritance patterns.'),
  ('a0d51484-4c0c-4029-a603-0b38d523384f','a811837a-7c0f-46d8-b3aa-84236929c747',1,'What were the ''factors'' that determine phenotype later understood to be?','The text mentions that ''factors'' represent the genetic basis of inheritance, which were later understood to be genes.'),
  ('d29b9e9e-0353-44e1-b84b-408322a6f300','b2537be4-86cd-4b56-94d7-5e7bb2c1d502',1,'Which of the following scientists proposed the double-helical structure of DNA?','Watson and Crick are credited with major contributions including the double-helical structure of DNA.'),
  ('774aeb4f-0fbb-4bbc-85b6-cd8dc96e0c04','e01c8309-ce34-4e09-a2ef-9bd7b98fda76',1,'What field of study enriched our understanding of the molecular basis of evolution?','The text explicitly states that awareness in molecular genetics, structural biology and bioinformatics enriched our understanding.'),
  ('d4960497-29d0-4db7-b176-9487f7fa0816','bf444ddd-20df-4cda-885b-7973781a6bfc',1,'What is the main focus of attention in biology after understanding Mendel''s work?','The text states that understanding the structure of genetic material and the structural basis of genotype and phenotype conversion became the focus.'),
  ('2b7ab9d9-42c7-4e03-86e5-d95899f265b9','7f69fc95-6176-438c-a527-b6c0167ce625',1,'Which chapter covers the story and theory of evolution?','Chapter 6 is titled ''Evolution'' and covers the story and theory of evolution.'),
  ('94461bd5-e1d8-4191-9a77-121d06c8381c','3c0a7275-5232-429f-8470-33ca90220a55',1,'What is the cause of Down''s syndrome?','Down''s syndrome is caused by the presence of an additional copy of chromosome number 21, a condition known as trisomy of 21.'),
  ('1bf5d701-0ec2-4fba-8ac3-a8305bce78fd','5904fafd-5121-47ce-92b8-44a876784f21',1,'Who first described Down''s syndrome?','Down''s syndrome was first described by Langdon Down in 1866.'),
  ('55931d24-5369-4e99-90a3-41999b404fdd','4ebf378d-4c7e-411b-b1af-4e79a93437a1',1,'Which of the following is NOT a characteristic feature of an individual with Down''s syndrome?','Individuals with Down''s syndrome are short statured with small round head, furrowed tongue and partially open mouth. Tall stature is not a feature.'),
  ('38c5e8ce-6574-4683-9964-68664d959b6d','b02cc100-6b54-47d3-b133-68681a0421fd',1,'What is the characteristic feature of the palm in Down''s syndrome?','The palm in Down''s syndrome is broad with a characteristic palm crease.'),
  ('79b401b7-86e6-4053-a2b6-e04c92c37f15','661b087b-d1b4-4782-8d01-31f6abf1505d',1,'Which type of development is retarded in Down''s syndrome?','In Down''s syndrome, physical, psychomotor and mental development is retarded.'),
  ('ae3d070a-ab2b-45b2-9ef2-a33191f381dc','a25c6324-90cc-4b48-9a6b-d972a918242d',1,'What is the karyotype of Klinefelter''s syndrome?','Klinefelter''s syndrome results from the presence of an additional copy of X chromosome, resulting in a karyotype of 47,XXY.'),
  ('8b53b8af-8f6f-46bd-9892-836946110946','ff52dc25-98f8-4bf9-b9c8-ba34baf14a82',1,'What is the total number of chromosomes in an individual with Klinefelter''s syndrome?','Klinefelter''s syndrome has a karyotype of 47,XXY, meaning the individual has 47 chromosomes.'),
  ('4621080c-808f-47d7-b06d-5aca560fb2ad','9324084d-8e47-4934-af36-46e7db233861',1,'Which of the following is a characteristic of Klinefelter''s syndrome?','Individuals with Klinefelter''s syndrome have overall masculine development, however, feminine development such as breast development (gynaecomastia) is also expressed.'),
  ('0fb805d3-afe9-4a3b-842f-1e6b47f62a8e','fa558371-ef3e-4e2b-a701-49456c639118',1,'What is gynaecomastia?','Gynaecomastia refers to the development of breast in males, which is a feature seen in Klinefelter''s syndrome.'),
  ('1aecd956-8689-42a9-9e06-d3777f51578d','712d4e0f-bd39-4954-ace0-7c1e895ba6e1',1,'Are individuals with Klinefelter''s syndrome fertile or sterile?','Individuals with Klinefelter''s syndrome are sterile.'),
  ('9ea45276-b11f-4d52-a8f3-66f2cfe2d581','1fb613b9-1ddc-483c-832c-71ee939baa23',1,'What is the karyotype of Turner''s syndrome?','Turner''s syndrome is caused due to the absence of one of the X chromosomes, resulting in a karyotype of 45,XO.'),
  ('fd523825-4d1a-48bc-8019-0dbfd3c9f0da','daa8878a-1516-4739-9266-ae84e826df71',1,'What is the total number of chromosomes in an individual with Turner''s syndrome?','Turner''s syndrome has a karyotype of 45,XO, meaning the individual has 45 chromosomes.'),
  ('a895fc12-c893-4af5-8818-dacce6319532','14a9941d-606a-460a-9b62-f2915b79ae9e',1,'Why are females with Turner''s syndrome sterile?','Females with Turner''s syndrome are sterile because their ovaries are rudimentary.'),
  ('cc462262-0bd8-4087-b96d-71687b02bd89','25293d08-634a-46c6-b614-d2081530142b',1,'Which of the following is a feature of Turner''s syndrome?','Turner''s syndrome is characterized by short stature and underdeveloped feminine characters, including lack of secondary sexual characters.'),
  ('93c7d185-0ecd-456c-8bbd-fe9764708dce','6f8fdf84-09db-432e-b057-dd134af11403',1,'Which syndrome is associated with trisomy of chromosome 21?','Down''s syndrome is associated with trisomy of chromosome 21, meaning there is an extra copy of chromosome 21.'),
  ('cea036f2-9524-40da-bbcd-50f6ee006ee7','8cc49c47-f1bc-4d4e-92ab-f5733f23378e',1,'Which condition is caused by aneuploidy involving sex chromosomes?','Both Klinefelter''s syndrome (47,XXY) and Turner''s syndrome (45,XO) are caused by aneuploidy involving sex chromosomes.'),
  ('61cae723-8b3d-4c9c-be30-35ce1229c87c','0d988164-52a4-49be-a8ed-d63b6af79a38',1,'What type of chromosomal abnormality is represented by 47,XXY?','47,XXY represents trisomy, specifically an extra X chromosome. Normal human karyotype is 46, so 47 indicates an extra chromosome.'),
  ('2c396729-506a-4ff0-adc0-13b2d8e54aaa','57e81e0a-6d01-4511-9d30-0a2d8fb3ecc5',1,'What type of chromosomal abnormality is represented by 45,XO?','45,XO represents monosomy, specifically the absence of one X chromosome. Normal human karyotype is 46, so 45 indicates a missing chromosome.'),
  ('0c855148-d7fb-44fd-a668-a2b38e6937f3','77f5a9dc-871f-484e-8406-4eef4f55c9ef',1,'Which syndrome shows both masculine and feminine characteristics in the same individual?','Klinefelter''s syndrome shows overall masculine development with feminine development such as gynaecomastia (breast development) also expressed.'),
  ('7ad14fcb-602b-4d19-a73e-70938049a36b','3534e8be-e9af-4293-919b-9e613a726383',1,'Which of the following syndromes is NOT a chromosomal disorder discussed on this page?','Phenylketonuria is a Mendelian disorder caused by a single gene mutation, not a chromosomal disorder. Down''s, Klinefelter''s, and Turner''s syndromes are chromosomal disorders discussed on this page.'),
  ('4204e361-04c5-436c-a140-de791c5bd11f','4dad34b7-b49b-47bb-87a1-09b94bce91d0',1,'What is the normal total number of chromosomes in a human cell?','The normal total number of chromosomes in a human cell is 46 (23 pairs).'),
  ('20061414-0492-4371-80fe-ffcabcdfc0e2','34b360ce-59fa-4dd3-8808-66024380a319',1,'How many pairs of autosomes are present in a normal human cell?','In a normal human cell, there are 22 pairs of autosomes and 1 pair of sex chromosomes, making 23 pairs total.'),
  ('869d2ebf-cbc0-4dbd-8faa-f3e7b9276d96','308daf31-1e10-42d7-9525-a598d4ea8a04',1,'Which of the following is true about individuals with Klinefelter''s syndrome?','Individuals with Klinefelter''s syndrome are sterile and show gynaecomastia (breast development) along with overall masculine development.'),
  ('31d1432b-2d12-4165-99a8-9d965d774745','f60c4af4-e36d-4781-b6eb-67cd99e659a3',1,'What is the sex chromosome composition in Turner''s syndrome?','Turner''s syndrome has XO sex chromosome composition, meaning only one X chromosome is present and the second sex chromosome is absent.'),
  ('f8223433-b39b-401a-af47-851e640ca16c','15e46902-2407-42b0-8d0f-a5539e97fe56',1,'Which syndrome is characterized by a partially open mouth?','Down''s syndrome is characterized by partially open mouth along with other features like short stature, small round head, and furrowed tongue.'),
  ('bb50c4ff-0dc2-4bf7-ac78-82f33151aa73','de9e9e7f-3a07-4925-820c-3d073c282fdd',1,'What does the term ''rudimentary ovaries'' mean in the context of Turner''s syndrome?','Rudimentary ovaries means very small or underdeveloped ovaries that do not function properly, leading to sterility in Turner''s syndrome.'),
  ('4181424e-2d69-45d7-bee6-f6547b29c822','8d051a8d-bbbc-410b-8d5e-e86f667e738b',1,'Which of the following best describes the inheritance pattern of chromosomal disorders mentioned on this page?','The chromosomal disorders on this page (Down''s, Klinefelter''s, Turner''s) are caused by chromosomal aberrations, specifically due to failure of segregation of chromatids during cell division (nondisjunction).'),
  ('ae27e61f-505b-4bff-b856-f446eb130a3e','b0bfd5f9-dd5a-4f0a-a07e-632c7d49ba2a',1,'What is the term for the gain of an extra copy of a chromosome?','Trisomy refers to the presence of an extra copy of a chromosome, resulting in three copies instead of the normal two. Down''s syndrome is trisomy 21.'),
  ('cd451bf4-d240-4752-9ecc-5db0f8fd320d','c0b279d4-1cc8-404e-8631-7ea280246cce',1,'What is the term for the loss of a chromosome?','Monosomy refers to the loss of one chromosome from a pair, resulting in only one copy instead of the normal two. Turner''s syndrome (45,XO) is an example.'),
  ('b4969b06-0033-4c9f-913b-8770f80a845b','e8aa77dd-3501-4009-b0ad-fd6da5f56050',1,'Which of the following is a secondary sexual character that is lacking in Turner''s syndrome?','Turner''s syndrome is characterized by lack of secondary sexual characters, which include breast development and other features that develop during puberty.'),
  ('919a3b12-cfbc-404d-a512-34df7ddc267b','8a6bdb91-4ac4-4e8b-afc6-1178b9a5d669',1,'Which figure on page 22 shows the chromosomes of an individual with Down''s syndrome?','Figure 4.16 on page 22 shows an individual inflicted with Down''s syndrome and the corresponding chromosomes of the individual, showing trisomy 21.'),
  ('601d8455-196f-484f-a178-c53ff34099ab','6099bc06-52d8-4a44-8bfd-34974c3f43d8',1,'Which figure on page 22 shows Klinefelter''s syndrome and Turner''s syndrome?','Figure 4.17 on page 22 shows diagrammatic representation of genetic disorders due to sex chromosome composition: (a) Klinefelter Syndrome and (b) Turner''s Syndrome.'),
  ('5ca1e090-5510-48ce-9921-ac70567107b9','f937cb2f-5f63-4a84-9b4e-8011ff2acb84',1,'What is the main difference between Klinefelter''s syndrome and Turner''s syndrome?','Klinefelter''s syndrome (47,XXY) has an extra X chromosome, while Turner''s syndrome (45,XO) lacks one X chromosome. This is the key difference between these two conditions.'),
  ('63781229-a63d-465f-be6a-9a05c0e79aac','65b285c5-402a-44bb-8eed-4709567cf823',1,'Which of the following statements about chromosomal disorders is correct?','Chromosomal disorders are caused due to absence or excess or abnormal arrangement of one or more chromosomes, as stated on page 22.'),
  ('b1b44434-374e-4284-8d26-83a7fb1ee00f','f507ec0e-05fd-4883-9a10-a96a96258197',1,'What happens when there is failure of segregation of chromatids during cell division?','Failure of segregation of chromatids during cell division cycle results in the gain or loss of a chromosome(s), called aneuploidy. This leads to conditions like Down''s, Klinefelter''s, and Turner''s syndromes.'),
  ('94248cb3-e2c4-471a-a408-041a8ca08e5f','b63e38fd-934e-4779-a32a-4519e630f368',1,'Which syndrome is an example of female sex chromosome abnormality?','Turner''s syndrome (45,XO) is an example of female sex chromosome abnormality where one X chromosome is missing. It affects only females.'),
  ('c784b2a0-9600-4e08-a2c7-b6a38d7ab196','e46f7e84-f386-4b90-8f8e-d121322fe652',1,'Which syndrome is an example of male sex chromosome abnormality?','Klinefelter''s syndrome (47,XXY) is an example of male sex chromosome abnormality where an extra X chromosome is present. It affects males.'),
  ('1309d8c5-25d8-41a5-83eb-b07aff1ca39c','f9f6fd5f-bcca-427c-8e41-30818e7559be',1,'What is the phenotypic ratio of tall to dwarf plants in the F2 generation of a monohybrid cross?','In a monohybrid cross, the phenotypic ratio in the F2 generation is 3:1 (tall:dwarf). This is based on Mendel''s law of segregation.'),
  ('f4a9a55f-e515-4f86-8ae5-b8575eb81d03','8924d1ad-5e78-498a-aa26-a6b0a1e1975c',1,'In incomplete dominance, what is the phenotype of the F1 generation when a true-breeding red-flowered snapdragon (RR) is crossed with a true-breeding white-flowered snapdragon (rr)?','In incomplete dominance, the F1 generation (Rr) shows an intermediate phenotype. When red (RR) and white (rr) snapdragons are crossed, the F1 offspring have pink flowers, which is an intermediate between red and white.'),
  ('ac7f5871-1219-43ec-b8a2-6e44a0f641a7','5f25c874-077a-4c03-b8b2-1a4c563f6553',1,'What is the phenotypic ratio obtained in the F2 generation when F1 pink snapdragons (Rr) are self-pollinated?','When F1 pink plants (Rr) are self-pollinated, the F2 generation shows a phenotypic ratio of 1 red (RR) : 2 pink (Rr) : 1 white (rr). This differs from the 3:1 ratio seen in complete dominance.'),
  ('cc04706d-9818-4425-b205-2d99a7ac4ffa','b19a9cab-1170-4cb4-b508-9530460cf39f',1,'What is the genotypic ratio in the F2 generation of a snapdragon cross showing incomplete dominance?','The genotypic ratio in F2 is 1 RR : 2 Rr : 1 rr, which is the same as any Mendelian monohybrid cross. However, because R is not completely dominant over r, each genotype shows a distinct phenotype.'),
  ('be18b1c1-4711-4b37-96b3-8ec6beaf2852','8bb889bc-0128-4a7b-b455-4e5cbd504cdc',1,'Which plant is used as a classic example to understand incomplete dominance in the NCERT textbook?','The snapdragon (Antirrhinum sp.), also called dog flower, is used as the classic example in NCERT to demonstrate incomplete dominance, where red and white flowers produce pink F1 offspring.'),
  ('88b0ff89-2794-4d37-9d50-975d1c879fca','76180d4e-9bc4-447b-8d91-49901529caff',1,'According to the explanation of dominance in the textbook, if a modified allele produces a non-functional enzyme, what is the effect on the phenotype?','When a modified allele produces a non-functional enzyme or no enzyme, the phenotype depends only on the functioning of the unmodified (normal) allele. The unmodified allele is dominant, and the modified allele is recessive.'),
  ('21f39974-d31a-4ab6-b1be-6cd3b5850199','4feac6dd-898d-494a-afb6-6481106b7329',1,'The textbook mentions three possible outcomes when a modified allele affects enzyme production. Which of the following is NOT one of those outcomes?','The textbook states three possibilities: (i) normal/less efficient enzyme, (ii) non-functional enzyme, or (iii) no enzyme at all. Over-efficient enzyme is not mentioned.'),
  ('fc43a106-658c-44e5-b698-5021f90bc75a','ef291388-01f8-405e-b3d5-cc7e22367d2b',1,'How does incomplete dominance differ from complete dominance in terms of F1 phenotype?','In complete dominance, F1 resembles the dominant parent. In incomplete dominance, F1 shows an intermediate phenotype that is between the two parental phenotypes, like pink flowers from red and white parents.'),
  ('1dc90194-0708-4f71-ae4c-1edd07a64ad7','0f8e72ae-839f-4f0e-ad9b-a6d91565dc33',1,'In the snapdragon example, what do the genotypes RR, Rr, and rr represent in terms of flower color?','In incomplete dominance: RR (homozygous dominant) produces red flowers, Rr (heterozygous) produces pink flowers, and rr (homozygous recessive) produces white flowers.'),
  ('51128834-584a-46f6-97a1-c1f4ce10ffb1','11a6e088-976a-449c-ae50-22872353597b',1,'The textbook mentions that the genotypic ratio 1/4 TT : 1/2 Tt : 1/4 tt can be mathematically condensed to which binomial expression?','The genotypic ratio is mathematically condensed to (1/2T + 1/2t)^2, which expands to 1/4 TT + 1/2 Tt + 1/4 tt. This represents the probability of gamete combinations.'),
  ('0b35bf20-8adb-4300-9135-12234f1995c9','4793f2d2-949f-4611-8a95-cfe805340d05',1,'What is the purpose of a test cross as described in the textbook?','A test cross is used to determine the genotype of an organism showing a dominant phenotype by crossing it with a recessive parent. The progeny ratios help identify whether the organism is homozygous or heterozygous.'),
  ('342f1995-335d-4ffd-bcb5-15d0f95de2fb','d529f89d-1a3f-49ff-ab15-5bbd980f7ef7',1,'What does ''true-breeding'' mean in the context of Mendelian genetics?','True-breeding organisms are homozygous for a particular trait and produce offspring identical to themselves when self-pollinated or crossed with similar true-breeding organisms.'),
  ('675a0bbe-132b-455f-b487-b42f8ff0adaa','f3f3070a-eeea-494d-af0c-4fb0f432a755',1,'In a monohybrid cross with complete dominance, what fraction of the F2 generation shows the dominant phenotype?','In a monohybrid cross with complete dominance, the F2 phenotypic ratio is 3 dominant : 1 recessive. Therefore, 3/4 of the F2 plants show the dominant phenotype (both TT and Tt genotypes).'),
  ('c601a1e7-696a-4a54-b0a8-31c3f13dc944','7eef0175-9092-4ef7-9eec-1d71e8a8c37b',1,'In the F2 generation of a monohybrid cross, can you distinguish between TT and Tt tall plants just by looking at them?','Externally, it is not possible to distinguish between TT and Tt plants because both show the dominant ''tall'' phenotype. Only a test cross can reveal the genotype.'),
  ('ca9a97b4-829d-4d2d-b5cd-16a8b8c41dcf','27497c70-d730-468d-9d5b-de2446fc2fd9',1,'A diploid organism is heterozygous for 4 loci. How many types of gametes can it produce?','The number of gamete types is calculated as 2^n, where n is the number of heterozygous loci. For 4 loci, it is 2^4 = 16 types of gametes.'),
  ('5dfbea68-f20a-4949-8418-5253aca5fb8e','73611a82-a92e-491f-966a-263395db3eae',1,'When Mendel self-pollinated dwarf F2 plants (tt), what did he observe in subsequent generations?','Mendel found that dwarf F2 plants (tt) continued to produce only dwarf plants in F3 and subsequent generations because they are homozygous recessive.'),
  ('8c1a3d78-c6f0-4bd7-8f5a-231095cebe80','d9f862cf-c6e6-417f-b426-5a4f5a2e8682',1,'Which law states that when two alleles of a gene are brought together in a hybrid, only one expresses itself while the other remains hidden?','The Law of Dominance states that when two contrasting alleles are brought together in a hybrid, only one (the dominant allele) expresses itself in the phenotype while the other (recessive) remains hidden.'),
  ('eba303f3-1b26-4db8-bf75-5ce62a59c868','ab6fffd1-617c-453c-852a-dcbe31cb5e9b',1,'What is the primary purpose of using a Punnett square in genetics?','A Punnett square is a diagram used to predict the probability of different genotypes and phenotypes in the offspring of a genetic cross by showing all possible combinations of gametes.'),
  ('bad9568b-5e01-4855-a42c-a4d29658c70b','4ae4e84b-4686-4386-9634-751fd88f2365',1,'What is the result of random fertilization in a monohybrid cross between Tt parents?','Random fertilization of gametes from Tt parents results in zygotes with genotypes TT, Tt, and tt in the ratio 1:2:1. This is because each parent produces T and t gametes in equal frequency.'),
  ('a255b983-c91e-4cde-8b22-788abe4960d4','88eff06a-4499-488b-bd59-87ff8d4dcb60',1,'According to the textbook''s explanation of dominance, why is the normal allele considered dominant?','The normal (dominant) allele produces a functional enzyme needed for the transformation of substrate S. The modified (recessive) allele may produce a non-functional enzyme or no enzyme, so the phenotype depends on the normal allele.'),
  ('9b688933-c8e7-4837-b2a6-3ae294c73878','c3aef785-be11-4c0c-87a9-ca2e72d70056',1,'In a monohybrid cross with complete dominance, what is the phenotypic ratio in F2?','In a monohybrid cross with complete dominance, the F2 phenotypic ratio is 3:1 (3 dominant phenotype : 1 recessive phenotype). The genotypic ratio is 1:2:1.'),
  ('6f5b6e27-0b71-4fc4-84e9-7291a787ca46','d4254bfb-39c0-44aa-8c6a-62c5cc4c9cc9',1,'In Mendel''s dihybrid cross involving seed colour and seed shape, which trait was dominant over green seed colour?','Yellow colour was dominant over green seed colour in Mendel''s dihybrid cross. According to the text, yellow colour was dominant over green and round shape was dominant over wrinkled.'),
  ('60077201-0b2c-4751-9c0c-1f683253ee54','ab2a1658-5ade-466a-ba35-ffc5ed5ac597',1,'In the dihybrid cross, what was the genotype of the parent plant with round yellow seeds?','The genotype of the parent plant with round yellow seeds was RRYY. The text states that the genotype of the parents can be written as RRYY and rryy for the two plants.'),
  ('70bf875a-5991-44b4-813f-be7e53472dfd','4098f005-f101-4613-bf85-dc0232383a8c',1,'What was the genotype of the F1 hybrid produced from the cross between RRYY and rryy parents?','The F1 hybrid genotype was RrYy. The text states that the gametes RY and ry unite on fertilisation to produce the F1 hybrid RrYy.'),
  ('1f72bb49-cd84-43e5-9931-7cb9c614bac9','b79ed48c-4b03-4e4a-9434-60b452ec1fb9',1,'When Mendel self-hybridised the F1 plants, what fraction of the F2 plants had yellow seeds?','3/4th of the F2 plants had yellow seeds. The text explicitly states that when Mendel self hybridised the F1 plants he found that 3/4th of F2 plants had yellow seeds and 1/4th had green.'),
  ('c8e6a213-f213-4aa9-94d0-f778923fe280','0930c90d-97c7-42c0-8266-3abc31d158ab',1,'What fraction of the F2 plants had green seeds in the dihybrid cross?','1/4th of the F2 plants had green seeds. The text states that 3/4th had yellow seeds and 1/4th had green seeds, segregating in a 3:1 ratio.'),
  ('639fa539-01ce-4a7e-b703-ed77f41849b5','d8baa2f5-e434-411e-b1a8-d95e512ecd48',1,'The segregation of yellow and green seed colours in the F2 generation followed which ratio?','The yellow and green colour segregated in a 3:1 ratio. The text states that the yellow and green colour segregated in a 3:1 ratio, just like in a monohybrid cross.'),
  ('5d3bcb20-aff6-48c8-8f7d-b8aaf205ce98','65467fa5-d64f-4d0b-906a-4c8188a97552',1,'What was the genotype of the parent plant with wrinkled green seeds?','The genotype of the parent plant with wrinkled green seeds was rryy. The text states that the genotype of the parents can be written as RRYY and rryy for the two plants.'),
  ('b398b6dd-0bc1-4889-b030-38ca8f07d741','900df8c6-9769-4377-9ea7-8fb538f6255a',1,'Which symbol did Mendel use for the dominant round seed shape?','Mendel used R for the dominant round seed shape. The text states: Let us use the genotypic symbols Y for dominant yellow seed colour and y for recessive green seed colour, R for round shaped seeds and r for wrinkled seed shape.'),
  ('776f0dd4-b58c-4814-86e6-c692541dbf2e','ff69a0e3-4e3f-4b12-ac74-cd77954644e1',1,'Which gametes unite during fertilisation to produce the F1 hybrid in the dihybrid cross?','The gametes RY and ry unite on fertilisation to produce the F1 hybrid RrYy. The text explicitly states this in the description of the dihybrid cross.'),
  ('b7a83a17-772a-4e5a-9422-6cbfff23d9f5','b6a7bd32-b1bb-47ce-b3e9-cf7c46765918',1,'The results of the dihybrid cross for each trait separately were identical to what type of cross?','The results were identical to those obtained in separate monohybrid crosses. The text states that these results were identical to those that he got when he made separate monohybrid crosses between yellow and green seeded plants and between round and wrinkled seeded plants.'),
  ('e4304675-df24-46bf-8a6c-42f9165eb8ca','885fd672-877a-40ea-b8e4-45ded9b2d08c',1,'Sickle-cell anaemia is inherited as which type of trait?','Sickle-cell anaemia is an autosomal recessive trait that is transmitted from parents to offspring when both partners are carriers for the gene.'),
  ('8573f733-a7ce-42d1-b70c-40d75c7e9a0d','8f26d774-9510-4142-9bc4-68b1cc654c51',1,'Which amino acid is substituted by Valine in sickle-cell anaemia?','In sickle-cell anaemia, Glutamic acid (Glu) is substituted by Valine (Val) at the sixth position of the beta globin chain of the haemoglobin molecule.'),
  ('2a250089-0965-44a7-aec3-112d72ad177e','33c9a57e-1f17-4462-9dcf-c1523db0345f',1,'What is the base substitution in the beta globin gene that causes sickle-cell anaemia?','The substitution of amino acid in the globin protein results from a single base substitution at the sixth codon of the beta globin gene from GAG to GUG.'),
  ('e6a09696-8762-4048-9a7b-e98cb7490ed5','63ec731f-30fe-4ddf-aeb4-40ea6b08e82e',1,'Which individuals show the diseased phenotype in sickle-cell anaemia?','Only homozygous individuals for HbS (HbS HbS) show the diseased phenotype. Heterozygous individuals (HbA HbS) appear unaffected but are carriers.'),
  ('7dbc17cb-f0bd-424a-a0e8-2340af7ed755','67499ba3-2fb5-4f8b-ac83-47819d2129b7',1,'What is the probability of a heterozygous parent transmitting the mutant gene for sickle-cell anaemia to offspring?','Heterozygous individuals (HbA HbS) are carriers of the disease and there is a 50 percent probability of transmission of the mutant gene to the progeny.'),
  ('6fd0e0d2-aca6-4fff-8d04-f7e5950906f1','f547996e-4f80-4276-95d4-bb4dcfd679d6',1,'Phenylketonuria is caused by the lack of which enzyme?','Phenylketonuria is an inborn error of metabolism inherited as an autosomal recessive trait where the affected individual lacks an enzyme that converts phenylalanine into tyrosine.'),
  ('a8cad490-ffd8-41dc-bc8a-0a105682385a','91cc5578-cedd-456f-8d79-4b2199f51b18',1,'What accumulates in the brain of an individual with phenylketonuria causing mental retardation?','In phenylketonuria, phenylalanine is accumulated and converted into phenylpyruvic acid and other derivatives. Accumulation of these in brain results in mental retardation.'),
  ('e8aa0e73-e588-4f71-86b5-34a0e24d19fa','35615c9c-6575-41f4-af89-d94f4f25a37c',1,'Thalassemia is classified based on which characteristic?','Thalassemia can be classified according to which chain of the haemoglobin molecule is affected. In alpha Thalassemia, production of alpha globin chain is affected while in beta Thalassemia, production of beta globin chain is affected.'),
  ('8da1933c-620d-4695-83ac-0f1d6453cc9f','9ad6db31-e42d-406f-83b8-1f4d105f5f47',1,'Alpha Thalassemia is controlled by how many genes on chromosome 16 from each parent?','Alpha Thalassemia is controlled by two closely linked genes HBA1 and HBA2 on chromosome 16 of each parent.'),
  ('ec3cbd7a-c0b2-4851-b819-c359547c4ae2','904081a7-6ace-440b-b7a0-5a130a92ca2f',1,'What is the key difference between sickle-cell anaemia and thalassemia?','Thalassemia differs from sickle-cell anaemia in that the former is a quantitative problem of synthesising too few globin molecules while the latter is a qualitative problem of synthesising an incorrectly functioning molecule.'),
  ('6bff1cf6-d53e-4a3e-970d-fa523130c011','15495ae0-d723-4786-8fb2-5fa11471fae3',1,'In the ABO blood grouping system, what is the relationship between alleles I^A and I^B?','I^A and I^B are co-dominant to each other. When both alleles are present together (I^A I^B), both express their own types of sugars, resulting in the AB blood group. This is a classic example of co-dominance.'),
  ('a86d34bb-17ac-4cba-b857-b005a7e87fc4','980731ac-130d-4686-bb3b-f6d3a34937d0',1,'What is the phenotype of an individual with genotype I^A i?','The genotype I^A i results in blood group A because I^A is completely dominant over i. The allele i does not produce any sugar, so only the A type sugar is expressed on the red blood cell surface.'),
  ('264d4962-fb57-4cfa-a706-1562d745dda2','369210fe-4b46-410b-a422-a09333462af0',1,'How many different genotypes are possible for the ABO blood group system?','There are six different genotypes possible: I^A I^A, I^A I^B, I^A i, I^B I^B, I^B i, and ii. These combinations arise from the three alleles (I^A, I^B, and i) and the fact that humans are diploid organisms with two alleles for each gene.'),
  ('d5388ff0-1a6a-4954-841a-7f203cd2bcba','3a2d38c1-232a-409f-ab64-9e204bacf30e',1,'Which of the following is an example of multiple alleles?','ABO blood groups in humans provide a good example of multiple alleles because there are three alleles (I^A, I^B, and i) governing the same character. Multiple alleles can only be found when population studies are made since an individual can only carry two alleles.'),
  ('6c97a823-d673-484f-a631-da290a233783','acb6abdc-d388-4aa7-9910-ddb816456bd3',1,'What is the phenotype of an individual with genotype I^A I^B?','The genotype I^A I^B results in blood group AB because both I^A and I^B are co-dominant. Both alleles express their own types of sugars on the red blood cell surface, so the individual has both A and B types of sugars.'),
  ('09482593-e2e9-480d-843a-c334598b2e1f','52acef4f-905b-4532-a8e4-3ca7df4846a5',1,'In the case of starch synthesis in pea seeds, what is the phenotype of BB homozygotes?','BB homozygotes synthesize starch effectively and produce large starch grains. After maturation, BB seeds are round. The B allele is dominant when considering seed shape, but when starch grain size is considered as the phenotype, the alleles show incomplete dominance with intermediate size in Bb seeds.'),
  ('1148cd40-48e1-4c43-8d72-cdf9fe9c5614','0754e4a7-d052-442d-b0f6-1e1ab792a027',1,'What is the relationship between allele i and alleles I^A and I^B in the ABO blood group system?','Both I^A and I^B are completely dominant over i. When I^A and i are present, only I^A expresses (because i does not produce any sugar). Similarly, when I^B and i are present, only I^B expresses. The allele i does not produce any sugar polymer on the red blood cell surface.'),
  ('2e02f505-8156-4cf9-bb75-6839fef1e54c','17de1c07-5fbf-443a-aa64-acd94efd24b9',1,'What is the phenotypic ratio in the F2 generation of a snapdragon cross between red (RR) and white (rr) flowers?','In snapdragon, when true-breeding red (RR) and white (rr) flowers are crossed, the F1 (Rr) is pink. When F1 is self-pollinated, the F2 generation shows a phenotypic ratio of 1 red (RR) : 2 pink (Rr) : 1 white (rr). This is because R is not completely dominant over r, resulting in incomplete dominance.'),
  ('fb0cf667-b36f-49d2-a0a5-18887d19f711','ef002088-4398-43b2-9c31-c863cf92db91',1,'Which statement correctly describes the concept of dominance in genetics?','Dominance is not an autonomous feature of a gene. It depends on the gene product and the production of a particular phenotype from this product, as well as on the particular phenotype that we choose to examine. For example, in pea seeds, B is dominant for seed shape but shows incomplete dominance for starch grain size.'),
  ('c4ccc380-068d-48b0-a807-2ab02dc59fc2','403c3ec8-5836-4fef-8808-120cfb093a69',1,'What is the genotype ratio in the F2 generation of a snapdragon cross showing incomplete dominance?','The genotypic ratio in the F2 generation of a snapdragon cross is 1 RR : 2 Rr : 1 rr. This is the same as any Mendelian monohybrid cross, but the phenotypic ratio changes from 3:1 to 1:2:1 because of incomplete dominance where heterozygotes show an intermediate phenotype (pink).'),
  ('190b25ac-0376-4774-9274-10efe70f5bde','ff88b496-85ea-4eea-a4b3-1508068cc00b',1,'In the ABO blood group system, what does the allele i code for?','The allele i does not produce any sugar polymer on the red blood cell surface. This is why individuals with genotype ii have blood group O - they lack both A and B type sugars on their red blood cells.'),
  ('f46c5a78-ec7b-409d-adc7-cae20792f8d0','a157b414-0d32-4a8c-9786-e7411b2447d0',1,'What is the phenotype of bb homozygotes in pea seed starch synthesis?','bb homozygotes have lesser efficiency in starch synthesis and produce smaller starch grains. After maturation of the seeds, bb seeds are wrinkled. This is because the recessive allele b reduces the efficiency of starch synthesis compared to the dominant B allele.'),
  ('ca6e3c45-7b76-4ff6-8a00-ac7772ce00c7','d60a786b-c479-4a82-acce-a21b0e5d4532',1,'Which of the following best describes co-dominance?','In co-dominance, the F1 generation resembles both parents simultaneously. A good example is the ABO blood grouping where I^A and I^B both express their own types of sugars when present together, resulting in the AB blood group. This is different from incomplete dominance where the F1 is intermediate.'),
  ('1fe3e841-13bb-4290-8768-c668062bab3b','38fc6acf-a5b4-4abd-93f5-6e1664682a39',1,'What is the blood group of an individual with genotype ii?','The genotype ii results in blood group O because the allele i does not produce any sugar polymer on the red blood cell surface. Since neither A nor B type sugars are produced, the individual has blood group O.'),
  ('bc28c9b3-9dae-4b17-9557-55aa342afe32','be338fa9-6f50-4b66-9c93-33f055ab4fee',1,'How many phenotypes are possible in the ABO blood group system?','There are four phenotypes possible in the ABO blood group system: A, B, AB, and O. Although there are six different genotypes, the number of phenotypes is limited to four because I^A and I^B are co-dominant with each other but both are dominant over i.'),
  ('da711c80-bd18-4747-bd41-2768ecfcca0b','e41ec560-70e9-467e-abdf-b159e271b874',1,'What happens when the gene controlling starch synthesis in pea seeds has two different alleles (B and b)?','B seems to be the dominant allele for seed shape (heterozygotes produce round seeds), but when starch grain size is considered as the phenotype, the alleles show incomplete dominance with intermediate size in Bb seeds. This demonstrates that dominance depends on the particular phenotype being examined.'),
  ('712c66eb-7165-404a-b4b0-713a6168d989','aab13744-032b-4a7a-a9d3-f967356c5c9c',1,'In snapdragon flowers, what is the phenotype of the F1 generation from a cross between red (RR) and white (rr) parents?','In snapdragon, the F1 generation from a cross between red (RR) and white (rr) parents has pink flowers (Rr). This is because R is not completely dominant over r, resulting in incomplete dominance where the heterozygote shows an intermediate phenotype between the two parents.'),
  ('49706267-2fed-429f-a791-fde4bf1ac072','ef853f23-9081-4ec7-8a9f-1fe639bda4b4',1,'Which sugar polymers are present on the red blood cells of an individual with blood group AB?','Individuals with blood group AB have both A and B type sugars on their red blood cells because they have the genotype I^A I^B, where both alleles are co-dominant and express their own types of sugars simultaneously.'),
  ('4db44275-b278-4dd7-bc9d-106060e8b90f','9f06ad1e-af0c-4bd1-b741-dc824dc2d8fe',1,'What is the key difference between incomplete dominance and co-dominance?','In incomplete dominance, the F1 phenotype is intermediate between the two parents (like pink snapdragon flowers). In co-dominance, the F1 generation shows both parental traits simultaneously (like AB blood group where both A and B sugars are expressed). This is the fundamental difference between these two non-Mendelian inheritance patterns.'),
  ('f623aaed-c648-4235-b4ee-4625ad423d8e','266d87a7-620c-4aa8-be78-e8beaa4e2fa8',1,'If a man with blood group A (genotype I^A i) marries a woman with blood group B (genotype I^B i), what blood groups are possible in their children?','When I^A i (blood group A) is crossed with I^B i (blood group B), the possible genotypes of offspring are: I^A I^B (AB), I^A i (A), I^B i (B), and ii (O). Therefore, all four blood groups (A, B, AB, and O) are possible in their children.'),
  ('9217fdfe-5aa5-4417-9ea4-bb6f6e3c4b97','33c9ac2a-05c9-4dee-8539-bf060d0fe1c5',1,'What is the phenotypic ratio obtained in the F2 generation of a monohybrid cross between true-breeding tall (TT) and dwarf (tt) pea plants?','The phenotypic ratio in F2 generation of a monohybrid cross is 3:1 (3 tall : 1 dwarf). This is because the dominant allele T masks the recessive allele t in heterozygous Tt plants, making them appear tall.'),
  ('62ef89df-1d0b-490b-add5-c3f5e7aec213','55b54a67-3f88-4412-9e3b-94260689a4c1',1,'What is the genotypic ratio in the F2 generation of a monohybrid cross between TT and tt pea plants?','The genotypic ratio in F2 is 1:2:1 (1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt). While phenotypically 3 tall and 1 dwarf, genotypically there are three distinct classes with TT and tt being homozygous and Tt being heterozygous.'),
  ('ce9e19df-46b5-4a37-8c17-9f8c9ee1b78c','a4d00fce-9bbc-41ea-8bfe-31c5b2f7ae63',1,'What is the purpose of a test cross in genetics?','A test cross is used to determine the genotype of an organism showing a dominant phenotype by crossing it with a homozygous recessive individual. If the test organism is TT, all offspring will be tall; if Tt, offspring will be in 1:1 ratio.'),
  ('57c3f649-7796-46d0-9d7c-b2b2681e8ece','a9ec2d61-de27-488f-956e-2bbe74d537d9',1,'In snapdragon (Antirrhinum), when red-flowered (RR) plants are crossed with white-flowered (rr) plants, what is the phenotype of F1 generation?','In incomplete dominance, the F1 generation shows an intermediate phenotype. Red (RR) crossed with white (rr) produces pink (Rr) flowers because neither allele is completely dominant over the other.'),
  ('b6f58013-38db-4f2e-9945-4a5a1bddeb16','801947c4-b192-4d46-bda2-bc50dc76e393',1,'Which of the following is the best example of co-dominance?','ABO blood group system shows co-dominance where both alleles IA and IB are expressed equally in the heterozygote (IAIB), producing AB blood group with both A and B antigens on RBCs.'),
  ('791b3ea1-961d-42fa-98ed-f6368b693b23','500449ee-4275-42d0-8952-28dfe008f0a1',1,'How many different phenotypes are possible in the ABO blood group system?','There are 4 phenotypes in ABO blood group system: A, B, AB, and O. These are produced by 6 possible genotypes (IAIA, IAi, IBIB, IBi, IAIB, ii).'),
  ('ec17760a-9091-4c0c-a6af-14c30a446a20','e91193ee-0121-40b9-9c15-e0d31cd5a268',1,'What is the phenotypic ratio in the F2 generation of a dihybrid cross between round yellow (RRYY) and wrinkled green (rryy) pea plants?','The dihybrid cross produces a 9:3:3:1 phenotypic ratio in F2: 9 round yellow : 3 round green : 3 wrinkled yellow : 1 wrinkled green. This demonstrates the Law of Independent Assortment.'),
  ('c86244ef-eef6-414d-9f46-fb870b67fcf5','5c7cda5d-2fc6-407d-ba1a-aa425dd02a02',1,'Mendel''s Law of Independent Assortment states that:','The Law of Independent Assortment states that when two pairs of traits are combined in a hybrid, segregation of one pair of characters is independent of the other pair during gamete formation.'),
  ('c58a6d82-7937-400c-8ccb-e5357b8cbabe','54643a5e-3729-494d-b063-6d957ad0071a',1,'Who proposed the Chromosomal Theory of Inheritance?','Walter Sutton and Theodore Boveri proposed the Chromosomal Theory of Inheritance in 1902, noting that chromosome behavior during meiosis parallels gene behavior. They observed that chromosomes and genes both occur in pairs, segregate during gamete formation, and assort independently.'),
  ('20af9d76-05b0-4fe2-a4ca-409e0376c10a','83b89d7e-34fa-486a-ab75-102410ffb8c9',1,'In humans, what determines the sex of a child?','In humans, sex determination is of XY type. Males produce two types of sperm: 50% with X and 50% with Y chromosome. Females produce only eggs with X chromosome. The sperm that fertilizes the egg determines the sex - X sperm produces female (XX), Y sperm produces male (XY).'),
  ('927d12ca-d931-4b52-b97e-a7a3f0d07df3','928e66b3-f888-409f-acc9-39426cc0fe86',1,'In birds, which sex is heterogametic (produces two different types of gametes)?','In birds, female heterogamety is observed. Females have ZW chromosomes and produce two types of gametes (Z or W), while males have ZZ chromosomes and produce only one type of gamete (Z).'),
  ('4013409c-88b9-4dde-82c5-1c37f5a4f35b','e7cc434c-d9ed-4111-836f-5b785f701ef2',1,'In honey bees, what is the chromosome number of a male (drone)?','In honey bees, males (drones) develop from unfertilized eggs through parthenogenesis and are haploid with 16 chromosomes. Females (queen and workers) develop from fertilized eggs and are diploid with 32 chromosomes.')
INSERT INTO question_translations (id, question_id, lang_id, question_text, explanation) VALUES
  ('789577aa-9f90-42a8-ab8e-381f0434ee9a','d478e05a-365e-4065-8173-9070bf5a1446',1,'What is a point mutation?','A point mutation is a change in a single base pair of DNA. A classic example is sickle cell anemia where a single base substitution (GAG to GTG) in the beta-globin gene changes the 6th amino acid from glutamic acid to valine.'),
  ('a7f8c9d3-a84b-4d23-8726-803f2afa73ea','6aa69c2b-7549-4852-8feb-e330f94273b0',1,'Sickle cell anemia is caused by substitution of which amino acid at the 6th position of beta-globin chain?','In sickle cell anemia, the 6th codon of beta-globin gene changes from GAG to GUG, resulting in substitution of glutamic acid (Glu) by valine (Val) at the 6th position of the beta-globin chain. This causes RBCs to become sickle-shaped under low oxygen.'),
  ('8a4d996c-2997-4329-8717-d31add7daca8','f7f50db6-4db0-4fdf-b0f5-f04b77642752',1,'Down''s syndrome is caused by:','Down''s syndrome is caused by trisomy of chromosome 21, where there is an extra copy of chromosome 21. The individual has 47 chromosomes instead of 46. Characteristics include short stature, round head, furrowed tongue, and mental retardation.'),
  ('7c1db1c9-ac32-4782-8432-4f85c46d3382','c4f4c503-8b87-4483-a426-55b118a19c41',1,'Which of the following is NOT an advantage of selecting pea plant for Mendel''s experiments?','Mendel selected pea plants because they have a short life cycle, are self-pollinating, can be cross-pollinated, and have distinct contrasting characters. They do NOT require complex laboratory equipment.'),
  ('f68d7380-c276-4bb9-9560-aaaa4f71abc4','1db3317c-bbba-4886-9760-47442b7e2093',1,'In Mendelian genetics, what is the key difference between a dominant and a recessive trait?','A dominant trait is expressed when at least one dominant allele is present (TT or Tt), while a recessive trait is expressed only when two recessive alleles are present (tt).'),
  ('781c04b0-b55d-45a4-961e-20940543c87f','88bc4984-448c-4b19-b897-3a6400504ef6',1,'Which statement correctly differentiates homozygous from heterozygous genotypes?','Homozygous means having two identical alleles (TT or tt) while heterozygous means having two different alleles (Tt). This applies to all diploid organisms.'),
  ('9af4700c-11e0-46dc-94e0-72982c80e9e9','23a8dac8-8c7a-416e-84a4-ae5af88c1693',1,'What is the main difference between a monohybrid cross and a dihybrid cross?','A monohybrid cross studies inheritance of one character (e.g., tall vs dwarf), while a dihybrid cross studies inheritance of two characters simultaneously (e.g., seed color and seed shape).'),
  ('6aecad30-8513-488c-b1cf-c2e12e76cf1e','af1868d0-9062-4651-a260-4900415db667',1,'A diploid organism is heterozygous for 4 loci. How many types of gametes can be produced?','The number of gamete types equals 2^n where n is the number of heterozygous loci. For 4 loci: 2^4 = 16.'),
  ('55506cc7-6d6f-4237-b497-8c4e943ff05a','d88a2e87-4b9a-4ecf-9ef4-183f73a7a641',1,'According to Mendel''s Law of Dominance, what happens when two alleles of a gene are brought together in a hybrid?','The Law of Dominance states that in a heterozygote, the dominant allele masks the expression of the recessive allele. The recessive allele is expressed only in homozygous condition.'),
  ('a8b815fc-6919-4f12-9e8d-754c6fc84671','a962e92e-1ea2-4148-ab08-f6bea6ed25bc',1,'What is a test cross and what is its purpose in genetics?','A test cross is used to determine the unknown genotype of an individual showing dominant phenotype by crossing it with a homozygous recessive individual.'),
  ('f48e97f0-18d4-4bef-8918-d61d69b0cfd2','496c9a73-ce4d-4f23-8a0a-5d2ace65f84a',1,'In a cross between a homozygous dominant female (TT) and a heterozygous male (Tt), what is the expected phenotypic ratio in the F1 generation?','TT x Tt cross produces genotypes TT and Tt in 1:1 ratio. Both genotypes show the dominant phenotype since T is dominant over t.'),
  ('243edbf4-d77a-4bbc-a819-4abdc9f18554','f8e826f0-ceee-4f66-a6ec-7e67c8258a23',1,'When a cross is made between a tall plant with yellow seeds (TtYy) and a tall plant with green seeds (Ttyy), what proportion of offspring will be tall and green?','Tt x Tt gives 3/4 tall and 1/4 dwarf. Yy x yy gives 1/2 yellow and 1/2 green. Tall and green = 3/4 x 1/2 = 3/8.'),
  ('5df935ad-a344-4740-ae57-a2bcfda2aee9','4f37e4da-f228-4b0c-816e-4088b68fc8d9',1,'When two loci are linked, what would be the distribution of phenotypic features in the F1 generation for a dihybrid cross?','When genes are linked, they tend to be inherited together, so parental phenotypes appear more frequently than expected, while recombinant phenotypes appear less frequently.'),
  ('d7a4f23a-27f6-4f67-bd93-be56833fcda5','0e8ffd74-2126-426b-9faf-03a681a929e5',1,'What was T.H. Morgan''s major contribution to genetics?','T.H. Morgan used Drosophila (fruit fly) to demonstrate that genes are located on chromosomes and discovered the phenomenon of genetic linkage.'),
  ('2c1a8cbf-5e81-463c-83f5-d46ff59ac76d','240a4558-2b0e-4d32-8b20-61d524885dba',1,'What is pedigree analysis and how is it useful in human genetics?','Pedigree analysis traces inheritance patterns through family trees, helping identify whether traits are dominant, recessive, autosomal, or sex-linked.'),
  ('dac91f00-801a-4ae1-bbae-7cc4b855391f','1e4948f1-6509-4f1d-92a1-286cb4f269d5',1,'How is sex determined in human beings?','In humans, females produce eggs with only X chromosomes. Males produce sperm with either X or Y chromosomes. The sperm that fertilizes the egg determines the sex.'),
  ('a29c2ec3-898f-4c99-94a0-e9ba9b6ae1e4','911dd846-c716-4a20-826c-eeae8d8f8432',1,'A child has blood group O. If the father has blood group A and the mother has blood group B, what are the possible genotypes of the parents?','For a child to be O (ii), both parents must carry the recessive i allele. Father with blood group A must be IAi and mother with blood group B must be IBi.'),
  ('971098c6-3af9-4918-8214-4f309a6bcbc3','34931a9d-31f7-4029-a395-160faa2088d4',1,'Which of the following is the best example of co-dominance?','In co-dominance, both alleles are fully expressed. In ABO blood group, AB individuals express both A and B antigens on their red blood cells.'),
  ('f7d167e4-6c7c-45cf-a2db-139093ab4a6a','4db7bd98-70fe-4074-ba99-d253e351d270',1,'What is incomplete dominance and which example illustrates it?','Incomplete dominance occurs when neither allele is completely dominant, producing an intermediate phenotype. Snapdragon flowers show pink (intermediate) when red and white are crossed.'),
  ('252b3933-0c1a-49da-b180-4f46d963fb2a','c19db287-1b24-4ee0-8487-46c280763069',1,'What is a point mutation and what is a classic example?','A point mutation is a change in a single base pair of DNA. Sickle cell anemia is caused by substitution of a single base in the beta-globin gene.'),
  ('64fe541d-06e3-4319-8cee-0cf4b871d370','82fdc4d2-b083-4e1d-b36e-688a49ebb5d1',1,'Who proposed the chromosomal theory of inheritance?','Walter Sutton and Theodore Boveri independently proposed the chromosomal theory of inheritance in 1902, correlating chromosome behavior with Mendel''s laws.'),
  ('a7e02ced-a4a3-45b3-b5ce-831fa2561fab','0f996590-b141-4cc2-a07e-de692dbee14a',1,'Which of the following is an autosomal recessive genetic disorder characterized by abnormal hemoglobin?','Sickle cell anemia is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by a mutation in the beta-globin gene, resulting in abnormal hemoglobin and sickle-shaped red blood cells.'),
  ('bc7b8d20-f982-46a8-a68b-7c51b8f9d758','c703f94c-96e4-4e83-9e30-dc1c67d74d4c',1,'What is the main characteristic of Thalassemia as a genetic disorder?','Thalassemia is a quantitative problem where there is reduced synthesis of globin chains (alpha or beta), resulting in abnormal hemoglobin formation and anemia.'),
  ('1634b583-18cc-4a3a-8278-916fe9d45e85','ebf245fa-4936-4b0e-98ca-db6d19a4217c',1,'Who first observed a specific nuclear structure during spermatogenesis in insects and named it the ''X body''?','Henking (1891) traced a specific nuclear structure during spermatogenesis in insects and named it the X body, though he could not explain its significance.'),
  ('05dd0b87-b445-4d01-852c-8d36bd3d541e','ba644d0d-d8ea-4cf1-9adf-ca75a2ab76d4',1,'In the XO type of sex determination, which organism is given as an example?','Grasshopper is an example of XO type sex determination where males have only one X-chromosome besides autosomes, while females have a pair of X-chromosomes.'),
  ('f6eca8d4-bc90-423c-9caf-19599307f6c4','37d6c283-55b0-4d34-820e-53d2380b9878',1,'In humans, what is the chromosomal composition of a normal male?','In humans, a normal male has 22 pairs of autosomes and a pair of sex chromosomes as XY, while females have 22 pairs of autosomes and XX.'),
  ('fbc0ba6f-bee7-4dad-8c4a-83b8b367d70c','3350bcbd-085d-495a-b64b-111e2330350a',1,'What percentage of sperm carry the X-chromosome in human males?','During spermatogenesis in males, 50% of sperm carry the X-chromosome and 50% carry the Y-chromosome besides autosomes.'),
  ('383f89b6-94e5-44bb-be59-1056836f9899','288118c8-5d75-4e16-90c8-60e74c341642',1,'In birds, what type of sex chromosomes do females possess?','In birds, females have ZW sex chromosomes (female heterogamety) while males have ZZ chromosomes.'),
  ('da5c00a7-dffe-40ee-8fa0-8fa5806f6cc4','43eb6e57-4b81-441d-96bb-714a6f907224',1,'How many chromosomes does a male honey bee (drone) have?','In honey bees, males (drones) are haploid with 16 chromosomes, while females (queen or worker) are diploid with 32 chromosomes.'),
  ('90074728-2189-4c83-90ea-13e3fabf3f1a','a51962e9-4912-44da-8a21-dd392d99078d',1,'How do male honey bees produce sperm?','Male honey bees produce sperm by mitosis because they are haploid and do not undergo meiosis.'),
  ('49b1b64d-078b-4eba-b81d-6ec89d8282c1','46b7b37b-dcd8-4754-b9f3-b49e4e245220',1,'What is the sex determination system in honey bees called?','Honey bees have a haplodiploid sex-determination system where males develop from unfertilized eggs (haploid) and females from fertilized eggs (diploid).'),
  ('e019fc48-2c07-4bfd-8730-a731815af6dd','d7a20a58-3d92-4c67-919d-a34f9f1a9981',1,'What is a mutation that involves change in a single base pair of DNA called?','A point mutation is a change in a single base pair of DNA. Sickle cell anemia is a classical example caused by substitution of one base in the beta-globin gene.'),
  ('8bf69718-cd5b-4b2b-b417-b7958de5b643','aca925d6-ec96-4915-811d-94da01c8e044',1,'Which of the following is a chemical or physical factor that induces mutations?','UV radiations can cause mutations in organisms and are referred to as mutagens.'),
  ('462050c5-a87d-4e9d-bbcd-456b3c85af79','e394d4c8-790e-4c24-8f77-9feeb70b325e',1,'What is the analysis of inheritance patterns of traits in human families called?','Pedigree analysis is the study of family history about inheritance of a particular trait, represented in a family tree over several generations.'),
  ('3d26735b-67ee-48f0-84c1-2998af5de088','16bd9415-4d85-444c-8e3d-e75e4e11b62d',1,'Which Mendelian disorder is caused by a defect in the gene for phenyl alanine hydroxylase enzyme?','Phenylketonuria is an inborn error of metabolism where the affected individual lacks the enzyme that converts phenylalanine to tyrosine, leading to mental retardation.'),
  ('bd166323-b96e-4fd8-8915-312968825bbe','3f648103-4017-4afd-8eef-4c710ed318bc',1,'Sickle-cell anemia is caused by the substitution of which amino acid at the sixth position of beta-globin chain?','In sickle-cell anemia, Glutamic acid (Glu) is substituted by Valine (Val) at the sixth position of the beta-globin chain due to a single base substitution from GAG to GUG.'),
  ('d0faaec1-3a32-4e3b-9413-80bc33023a9c','a9375784-1953-4b25-b31a-e0b4463869d0',1,'What is the probability of a son being color blind if his mother is a carrier?','The son of a woman who carries the color blindness gene has a 50% chance of being color blind because males have only one X chromosome.'),
  ('f22bc151-2177-4bf2-96d9-c54ce0cea923','110c80f2-1e46-4560-aaa4-6a2e6f992056',1,'Down''s syndrome is caused by which chromosomal abnormality?','Down''s syndrome is caused by trisomy of chromosome 21, where there is an extra copy of chromosome 21, making the total chromosome number 47.'),
  ('baf573d3-4ef9-4e71-9dfc-1a2bd084a079','49112bc2-0708-4462-8023-6f66f7e476e4',1,'What is the karyotype of an individual with Klinefelter''s syndrome?','Klinefelter''s syndrome is caused by the presence of an additional X chromosome, resulting in a karyotype of 47, XXY.'),
  ('798d6f40-c39f-4c62-8c9e-97d92a9d31d8','8ff08b4a-1af1-4b5b-bb8f-b08c0c575022',1,'Turner''s syndrome is characterized by which chromosomal composition?','Turner''s syndrome is caused by the absence of one X chromosome, resulting in 45 chromosomes with XO sex chromosome composition.'),
  ('2a27c4c2-919f-46b4-a007-f86f1613a988','2a6c160c-1b9e-452a-a569-003c990948b4',1,'What is the phenotypic ratio in a typical monohybrid cross?','In a monohybrid cross, the phenotypic ratio is 3:1 (dominant:recessive) while the genotypic ratio is 1:2:1 (TT:Tt:tt).'),
  ('df71d4d8-1efb-4da4-8fa6-a8619567bb0e','fb3abbe6-0c18-4a7a-8bf7-cd95abc35b33',1,'What is the phenotypic ratio in a dihybrid cross?','In a dihybrid cross, the phenotypic ratio is 9:3:3:1 representing round yellow, round green, wrinkled yellow, and wrinkled green in Mendel''s pea experiments.'),
  ('b09d1a73-b43c-4c80-a52e-208b5271f6f3','44ea9a8d-1ae8-4634-8d2d-3b190b36ec80',1,'Which law states that ''when two pairs of traits are combined in a hybrid, segregation of one pair is independent of the other pair''?','Mendel''s Law of Independent Assortment states that when two pairs of traits are combined in a hybrid, segregation of one pair of characters is independent of the other pair.'),
  ('9df1c6b6-81e2-45e6-8dd5-1533d9a9c6ab','214a4696-6394-43d5-9308-feac175f8f36',1,'How many alleles control the ABO blood grouping in humans?','ABO blood groups are controlled by the gene I which has three alleles: IA, IB, and i. This is an example of multiple alleles.'),
  ('bdce7832-5e16-41cd-8d4d-0ea39ca62848','3144e258-9f3c-491b-bda8-bb90f7280df9',1,'What is the blood group of an individual with genotype IAIB?','The genotype IAIB results in blood group AB because both IA and IB are co-dominant and both express their own types of sugars on RBCs.'),
  ('1692bd39-e56c-4f65-9b8d-cb9875732c52','8c09cce6-fbd5-4c88-98b3-caf31898ecd2',1,'In snapdragon flowers, what is the phenotype of F1 generation when red (RR) is crossed with white (rr)?','In snapdragon (Antirrhinum), when red (RR) is crossed with white (rr), the F1 (Rr) shows pink flowers due to incomplete dominance.'),
  ('d0fd0dea-3c23-443d-965a-d80b8cd9c760','42ef068c-f585-454e-8db2-4da351422151',1,'Which of the following is an example of co-dominance?','ABO blood grouping is an example of co-dominance where both IA and IB alleles express themselves when present together.'),
  ('c0a17ac9-8377-4788-a108-1ca53471fc4a','fb447b5a-a8bf-4077-bc80-154ab3334010',1,'What is the purpose of a test cross?','A test cross is used to determine the genotype of an organism showing a dominant phenotype by crossing it with a homozygous recessive individual.'),
  ('285e3be6-8095-4eba-a35f-b0d1e082f9aa','3423b121-db2c-4120-8047-54263d62bc7c',1,'Who proposed the chromosomal theory of inheritance?','Walter Sutton and Theodore Boveri proposed the chromosomal theory of inheritance by noting the parallel behavior of chromosomes and genes.'),
  ('47945b51-6259-49dd-b73d-406540ad0c1c','0e6464bb-25f9-45aa-90d8-080532bcbe19',1,'What is the term for genes that are located on the same chromosome and tend to be inherited together?','Linked genes are located on the same chromosome and tend to be inherited together, not following the law of independent assortment.'),
  ('fb5df912-de53-41ad-9bc7-7ef3b9093267','eb72a319-500f-4841-8921-23f76ea1e229',1,'Which of the following is an example of polygenic inheritance?','Human skin color is a classic example of polygenic inheritance controlled by three or more genes with additive effects of each allele.'),
  ('3464ccef-0270-426d-8eb4-aba2e4d6332a','5712a6cb-7798-4e68-b7af-15441224834e',1,'What is the phenomenon where a single gene affects multiple phenotypic traits called?','Pleiotropy is when a single gene exhibits multiple phenotypic expressions, such as phenylketonuria causing mental retardation and reduced hair/skin pigmentation.'),
  ('b0b35738-cb9a-465b-b278-d55e7b92efa4','fffd4f17-34a4-4a25-ab54-6eb7dc89bb53',1,'Thalassemia differs from sickle-cell anemia in that it is a:','Thalassemia is a quantitative problem of synthesizing too few globin molecules, while sickle-cell anemia is a qualitative problem of synthesizing incorrectly functioning hemoglobin.'),
  ('432d12f8-4976-462b-9dcd-be9c6fff59be','45ebc2af-f9f8-4134-845f-8fb2c40e5e5b',1,'In a dihybrid cross, what phenotypic ratio did Mendel observe in the F2 generation?','In a dihybrid cross, the phenotypes round yellow, wrinkled yellow, round green, and wrinkled green appeared in the ratio 9:3:3:1. This was observed for several pairs of characters that Mendel studied.'),
  ('fcef04d7-9311-48aa-976f-f94698fa0a15','d9493e14-ae45-438b-bc36-0ecff295b79f',1,'How can the 9:3:3:1 ratio be mathematically derived?','The ratio of 9:3:3:1 can be derived as a combination series of 3 yellow:1 green, with 3 round:1 wrinkled. The derivation is written as (3 Round:1 Wrinkled) x (3 Yellow:1 Green) = 9 Round,Yellow:3 Wrinkled,Yellow:3 Round,Green:1 Wrinkled,Green.'),
  ('1837063f-07a4-469e-8c74-fefc532e8774','d5c12940-0be7-4ea5-9b2d-84cf6a95d160',1,'What does Mendel''s Law of Independent Assortment state?','Mendel''s Law of Independent Assortment states that when two pairs of traits are combined in a hybrid, segregation of one pair of characters is independent of the other pair of characters.'),
  ('60fea9e1-52fd-44f7-8211-b90537fd0eb7','19347525-cc6d-4c10-b983-5b9e9bccecfc',1,'In a dihybrid cross with genes R and r, what percentage of gametes carry the gene R?','Fifty per cent of the gametes have the gene R and the other 50 per cent have r. This is because segregation is a random process with equal probability.'),
  ('1cee09ee-78cf-4218-b9a4-ff51137c080c','cc6e222c-003a-49c8-935a-42f7e63dd60a',1,'How many different genotypes of gametes are produced in a dihybrid cross involving RrYy?','There are four genotypes of gametes: RY, Ry, rY, and ry. Each has a frequency of 25 per cent or 1/4th of the total gametes produced.'),
  ('0e910997-e4d9-4ebc-8ea7-834eb8341745','2c370b19-bfff-44b8-9534-2d6cf05fe79a',1,'What is the frequency of each gamete type (RY, Ry, rY, ry) in a dihybrid cross?','The four types are RY, Ry, rY, and ry each with a frequency of 25 per cent or 1/4th of the total gametes produced.'),
  ('6cf3e038-f63f-40c6-aed7-2cbab21a22ad','30704122-b97f-426d-b85e-ab9aeed99471',1,'How many squares are there in a Punnett square for a dihybrid cross?','Although there are 16 squares in the Punnett square for a dihybrid cross, the question asks how many different types of genotypes and phenotypes are formed.'),
  ('34148ec0-afd6-4912-b44b-365d3ef9451c','ec6032ec-b0a7-47ff-9207-9de7f79fb540',1,'Is the genotypic ratio in F2 of a dihybrid cross also 9:3:3:1?','The phenotypic ratio is 9:3:3:1, but the genotypic ratio is different. The text asks students to work out the genotypic ratio using Punnett square data, implying it is not the same as the phenotypic ratio.'),
  ('36f9df46-e060-4c7e-86f5-136d3025ac9f','cd19bee8-f838-45c9-9963-d4ca96443bc5',1,'When was Mendel''s work on inheritance first published?','Mendel published his work on inheritance of characters in 1865 but for several reasons, it remained unrecognised till 1900.'),
  ('dcf9fd06-3c18-4a1f-b910-45517d8b9ec4','8711a19d-81d4-4aba-aa8f-fb1a48c66bc8',1,'Why did Mendel''s work remain unrecognised until 1900?','Mendel''s work remained unrecognised because communication was not easy, his concept of genes as discrete units was not accepted, and his mathematical approach to biology was new and unacceptable.'),
  ('edef4e82-aac1-48d1-97ec-581c82178299','f2916a1c-fa80-4344-a3ea-cdf968f8220e',1,'Which three scientists independently rediscovered Mendel''s results in 1900?','In 1900, three scientists (de Vries, Correns, and von Tschermak) independently rediscovered Mendel''s results on the inheritance of characters.'),
  ('ae8596ff-5464-4f27-9424-17fc770c4510','f4101716-9383-4615-b6ed-addb32544caa',1,'What discovery helped scientists understand the physical basis of Mendel''s laws?','Advancements in microscopy led to the discovery of chromosomes. By 1902, chromosome movement during meiosis was worked out, helping explain Mendel''s laws physically.'),
  ('7d9ed4dc-de02-42d0-9594-01ce0d6f9e32','11566ca2-1083-46d3-a2a5-38b2edcab935',1,'How many pairs of chromosomes are present in a normal human cell?','In humans, there are 23 pairs of chromosomes present in a normal cell. Out of these, 22 pairs are autosomes and one pair consists of sex chromosomes.'),
  ('938a86d1-cfb0-4190-a82c-875c745b4041','33506ad6-5cfd-47ef-b589-55777a43319a',1,'Which type of sex determination mechanism is found in human beings?','The sex determining mechanism in humans is XY type. Males have one X and one Y chromosome, while females have a pair of X chromosomes.'),
  ('e506d74a-ed60-4673-bba5-d45c6981bd39','ae2ed667-bc21-4461-bcfa-0b51694cff1d',1,'How many pairs of autosomes are present in both males and females?','Out of 23 pairs of chromosomes, 22 pairs are exactly the same in both males and females. These are called autosomes.'),
  ('f72b1f38-e47f-42a6-990a-bb283a22e63f','8c679567-17e4-41ab-a119-b912f49c4984',1,'What determines the sex of a child in human beings?','It is the genetic makeup of the sperm that determines the sex of the child. If the sperm carries an X chromosome, the child will be female; if it carries a Y chromosome, the child will be male.'),
  ('b3175ef5-abeb-441e-99c1-5beb799e1813','66e0e354-66ef-4b1c-a450-0beaca190f9b',1,'What percentage of sperm carry the X chromosome during spermatogenesis?','During spermatogenesis among males, 50% of the total sperm produced carry the X chromosome and the remaining 50% carry the Y chromosome.'),
  ('5a366d4e-cd5b-4b9f-bc11-647ce920db5f','cbaf227e-cf8b-49c2-8b29-38e6bb26cc7f',1,'What type of ovum do females produce?','Females produce only one type of ovum with an X chromosome. All eggs carry the X chromosome.'),
  ('4841bccd-2978-4827-be1d-2b63f2696d77','39e777b7-768b-4b6e-b07b-4965a7480b1e',1,'What is the probability of having a male child in each pregnancy?','In each pregnancy, there is always a 50% probability of either a male or a female child. This is because there is an equal probability of fertilization with either X or Y carrying sperm.'),
  ('edca7a0f-4a5a-4b7b-8696-85f33c93e552','e6b2d199-54ea-4b76-b145-ac2dfcd06215',1,'Which of the following is a false notion in society regarding female children?','It is unfortunate that in society, women are blamed for giving birth to female children and have been ostracized and ill-treated because of this false notion. The sperm actually determines the sex.'),
  ('b91ab90e-bdcc-4621-b8c2-c14bea815abd','70c7bbd7-eb3c-4a2e-b169-6f224dd9b072',1,'What is the sex determination system in honey bees called?','The sex determination in honey bee is based on the number of sets of chromosomes and is called the haplodiploid sex-determination system.'),
  ('ddb3c120-ac54-41ba-a261-22eaf8b934c2','c30a849a-40a0-4160-a226-d53ce3ccceae',1,'How many chromosomes do female honey bees have?','Female honey bees (queen or worker) are diploid and have 32 chromosomes. They develop from the union of a sperm and an egg.'),
  ('6f80b9ef-3efb-459e-8fae-8163994625c5','cfa51a98-a342-4456-bf64-1ba5a3825863',1,'How many chromosomes do male honey bees (drones) have?','Male honey bees (drones) are haploid and have 16 chromosomes. They develop from unfertilized eggs through parthenogenesis.'),
  ('7cca7293-3aeb-46d3-8dda-0d17acd53fe6','8132a3ed-94b3-4199-9384-ba9ce0331600',1,'How do male honey bees produce sperm?','Male honey bees produce sperm by mitosis because they are haploid and do not undergo meiosis.'),
  ('402707ae-9a8a-4eeb-9f93-2466808606a1','30687ab7-2bfd-4e9e-b34d-3232eff711f9',1,'What is the process by which male honey bees develop from unfertilized eggs?','Male honey bees develop from unfertilized eggs by means of parthenogenesis, which is development without fertilization.'),
  ('2c110800-d3f6-4eb9-ad59-1c77670f379a','0e2a3a18-6def-4284-8db1-83f23ce7e4b4',1,'Which statement is true about male honey bees?','Male honey bees do not have a father and thus cannot have sons, but they have a grandfather and can have grandsons.'),
  ('accf4a62-92a2-4e22-8746-0bbf27e08b03','627cac81-4e0d-4021-a50f-1edfdcf11240',1,'In which organisms is female heterogamety observed?','In birds, a different mechanism of sex determination is observed where females produce two different types of gametes (Z and W chromosomes). This is called female heterogamety.'),
  ('17097084-dea8-4ac1-b21f-56ff56728925','a3df5beb-bccd-4d44-88fa-848aa4f0446c',1,'What type of genetic disorder is Thalassemia?','Thalassemia is an autosome-linked recessive blood disease transmitted from parents to offspring when both partners are unaffected carriers (heterozygous).'),
  ('04831466-5099-41cf-b087-9303b47cca46','0a6e80c3-b298-43dd-938b-5db01ea0ba3c',1,'What causes Thalassemia?','Thalassemia is caused by either mutation or deletion which ultimately results in reduced rate of synthesis of one of the globin chains (alpha and beta chains) that make up haemoglobin.'),
  ('5b0f6001-5748-4a9e-b21c-4d4f3f9d8fbe','40f2f240-d645-4ad4-a0d8-abfa00e44417',1,'In alpha Thalassemia, which chain production is affected?','In alpha Thalassemia, production of alpha globin chain is affected while in beta Thalassemia, production of beta globin chain is affected.'),
  ('b15da817-f732-468e-b389-bcb54312f2c7','3658a68a-160e-4248-aa6e-e30176aea7c1',1,'How many genes control alpha Thalassemia in each parent?','Alpha Thalassemia is controlled by two closely linked genes HBA1 and HBA2 on chromosome 16 of each parent.'),
  ('0b24791b-8d99-4ee1-92f0-3bc1cd333887','66d9e24c-2fcc-4759-87c9-4827e0fccf4b',1,'Which chromosome carries the gene for beta Thalassemia?','Beta Thalassemia is controlled by a single gene HBB on chromosome 11 of each parent.'),
  ('372e11d2-7a44-4dd4-b510-6a16bb028163','a660710e-24ef-4d5b-833a-42f79183d0a7',1,'What is the main difference between Thalassemia and sickle-cell anemia?','Thalassemia differs from sickle-cell anemia in that the former is a quantitative problem of synthesizing too few globin molecules while the latter is a qualitative problem of synthesizing an incorrectly functioning globin.'),
  ('f857e9ef-8603-45d4-bf57-d1e63c95b41d','323fe26e-34ad-42c4-b968-78a827dcb30a',1,'What are chromosomal disorders caused by?','Chromosomal disorders are caused due to absence or excess or abnormal arrangement of one or more chromosomes.'),
  ('71ea1902-46fe-4596-8737-675fbd3dc80e','6d0842d7-e0b7-4110-88ef-a69c3777a0e3',1,'What is aneuploidy?','Failure of segregation of chromatids during cell division cycle results in the gain or loss of a chromosome(s), called aneuploidy.'),
  ('2362b0af-9f82-42f1-b0a0-08a6c989d7b2','f12f65ba-7a5e-4dd9-b073-0bd3f6da8fa8',1,'Which syndrome results from trisomy of chromosome 21?','Down''s syndrome results in the gain of extra copy of chromosome 21 (trisomy of 21).'),
  ('26115b49-6c4f-4e43-8212-3c3a2a31edb5','26b823eb-a694-4809-b358-8f475bc35321',1,'What is polyploidy?','Failure of cytokinesis after telophase stage of cell division results in an increase in a whole set of chromosomes in an organism, known as polyploidy.'),
  ('059b3e30-2d64-4193-9433-abb043708ed9','adb3de4c-0958-4446-b2b8-e6351e09383c',1,'What is the total number of chromosomes in a normal human cell?','The total number of chromosomes in a normal human cell is 46 (23 pairs). Out of these 22 pairs are autosomes and one pair are sex chromosomes.'),
  ('5fb9aa93-12db-471d-a38a-38fb5d1c81d9','2bbaf527-7b4a-488f-a74e-febac5fbef15',1,'How many pairs of autosomes are present in humans?','In humans, out of 23 pairs of chromosomes, 22 pairs are autosomes and one pair are sex chromosomes.'),
  ('28a16744-dfc5-4528-b5c9-50992a92106e','77507fa6-650c-48e2-a36e-97ec50fa3e54',1,'What is trisomy?','Trisomy refers to the situation where an additional copy of a chromosome may be included in an individual.'),
  ('aa120bbc-bf9d-4eea-812e-34970d7b2e4c','c8b44149-d6c0-4a7c-9259-36aea0d12f40',1,'What is monosomy?','Monosomy refers to the situation where an individual may lack one of any pair of chromosomes.'),
  ('eb742a9f-31a6-4f3b-a063-b4103cce1a29','2a65dccb-91c1-45e4-8b3c-b3f0da450a98',1,'Which scientist first described Down''s syndrome?','Down''s syndrome was first described by Langdon Down in 1866.'),
  ('404bbebe-e92e-4326-8701-5235f9ed4537','abc53c30-809e-4ad6-b59e-316d137f3699',1,'What are the characteristic features of Down''s syndrome?','The affected individual with Down''s syndrome is short statured with small round head, furrowed tongue and partially open mouth. Palm is broad with characteristic palm crease.'),
  ('dc5c65ba-d85e-42ef-9fef-838c235bcc05','8d53f3b3-e0a3-40bb-83cf-f8f6edc3ab35',1,'What is the karyotype of Klinefelter''s syndrome?','Klinefelter''s syndrome is caused due to the presence of an additional copy of X chromosome resulting into a karyotype of 47, XXY.'),
  ('d8cbaa81-d5ce-4040-9e1a-7f7d0314e119','501b5236-8839-4349-af85-d6eb820e1751',1,'What is a characteristic feature of individuals with Klinefelter''s syndrome?','Individuals with Klinefelter''s syndrome have overall masculine development, however, feminine development including gynaecomastia is also expressed.'),
  ('4df181dc-9621-4291-8e9d-c12085bad6ae','40ab62d3-6cc7-47a1-b7fa-8f489d187aab',1,'What causes Turner''s syndrome?','Turner''s syndrome is caused due to the absence of one of the X chromosomes, i.e., 45 with XO. Such females are sterile as ovaries are rudimentary.'),
  ('8a99c3b2-c8de-41b9-8d87-cb217ee5a455','871db13f-3b58-48ef-84dc-a4afa4f85a0d',1,'Which of the following is a feature of Turner''s syndrome?','Turner''s syndrome females show short stature and underdeveloped feminine character including lack of secondary sexual characters.'),
  ('1a745493-a2b7-42cf-b2b7-999cf335149f','df01882a-2562-4006-a8b3-0f29674131be',1,'In which year was James Dewey Watson born?','James Dewey Watson was born in Chicago on 6 April 1928.'),
  ('14717481-c524-457c-a65c-af5d6f4d0c32','58152159-2f5f-48ad-81e1-7882c4420cdf',1,'What degree did James Watson receive in 1947?','In 1947, James Watson received his B.Sc. degree in Zoology.'),
  ('1deef7fd-bad1-4835-b188-190dae146890','63beacd4-651e-4fbf-9629-31983fd063da',1,'Where did James Watson receive his Ph.D. degree?','James Watson received his Ph.D. degree from Indiana University, Bloomington in 1950.'),
  ('572fbf16-57b0-43c4-806e-0141abc3b378','ce90d576-5f6f-45dd-ae20-a56cdd9f5393',1,'What was the topic of James Watson''s Ph.D. thesis?','Watson''s Ph.D. thesis was on the effect of hard X-rays on bacteriophage.'),
  ('3b75ec93-64ae-4b8d-94c3-10fb05f66939','bc47d387-8018-408a-b3ab-5c1d91bbbab9',1,'When was Francis Harry Compton Crick born?','Francis Harry Compton Crick was born on 8 June 1916 at Northampton, England.'),
  ('39e2d7a1-b8d3-488d-b1c0-b4e40d0da8d7','c2e6de3c-6558-41cf-854d-2ec7ef0f46b7',1,'What subject did Francis Crick study at University College, London?','Francis Crick studied physics at University College, London and obtained a B.Sc. in 1937.'),
  ('717c1ea2-c3b5-4b05-be9c-642e342933db','29726f3e-b287-4ada-b784-aa4a3a202770',1,'In which year did Francis Crick complete his Ph.D.?','Francis Crick completed his Ph.D. in 1954 on a thesis about X-ray Diffraction of Polypeptides.'),
  ('73f87c55-d412-47eb-b2d1-68fbd8542f03','b9e6cb90-ae50-4f29-9c04-6069db631b33',1,'What was the title of Francis Crick''s Ph.D. thesis?','Crick''s Ph.D. thesis was entitled ''X-ray Diffraction: Polypeptides and Proteins''.'),
  ('de40d0d3-8355-458b-bd1b-920b1020a0e9','60bb0a8e-1d64-4675-970e-4421c2bcb4b0',1,'In which year did Watson and Crick propose the double-helical structure for DNA?','Watson and Crick proposed the double-helical structure for DNA early in March 1953.'),
  ('1edb7380-e182-479a-aed2-bd822eab53d0','947d629d-0558-40b1-89ba-c4063c277e71',1,'What was James Watson''s age when he met Crick?','Watson was a young man of 23 when he met Crick and they developed their friendship.'),
  ('a5323a6c-4c1e-4d55-9396-a920a1a05c57','bab1a95a-7aa9-4569-bfec-a97086392bb6',1,'When was Francis Crick made a Fellow of the Royal Society (F.R.S.)?','Francis Crick was made a Fellow of the Royal Society (F.R.S.) in 1959.'),
  ('a0f4e53f-61f3-4ae6-b1d7-912e964a32eb','88576891-8a90-4460-a82d-58b7dca5b837',1,'Which prize did Watson and Crick receive in 1959?','Watson and Crick received the John Collins Warren Prize of the Massachusetts General Hospital in 1959.'),
  ('6bdc7297-cd4d-43ef-a8ed-c0b42f7b3b67','d38aa310-285a-4878-8c82-08adca9ef7bf',1,'In which year did Watson and Crick receive the Lasker Award?','Watson and Crick received the Lasker Award in 1960.'),
  ('c2c6bf7e-44a4-49dc-8fe7-29561ee365bc','1703b721-090d-44f5-8ca7-0c39bf777ef0',1,'In which year did Watson and Crick receive the Nobel Prize?','Watson and Crick received the Nobel Prize in 1962 for their discovery of the DNA structure.'),
  ('7c85e990-8133-4397-a88a-68f652c065e3','b9181e50-080c-430a-8c58-50065c64bb73',1,'What was the common interest that brought Watson and Crick together?','Watson and Crick discovered their common interest in solving the DNA structure.'),
  ('eb1d3f07-af58-4545-ac9e-c27a0a8da327','5430f416-40c5-46d3-890a-03d5f4b3b5cc',1,'How many serious efforts did Watson and Crick make before successfully proposing the DNA structure?','Their first serious effort was unsatisfactory, but their second effort based on more experimental evidence resulted in the proposal of the DNA structure.'),
  ('a8423ffc-7784-4c74-92b3-238cb816b1f6','20ad66ae-63f1-4fc9-a193-5387c992cd5c',1,'What was the basis of Watson and Crick''s second successful effort to determine DNA structure?','Their second effort was based upon more experimental evidence and better appreciation of the nucleic acid literature.'),
  ('d827721b-b722-431a-93a3-392c1c314fec','bc0a0590-79f3-4840-8f4a-690fa7648aba',1,'Where was James Watson born?','James Dewey Watson was born in Chicago.'),
  ('c36b76ec-0079-4354-86a0-0678e3e1b73c','edeea57c-5866-4c22-8917-f8a907f43053',1,'Where was Francis Crick born?','Francis Harry Compton Crick was born at Northampton, England.'),
  ('39841604-4df8-49ae-975e-fee4003042ef','60a4f409-4536-49f7-81b6-5203a93008f8',1,'What was James Watson''s early interest that matured into a desire to learn genetics?','During his early years, Watson''s interest in bird-watching had matured into a serious desire to learn genetics.'),
  ('65fbfd45-0ba2-424e-9c38-483bc6c7e4fc','f3f03556-8875-4bde-b74e-61bcfa3b2d16',1,'What is the phenotypic ratio observed in the F2 generation of incomplete dominance in snapdragon flower color?','In incomplete dominance, the F2 generation shows a phenotypic ratio of 1 red : 2 pink : 1 white, which is 1:2:1. This is because the heterozygous condition produces an intermediate phenotype (pink) rather than the dominant phenotype.'),
  ('dcb432f0-876f-4de6-9910-f10e505a69bb','cbee3569-b4ad-4de4-8b9b-b15947fbcc34',1,'In incomplete dominance, what is the genotype of the F1 generation when crossing true-breeding red (RR) and white (rr) snapdragon plants?','The F1 generation produced from crossing RR (red) and rr (white) parents has the genotype Rr. Due to incomplete dominance, these plants show a pink phenotype instead of red.'),
  ('855b83b3-a9f7-42f8-9f90-01e9e7d150f2','dd894104-cf2b-4094-ba01-ba20924dc520',1,'What is the genotypic ratio in the F2 generation of incomplete dominance in snapdragon?','The genotypic ratio in the F2 generation of incomplete dominance is 1 RR : 2 Rr : 1 rr, which equals 1:2:1. This is the same as the phenotypic ratio in this case.'),
  ('871a7e89-044a-49dd-8af3-b58b8fa17bce','b1437857-d082-44dc-947b-68a7eb4e87b2',1,'Which plant is used as a classic example to understand incomplete dominance?','Snapdragon (Antirrhinum sp.) or dog flower is the classic example used to understand incomplete dominance. When true-breeding red (RR) and white (rr) plants are crossed, the F1 shows pink flowers (Rr).'),
  ('f7db0cda-798f-4fd4-9771-dd3b26340dc2','80216d24-69af-43b9-a618-e4f1c8509132',1,'What does the modified allele produce in the first case of enzyme production explanation?','In the first case, the modified allele produces the normal or less efficient enzyme, which is equivalent to the unmodified allele and results in the same phenotype.'),
  ('93154768-f060-48ef-ae4a-3edb4a53f70b','d4d5a3b2-c136-48f3-96d8-c80c409ed74a',1,'What happens when the modified allele produces a non-functional enzyme or no enzyme?','When the modified allele produces a non-functional enzyme or no enzyme, the phenotype depends only on the functioning of the unmodified (dominant) allele. The recessive trait is seen only when both alleles are modified.'),
  ('05bc654f-02a3-4b55-bc60-e765eaf8e565','3e42688a-ec73-4cf8-9bc2-15923e05c436',1,'What is the flower color of the F1 generation in incomplete dominance between red (RR) and white (rr) snapdragon?','In incomplete dominance, the F1 generation (Rr) shows an intermediate phenotype. When red (RR) and white (rr) snapdragon plants are crossed, the F1 flowers are pink, which is intermediate between red and white.'),
  ('3c9d992f-0595-4434-91dc-accf043c60f5','2fdb3d23-ac01-40d8-bd50-ce2741023367',1,'Which of the following is NOT a possible outcome of a modified allele in enzyme production?','The text describes three possible outcomes of a modified allele: (i) normal or less efficient enzyme, (ii) non-functional enzyme, or (iii) no enzyme at all. A super-efficient enzyme is not mentioned.'),
  ('258a5ab1-e372-4906-b5d2-b1de68ad39bc','868baca9-4633-48ef-94c8-3c91b1dd46e4',1,'In the context of incomplete dominance, what does the term ''dominance'' refer to?','In incomplete dominance, neither allele is completely dominant. Instead, both alleles are partially expressed, resulting in an intermediate phenotype (like pink flowers from red and white parents).'),
  ('b1b72b97-5ab7-48a0-a175-603c0e5082ec','7d05d199-21b4-4ea0-8aa9-7b01dd15d548',1,'What is the phenotype of the parental generation (P generation) in the snapdragon incomplete dominance example?','The parental generation consists of true-breeding red (RR) and true-breeding white (rr) snapdragon plants. Their phenotypes are red and white respectively.'),
  ('f1f3aee9-8aed-4282-b53e-edfa1d63c6e5','a498813a-b4f6-4375-8de2-42ccb697689d',1,'What is the genotype ratio of the F2 generation in incomplete dominance?','The F2 genotype ratio in incomplete dominance is 1 RR : 2 Rr : 1 rr, which is 1:2:1. This is the same as the phenotypic ratio because the heterozygote shows a distinct intermediate phenotype.'),
  ('800b6ecd-f35c-4cc8-96bc-94427c85bddb','89c50668-37d0-4ca1-ba33-dde1bd626f86',1,'Why is the F2 phenotypic ratio different from the typical 3:1 ratio in incomplete dominance?','In incomplete dominance, the F2 phenotypic ratio is 1:2:1 instead of 3:1 because the heterozygote (Rr) shows an intermediate phenotype (pink) that can be distinguished from both homozygotes (RR = red, rr = white).'),
  ('8d58ab1f-d56a-4186-85d9-bec7f76f599d','0545ff75-9891-425c-86b1-6580673d4714',1,'What is the key difference between complete dominance and incomplete dominance?','The key difference is that in complete dominance, the heterozygote shows the same phenotype as the dominant homozygote, while in incomplete dominance, the heterozygote shows an intermediate phenotype between the two parents.'),
  ('3a902629-a8c8-4c69-815a-b8943faf72aa','826df26a-fb83-4dda-8105-f66eb8ad210d',1,'Which of the following best explains the concept of dominance at the molecular level?','At the molecular level, dominance can be explained by enzyme production. The dominant (unmodified) allele produces a functional enzyme needed for a particular phenotype, while the recessive (modified) allele may produce a non-functional enzyme or no enzyme at all.'),
  ('4b2c0fd0-e8d3-484a-8cbf-e6b9a082d3bb','d523d597-b639-4fc9-9b00-5bed7481507b',1,'What is the phenotype of the F2 generation snapdragon plant with genotype RR?','In the F2 generation of incomplete dominance in snapdragon, plants with genotype RR have red flowers, plants with genotype Rr have pink flowers, and plants with genotype rr have white flowers.'),
  ('f35dfa79-c932-4415-8840-61f20d0ed277','2f3b5de0-5989-474b-8552-c17807caaf3a',1,'What is the phenotype of the F2 generation snapdragon plant with genotype rr?','In the F2 generation of incomplete dominance, plants with genotype rr have white flowers, as this is the homozygous recessive condition that produces the original white phenotype.'),
  ('de88f290-16db-4be9-a773-498d750291ab','56e1fd5c-2203-469e-8345-909099fd7f7e',1,'In the enzyme production example, what is the role of the substrate S?','The substrate S is the substance that the normal enzyme transforms. The enzyme catalyzes the conversion of substrate S into a product, which contributes to the phenotype.'),
  ('823772a8-bd15-4a97-b069-a7698837c5e8','965ca107-b818-4a1d-9f1a-458e77d4d872',1,'What is the term for alleles that are slightly different forms of the same gene?','Alleles are slightly different forms of the same gene that code for a pair of contrasting traits. For example, R and r are alleles that control flower color in snapdragon.'),
  ('d8972ab5-6c96-48bd-9d01-034fb10ad1ce','a9c6cc64-8273-4178-9492-2013146e54b1',1,'In incomplete dominance, what happens when the F1 generation (Rr) is self-pollinated?','When the F1 generation (Rr) is self-pollinated, the F2 generation shows a phenotypic ratio of 1 red (RR) : 2 pink (Rr) : 1 white (rr), which is the characteristic ratio of incomplete dominance.'),
  ('de3f18e8-712b-4252-8adf-4f0fe1ad46f8','8a73d2f3-d1d8-4ccb-9b3a-086652403f27',1,'What is the significance of the 1:2:1 ratio in incomplete dominance?','In incomplete dominance, the 1:2:1 ratio is significant because both the genotypic ratio (1 RR : 2 Rr : 1 rr) and the phenotypic ratio (1 red : 2 pink : 1 white) are the same. This is because each genotype produces a distinct phenotype.'),
  ('8a4cb06c-289f-4d96-b0fe-ea61e9aa80b9','90d780f3-b2c9-4320-a447-04eacb166997',1,'In which year did Mendel publish his work on inheritance of characters?','Mendel published his work on inheritance of characters in 1865.'),
  ('f2f605bd-abe9-4d14-93c2-508a1e5ef5ef','dde249a4-0c45-4f93-84ec-6168e7c56427',1,'Mendel''s work remained unrecognized until which year?','Mendel''s work remained unrecognized till 1900 due to several reasons including communication difficulties and lack of acceptance of his concepts.'),
  ('c9b5e516-ebf6-4510-afb5-7e9ecb12efd9','e53f109c-dc2b-4e10-9e00-79acf92700bc',1,'Which of the following was NOT a reason for Mendel''s work remaining unrecognized?','Mendel could not provide any physical proof for the existence of factors or say what they were made of. This was actually a reason his work was not accepted, not something that helped recognition.'),
  ('338a7700-44a1-4f24-960c-63797c171948','1a841a45-b029-4055-a9b8-ed5c04fb9051',1,'How many scientists independently rediscovered Mendel''s results in 1900?','Three scientists - de Vries, Correns, and von Tschermak - independently rediscovered Mendel''s results on the inheritance of characters in 1900.'),
  ('284eda4f-39f6-45d0-b54e-6a1d7b88abcd','b2c3d1d1-f01f-4784-8782-4f1c70aea5ad',1,'Which advancement helped scientists discover chromosomes?','Advancements in microscopy allowed scientists to carefully observe cell division and discover structures in the nucleus called chromosomes.'),
  ('6e4a9802-e9e7-4afe-af24-858d6caf1578','b86c88c3-0f3d-4a97-9a6d-75a0a7832f86',1,'What are chromosomes literally called?','Chromosomes are called colored bodies because they were visualized by staining. The word chromosome comes from Greek words meaning colored body.'),
  ('50f18995-81dd-40ba-8c2a-d72770e2eac7','e7243cc4-62a8-4cd0-aab4-81e9fff91b30',1,'By which year was the chromosome movement during meiosis worked out?','By 1902, the chromosome movement during meiosis had been worked out, allowing scientists to understand how chromosomes behave during cell division.'),
  ('e8c50e8f-f2c8-4e72-9e3c-9d5fd0f482f7','497f28ba-2b57-4a98-8f61-8fa8fb00bdf8',1,'Which scientists noted that the behavior of chromosomes was parallel to the behavior of genes?','Walter Sutton and Theodore Boveri noted that the behavior of chromosomes was parallel to the behavior of genes and used chromosome movement to explain Mendel''s laws.'),
  ('37277cb3-511a-4fe8-952f-ea10ee90fe2b','c35d3240-158c-41dd-8d0a-91514088c3de',1,'According to the chromosomal theory, where are the two alleles of a gene pair located?','The two alleles of a gene pair are located on homologous sites on homologous chromosomes. This is a key principle of the chromosomal theory of inheritance.'),
  ('ecd732ae-b661-49a1-96ab-b4886b968186','edac2100-1abd-49b5-91ed-5f56e0a4778e',1,'What do chromosomes and genes have in common according to the text?','Both chromosomes and genes occur in pairs. The two alleles of a gene pair are located on homologous sites on homologous chromosomes, showing the parallel behavior between genes and chromosomes.'),
  ('2e98d5e0-4ba3-438f-9398-9a2b2719f2fc','ffb1a76f-096b-4bc4-b781-417d31c46681',1,'What is mutation defined as in genetics?','Mutation is defined as a phenomenon which results in alteration of DNA sequences and consequently results in changes in the genotype and the phenotype of an organism.'),
  ('93e0e0a2-c44a-4a4b-a5e4-e9af28b19907','5e2f3b85-a648-4712-b1d0-4cb21139b82f',1,'Which of the following can cause mutations in organisms?','UV radiations can cause mutations in organisms. These chemical and physical factors that induce mutations are referred to as mutagens.'),
  ('4008df05-634f-4171-8830-c8d86d19dd84','cb1d99da-5521-4a84-89d2-2f621063742e',1,'What is a point mutation?','Point mutation arises due to change in a single base pair of DNA. A classical example of such a mutation is sickle cell anemia.'),
  ('268bf7e0-07c3-4122-9f5d-81c1b0174b6a','dff1aa9d-f691-4af4-abe9-48d6b0f9e94f',1,'What is pedigree analysis?','Pedigree analysis is the analysis of traits in several generations of a family to trace the inheritance of a particular trait, abnormality or disease.'),
  ('ecb32ef8-8a24-4877-8d16-c18c09f1c504','2e5c7c97-d8f6-4caa-92fa-aefdb5d616a4',1,'Which of the following is NOT a Mendelian disorder?','Down''s syndrome is a chromosomal disorder, not a Mendelian disorder. Mendelian disorders are mainly determined by alteration or mutation in a single gene, such as Haemophilia, Cystic fibrosis, Sickle-cell anaemia, and Phenylketonuria.'),
  ('814563fd-dc13-4455-9ee3-7f180e0711bb','4c5b33f2-5e45-4179-87f9-72bf0acdfe41',1,'How are Mendelian disorders transmitted to offspring?','Mendelian disorders are transmitted to the offspring on the same lines as studied in the principle of inheritance. The pattern of inheritance can be traced in a family by pedigree analysis.'),
  ('44f9a7a8-ebde-4eb5-8308-b74cb047130d','06e8efa3-99ef-4457-a15b-e77436d066e6',1,'Which of the following is an X-linked recessive trait?','Haemophilia is an X-linked recessive trait that shows transmission from carrier female to male progeny. Sickle-cell anaemia, Cystic fibrosis, and Phenylketonuria are autosomal disorders.'),
  ('0077660c-be42-43ac-91b1-064bd664261a','dfcbb836-efe8-4126-adb7-f029007ebd37',1,'What causes chromosomal aberrations?','Loss (deletions) or gain (insertion/duplication) of a segment of DNA result in alteration in chromosomes, leading to chromosomal aberrations. Since genes are located on chromosomes, alteration in chromosomes results in abnormalities.'),
  ('67213872-17fb-47d0-ab8e-8f8ff809d7be','be72a9ec-2014-428b-9106-8326204c5fd2',1,'What are the factors that induce mutations called?','The chemical and physical factors that induce mutations are referred to as mutagens. Examples include UV radiations and certain chemicals.'),
  ('5ffe327e-21a6-44b2-88c9-001c7a4b61ae','d90f271c-ccc9-407a-b7c7-a06e308efc47',1,'What is the main carrier of genetic information in organisms?','DNA is the carrier of genetic information. It is transmitted from one generation to the other without any change or alteration. However, changes do take place occasionally, which are called mutations.'),
  ('cdd80e63-d812-455f-ba01-03ea56f27351','8ebe8b10-a771-4ec4-ab53-7139252266de',1,'Which of the following is a chromosomal disorder?','Down''s syndrome is a chromosomal disorder caused due to the presence of an additional copy of chromosome 21 (trisomy of 21). Haemophilia, Cystic fibrosis, and Phenylketonuria are Mendelian disorders.'),
  ('6e76532d-751e-41f0-9d5d-3faaaee8adca','6927bd73-c968-4cba-9bee-c593c46c6ae6',1,'What is the inheritance pattern of sickle-cell anaemia?','Sickle-cell anaemia is an autosome-linked recessive trait that can be transmitted from parents to offspring when both partners are carriers for the gene (heterozygous).'),
  ('0bd6762b-7768-4937-9c3c-6aa8fd59e1a8','d4b27a1d-4b80-4d5a-9562-165fd8e24030',1,'What is the probability of a carrier mother passing haemophilia to her son?','The heterozygous female (carrier) for haemophilia may transmit the disease to sons with 50% probability. This is because the son has a 50% chance of receiving the affected X chromosome from the mother.'),
  ('0f366b86-209c-471a-abd6-1847fddd1a04','bb1714f6-8e49-4f5d-bcd8-170809123c32',1,'What is the main purpose of pedigree analysis in human genetics?','In human genetics, pedigree study provides a strong tool to trace the inheritance of a specific trait, abnormality or disease across generations in a family.'),
  ('f3d2ff24-a239-442c-89bf-522562ba1a6f','26dd145d-8f98-45ef-b58c-8b94eb27b7b0',1,'Which disorder is caused by a mutation in the gene coding for phenyl alanine hydroxylase enzyme?','Phenylketonuria is caused by mutation in the gene that codes for the enzyme phenyl alanine hydroxylase. This is an example of pleiotropy where a single gene mutation affects multiple phenotypes.'),
  ('ce06733f-3a8c-4086-8635-60ebb18be71d','e4a05187-ae86-4678-b7af-d35e0b0cfb2a',1,'What is the main difference between Mendelian and chromosomal disorders?','Mendelian disorders are mainly determined by alteration or mutation in a single gene. Chromosomal disorders are caused due to absence, excess, or abnormal arrangement of one or more chromosomes.'),
  ('765d7b5a-3af4-439b-8cf5-1518397bdd05','084875b2-4fd0-4815-b46c-d27ed2998d96',1,'What is the inheritance pattern of colour blindness?','Colour blindness is a sex-linked recessive disorder due to defect in genes present in the X chromosome. It occurs in about 8% of males and only about 0.4% of females because males have only one X chromosome.'),
  ('51cd51ab-fd21-4111-880d-22868bbffa17','eda36995-125b-4690-96f0-8966bb8f29e1',1,'What is the probability of a son being colour blind if his mother is a carrier?','The son of a woman who carries the gene for colour blindness has a 50% chance of being colour blind. The mother is not herself colour blind because the gene is recessive and its effect is suppressed by her matching dominant normal gene.'),
  ('d42845d2-6962-469b-bc1f-37b5e7328f9c','35ee42dd-47ee-471b-97be-d614ae7c515b',1,'Which of the following is true about Thalassemia?','Thalassemia is an autosome-linked recessive blood disease transmitted from parents to offspring when both partners are unaffected carriers for the gene. It is caused by reduced rate of synthesis of globin chains.'),
  ('39e1cf13-e403-42eb-809e-0a9112da53fe','52b247ae-b192-4100-a50a-e713412b3c51',1,'What is the main characteristic of chromosomal disorders?','Chromosomal disorders are caused due to absence, excess, or abnormal arrangement of one or more chromosomes. This includes conditions like aneuploidy (gain or loss of chromosomes) and polyploidy (increase in whole set of chromosomes).'),
  ('2202ca3e-c21c-4194-836f-0d51128abc96','f0549355-6242-4256-83c0-2b93f3f8c53e',1,'What is the branch of biology that deals with inheritance and variation of characters from parents to offspring?','Genetics is the branch of biology which scientifically deals with the inheritance and variation of characters from parents to offspring.'),
  ('81085de4-e166-491d-a1c9-b5c64f3ce586','34f00610-13ba-4288-bda9-6a35b32351e6',1,'What is the process by which characters are passed on from parent to progeny called?','Inheritance is defined as the process by which characters are passed on from parent to progeny, and it forms the basis of heredity.'),
  ('6b4e04c4-9850-487f-990d-40ddd5dd9d70','f53e480e-9b70-4b8a-8467-3c9e54fcdfa1',1,'What does variation refer to in genetics?','Variation is defined as the degree by which progeny differ from their parents, representing differences in characteristics among offspring.'),
  ('ed201ca9-286f-4e74-805f-f3677eb93ad0','9e2dca49-6cff-4c43-bd9d-a668e0e5beac',1,'When did humans first know that one cause of variation was hidden in sexual reproduction?','According to the text, humans knew from as early as 8000-1000 B.C. that one of the causes of variation was hidden in sexual reproduction.'),
  ('d5bebcdb-c095-45d2-b448-5df0f84f2717','72f4a54e-a97e-4c25-a294-64011d79b95b',1,'What did early humans exploit from wild populations of plants and animals?','Early humans exploited the natural variations present in wild populations to selectively breed organisms with desirable characters through artificial selection.'),
  ('eedf499a-f57a-4f0e-9a75-0ca97b3d7b93','d5288df9-c464-4caa-a58a-06dba4c0c929',1,'Why does an elephant always give birth to a baby elephant and not some other animal?','This phenomenon is explained by the principles of inheritance and heredity, where offspring inherit characteristics from their parents of the same species.'),
  ('c2534077-ba0c-41db-bdd4-0368cbed5185','9be0c93d-1c93-463c-b505-a57201187138',1,'What is the term for the process where humans selectively breed organisms with desirable characters?','Artificial selection is the process where humans selectively breed and select for organisms that possess desirable characters from naturally occurring variations.'),
  ('18a5a3e9-c740-41b0-8e72-3be03e61a5ff','d56cc64a-2298-4598-973d-733dd2ff885b',1,'Which of the following is an example of a question that genetics helps answer?','Genetics helps explain why siblings sometimes look so similar to each other or sometimes even so different, through the study of inheritance and variation.'),
  ('c2f1c87c-56a6-41b4-adab-395a31e12eff','a82bb210-6982-4120-beb8-ac282eea31a8',1,'What is the basis of heredity according to the text?','The text states that inheritance is the basis of heredity, as it is the process by which characters are passed from parents to offspring.'),
  ('9b0da1c4-b5ae-4196-9cdc-81073dfa43f3','d1a22a8b-5fde-4b02-9a03-eef45670037b',1,'What did Mendel''s work and others who followed him help us understand?','The work of Mendel and others who followed him gave us an idea of inheritance patterns, which forms the foundation of classical genetics.'),
  ('d5f7ddd2-453b-4b21-90bd-ae686c8f7021','2932b73a-2a53-4e13-91e6-46aff9ac1da9',1,'What is pedigree analysis?','Pedigree analysis is the study of family history about inheritance of a particular trait, represented in a family tree over several generations.'),
  ('5595164c-3ab2-4e84-9043-ebdaf2ede6c6','a1d882f4-c568-42c1-8b6e-d129b8f1ee22',1,'Which of the following is NOT a Mendelian disorder mentioned in the text?','Down''s syndrome is a chromosomal disorder, not a Mendelian disorder. The Mendelian disorders mentioned are Haemophilia, Cystic fibrosis, Sickle-cell anaemia, Colour blindness, Phenylketonuria, and Thalassemia.'),
  ('71e4da35-265e-4e27-ad5f-045acaa29dec','a12c2adf-b368-4093-b942-a7e103e9f07d',1,'What is the approximate percentage of males affected by colour blindness?','Colour blindness occurs in about 8 percent of males and only about 0.4 percent of females because the genes are located on the X chromosome.'),
  ('c091e7f0-1497-4722-ac54-70e60ee52a67','6c1a34f9-b85b-42ef-b2df-8cd6c886cd3b',1,'Why is haemophilia called a sex-linked recessive disease?','Haemophilia is a sex-linked recessive disease that shows transmission from unaffected carrier female to some of the male progeny.'),
  ('71363bdc-0188-4ff7-9dcd-cadc4447e49a','aee85b2d-ea9d-44ac-85c8-a1abb06ad7b3',1,'What type of inheritance pattern does sickle-cell anaemia follow?','Sickle-cell anaemia is an autosome-linked recessive trait transmitted when both parents are carriers (heterozygous).'),
  ('7dd83c1f-7c96-4f03-adde-e92932599d52','4398086c-cb25-43d7-ab4f-b346d8c66993',1,'Which amino acid is substituted in sickle-cell anaemia at the sixth position of beta globin chain?','In sickle-cell anaemia, Glutamic acid is substituted by Valine at the sixth position of the beta globin chain of haemoglobin.'),
  ('8af79e8c-5d55-480e-9850-af3949f305e8','e1eac9db-e2bc-4a93-a430-0e4c4f9e2909',1,'What is the codon change in the beta globin gene that causes sickle-cell anaemia?','The substitution of amino acid results from a single base substitution at the sixth codon of the beta globin gene from GAG to GUG.'),
  ('57b3871a-5b8a-4f92-abe9-34e8fdbb7607','8947bcc9-0288-4dd2-90f3-c2fa44d5c7b5',1,'Which British monarch''s family showed haemophilic descendants?','The family pedigree of Queen Victoria shows a number of haemophilic descendants as she was a carrier of the disease.'),
  ('005ab845-544d-4e79-bc57-9c55d2d29fed','2321f7ef-b3c3-4bb7-94e5-610945f9f2cf',1,'What is the probability of a son being colour blind if the mother carries the gene?','The son of a woman who carries the gene has a 50 percent chance of being colour blind.'),
  ('932bfb6c-1bd3-4101-869c-46eaab4b14c5','4c93b849-b4d8-4260-b046-da497e9b43b7',1,'What is the genotype of individuals who show the diseased phenotype in sickle-cell anaemia?','Only homozygous individuals for HbS (HbS HbS) show the diseased phenotype. Heterozygous individuals (HbA HbS) are carriers but appear unaffected.'),
  ('ff4e4793-b26e-40a8-91a5-4ba93cc05eca','382a666e-f6ae-4fff-9bce-3eeda2c874b4',1,'What is the primary focus of the branch of biology known as Genetics?','Genetics is defined as a branch of biology which deals with principles of inheritance and its practices.'),
  ('58d70700-3e44-4ea1-8cbe-f0fe90a2e647','dcd24214-c8ce-4b89-ae56-8da541df7f87',1,'Who was the first scientist to systematically study the pattern of inheritance in pea plants?','Mendel was the first to study the phenomenon of inheritance systematically while studying the pattern of inheritance in pea plants of contrasting characters.'),
  ('fe7e91b9-0b78-46a2-8e95-961b07cbb211','cb9d8942-faf8-46fb-8dd2-bf92c235d35f',1,'What term did Mendel originally use to describe the factors that regulate characters?','Mendel proposed that the factors (later named as genes) regulating the characters are found in pairs known as alleles.'),
  ('3c0bcad9-54a3-434e-85e2-f1fa397fed19','9d4c7acc-6458-4667-b023-9c1b5dcb3fe3',1,'What are the paired units of inheritance that regulate characters called?','The factors (genes) regulating the characters are found in pairs known as alleles.'),
  ('a98e7d49-e0fd-4d0a-ba56-fd6f569d5887','af834603-94eb-424a-8b1c-62d5c9e631ef',1,'According to the Law of Dominance, when are dominant characters expressed?','The dominant characters are expressed when factors are in heterozygous condition and also in homozygous condition.'),
  ('914bf379-1076-48f1-bf5f-33918956c2e6','cc74e125-381d-4ece-ac10-580b4374f951',1,'Under what condition are recessive characters expressed according to Mendel''s laws?','The recessive characters are only expressed in homozygous conditions.'),
  ('5d83eb3d-41f0-48ed-ade0-c8502c448394','3bbe16d9-8f01-424c-beba-82f2b14df753',1,'Do characters blend in heterozygous condition according to Mendel''s observations?','The characters never blend in heterozygous condition according to Mendel''s observations.'),
  ('ae15afb6-bfe0-4eca-ad30-8b646835b3c2','1ae872f1-4768-474e-ad98-bb5197f47922',1,'What happens to a recessive character that was not expressed in heterozygous condition?','A recessive character that was not expressed in heterozygous condition may be expressed again when it becomes homozygous.'),
  ('7aceddad-0fee-4ba0-a63b-fc577569aa9f','270d78e1-70c5-4dad-9101-16752c7b3a38',1,'What is the Law of Segregation?','The Law of Segregation states that characters segregate while formation of gametes.'),
  ('3bef216a-8221-484c-9ad4-c987e073d26e','06b270e1-269e-4810-a5e1-5de358a48ffe',1,'Which of the following is NOT a type of dominance relationship mentioned in the summary?','The summary mentions complete dominance, incomplete dominance, and co-dominance. Over-dominance is not mentioned.'),
  ('f3cceed7-8c6a-42d4-8183-25b1d18fe32e','d9b0fa91-baf0-4cce-bb06-fded5391efd7',1,'What does the Law of Independent Assortment state?','When Mendel studied the inheritance of two characters together, it was found that the factors independently assort and combine in all permutations and combinations.'),
  ('3943c068-65a8-4512-8ad7-f4c5b7aae791','f9d43a9a-1f68-459a-b332-704ec55d5d66',1,'What is a Punnett Square?','Different combinations of gametes are theoretically represented in a square tabular form known as Punnett Square.'),
  ('ebe72e9c-971e-4244-9f08-5c7521695c23','a957d42b-985e-485d-b43a-862c5b10d819',1,'What is the term for the factors on chromosomes regulating characters?','The factors (now known as gene) on chromosomes regulating the characters are called the genotype.'),
  ('a35636f4-7647-4bee-8e95-af07aab86c7e','3c772a80-a735-47a2-a3ed-f2a4c1725504',1,'What is the physical expression of characters called?','The physical expression of the characters is called phenotype.'),
  ('3cf7be55-909e-4422-a847-bfbee1fd18e2','2ca35560-0ef7-4f1f-8494-d1bb59aa279f',1,'What theory was developed after discovering that genes are located on chromosomes?','After knowing that the genes are located on the chromosomes, a good correlation was drawn between Mendel''s laws and the Chromosomal Theory of Inheritance was developed.'),
  ('1411444c-7900-46a8-b66b-cc6d8b890919','30266b27-0b71-4f57-af9a-663f34c9c95e',1,'Which process during meiosis was correlated with Mendel''s laws of segregation and assortment?','A good correlation was drawn between Mendel''s laws: segregation and assortment of chromosomes during meiosis.'),
  ('2cd05e9e-2f2d-4d63-bab7-09ba23cc716e','fdeb10d5-b52c-4c9d-88f3-c7a9d54bba99',1,'What are genes located on the same chromosome called?','Mendel''s law of independent assortment does not hold true for the genes that were located on the same chromosomes. These genes were called as linked genes.'),
  ('fee2dfaa-5799-4328-a901-ebc9977d0836','ded3be07-247f-49a6-91a1-44a3c25694cb',1,'How do closely located genes on the same chromosome behave?','Closely located genes assorted together.'),
  ('e6e62c36-b489-45ee-a48e-3d8bda605ea4','26a6cec0-8ba1-4999-af4e-9d0cc168869f',1,'How do distantly located genes on the same chromosome behave?','Distantly located genes, due to recombination, assorted independently.'),
  ('4877edd0-aa06-46cf-a71e-e6cfdf7c9dab','8f63e501-ef95-4f2d-938c-8a32a44d7c25',1,'What do linkage maps correspond to?','Linkage maps corresponded to arrangement of genes on a chromosome.'),
  ('45b270de-bf5b-4198-bab6-5a501ab68872','93b03d1b-f31a-4e92-9252-591062b2bd17',1,'What are genes linked to sexes called?','Many genes were linked to sexes also, and called as sex-linked genes.'),
  ('9bc54582-683b-4d22-b1ab-fa550f5bf57e','23231498-b259-429f-b407-811ee5aadbc3',1,'What are the chromosomes that are common in both sexes called?','The two sexes were found to have a set of chromosomes which were common, and another set which was different. The common set was named as autosomes.'),
  ('5c7a0f64-13f0-4fca-afa9-575d937f32e8','44b0e6d0-2fa0-40da-98d9-8f4baf56d57d',1,'What are the chromosomes that are different in two sexes called?','The chromosomes which were different in two sexes were named as sex chromosomes.'),
  ('b2b84816-3e68-4fa0-96dd-21137c0d4e1e','8522ee14-b5af-48a8-be87-3ba2e49b3386',1,'What is the sex chromosome composition of a normal human female?','In humans, a normal female has 22 pairs of autosomes and a pair of sex chromosomes (XX).'),
  ('dc8b85ea-d9f9-4064-83f2-0276fe6b470f','6dad90b3-dd1e-4426-ad80-cc6881c22633',1,'What is the sex chromosome composition of a normal human male?','A male has 22 pairs of autosomes and a pair of sex chromosome as XY.'),
  ('bd1e7629-775f-4778-996b-722a2b9fe888','1126579c-26e6-42e4-90c1-a51cd1a3c6d5',1,'What are the sex chromosomes in male chickens?','In chicken, sex chromosomes in male are ZZ.'),
  ('13477155-4ed3-458f-b6ca-a9c4f9d64bbe','6c112102-7044-4dbd-a351-1a679c399fde',1,'What are the sex chromosomes in female chickens?','In chicken, sex chromosomes in females are ZW.'),
  ('61b43cad-870e-45e9-b7ad-c1adbb43cb0b','27a752a6-a2f5-4853-9662-68c2cb0ff272',1,'What is mutation defined as?','Mutation is defined as change in the genetic material.'),
  ('69444392-8e4b-41b4-af6f-3c47f27d1997','179971eb-41aa-43cd-bc68-4623431de7d7',1,'What is a point mutation?','A point mutation is a change of a single base pair in DNA.'),
  ('c07a8236-8dd9-4b75-8247-594219b65d88','cd90e16a-69ec-4806-850c-886e42f7384d',1,'Which disease is caused due to change of one base in the gene coding for beta-chain of hemoglobin?','Sickle-cell anemia is caused due to change of one base in the gene coding for beta-chain of hemoglobin.'),
  ('62327e53-35da-4b7b-b4f0-1b24b413f55a','e9b6afb3-21d9-4296-bc53-4b7b114d3314',1,'How can inheritable mutations be studied?','Inheritable mutations can be studied by generating a pedigree of a family.'),
  ('8b9f70b2-df15-4fb0-97ea-f63b165af0ab','e31f9a26-8270-4f65-a2f0-25ea9bff66dc',1,'What is polyploidy?','Some mutations involve changes in whole set of chromosomes (polyploidy).'),
  ('95adfded-2f98-4a90-8902-ff9ed3e68f5c','fef1920d-231a-4e6b-aa3f-360d837ca1d5',1,'What is aneuploidy?','Aneuploidy is change in a subset of chromosome number.'),
  ('ac86c16d-ff06-4280-8553-e3350b8ec14e','e6cbb97b-923b-41bd-aa96-e79e8707dc8d',1,'What is the cause of Down''s syndrome?','Down''s syndrome is due to trisomy of chromosome 21, where there is an extra copy of chromosome 21 and consequently the total number of chromosome becomes 47.'),
  ('a0ace1ce-afca-47bd-8ea8-d21877e1e3ca','7c1bb5a7-81f2-4f39-ab00-dd9f226901f6',1,'How many total chromosomes are present in a person with Down''s syndrome?','In Down''s syndrome, the total number of chromosome becomes 47.'),
  ('344015b9-097d-4442-af44-3c9afeced8df','5d6038fb-0bb1-478d-903e-f6d6b842d17c',1,'What is the chromosomal composition in Turner''s syndrome?','In Turner''s syndrome, one X chromosome is missing and the sex chromosome is as XO.'),
  ('039ce887-6025-41a2-bee8-6013b18ed1a8','5f6811ca-ed3e-4639-9056-939b24364147',1,'What is the chromosomal composition in Klinefelter''s syndrome?','In Klinefelter''s syndrome, the condition is XXY.'),
  ('9c4610f1-24ce-4317-971b-2d29fc6640ce','207b8110-bfb3-4059-92d4-1658d0dac2b7',1,'How can chromosomal disorders like Down''s, Turner''s, and Klinefelter''s syndromes be easily studied?','These can be easily studied by analysis of Karyotypes.'),
  ('cab5430d-5370-486c-9948-a76481d3e081','444d6d95-07f6-4894-862d-4c4b7e7960e4',1,'Which generation shows the expression of characters following a definite pattern according to Mendel?','He observed that the expression of the characters in the offspring follow a definite pattern in different first generations (F1), second (F2) and so on.'),
  ('5f16e797-dbea-4ae7-a11a-bb8b5156c835','864319a3-c68d-4194-8261-6a12ec7b36bf',1,'Which of the following best describes the relationship between genotype and phenotype?','The factors (genes) on chromosomes regulating the characters are called the genotype and the physical expression of the characters is called phenotype.'),
  ('84653244-5733-4cc3-bf8c-f6527c9476ae','285f0a9b-2e21-407c-94ad-d21d513ade08',1,'In honey bees, how many chromosomes are present in a female?','Female honey bees have 32 chromosomes because they develop from fertilized eggs and are diploid.'),
  ('3ccae2d6-42f0-4589-84fc-32fb627d9f65','b616fd07-0348-4bf7-a025-3438d273f828',1,'How many chromosomes are present in a male honey bee (drone)?','Male honey bees (drones) have 16 chromosomes because they develop from unfertilized eggs and are haploid.'),
  ('b2624409-3ec3-45e5-8c21-9b4d3ac2f7b0','c35c771c-b9c5-4ef7-9fac-51017e5d93b9',1,'What type of sex-determination system is found in honey bees?','Honey bees have a haplodiploid sex-determination system where females are diploid and males are haploid.'),
  ('9111709b-75a4-487d-868d-ab833655e520','79b45cbe-d989-40e5-8d2e-05799062fad1',1,'In honey bees, how do males produce sperm?','Male honey bees produce sperm by mitosis because they are haploid and do not undergo meiosis.'),
  ('2603e38d-c0c5-4426-ab50-371cca4a07ec','b40bbf30-a0a3-47f1-9c10-e85dcbc1745e',1,'Which of the following is TRUE about male honey bees?','Male honey bees have no father, cannot have sons, but have a grandfather and can have grandsons.'),
  ('b58169cd-aafc-42dd-b1ef-436d565e4525','5721418e-5392-4bfa-a828-89e48deeb8df',1,'In honey bees, what develops from an unfertilized egg?','In honey bees, unfertilized eggs develop into male drones through parthenogenesis.'),
  ('fb4db5f0-2518-4ac5-8224-e2eae506d6cc','d7930adb-dd99-4655-986e-781859e90034',1,'What is the term for development of an organism from an unfertilized egg?','Parthenogenesis is the development of an organism from an unfertilized egg, as seen in honey bee males.'),
  ('f15ccbb4-5f83-430e-a235-c6dbd3846692','4e5e2f27-3ec1-4966-9ece-306eab02a637',1,'In birds, which sex is heterogametic?','In birds, females are heterogametic with ZW chromosomes while males are homogametic with ZZ chromosomes.'),
  ('0439948d-988e-47de-9efb-56c5ec430f67','ba154e88-ba37-46e6-b0ba-56902cb2831a',1,'What chromosomes do female birds possess?','Female birds have ZW sex chromosomes, while male birds have ZZ chromosomes.'),
  ('b16d3c1f-cec1-46bd-af64-a722cffa38bd','804d56c4-0b02-441b-900c-6e280a3e3bc2',1,'In the ZW sex-determination system, which sex determines the sex of the offspring?','In the ZW system, the female is heterogametic and determines the sex of the offspring, unlike the XY system where the male determines sex.'),
  ('faf29f71-949f-411e-994a-0dbde9d60378','6c143c45-063d-4649-8643-b9bcc89b36c7',1,'How many pairs of autosomes are present in humans?','Humans have 22 pairs of autosomes and one pair of sex chromosomes, making 23 pairs total.'),
  ('0e9e357d-a02f-4281-8f9e-3c40db3c68e3','f398f27d-7d74-4956-8e99-a768fc3a39ca',1,'What is the sex chromosome composition in a normal human female?','A normal human female has two X chromosomes (XX), while a male has one X and one Y chromosome (XY).'),
  ('574a2bfe-a63b-41fe-afb9-fd19fb3638d9','2e384492-e633-461a-bf07-d176f5239d59',1,'What percentage of sperm carry the X chromosome in humans?','During spermatogenesis, 50% of sperm carry the X chromosome and 50% carry the Y chromosome.'),
  ('6d2e103b-232a-45d1-965d-cd2cba60f836','352225f1-991b-4dad-bf52-83d4fe77ee12',1,'If an ovum is fertilized by a sperm carrying the X chromosome, what is the sex of the offspring?','When an X-bearing sperm fertilizes an ovum, the zygote develops into a female (XX).'),
  ('e2a0c498-09de-41e8-af6d-8914de4a8e5f','d3fec8ef-796f-4e4d-9958-5eca4f3c3b1c',1,'Which gamete determines the sex of the child in humans?','The genetic makeup of the sperm determines the sex of the child because sperm can carry either X or Y chromosome.'),
  ('9cc7a0a2-2d62-42da-9a83-8f030c54b783','b9fb0094-dc1d-4b11-9c05-c4644a658554',1,'What is the probability of having a male child in each pregnancy?','In each pregnancy, there is always a 50% probability of either a male or female child.'),
  ('7e13a059-f9b3-4e21-9c66-8359eea545ed','69f05340-31b2-4c30-8f19-17558168e051',1,'What is the sex chromosome composition in a normal human male?','A normal human male has one X and one Y chromosome (XY).'),
  ('bb730a5b-4c22-4987-a86c-5fbc3f9823e9','7b9d9919-5778-47d9-a15a-341c8b4fff12',1,'In humans, what type of ovum is produced by females?','Females produce only one type of ovum with an X chromosome.'),
  ('71975d0e-0f17-4ad8-a6ae-d8a97a5ef350','ace2360a-d3fc-4712-acb5-05b6aa5b080f',1,'Which type of sex determination is found in grasshoppers?','Grasshoppers have the XO type of sex determination where males have one X chromosome and no Y chromosome.'),
  ('d7d51e3b-084e-4faf-b316-b38cf36aaa1c','f7637e83-ad80-43e8-974e-e749f1ec4d39',1,'What is the term for the sex determination system where males produce two different types of gametes?','Male heterogamety refers to the system where males produce two different types of gametes, such as in humans and Drosophila.'),
  ('c117f8fe-fb6b-41d6-baf2-52215fa4a244','1caa0793-f6b1-4590-8125-bbed0d5bfd00',1,'According to Mendel''s Law of Dominance, which of the following statements is correct?','According to Mendel''s Law of Dominance, when two alleles of a gene pair are different (heterozygous), only the dominant allele is expressed in the phenotype while the recessive allele remains hidden.'),
  ('7cdc7723-2359-4c78-b1e2-eb667d7217ab','80647a85-11d2-4567-933c-a3913731148c',1,'What is the phenotypic ratio obtained in the F2 generation of a monohybrid cross between true-breeding tall (TT) and dwarf (tt) pea plants?','In a monohybrid cross, the F2 generation shows a phenotypic ratio of 3 tall : 1 dwarf. The genotypic ratio is 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt, but since both TT and Tt express the dominant tall phenotype, the phenotypic ratio becomes 3:1.'),
  ('69a6144e-39d0-4555-a41d-06c9e91dc7e9','8d9169eb-0267-4a34-b39c-b5aa4d8b6cd2',1,'A test cross is performed to determine the genotype of an organism showing a dominant phenotype. Which cross represents a test cross?','A test cross involves crossing an organism with a dominant phenotype (but unknown genotype) with a homozygous recessive individual. For example, a tall plant (TT or Tt) is crossed with a dwarf plant (tt) to determine whether the tall plant is homozygous dominant or heterozygous.'),
  ('9ebe7ea0-e114-483a-b614-b207271c42d2','78cd1c72-9123-4c30-8992-7e6d2732c139',1,'In snapdragon (Antirrhinum), when a true-breeding red-flowered plant (RR) is crossed with a true-breeding white-flowered plant (rr), what is the phenotype of the F1 generation?','In incomplete dominance, the F1 heterozygote (Rr) shows an intermediate phenotype. In snapdragon, RR produces red flowers, rr produces white flowers, and Rr produces pink flowers - an intermediate between the two parents.'),
  ('626009d6-1cfb-4ca8-b22a-563dceee36f2','fef23295-9248-4e63-8fa9-4498b1fbbac3',1,'In the ABO blood group system, when both IA and IB alleles are present together in an individual, what is the blood type?','In co-dominance, both alleles express themselves equally. When IA and IB are present together (IAIB genotype), both A and B antigens are produced on the RBC surface, resulting in AB blood type. This is different from incomplete dominance where an intermediate phenotype appears.'),
  ('2c945567-fefa-4594-bd17-ac91af09e9c0','178f903a-f11c-436c-8e64-e981f078436b',1,'What is the phenotypic ratio in the F2 generation of a dihybrid cross between round yellow (RRYY) and wrinkled green (rryy) pea plants?','In a dihybrid cross involving two pairs of contrasting traits, the F2 generation shows a phenotypic ratio of 9 round yellow : 3 round green : 3 wrinkled yellow : 1 wrinkled green. This is derived from (3:1) x (3:1) = 9:3:3:1, demonstrating the Law of Independent Assortment.'),
  ('d097e92b-fb79-40f9-92d1-e4682bda3a6b','e48b31e0-6dba-4366-9efc-a208e31006d0',1,'Mendel''s Law of Independent Assortment states that:','The Law of Independent Assortment states that when two pairs of traits are combined in a hybrid, the segregation of one pair of characters is independent of the other pair. This means different genes are inherited independently of one another during gamete formation.'),
  ('5ed779d7-ab8f-42be-a3ea-32b7e95c26ed','27ee2b8c-c51d-4321-91b9-4dbe400c78a7',1,'The Chromosomal Theory of Inheritance was proposed by:','Walter Sutton and Theodore Boveri independently proposed the Chromosomal Theory of Inheritance in 1902. They noted that the behavior of chromosomes during meiosis was parallel to the behavior of genes, and used chromosome movement to explain Mendel''s laws.'),
  ('341b28f1-3817-47b0-9c17-0dd0f2a78368','8f8170bb-c065-49d8-ad8d-3138adade9bf',1,'In human beings, sex determination is of which type?','Humans have XY type of sex determination. Males have 22 pairs of autosomes plus XY sex chromosomes, while females have 22 pairs of autosomes plus XX sex chromosomes. The male is heterogametic, producing 50% X-bearing and 50% Y-bearing sperm.'),
  ('f703b8d6-cb5f-4112-ba38-4414cc1829b4','fd553d37-5a6a-4eb9-82ce-93b9cbd51b31',1,'In honey bees, males (drones) develop from:','Honey bees show haplodiploid sex determination. Males (drones) develop from unfertilized eggs through parthenogenesis and are haploid (16 chromosomes). Females (queens and workers) develop from fertilized eggs and are diploid (32 chromosomes).'),
  ('b589a4ae-84cc-484e-ae6f-e0ab87c5760e','23df5177-a9e6-4511-a69d-d1a22264b9f2',1,'Sickle cell anemia is caused by which type of mutation?','Sickle cell anemia is caused by a point mutation - a change in a single base pair in the DNA. The sixth codon of the beta-globin gene is changed from GAG (glutamic acid) to GTG (valine), resulting in the production of abnormal hemoglobin (HbS).'),
  ('95fa69fa-061e-4d48-8744-9598dcb413c2','d6ba3692-d33d-425d-8fe4-0e3ca60177f7',1,'What is the main purpose of pedigree analysis in human genetics?','Pedigree analysis is a tool used to trace the inheritance of a specific trait, abnormality, or disease through several generations of a family. It helps determine whether a trait is dominant or recessive, autosomal or sex-linked, and identifies carriers.'),
  ('d26e41ae-8e00-4f00-a2b9-99653104c421','9c8a03ac-a2ac-40d6-bdbc-46f3cdf163d9',1,'Haemophilia is a sex-linked recessive disorder. Which statement about its inheritance is correct?','Haemophilia is an X-linked recessive disorder. An affected father (XhY) passes his X chromosome to all daughters (making them carriers) but his Y chromosome to sons. Therefore, an affected father cannot pass haemophilia to his sons. Sons can only inherit it from carrier mothers.'),
  ('8e261157-c707-410b-ac0a-6c160c793a9e','8a3f762f-54e1-4dea-866b-ae5eb43c0a33',1,'Down''s syndrome is caused by:','Down''s syndrome is caused by trisomy of chromosome 21, meaning there is an extra copy of chromosome 21. The total chromosome number becomes 47 instead of 46. This results in characteristic features including short stature, round head, furrowed tongue, and mental retardation.'),
  ('f901996b-5b1b-412d-978a-a54f45c9d50b','4f0b836c-ec0a-4ea7-9b4c-e6fe583d3e5b',1,'Human skin color is an example of:','Human skin color is a classic example of polygenic inheritance, where a trait is controlled by three or more genes. The phenotype reflects the additive contribution of each allele, and environmental factors also influence the expression. The more dominant alleles present, the darker the skin color.'),
  ('8ded51bc-50a1-431f-9169-90daff541349','2e7a0627-5557-44c8-b638-1c1b8d6938b9',1,'Klinefelter''s syndrome is characterized by which chromosomal composition?','Klinefelter''s syndrome is caused by the presence of an additional X chromosome, resulting in a karyotype of 47, XXY. The individual has overall masculine development but also shows feminine characteristics like breast development (gynaecomastia).'),
  ('14e6dc7a-7e0d-45f2-bd5f-cad99fcb8c02','c0c8f88a-b6f0-4002-8d43-0fd060fb0cb0',1,'Turner''s syndrome results from:','Turner''s syndrome is caused by the absence of one X chromosome, resulting in a karyotype of 45, X0. Such females are sterile with rudimentary ovaries and lack secondary sexual characteristics.'),
  ('f727a6d3-002f-45ac-951a-a68fe25baf98','036df85d-ccb0-4e99-8519-63b380184fd6',1,'The ABO blood group system in humans is an example of:','ABO blood grouping demonstrates both co-dominance (IA and IB both express when present together) and multiple alleles (three alleles: IA, IB, and i). IA and IB are co-dominant to each other but both are dominant over i.'),
  ('87ec5ecb-690f-41fb-b1ee-1b234ee96b76','3472dfa8-db7b-49e6-8a65-f1ff4964f54e',1,'Phenylketonuria is an example of pleiotropy because:','Pleiotropy occurs when a single gene influences multiple phenotypic traits. In phenylketonuria, a mutation in the gene coding for phenylalanine hydroxylase enzyme leads to multiple effects: mental retardation, reduced hair and skin pigmentation, and accumulation of phenylpyruvic acid in urine.'),
  ('d537b851-798e-4e69-b803-72982c7540b1','6afcc53e-d06c-4b7f-8acd-e605e8cbc1ee',1,'According to Mendel''s Law of Segregation, during gamete formation:','The Law of Segregation states that during gamete formation, the two alleles of a gene pair separate (segregate) from each other so that each gamete receives only one allele. This is the basis for the 3:1 ratio in F2 generation.'),
  ('e78344de-bc00-490b-bc5f-463dbf089dc7','4fa24d28-9d19-49d6-b65a-202e26eb52cb',1,'In Mendel''s experiments, TT and Tt pea plants both appeared tall. This demonstrates that:','TT (homozygous dominant) and Tt (heterozygous) have different genotypes but both express the tall phenotype because T is dominant over t. This shows that genotype (genetic makeup) and phenotype (observable traits) are not always the same.'),
  ('c6d67ecf-86fb-489f-975d-6d1702b30cbd','f349d4ce-cad5-4455-9677-eb8b4f18a4b1',1,'Thalassemia differs from sickle cell anemia in that:','Thalassemia is a quantitative problem - there is reduced synthesis of globin chains (alpha or beta), resulting in fewer normal hemoglobin molecules. Sickle cell anemia is a qualitative problem - a normal amount of hemoglobin is produced but it is functionally abnormal (HbS) due to a single amino acid substitution.'),
  ('1aa0bd28-ee66-43db-bc84-30b0b54beaa2','336c6058-8fb3-4a4d-88b6-3f3ea95735c4',1,'T.H. Morgan''s work with Drosophila showed that genes located on the same chromosome:','Morgan discovered that genes on the same chromosome are linked and tend to be inherited together. However, distantly located genes can be separated by crossing over during meiosis, allowing for recombination. Closely linked genes show very low recombination frequency.'),
  ('0000b5a6-a39e-4b48-8390-21b557f92d9b','dce69e9c-587e-47b1-a087-c4a7b16b2578',1,'Red-green colour blindness is more common in males than females because:','Colour blindness is an X-linked recessive disorder. Males have only one X chromosome (XY), so if they inherit the recessive allele on their single X, they will be colour blind. Females have two X chromosomes (XX), so they need two copies of the recessive allele to express the trait, making it much rarer.'),
  ('2bfc49d8-9d56-476f-90e2-b471b2797d36','40719158-d58e-4d3a-9450-136ddb9ab9f4',1,'Mutation is defined as:','Mutation is a phenomenon that results in alteration of DNA sequences, consequently leading to changes in the genotype and phenotype of an organism. Mutations can be caused by physical factors (UV radiation) or chemical factors (mutagens).'),
  ('be6f2b60-cffe-40e1-ac9d-ca276aef28ee','d249611f-b847-4d39-a461-bd1025d0e8f1',1,'Which seed colour was found to be dominant in Mendel''s dihybrid cross between yellow round and green wrinkled pea plants?','Yellow colour was dominant over green. Mendel found that the seeds resulting from crossing the parents had yellow coloured and round shaped seeds, indicating yellow dominance over green and round dominance over wrinkled.'),
  ('0fd1ca43-100d-4141-b04b-766aed7b3a82','17146a1c-e0a6-4aac-a429-35926c828ea8',1,'Which seed shape was dominant in Mendel''s dihybrid cross experiment?','Round shape was dominant over wrinkled. Mendel found that the F1 seeds resulting from the cross were round shaped, demonstrating that round is dominant over wrinkled.'),
  ('390f771d-9002-4090-b0ac-55737ef2753b','b68b157c-9123-4250-acc6-3fbe796e759a',1,'What were the genotypes of the parent plants in Mendel''s dihybrid cross for seed colour and seed shape?','The genotype of the parents was RRYY (round yellow) and rryy (wrinkled green). RRYY represents homozygous dominant for both traits, while rryy represents homozygous recessive for both traits.'),
  ('cbc5348a-23b0-4cbd-92a6-e09886cd88cb','2abeb180-d851-4e26-8d27-72e6a379492b',1,'What is the genotype of the F1 hybrid produced from the dihybrid cross between RRYY and rryy parents?','The gametes RY and ry unite on fertilisation to produce the F1 hybrid RrYy. This is a heterozygous genotype for both seed colour and seed shape traits.'),
  ('e85b2a2a-a046-46d6-8949-8cba4485af43','62b0b82d-81f3-4812-b360-b235031706f8',1,'When Mendel self-hybridised the F1 plants (RrYy), what phenotypic ratio was observed for seed colour in the F2 generation?','When Mendel self hybridised the F1 plants he found that 3/4th of F2 plants had yellow seeds and 1/4th had green. The yellow and green colour segregated in a 3:1 ratio, just like in a monohybrid cross.'),
  ('c05ed72b-1c36-4a0b-b857-aa5ec21793e3','9d7e3629-f58b-4e7a-9458-4c6663bd7a8c',1,'What was the phenotypic ratio for seed shape when F1 plants were self-hybridised?','Round and wrinkled seed shape also segregated in a 3:1 ratio, just like in a monohybrid cross. This demonstrates independent segregation of each trait.'),
  ('f7149e04-5998-4d0f-8225-0b2c3c1e4414','cf63d052-5573-4757-9016-0b43ee7fb6a7',1,'Which genotypic symbol represents the dominant yellow seed colour in Mendel''s dihybrid cross?','Y is used for dominant yellow seed colour and y for recessive green seed colour. Capital letters represent dominant alleles while lowercase letters represent recessive alleles.'),
  ('b3b80949-680f-4488-b151-9d0f671ae885','e9730d91-82f8-4287-bf3a-4353ba3fa15d',1,'What does the genotypic symbol ''r'' represent in Mendel''s dihybrid cross?','R is used for round shaped seeds (dominant) and r for wrinkled seed shape (recessive). The lowercase r represents the recessive allele for wrinkled seed shape.'),
  ('e6f567ce-6b85-43e1-89ce-d2213fc640a2','a2eb81a9-96b2-4bd8-9c18-23cd2043ff86',1,'What type of gametes are produced by the parent plant with genotype RRYY?','The parent plant with genotype RRYY can only produce gametes containing RY because it is homozygous dominant for both traits. All gametes will carry the dominant alleles R and Y.'),
  ('486c7d16-9c8f-4125-9174-d1ce198837fd','72990ec3-a792-4d47-a247-82ee8ab17b66',1,'What type of gametes are produced by the parent plant with genotype rryy?','The parent plant with genotype rryy can only produce gametes containing ry because it is homozygous recessive for both traits. All gametes will carry the recessive alleles r and y.'),
  ('c6d279e8-fed8-499c-882f-07d22b8f8f3f','f2e03825-4be4-4908-b039-9e5f1e71052b',1,'How did the results of the dihybrid cross compare to separate monohybrid crosses?','These results were identical to those that Mendel got when he made separate monohybrid crosses between yellow and green seeded plants and between round and wrinkled seeded plants. Each trait segregated independently in a 3:1 ratio.'),
  ('d713250c-8b96-4305-8419-007a659b9cb3','a955ccce-6b0f-406b-b8ae-16f7dd10d2a1',1,'In the dihybrid cross, what fraction of F2 plants had green seeds when F1 was self-hybridised?','When Mendel self hybridised the F1 plants he found that 3/4th of F2 plants had yellow seeds and 1/4th had green. Therefore, 1/4 of F2 plants had green seeds.'),
  ('65586c2c-8fb3-4b3b-8c57-3f4033a566f8','cec18a3e-5f03-40c4-8540-7af1ab637492',1,'What fraction of F2 plants had yellow seeds when F1 was self-hybridised?','When Mendel self hybridised the F1 plants he found that 3/4th of F2 plants had yellow seeds. This demonstrates the dominance of yellow over green in a 3:1 phenotypic ratio.'),
  ('4e235188-2231-46de-8212-cd807fc1d6f9','31baa261-4d65-4a41-b5c2-137319f412e1',1,'Which of the following correctly describes the F1 phenotype in Mendel''s dihybrid cross?','Mendel found that the seeds resulting from the crossing of the parents had yellow coloured and round shaped seeds. The F1 phenotype was yellow and round because both yellow and round are dominant traits.'),
  ('1def09d1-e445-4b00-8d12-0449ed8bc4b3','8c4e846e-860b-404e-9e95-993f127fcb8d',1,'What is the term for a cross involving two pairs of contrasting traits?','A dihybrid cross involves two pairs of contrasting traits. In this case, Mendel crossed pea plants differing in seed colour (yellow/green) and seed shape (round/wrinkled), making it a dihybrid cross.'),
  ('a05d3e10-8f6e-43e5-90a4-9f28878589d7','b8924505-ee5b-468f-b8d6-0704157d555d',1,'The genotype RrYy is an example of which type of genotype?','RrYy is heterozygous for both seed colour (Yy) and seed shape (Rr). It contains one dominant and one recessive allele for each trait, making it heterozygous for both traits.'),
  ('9a9b5d0f-a11d-4b6b-b2f7-d4a9ff9b58cb','6d5cb753-0b2c-4da9-966d-b67f11097326',1,'What happens when gametes RY and ry unite during fertilisation?','The gametes RY and ry unite on fertilisation to produce the F1 hybrid RrYy. Each parent contributes one allele for each trait, resulting in a heterozygous genotype.'),
  ('31590aa1-18ac-45b6-80e1-509f2b6bb9ee','6fd87659-27ca-49d7-8928-592b18de6f62',1,'Which trait pair was NOT studied by Mendel in the dihybrid cross described on this page?','On this page, Mendel studied seed colour (yellow/green) and seed shape (round/wrinkled) in the dihybrid cross. Tall and dwarf plant height was studied in monohybrid crosses, not in this particular dihybrid cross described on page 10.'),
  ('bb7a5f43-25e7-4bf8-8728-b9ef11402f64','57174c6e-0389-4f27-b449-04ed29a860ae',1,'What does the capital letter Y represent in the genotypic notation?','Y represents the dominant yellow seed colour allele. By convention, capital letters are used for dominant alleles and lowercase letters for recessive alleles.'),
  ('867ef317-4295-4992-8dca-7583dc8d2cb7','7f6e4ec0-6f81-4bbd-bb11-f4dab0e75805',1,'What does the lowercase letter y represent in the genotypic notation?','y represents the recessive green seed colour allele. Lowercase letters are used for recessive alleles, while capital letters represent dominant alleles in genetic notation.'),
  ('577288af-7c63-4ae3-a0d4-4f99b4491e73','07b8fc7b-7985-48d5-835e-0868a35cc55f',1,'In a dihybrid cross between pea plants with round yellow seeds (RRYY) and wrinkled green seeds (rryy), what is the phenotype of the F1 generation?','When RRYY (round yellow) is crossed with rryy (wrinkled green), the F1 generation will have genotype RrYy. Since round (R) is dominant over wrinkled (r) and yellow (Y) is dominant over green (y), all F1 plants show the dominant phenotype which is round yellow seeds.'),
  ('95b6726f-3d30-4cae-97af-6bc5b1c3b95f','7bab520a-ba48-43fb-9cea-3192efab0f82',1,'What is the phenotypic ratio observed in the F2 generation of a dihybrid cross?','In a dihybrid cross, the F2 generation shows a phenotypic ratio of 9:3:3:1. This means 9/16 round yellow, 3/16 wrinkled yellow, 3/16 round green, and 1/16 wrinkled green. This ratio is derived from combining two monohybrid ratios: (3 round:1 wrinkled) x (3 yellow:1 green) = 9:3:3:1.'),
  ('605a2016-34b6-4a04-bfa6-1b832656e424','dca946dd-98a9-4864-a285-43da3338af9b',1,'According to Mendel''s Law of Independent Assortment, what happens when two pairs of traits are combined in a hybrid?','Mendel''s Law of Independent Assortment states that when two pairs of traits are combined in a hybrid, segregation of one pair of characters is independent of the other pair of characters. This means the inheritance of one trait does not affect the inheritance of another trait.'),
  ('033923bc-9b32-405e-8498-0142b4016425','8fd87bdf-4c85-4ef9-b7e5-1282fbb3aa6d',1,'How many different genotypes of gametes are produced by an F1 plant with genotype RrYy?','An F1 plant with genotype RrYy produces four types of gametes: RY, Ry, rY, and ry. Each gamete has a frequency of 25% or 1/4th of the total gametes produced. This is because the segregation of R/r is independent of the segregation of Y/y.'),
  ('4c3e3e18-d53b-4a6c-9f1e-782d2cd16678','9dfa1777-9ab8-4825-b610-3d9c67964344',1,'Which of the following correctly represents the derivation of the 9:3:3:1 ratio?','The 9:3:3:1 ratio is derived as a combination series of two monohybrid ratios: (3 Round:1 Wrinkled) x (3 Yellow:1 Green) = 9 Round Yellow:3 Wrinkled Yellow:3 Round Green:1 Wrinkled Green. This is a multiplication of probabilities, not addition.'),
  ('d2c531a0-d107-4db5-9293-2acf2004f8c7','a8b82c1e-cbd8-4a59-ba98-395d7d23e643',1,'What percentage of gametes produced by an RrYy plant will carry the allele combination rY?','The four gamete types (RY, Ry, rY, ry) are produced in equal proportions of 25% each. Since 50% of gametes carry r and 50% of those carry Y, the probability of rY is 0.5 x 0.5 = 0.25 or 25%.'),
  ('87ac5841-09a7-4625-9d04-46c5e37d0b90','33ca5608-1a68-4927-85d4-9c850e3e6e5f',1,'In the dihybrid cross, yellow seed color is dominant over green and round shape is dominant over wrinkled. What is the genotype of the parental plant with round yellow seeds?','The parental plant with round yellow seeds is a true-breeding plant, so its genotype is homozygous dominant for both traits: RRYY. True-breeding plants are homozygous and produce offspring identical to themselves when self-pollinated.'),
  ('cea8ba65-4974-4fb5-9ef6-60b7646ae524','9093d25a-2bf8-483f-bd44-54eddc45e51b',1,'Mendel''s work on inheritance was published in 1865 but remained unrecognized until 1900. Which of the following was NOT a reason for this?','Mendel could NOT provide any physical proof for the existence of factors (genes) or say what they were made of. This was one of the reasons his work was not immediately accepted. The other reasons included difficult communication, his mathematical approach being new, and his concept of discrete genes not being accepted.'),
  ('138468b3-cc8e-4725-a612-552b5ae6aa99','56426c84-27c0-4961-9920-afe587346b78',1,'Who independently rediscovered Mendel''s results on inheritance in 1900?','In 1900, three scientists - de Vries, Correns, and von Tschermak - independently rediscovered Mendel''s results on the inheritance of characters. This led to the recognition of Mendel''s work as the foundation of genetics.'),
  ('8196ce69-691b-437d-aec2-692ac0912d93','3c357bfb-f6d8-4719-9fb7-2ab6f2c373b2',1,'What structures in the nucleus were discovered due to advancements in microscopy and appeared to double and divide before cell division?','Due to advancements in microscopy, scientists discovered structures in the nucleus called chromosomes (colored bodies, as they were visualized by staining). These appeared to double and divide just before each cell division.'),
  ('a95b7700-3559-4f46-a596-1f403277d9be','10bf3a09-bcff-4d0d-9b14-6de8363f6bf5',1,'Walter Sutton and Theodore Boveri used what observation to explain Mendel''s laws?','Walter Sutton and Theodore Boveri noted that the behavior of chromosomes was parallel to the behavior of genes and used chromosome movement during meiosis to explain Mendel''s laws. This became known as the Chromosomal Theory of Inheritance.'),
  ('39926b48-a2e1-4904-8033-7e044cb38d04','0d0b6631-dab6-455c-9ad0-fcc08c8eef0c',1,'In the dihybrid cross Punnett square, how many squares are there in total for the F2 generation?','The Punnett square for a dihybrid cross has 16 squares in total. This is because there are 4 types of gametes from each parent (RY, Ry, rY, ry), and 4 x 4 = 16 possible combinations in the F2 generation.'),
  ('d126e095-915e-477a-b626-82ac116b4673','308559ee-32ab-40f6-83cc-79e37a6b9508',1,'What is the genotypic ratio of a monohybrid cross in the F2 generation?','The genotypic ratio of a monohybrid cross in F2 is 1:2:1 (1 TT:2 Tt:1 tt). This is different from the phenotypic ratio of 3:1. The genotypic ratio shows the actual genetic makeup, while the phenotypic ratio shows the observable characteristics.'),
  ('e921bca0-b423-48d6-84b5-f2f960f7de5d','1a6b9d7c-fd52-4bad-8088-0ae0195d6503',1,'If yellow color (Y) is dominant over green (y) and round shape (R) is dominant over wrinkled (r), what is the phenotype of a plant with genotype RrYY?','A plant with genotype RrYY has one dominant allele for round (R) and is homozygous dominant for yellow (YY). Since R is dominant over r, the plant shows round seeds. Since YY is homozygous dominant, the plant shows yellow seeds. Therefore, the phenotype is round yellow.'),
  ('e1eed6b6-9816-4d69-a750-f58eacd41631','342f9257-6184-44bc-991e-33eaf9ce7074',1,'Which statement best describes the relationship between genes and chromosomes according to the Chromosomal Theory of Inheritance?','According to the Chromosomal Theory of Inheritance, genes are located on chromosomes and the behavior of chromosomes is parallel to the behavior of genes. Both occur in pairs, segregate during gamete formation, and independent pairs segregate independently of each other.'),
  ('a811a20e-ae48-4ca4-82eb-be450d66b064','06ddcb77-4bbc-4bbc-b4c9-552437957675',1,'In the context of independent assortment, what is the probability that a gamete from an RrYy plant will contain both dominant alleles (RY)?','The probability of a gamete containing R is 1/2, and the probability of containing Y is 1/2. Since these are independent events, the probability of both occurring together is 1/2 x 1/2 = 1/4.'),
  ('5e149d80-d82f-4463-b95c-2b7625175456','cb86af15-3c0b-4c44-b7a6-fed8f51cb6fa',1,'What is the frequency of each gamete type (RY, Ry, rY, ry) produced by an F1 RrYy plant?','An F1 RrYy plant produces four types of gametes (RY, Ry, rY, ry) each with a frequency of 25% or 1/4th of the total gametes. This equal distribution occurs because segregation of each gene pair is independent and random.'),
  ('3f88e528-c502-493f-9285-a5b68348f89f','2c705163-c0da-42e7-babe-376c542cfd11',1,'The Chromosomal Theory of Inheritance was proposed based on observations of which cellular process?','The Chromosomal Theory of Inheritance was based on observations of chromosome movement during meiosis. Scientists observed that chromosomes segregate and assort independently during meiosis, paralleling Mendel''s laws of segregation and independent assortment.'),
  ('b146f9e7-38c7-484e-9488-ce88f415dff0','ebf1b10d-a4c7-4c6d-b1e3-aee659d83596',1,'In a dihybrid cross, if the two genes are on different chromosomes, what is the expected phenotypic ratio in F2?','When two genes are on different chromosomes (unlinked), they assort independently according to Mendel''s Law of Independent Assortment, producing the classic 9:3:3:1 phenotypic ratio in the F2 generation.'),
  ('2ca7e1db-e7a2-42dd-a906-3774c46d3cd5','9e877c1b-06ac-42d8-81e7-f48f4e6487f0',1,'Which of the following is a key feature that both chromosomes and genes share according to the Chromosomal Theory?','Both chromosomes and genes occur in pairs. This is one of the key parallels that led to the Chromosomal Theory of Inheritance. They also both segregate during gamete formation and independent pairs segregate independently.'),
  ('3ef16ba5-4acc-4745-b996-dfe0578ba441','b1ab38a4-9b10-49ba-8998-60307089ceab',1,'During which stage of meiosis do chromosome pairs align independently at the metaphase plate?','During Anaphase of meiosis I, the two chromosome pairs can align at the metaphase plate independently of each other. This is the stage where independent assortment occurs.'),
  ('c58703a2-d9b3-4c28-bd9a-fd3372430893','3b4f0585-99f6-4350-8956-d6aab3a8e7bb',1,'What is the phenomenon called when chromosome pairs align independently during meiosis I?','The independent alignment of chromosome pairs at the metaphase plate during meiosis I is called independent assortment. This is demonstrated in Figure 4.9 of the textbook.'),
  ('5088cf99-3051-4f31-b733-173694b725fe','38531f92-9f98-4dce-9da8-9c168f85257a',1,'In Possibility I shown in Figure 4.9, which chromosomes segregate together?','In the left column (Possibility I), orange and green chromosomes are segregating together during anaphase I of meiosis.'),
  ('2deb90c7-8f7e-45c6-802b-dd96699075ae','a167879a-b1a5-4b02-915c-961406aca24a',1,'In Possibility II shown in Figure 4.9, which chromosome segregates with the red chromosome?','In the right hand column (Possibility II), the orange chromosome is segregating with the red chromosome during anaphase I.'),
  ('db9ffc57-e332-4200-beb4-89f6864cd7a6','8872d949-bae0-4f4e-a99f-1d320150b1a9',1,'How many different possibilities of chromosome segregation are shown in Figure 4.9?','Figure 4.9 shows two possibilities: Possibility I (left column) and Possibility II (right column), demonstrating independent assortment of chromosomes.'),
  ('5d57c6c1-6a5a-496c-9c55-5eb61c429e51','a937b1a9-6256-48a8-b87e-7e9df88faf11',1,'What does the comparison between left and right columns in Figure 4.9 demonstrate?','The comparison between the left column (Possibility I) and right column (Possibility II) demonstrates independent assortment of chromosomes during meiosis I.'),
  ('8dd06daf-ef5c-4e3f-bc3a-9befce2a92f7','7d1eb5d8-eff7-459d-99aa-b5e835071173',1,'Which chromosomes are mentioned as having four different colours in Figure 4.9?','The text mentions chromosomes of four different colours in the left and right columns, representing two pairs of homologous chromosomes that assort independently.'),
  ('50e590d0-8f66-4504-94c3-97d52bc99de2','81832a6c-4105-4610-bfd6-b1c3a61680b5',1,'What happens to the chromosome pairs during anaphase I of meiosis?','During anaphase I of meiosis, the two chromosome pairs segregate independently of each other, demonstrating the law of independent assortment.'),
  ('ea6f458d-a4ea-475c-92da-0cfb9ab8298c','88542634-0587-4e77-98f0-ff97683d93bc',1,'The independent assortment of chromosomes shown in Figure 4.9 is related to which Mendelian law?','The independent assortment of chromosomes during meiosis I, as shown in Figure 4.9, is the cellular basis of Mendel''s Law of Independent Assortment.'),
  ('4159f77b-335f-437b-83db-f8da0e8b5bb2','1282f5b1-6e8e-4a51-a7ea-a1058ce69c45',1,'In Figure 4.9, what are the two columns labeled as?','The two columns in Figure 4.9 are labeled as Possibility I (left column) and Possibility II (right column), showing different segregation patterns.'),
  ('f8874ccd-0ad6-4937-89a0-28ad990e0b52','593d2f0d-3196-4c01-a9a5-44afb4489b00',1,'What is polygenic inheritance?','Polygenic inheritance refers to traits controlled by three or more genes, where each allele contributes additively to the phenotype. Examples include human height and skin color.'),
  ('143b91b6-4b0c-4ccc-aa3a-925dfd41de6b','01090510-c575-462e-9b73-1e51dea43655',1,'Which of the following is a classic example of polygenic inheritance in humans?','Human skin color is a classic example of polygenic inheritance, controlled by multiple genes (A, B, C) with dominant forms producing dark skin and recessive forms producing light skin.'),
  ('2b2f676e-ab51-42c2-8964-0f42dbe0de54','791e12fc-700e-4ea0-a35b-61fcd726c14c',1,'In polygenic inheritance, how does each allele contribute to the phenotype?','In polygenic inheritance, the effect of each allele is additive. The phenotype reflects the contribution of each allele, creating a gradient of expression rather than distinct categories.'),
  ('e0dec736-c32f-4d18-a11e-fd5f77fe67ed','357f7a0d-0591-43c0-9e5c-db924335c803',1,'What is pleiotropy?','Pleiotropy is when a single gene exhibits multiple phenotypic expressions. The underlying mechanism involves the effect of a gene on metabolic pathways contributing to different phenotypes.'),
  ('3dfc931c-13c1-40c9-8011-480acebbee48','f942f03a-bc07-40f1-8191-0dbaf2e17d86',1,'Which disease is given as an example of pleiotropy in the text?','Phenylketonuria is the example of pleiotropy given in the text. It is caused by mutation in the gene coding for phenylalanine hydroxylase enzyme, manifesting as mental retardation and reduced hair and skin pigmentation.'),
  ('2b9816f0-2411-4102-aedc-a921473a588b','850dd1b1-034d-405a-b36c-5f49aaae692a',1,'Who first observed a specific nuclear structure during spermatogenesis that was later identified as the X chromosome?','Henking (1891) traced a specific nuclear structure during spermatogenesis in insects and named it the X body, which was later identified as the X chromosome.'),
  ('49235b14-b0db-4ada-acfd-2cde19074d22','afa3df1c-4df7-49a8-810d-bf1fdc771349',1,'In XO type sex determination, which sex has only one X chromosome?','In XO type sex determination, males have only one X chromosome besides autosomes (XO), while females have a pair of X chromosomes (XX). Grasshopper is an example.'),
  ('e21a3a20-8d04-4216-b54d-cc63a8c6fa00','086ce79a-0ff3-4230-9f03-9e6ef2e2b902',1,'In XY type sex determination found in humans, what determines the sex of the offspring?','In XY type sex determination, males produce two types of sperm: 50% with X chromosome and 50% with Y chromosome. The sex of the offspring is determined by which sperm fertilizes the egg.'),
  ('1b8861cc-32d5-49aa-b8b9-f4842c034af2','ab90e4b4-707d-459d-847f-a16848478fa2',1,'What is male heterogamety?','Male heterogamety refers to the condition where males produce two different types of gametes: some with X chromosome and some with Y chromosome (or without X in XO type). This is seen in humans and Drosophila.'),
  ('663f3168-c1d1-4bf4-b493-885cbcc563d7','47e35779-db6b-4c2a-8ccf-76b79cea1c76',1,'Which type of sex determination shows female heterogamety?','In birds, female heterogamety is observed where females have ZW chromosomes and males have ZZ chromosomes. This is the opposite of male heterogamety in XY systems.'),
  ('2693ad36-965a-4731-b949-3aa4e66cee52','4a7352a2-6360-4e02-a336-34cfb236cec2',1,'What is the sex determination mechanism in honey bees called?','Honey bees use the haplodiploid sex-determination system where females are diploid (32 chromosomes) and males are haploid (16 chromosomes). Males develop from unfertilized eggs through parthenogenesis.'),
  ('740a4a07-a3f8-4100-9bc2-3e6a98bf2714','b931043b-068d-4827-b1a6-4b617344a567',1,'How many chromosomes do male honey bees have?','Male honey bees (drones) are haploid and have 16 chromosomes, while female honey bees (queen and workers) are diploid with 32 chromosomes.'),
  ('dfc91d7f-af5c-403a-aba0-71cbfacc8bde','e0a3d3e4-22fe-4689-9898-4d91fb14773f',1,'Besides multiple genes, what other factor influences polygenic traits?','Polygenic inheritance takes into account both the involvement of multiple genes and the influence of environment on the phenotype. This creates a range of possible expressions for traits like height and skin color.'),
  ('d4d96be9-4d27-4c3a-b0bc-4dbf55ac903a','8802ac18-434d-4305-ac39-dd11ffd8931c',1,'According to the text, which genotype would result in the darkest skin color?','The genotype with all dominant alleles (AABBCC) will have the darkest skin color, while the genotype with all recessive alleles (aabbcc) will have the lightest skin color in polygenic inheritance.'),
  ('3e2e1465-1469-4d2d-b841-8a733094393c','ecba74de-10a2-4ec9-b289-cd42309a6aef',1,'What is the underlying mechanism of pleiotropy in most cases?','The underlying mechanism of pleiotropy in most cases is the effect of a gene on metabolic pathways which contribute towards different phenotypes. A single gene mutation can affect multiple traits.'),
  ('3662f79f-1986-431f-aa80-3b1e57fe57d9','500527bb-aa2f-4997-89c2-0b3fb53036b7',1,'What are sex chromosomes?','Sex chromosomes are the chromosomes that are different in the two sexes and are involved in sex determination. The rest of the chromosomes are called autosomes.'),
  ('5e1e01db-5f18-409f-9ac2-294ef9a09478','12fa8558-a101-49a7-83cd-4b80025546bc',1,'What are autosomes?','Autosomes are the chromosomes that are identical in both sexes and are not involved in sex determination. In humans, 22 pairs are autosomes and 1 pair is sex chromosomes.'),
  ('5dec2181-2049-49fa-9bc1-3e5dac51fd26','edd95929-060d-44bf-abdf-409ac9a72169',1,'In honey bees, how do male drones develop?','In honey bees, male drones develop from unfertilized eggs through parthenogenesis. This means males have half the number of chromosomes compared to females and do not have a father.'),
  ('3c46ab32-aa88-40f7-919a-2fb78d1d6445','7254b29a-cd71-4226-9e27-51b3b7043ec8',1,'What is unique about male honey bee reproduction?','Male honey bees produce sperm by mitosis (not meiosis) because they are haploid. They do not have a father and thus cannot have sons, but can have grandsons.'),
  ('cb71a23f-b8a3-4419-9cce-afe333103bbc','53017524-23b9-428f-9a56-9c19a9d09142',1,'What phenotype would a genotype with three dominant and three recessive alleles (AaBbCc) show?','In polygenic inheritance, a genotype with three dominant alleles and three recessive alleles (AaBbCc) would show an intermediate skin color, as the effect of each allele is additive.'),
  ('d9b63363-37e7-4566-84ca-24d63f9267fc','4bd40856-624b-4f0f-9b7d-ad8422b6a46c',1,'How many alleles control the ABO blood grouping system in humans?','The ABO blood groups are controlled by the gene I, which has three alleles: IA, IB, and i.'),
  ('f7b30c28-a2a3-4cc1-9128-a0b0fd4a5e56','57a891ab-4af0-41d8-9577-a7372ddc5a97',1,'Which alleles of the ABO blood group system show co-dominance?','When IA and IB are present together, they both express their own types of sugars on the red blood cell surface, demonstrating co-dominance.'),
  ('45947227-fddb-43a7-8f26-b990ef3c89f4','290e350f-23cd-48b1-a938-76382e9ad5eb',1,'What is the genotype of an individual with blood group AB?','Blood group AB individuals have the genotype IAIB, where both IA and IB alleles are co-dominant and express simultaneously.'),
  ('9be52588-efee-4735-ac15-28ba614d2f27','eb635f5b-92f0-4e6f-bb39-bf8a6b09f9d0',1,'What is the blood group phenotype when the genotype is IAi?','IA is completely dominant over i, so when IA and i are present, only IA expresses, resulting in blood group A.'),
  ('f59e5c62-6ae1-4311-b443-fdd195d5649a','f66866b3-6563-4757-a5f6-37d1f3bf60b5',1,'How many different genotypes are possible for the ABO blood group system?','There are six possible genotypes: IAIA, IAIB, IAi, IBIB, IBi, and ii.'),
  ('e70042b4-df5e-48fa-87ef-e0a51ade2f5e','89571759-50c1-4886-8341-4896edb40e1d',1,'Which allele in the ABO system does not produce any sugar on the RBC surface?','The allele i does not produce any sugar on the red blood cell surface, while IA and IB produce slightly different forms of sugar.'),
  ('9d87147c-ad18-4615-ae3a-3260b46c3100','a7a6828e-9663-4608-bab7-d47b01adca70',1,'What is the blood group phenotype when the genotype is ii?','When both alleles are i (ii genotype), no sugar is produced on the RBC surface, resulting in blood group O.'),
  ('9705db7d-90ac-4178-8daf-f4bbd50d58b8','599b8e0c-18ef-4655-8cd6-fe3906754d25',1,'In the ABO system, what is the relationship between IA and i?','IA is completely dominant over i, meaning when both are present, only IA expresses and blood group A is observed.'),
  ('0b7235ef-288f-4ec7-ac69-8ce9eeaa6c89','3ae01926-2fd1-4c44-b77e-1acba7217055',1,'What is the phenomenon called when a single gene has more than two alleles in a population?','Multiple alleles refer to the presence of more than two alleles for a single gene in a population, such as the three alleles (IA, IB, i) for ABO blood groups.'),
  ('c65b0d26-cbdc-4369-88e6-394d37a0b4a7','2aaa41ff-f2ce-439c-85f9-a35ac139023b',1,'Which of the following is an example of co-dominance?','ABO blood grouping is a classic example of co-dominance where both IA and IB alleles express simultaneously when present together.'),
  ('599a59b4-64bf-456a-bac5-99311b775ed9','f983ecb2-7bf1-4020-a727-45be9c9b8836',1,'In pea seeds, starch synthesis is controlled by one gene with two alleles B and b. What is the phenotype of BB homozygotes?','BB homozygotes synthesize starch effectively and produce large starch grains, resulting in round seeds after maturation.'),
  ('5216ed5e-8b2d-4f1b-93bf-54b57c4628be','a7fab26e-c590-4c52-be99-7650898701fb',1,'What is the starch grain size in Bb heterozygotes for pea seed starch synthesis?','Heterozygotes Bb produce starch grains of intermediate size, showing incomplete dominance when considering starch grain size as the phenotype.'),
  ('9cad4114-13fa-4f22-b0e6-9225cc168914','f38a513e-8eb6-4ef9-bdbb-bb2a43be95d3',1,'What is the seed shape of bb homozygotes in pea plants?','bb homozygotes have lesser efficiency in starch synthesis and produce smaller starch grains, resulting in wrinkled seeds after maturation.'),
  ('a2d8f14e-d716-4993-bf70-0075c373be98','cc91023b-c45d-44b3-82a1-5d40137870f9',1,'When considering seed shape, what is the dominance relationship of B over b?','When considering seed shape, B seems to be the dominant allele because heterozygotes Bb produce round seeds similar to BB homozygotes.'),
  ('32b97d8d-6afa-4917-9538-07e523dff891','2e295b56-d3bb-49a1-a471-a47fd4cb0ffe',1,'What is the phenotypic ratio of F2 generation in incomplete dominance of snapdragon flower color?','In incomplete dominance of snapdragon, the F2 phenotypic ratio is 1 red : 2 pink : 1 white, which is the same as the genotypic ratio.'),
  ('9be4d070-8c81-48db-b1ca-57850feb721c','f2b5d1a3-0478-4737-81b7-3f3d18ba428f',1,'What is the genotype of pink flowers in snapdragon incomplete dominance?','In snapdragon incomplete dominance, the heterozygote Rr has a pink phenotype intermediate between red (RR) and white (rr).'),
  ('c5a3651b-8ca0-4d88-b186-24457763a71a','0dd2953f-be93-4d5a-9c2a-4588d30022ce',1,'If a person has blood group B, which of the following could be their genotype?','Blood group B can result from either homozygous IBIB or heterozygous IBi genotype.'),
  ('17962710-1adb-414a-b115-ee4a2af11af6','d46a0172-dcf3-4a1e-a1b2-9eed69b4ea80',1,'What is the phenotypic ratio of F2 generation in a typical monohybrid cross with complete dominance?','In a monohybrid cross with complete dominance, the F2 phenotypic ratio is 3 dominant : 1 recessive.'),
  ('0463cb44-6c39-4a7e-ae20-6f2f64401d38','ea6ebfb1-5f1f-4d06-8bbb-bdaf3976e20a',1,'Which blood group genotypes can produce gametes carrying the i allele?','Genotypes IAi, IBi, and ii can all produce gametes carrying the i allele, while IAIA and IBIB cannot.'),
  ('e2041efc-21ba-4107-920b-0947fd89f302','6cb1099d-8fa9-4e60-a9bb-2b2f025d6d0f',1,'What is the relationship between dominance and the phenotype examined?','Dominance is not an autonomous feature of a gene; it depends on the gene product and the particular phenotype that we choose to examine.'),
  ('76ad457f-a5a4-4e2f-a42a-4542f99b7040','bee46dbe-ee7b-4614-8733-7cbcca0dab17',1,'What percentage of base sequences is identical among all humans?','According to the text, 99.9 percent of nucleotide bases are exactly the same in all humans. Only 0.1% differs, which accounts for individual uniqueness.'),
  ('d428a39b-1347-4ea6-8722-5d35a39c6240','cea873bd-a8c7-41a3-8d3d-99891b857b5d',1,'What makes every individual unique in their phenotypic appearance?','The text states that differences in sequence of DNA make every individual unique in their phenotypic appearance. These variations account for physical and genetic diversity.'),
  ('e76bdd3a-ca67-4047-a307-41a091637f68','5a57107d-5a71-4f51-8c59-e85bf59b4cda',1,'What is DNA fingerprinting primarily used for?','DNA fingerprinting is described as a very quick way to compare the DNA sequences of any two individuals. It helps identify genetic differences without sequencing entire genomes.'),
  ('78d3137e-ded7-4fd1-88c7-3e5f9038d0d4','55b76275-135a-4b48-85d2-0f6aa200a7a4',1,'What are repetitive DNA sequences?','Repetitive DNA refers to specific regions in DNA sequence where a small stretch of DNA is repeated many times. These regions are crucial for DNA fingerprinting analysis.'),
  ('43191c95-0c1a-496c-ab8a-bbdb524d5288','c3ef0ad0-960f-4b8c-8ab9-a0189beef521',1,'How is satellite DNA separated from bulk genomic DNA?','Satellite DNA is separated from bulk genomic DNA as different peaks during density gradient centrifugation. The bulk DNA forms a major peak while satellite DNA appears as smaller peaks.'),
  ('8dbb2216-56ed-43e4-888f-c42326200472','3fe3e794-b883-4431-9073-31d2bcce1cf3',1,'Which of the following is NOT a category of satellite DNA?','The text mentions that satellite DNA is classified into categories such as micro-satellites and mini-satellites based on base composition, length, and number of repetitive units. Macro-satellites are not mentioned.'),
  ('af2dc645-ed50-4363-83f2-feecc251c08f','84a95bc6-ff88-4e9f-a869-ac0fdd9f46e3',1,'Do repetitive DNA sequences code for proteins?','The text explicitly states that these repetitive sequences normally do not code for any proteins. However, they form a large portion of the human genome and show high polymorphism.'),
  ('5aab7e54-25b2-4f94-a403-a69ade07a47e','e8d4dabe-d634-40a4-bcc8-d5683bb5dd6d',1,'What property of repetitive DNA sequences makes them useful for DNA fingerprinting?','The text states that these sequences show high degree of polymorphism and form the basis of DNA fingerprinting. This variation allows identification of individuals.'),
  ('f6c5a522-bc9e-41f7-9b32-b35a1956b82e','710eab15-6731-4cd9-a691-b5bdd6c0ed11',1,'Which tissues from an individual can be used for DNA fingerprinting?','The text lists multiple tissues: blood, hair-follicle, skin, bone, saliva, and sperm. DNA from every tissue shows the same degree of polymorphism, making them useful identification tools.'),
  ('695d8244-d51f-42e3-96a7-8a08a3917f84','376dc46b-cc57-4dcb-99a7-10946bf2b632',1,'What causes DNA polymorphism?','The text states that polymorphism arises due to mutations. New mutations may arise in somatic cells or germ cells, and if transmitted to offspring, they can spread in the population.'),
  ('0347ddff-d9b7-4ded-b045-81a017f756ea','7a606d12-0dd0-4ec2-947f-7c2df15f6fd5',1,'What is the minimum frequency of a variant allele to be considered a DNA polymorphism?','The text defines DNA polymorphism as an allelic sequence variation where more than one variant at a locus occurs in human population with a frequency greater than 0.01.'),
  ('72d3bd7d-037f-4388-bff4-a8fff4792666','8fa8febb-4180-4d9c-b37c-d5ae31fe104b',1,'Why is the probability of observing polymorphism higher in noncoding DNA sequences?','The text explains that mutations in noncoding DNA sequences may not have any immediate effect on an individual''s reproductive ability, so these mutations accumulate and form variability.'),
  ('1c26af20-47f1-4de5-b090-814cd5adc50c','0a8967c3-1e9b-4273-80e0-46383f11f5ca',1,'What role do polymorphisms play in evolution and speciation?','The text explicitly states that for evolution and speciation, such polymorphisms play a very important role. Genetic variation is the raw material for natural selection and adaptation.'),
  ('7329b253-152c-41f9-9990-b9184669b4fe','ce1c9746-06b4-48fe-a39a-d5abf137d04f',1,'Where can new mutations arise in an individual?','The text states that new mutations may arise in an individual either in somatic cells or in germ cells. Only germ cell mutations can be transmitted to offspring through sexual reproduction.'),
  ('89f223d2-e855-4ece-b526-142ffe66ea2c','d6250bbf-d329-460e-ad9b-d13f022cbe1d',1,'What happens if a germ cell mutation does not impair reproductive ability?','The text explains that if a germ cell mutation does not seriously impair the individual''s ability to have offspring who can transmit the mutation, it can spread to other members of the population through sexual reproduction.'),
  ('3cabffc8-5329-4177-af71-fc6672bce541','6074a404-6e20-458a-9ce5-ca5af6d6cc1c',1,'What is the range of polymorphism types mentioned in the text?','The text mentions a variety of different types of polymorphisms ranging from single nucleotide change to very large scale changes. This spectrum of variation contributes to genetic diversity.'),
  ('576ba8a4-c56b-4b5f-bed0-be8557221748','1575d45c-17c8-4d5c-b77e-02bba8427bf1',1,'According to the RNA World hypothesis, which molecule was the first genetic material?','RNA was the first genetic material. There is now enough evidence to suggest that essential life processes such as metabolism, translation, and splicing evolved around RNA.'),
  ('29fd0cdd-4a31-4a92-9b4b-7998a7502087','d34eb862-9dea-46f8-80e0-7ba07b436645',1,'What dual role did RNA play in early life according to the RNA World concept?','RNA used to act as a genetic material as well as a catalyst. There are some important biochemical reactions in living systems that are catalyzed by RNA catalysts and not by protein enzymes.'),
  ('33b55153-8ade-4bf8-bee0-c5739eb9686d','28612340-298f-4c51-a8e1-9de1d5faeca1',1,'Why did DNA evolve from RNA according to the text?','RNA being a catalyst was reactive and hence unstable. Therefore, DNA has evolved from RNA with chemical modifications that make it more stable.'),
  ('2883ebe1-0692-4bdc-9fed-381c07e81f84','d7bc553e-7652-4e00-ab39-d8cec0e9194b',1,'What feature of DNA makes it more resistant to changes compared to RNA?','DNA being double stranded and having complementary strand further resists changes by evolving a process of repair.'),
  ('f2aa9573-b091-4948-8f57-27155f03da98','d31de93b-da08-4b5f-822f-110b3da6b9a9',1,'What scheme did Watson and Crick propose for DNA replication?','Watson and Crick proposed a scheme where the two strands would separate and act as a template for synthesis of new complementary strands. After replication, each DNA molecule would have one parental and one newly synthesized strand. This was termed semiconservative DNA replication.'),
  ('2edbf4b3-ec03-45db-a5b0-e61a33708dcc','4021ae3a-6fba-4c61-8aa6-d180f34237bd',1,'In the Meselson-Stahl experiment, what isotope was used to label DNA?','They grew E. coli in a medium containing 15NH4Cl (15N is the heavy isotope of nitrogen) as the only nitrogen source. This heavy DNA molecule could be distinguished from normal DNA by centrifugation in a cesium chloride density gradient.'),
  ('5f88ceb5-2f60-47a8-86cf-cbf69c7d7686','9916d032-da60-4dec-bb74-7c43795032a3',1,'What was the density of DNA after one generation in the Meselson-Stahl experiment?','The DNA extracted from the culture one generation after transfer from 15N to 14N medium had a hybrid or intermediate density.'),
  ('dbb58675-41b2-4697-b3c0-d7147a73c0ae','b8d71d75-da53-4d4f-ab97-7bef80d6093a',1,'After two generations in the Meselson-Stahl experiment, what was the composition of DNA?','DNA extracted after another generation was composed of equal amounts of hybrid DNA and light DNA.'),
  ('768cbdd7-8256-4a69-a6f8-cb39a61d82a3','c17066fb-4207-49f8-9972-597c13113763',1,'Which organism was used by Taylor and colleagues to prove semiconservative replication in chromosomes?','Very similar experiments involving use of radioactive thymidine to detect distribution of newly synthesized DNA in chromosomes was performed on Vicia faba (faba beans) by Taylor and colleagues in 1958.'),
  ('ceb3aaf5-78ce-4364-9a6b-e17d5d160c7e','a8879080-7b56-46a5-9dfe-29b065fc1f69',1,'What method was used to separate DNA molecules based on density in the Meselson-Stahl experiment?','The heavy DNA molecule could be distinguished from the normal DNA by centrifugation in a cesium chloride (CsCl) density gradient.'),
  ('0fc111de-b038-4538-a025-3e5465a52567','f219adbf-8d06-4324-b14d-35a611ac63ba',1,'Which enzyme is primarily responsible for catalyzing DNA replication?','The main enzyme is referred to as DNA-dependent DNA polymerase, since it uses a DNA template to catalyze the polymerization of deoxynucleotides.'),
  ('c43db477-3433-4227-9efd-42b85ebdd2f4','ef436f84-63d0-4ab0-88de-a446946bf031',1,'In which direction does DNA polymerase catalyze polymerization?','The DNA-dependent DNA polymerases catalyze polymerization only in one direction, that is 5'' to 3''.'),
  ('539fa420-6f45-4117-b9a5-5d1b8a256948','e760e63d-eeba-4fda-ac30-2fdd2efe4cdd',1,'What are the fragments called that are synthesized discontinuously during DNA replication?','The discontinuously synthesized fragments are later joined by the enzyme DNA ligase. These are known as Okazaki fragments.'),
  ('037ec123-b05a-4bbf-8e70-1ebdd11cb47d','181210cc-434a-4508-b2fe-cbd69621ff84',1,'What is the region in DNA where replication originates called?','There is a definite region in E. coli DNA where the replication originates. Such regions are termed as origin of replication.'),
  ('18054380-0fff-4015-afdd-a48a366621ca','fa4cb004-ffae-4a0f-ac82-7bebb4bf9e1f',1,'During which phase of the cell cycle does DNA replication occur in eukaryotes?','In eukaryotes, the replication of DNA takes place at S-phase of the cell-cycle.'),
  ('c46a1157-97a8-4cda-8c65-43818f036931','2f4b9e8e-2a67-49ca-8c06-dcf30a92c0f7',1,'How many codons code for amino acids according to the genetic code?','Out of 64 possible codons (4^3), 61 codons code for amino acids while 3 codons function as stop codons.'),
  ('101a3a23-d7a0-4733-a589-3b076a843f95','1b4af386-b621-41a0-ad95-d50ee3ec3df8',1,'Which codons function as stop codons in the genetic code?','UAA, UAG, and UGA are the three stop or terminator codons that do not code for any amino acid.'),
  ('016efcf8-96f2-4e17-adb6-fee987777087','7850d281-7ad1-4113-a820-646d7c7d2637',1,'What does the term ''degenerate'' mean in the context of genetic code?','The genetic code is degenerate because some amino acids are coded by more than one codon. For example, multiple codons can code for the same amino acid.'),
  ('a279bbe8-a501-47ee-953b-1f86532132c1','89c74c1d-b761-4279-8485-38353c8e244c',1,'Which property of genetic code states that the code is read without any punctuation?','The codon is read in mRNA in a contiguous fashion, meaning there are no punctuations between codons. The reading frame continues without interruption.')
INSERT INTO question_translations (id, question_id, lang_id, question_text, explanation) VALUES
  ('942235aa-c2a6-4b14-b10e-819b08b10058','9cfe63a9-8893-4550-b6e6-bc37dee9ded6',1,'What is the dual function of the AUG codon?','AUG has dual functions: it codes for Methionine (met) and it also acts as the initiator codon that signals the start of protein synthesis.'),
  ('d7bdbccb-02e1-4579-8e53-66d575e75382','113bbe93-8659-4adc-a09e-b2c2e9030c2f',1,'The genetic code is described as nearly universal. What does this mean?','The genetic code is nearly universal, meaning the same codon codes for the same amino acid across most organisms. For example, UUU codes for Phenylalanine from bacteria to humans. Some exceptions exist in mitochondrial codons and some protozoans.'),
  ('42c8315c-bed8-4d93-ad43-2e28c6a27311','0c7bf4e3-a1e9-4be5-aaa5-99035069d470',1,'For the mRNA sequence AUG UUU UUC UUC UUU UUU UUC, what is the first amino acid in the polypeptide chain?','AUG is the initiator codon that codes for Methionine. Therefore, the first amino acid in the polypeptide chain would be Methionine.'),
  ('19865510-92bc-4293-9ea1-d9fc2712a1fc','8e5e4aaa-d229-4b0d-bcbc-012a0c238f05',1,'Which amino acid is coded by the codon UUU?','UUU codes for Phenylalanine (Phe). This is a standard example used to illustrate the universality of the genetic code.'),
  ('ecfc8e7b-d7ef-4be8-92b4-845a450133ea','05a03d4b-9dea-4bb2-8e1e-3f333993477a',1,'Why is it difficult to predict the mRNA sequence from a given amino acid sequence?','Due to the degeneracy of the genetic code, multiple codons can code for the same amino acid. Therefore, knowing the amino acid sequence does not uniquely determine the mRNA sequence.'),
  ('38d9c6c5-e894-464e-98da-c8efa9334134','77caa93d-c343-46fb-a987-ec7fdb23bb91',1,'What is the total number of possible codons in the genetic code?','Since there are 4 bases and each codon consists of 3 nucleotides, the total number of possible codons is 4^3 = 64.'),
  ('c4cf4215-31a7-4828-9a94-95624fa38495','253a58ba-eac0-4485-b4ec-aad73313da6d',1,'Which of the following is NOT a property of the genetic code?','The genetic code is read in a contiguous fashion without any punctuations. The other options (triplet, degenerate, nearly universal) are all established properties of the genetic code.'),
  ('8973cb8a-64b2-4699-8ae2-537312998074','2e3d7da9-e48b-4e63-90ec-202bd4467636',1,'In the mRNA sequence AUG UUU UUC UUC UUU UUU UUC, how many times does Phenylalanine appear?','After the initiator AUG (Methionine), the sequence contains UUU, UUC, UUC, UUU, UUU, UUC. Both UUU and UUC code for Phenylalanine, so Phenylalanine appears 6 times in total.'),
  ('8cfdb49f-b593-488c-b022-d5a1e48d43c4','4375869d-e0ec-4b34-917e-99ad13913e4f',1,'Where are exceptions to the universal genetic code commonly found?','Exceptions to the universal genetic code have been found in mitochondrial codons and in some protozoans. These are specific cases where the standard code is slightly modified.'),
  ('9d462879-ebe2-4a35-a5b2-cd723260a792','b4b62f13-a835-44fe-b939-4df0409732f0',1,'What does the term ''triplet'' mean regarding the genetic code?','The genetic code is triplet, meaning each codon consists of three nucleotides. This was proposed by George Gamow, who calculated that 4^3 = 64 codons would be sufficient to code for 20 amino acids.'),
  ('d74ad447-facf-446b-89f8-2c2ea1581c41','337efd61-e82f-4762-acdb-597d76a66c76',1,'For the amino acid sequence Met-Phe-Phe-Phe-Phe-Phe-Phe, which codon must definitely be present?','AUG is the only codon that codes for Methionine and also serves as the initiator codon. Since the sequence starts with Methionine, AUG must be present. UUU and UUC both code for Phenylalanine, so either or both could be used.'),
  ('80d4a3be-e712-43f0-b975-2f531f34f82a','12349854-1bc3-4353-9f21-4f1f12f8a2c2',1,'Which two properties of the genetic code are demonstrated when predicting an amino acid sequence from mRNA versus predicting mRNA from an amino acid sequence?','Predicting amino acids from mRNA is straightforward due to universality, but predicting mRNA from amino acids is difficult due to degeneracy (multiple codons for same amino acid). These two properties are highlighted in this exercise.'),
  ('ba9b5fa9-a725-4df8-ad07-e159cc797bec','ef7560aa-7552-47c1-8d7f-0463565a6ebe',1,'How many stop codons are present in the genetic code?','There are 3 stop codons (UAA, UAG, UGA) that do not code for any amino acid. These signal the termination of protein synthesis.'),
  ('7b9cad69-94d9-41a4-a58d-c7980c6422a5','3f58c58a-dc3c-4ae6-be5a-5b3a9e0a958b',1,'What is the minimum number of nucleotides required to code for one amino acid?','The genetic code is a triplet code, meaning a minimum of 3 nucleotides (one codon) is required to code for one amino acid. With only 4 bases, 2 nucleotides would only give 16 combinations (4^2), which is insufficient for 20 amino acids.'),
  ('6973d6db-62a5-4d44-82a2-99823ec94214','17cfa578-2667-407e-9a7d-c82fcf3104cc',1,'Which scientist proposed that the genetic code should be made up of three nucleotides to code for 20 amino acids?','George Gamow, a physicist, proposed that the genetic code should be a triplet code made up of three nucleotides, as 4^3 = 64 codons would be sufficient to code for 20 amino acids.'),
  ('1aa4fa4a-767e-45ab-8951-5f221051d67c','e78b24a3-01d8-40ad-a7d1-52352d8693f3',1,'If a mutation changes a single nucleotide in a codon but the same amino acid is still produced, what property of the genetic code is being demonstrated?','This demonstrates degeneracy, where multiple codons can code for the same amino acid. A mutation in the third position of a codon often does not change the amino acid due to this redundancy.'),
  ('d3432aaf-d531-482a-b7b6-7b24e71c7e15','218e53a2-65cc-47ed-bf50-512f2bd98c3d',1,'What role did Francis Crick propose for tRNA before the genetic code was deciphered?','Francis Crick postulated that an adapter molecule must exist to read the genetic code and link it to specific amino acids, since amino acids lack structural features to directly read the code. This adapter function was later assigned to tRNA.'),
  ('073bb3ef-e78c-46b2-bce3-c733ac362de7','967cc456-b088-49a6-900a-1ef765f8979b',1,'Which part of tRNA contains bases complementary to the mRNA codon?','The anticodon loop of tRNA contains three bases that are complementary to the codon on mRNA. This complementarity allows tRNA to correctly pair with the appropriate codon during translation.'),
  ('c1924c30-3a5f-4d2d-887e-af2fbac850d1','54829581-c9d6-430e-a40b-bf24287b1b5c',1,'Where on the tRNA molecule is the amino acid attached?','The amino acid is attached to the amino acid acceptor end of tRNA, which is located at the 3'' end. This end has the sequence CCA where the amino acid binds.'),
  ('8a0b586a-4179-412f-a537-3e15de33d7de','292c3592-af85-4900-862c-87d827278d67',1,'Are tRNAs specific for each amino acid?','tRNAs are specific for each amino acid. Each type of tRNA can only carry one specific amino acid, ensuring accuracy during protein synthesis.'),
  ('9271560a-b326-460d-917f-f504b4acd2d1','9b28a55b-a4ad-4161-9f76-08d87c43c7e9',1,'What is special about the initiator tRNA?','The initiator tRNA is a specific tRNA that is used to start the translation process. It carries methionine (or formylmethionine in bacteria) and recognizes the start codon AUG.'),
  ('34b86551-e137-4db6-8c41-09ef26616c49','a1155d58-3297-4635-a5f1-c835a7cad438',1,'Which statement about stop codons is correct?','There are no tRNAs for stop codons (UAA, UAG, UGA). Stop codons are recognized by release factors, not tRNAs, which signal the termination of translation.'),
  ('2ef81ff5-edf4-4282-b30e-c25878928977','f3328f9a-aaea-46a1-9d61-d3b7875b7607',1,'What does the secondary structure of tRNA resemble?','The secondary structure of tRNA is depicted as a clover-leaf shape with four main arms: the acceptor arm, D-arm, anticodon arm, and T-arm.'),
  ('d51c5ffe-c6b0-4e0a-acf5-517f29c2fd40','000367b5-ac3e-46e2-b323-19c7a240b89c',1,'What is the actual three-dimensional structure of tRNA described as?','In its actual three-dimensional structure, tRNA is a compact molecule that looks like an inverted L. This structure allows it to fit into the ribosome during translation.'),
  ('c824f485-6594-4abf-aba3-9697b16c5d13','310abe1e-66ed-458e-a828-f19932b30b3c',1,'What is translation in molecular biology?','Translation refers to the process of polymerization of amino acids to form a polypeptide. The sequence of amino acids is determined by the sequence of bases in mRNA.'),
  ('09a6c5ef-eeb2-4a70-8dad-9adb108337af','60f92b08-d094-4239-817c-de6496ad511e',1,'What type of bond joins amino acids during translation?','Amino acids are joined by a peptide bond during translation. This bond forms between the carboxyl group of one amino acid and the amino group of another, with the release of water.'),
  ('18a5b9ed-d14a-4588-872f-1d87b6b2f7eb','243c787d-6a9e-4d07-b07e-a6f20f85a0d0',1,'What provides energy for peptide bond formation during translation?','Formation of a peptide bond requires energy. In the first phase, amino acids are activated in the presence of ATP and linked to their cognate tRNA in a process called charging or aminoacylation.'),
  ('8bcbaaa8-0f39-4484-8e0c-35b38c1ca9dd','1f1f3ebc-c4e8-4a99-99da-9e15f1a3a151',1,'What is the process called when amino acids are linked to tRNA?','The process of linking amino acids to their specific tRNAs is called charging of tRNA or aminoacylation of tRNA. This requires ATP and specific aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase enzymes.'),
  ('94388177-67dd-4d0a-89be-c1f8dd5ea81f','8cbc1ba8-2fde-4999-9be3-b5565329e69a',1,'Why must gene expression be tightly regulated?','Transcription and translation are energetically very expensive processes. Therefore, these processes must be tightly regulated to conserve cellular energy and produce proteins only when needed.'),
  ('55e710ec-85b9-4bee-b7db-0ae581dd12f3','ca57f4f6-faff-481c-82bd-37eebc77d632',1,'What is the function of beta-galactosidase in E. coli?','Beta-galactosidase is an enzyme synthesized by E. coli that catalyzes the hydrolysis of the disaccharide lactose into its monomeric units, galactose and glucose, which the bacteria use as an energy source.'),
  ('ba3eb243-6647-4edf-8e90-7295beb7d367','5c8e3452-be39-4b28-a6e3-522739e32634',1,'When would E. coli no longer require synthesis of beta-galactosidase?','If the bacteria do not have lactose around them to be utilized for energy source, they would no longer require the synthesis of the enzyme beta-galactosidase. This is because the enzyme is specifically needed to break down lactose.'),
  ('8fe94d62-4bb9-4b4a-88bf-7259c3d39907','fb43ce8f-1e11-4062-814e-8c7d0aba7b6e',1,'In prokaryotes, what is the predominant site for control of gene expression?','In prokaryotes, control of the rate of transcriptional initiation is the predominant site for control of gene expression. This is where RNA polymerase activity is regulated by accessory proteins.'),
  ('6d15a943-165a-44bd-bbab-98aa029df5f2','474a03de-6a96-409f-9509-efacab1a421d',1,'How do regulatory proteins affect RNA polymerase in prokaryotes?','Regulatory proteins can act both positively (activators) and negatively (repressors) by affecting the ability of RNA polymerase to recognize start sites. This controls whether transcription begins or not.'),
  ('61f317f7-3941-4589-9b1c-71e4f14cd436','e248ecfd-3389-4103-b749-e215b9f3b52a',1,'What is the operator region in prokaryotic gene regulation?','The operator region is adjacent to the promoter elements in most operons. In most cases, the sequences of the operator bind a repressor protein, preventing RNA polymerase from transcribing the operon.'),
  ('271e7da3-b69f-4199-8de2-3628b3b496ce','65677b96-a875-4032-88a8-71855b380ef3',1,'Which statement about operon specificity is correct?','Each operon has its specific operator and specific repressor. For example, the lac operator is present only in the lac operon and it interacts specifically with lac repressor only.'),
  ('aa918284-0aa7-4225-ab22-88c283c93845','316400c9-a40d-4eb2-bf29-ede1cc21068b',1,'Who elucidated the lac operon?','The lac operon was elucidated by Francois Jacob, a geneticist, and Jacques Monod, a biochemist. They were the first to elucidate a transcriptionally regulated system.'),
  ('e95dc2b0-20c8-4e49-bd14-3b813de81da8','ce6069c0-2786-4bad-8766-e8763c72b677',1,'What does ''lac'' in lac operon refer to?','In lac operon, ''lac'' refers to lactose. The lac operon codes for genes responsible for metabolism of lactose in bacteria.'),
  ('cc973db6-329b-4970-bcb8-a080c72f1e26','7fd5a77b-e0fb-4b17-b38d-3231ec4c8c82',1,'What is an operon?','An operon is a polycistronic structural gene regulated by a common promoter and regulatory genes. This arrangement is very common in bacteria.'),
  ('fd871ce7-9aa2-42b6-b383-8f47cd6a3290','18318a5c-82b4-4fba-a271-2cc7a9b01e03',1,'How many structural genes are in the lac operon?','The lac operon consists of one regulatory gene (i gene) and three structural genes (z, y, and a). The z gene codes for beta-galactosidase, y for permease, and a for transacetylase.'),
  ('3226c940-75e7-40a5-9463-34033ecb6b47','658acfd4-5e2c-41cb-ad02-ce6a62602590',1,'What does the i gene in the lac operon code for?','The i gene codes for the repressor of the lac operon. This repressor protein binds to the operator region and prevents transcription of the structural genes.'),
  ('ba69e4ec-bd53-4a26-a190-fbc70cb7785f','9d38bef1-f8b6-415d-9dcc-097ba7058b34',1,'What enzyme is coded by the z gene in the lac operon?','The z gene codes for beta-galactosidase, which is primarily responsible for the hydrolysis of lactose into galactose and glucose.'),
  ('f0c8bc5d-8959-4304-bc87-9e45bc30d8c5','789a6f9e-085f-443f-aa05-0a0ff150550e',1,'What is the function of the protein coded by the y gene in the lac operon?','The y gene codes for permease, which increases the permeability of the cell to beta-galactosides, allowing lactose to enter the cell.'),
  ('b10fef25-3711-427b-a72e-4df3e73a2b3b','d769d8be-f1c5-40d6-9c58-d7bb6a957321',1,'What enzyme is coded by the a gene in the lac operon?','The a gene encodes transacetylase. All three gene products (beta-galactosidase, permease, and transacetylase) are required for metabolism of lactose.'),
  ('8b047166-cbda-4214-bc74-f2ab19f3b8b4','6cf4d2d3-5381-46db-b9f3-36ab733ecf5a',1,'What is the inducer in the lac operon?','Lactose is the substrate for beta-galactosidase and it regulates switching on and off of the operon. Hence, it is termed as the inducer.'),
  ('3d41125d-1f70-4598-821c-c226d0588a23','5abae462-d9c2-4c1e-ac18-a43b28332965',1,'Can glucose or galactose act as inducers for the lac operon?','Glucose or galactose cannot act as inducers for the lac operon. Only lactose (or its isomer allolactose) can act as the inducer.'),
  ('f4a76894-3369-4152-a068-04250c98689c','32b1c05c-c2cb-4254-9622-ad6f2ab9cbb6',1,'What is the regulation of lac operon by repressor called?','Regulation of lac operon by repressor is referred to as negative regulation because the repressor prevents transcription by binding to the operator.'),
  ('0e76b383-766b-471f-88dd-8e28c282cd25','6849b165-a30e-41e1-8fe9-d6e2226b0559',1,'What type of mutation is caused by a change of a single base pair in the gene for beta globin chain?','A point mutation is a change in a single base pair of DNA. In the case of sickle cell anemia, a single base pair change in the beta globin gene results in the substitution of amino acid glutamate with valine.'),
  ('b34f94f1-ed75-46ab-a12a-286dc8caadbc','2498fe64-a668-4c65-a3a4-a164e6140500',1,'Which amino acid is replaced in sickle cell anemia due to the point mutation in the beta globin gene?','The point mutation in the beta globin gene changes the amino acid glutamate to valine, resulting in sickle cell anemia. This is a classical example of how a single base change can alter protein function and cause disease.'),
  ('4d58d65a-d932-4f1f-8ca9-e203cc222799','49f3dbf6-1931-4e93-baac-91ffdc867872',1,'What disease is caused by the point mutation that changes glutamate to valine in the beta globin chain?','Sickle cell anemia is caused by a point mutation in the beta globin gene where glutamate is replaced by valine. This changes the shape of red blood cells from normal biconcave discs to sickle-shaped cells, causing various health complications.'),
  ('8238fd0b-6590-401d-b4b2-6f5135f515a1','2f9f49a9-c80c-4f56-9edc-50582c85060a',1,'What happens to the reading frame when one or two bases are inserted or deleted in a structural gene?','Insertion or deletion of one or two bases changes the reading frame from the point of insertion or deletion. These are called frameshift mutations because they shift the triplet reading frame, altering all subsequent amino acids in the protein sequence.'),
  ('0982b816-1f35-44b2-ad46-a869b35dffee','6c476285-12f9-4193-85f9-5c3893d21610',1,'What is the term for mutations caused by insertion or deletion of one or two bases?','Frameshift insertion or deletion mutations occur when one or two bases are inserted or deleted. This changes the reading frame from the point of mutation, causing completely different amino acids to be incorporated into the protein from that point onward.'),
  ('5699beb5-0cc3-48f4-9c63-fe42951e3b11','386e4b7f-4b6c-4cea-a751-519895428b8a',1,'What happens when three or multiple of three bases are inserted or deleted in a gene?','Insertion or deletion of three or its multiple bases inserts or deletes one or multiple codons, hence one or multiple amino acids, but the reading frame remains unaltered from that point onwards. This is less disruptive than frameshift mutations.'),
  ('6313169e-e154-4960-ae4c-a54802ce24e7','7bc0dfb4-da4c-4e12-ade2-509e87773915',1,'In the textbook example, what is the original statement used to demonstrate reading frames?','The textbook uses the statement ''RAM HAS RED CAP'' where each word has three letters, similar to codons having three bases. This analogy helps students understand how insertion or deletion of letters (bases) changes the reading frame and produces completely different words (amino acids).'),
  ('26709044-f19c-49f8-a605-4c67d2d64b04','1c42260b-4b4c-4e69-a497-6a56119ad265',1,'If the letter ''B'' is inserted between ''HAS'' and ''RED'' in ''RAM HAS RED CAP'', what does the statement become?','When ''B'' is inserted between ''HAS'' and ''RED'', the reading frame shifts. The statement becomes ''RAM HAS BRE DCA P'' where the three-letter word pattern is disrupted, producing meaningless words. This demonstrates how a single base insertion causes frameshift mutation.'),
  ('57f3868a-7236-4cfc-80f7-8ee737e00ac5','2e008a40-057c-4787-9b08-d046b18e8c5c',1,'If two letters ''BI'' are inserted between ''HAS'' and ''RED'' in ''RAM HAS RED CAP'', what is the result?','Inserting two letters ''BI'' between ''HAS'' and ''RED'' changes the reading frame to ''RAM HAS BIR EDC AP''. This demonstrates that insertion of two bases (not divisible by 3) also causes frameshift mutation, altering all subsequent codons.'),
  ('155b52d3-722c-4921-beca-d5c11c1c0ae7','3abba67c-c7b0-46c7-8866-c08302f889c7',1,'If three letters ''BIG'' are inserted between ''HAS'' and ''RED'' in ''RAM HAS RED CAP'', what is the outcome?','When three letters (a multiple of 3) are inserted, the reading frame remains unchanged. The statement becomes ''RAM HAS BIG RED CAP'' which still makes sense with the original three-letter word pattern intact. This demonstrates that insertion of 3 or multiple of 3 bases adds amino acids without changing the reading frame.'),
  ('b191ab5b-9e77-4f60-ab19-2ffe5772da2c','8b6bf0d7-439c-42c2-b076-8aa96a69ee43',1,'What is the effect of deleting one letter ''R'' from ''RAM HAS RED CAP''?','Deleting one letter (one base) from the sequence changes the reading frame from the point of deletion. Using the analogy, if ''R'' is deleted from ''RED'', the subsequent letters regroup into different three-letter combinations, demonstrating frameshift deletion mutation.'),
  ('03470b6a-1c63-4a1d-95b3-e64031ffc344','61d1e63b-8f4d-4e01-853a-062cc2cada92',1,'Which of the following best describes why the ''RAM HAS RED CAP'' analogy is used in genetics?','The ''RAM HAS RED CAP'' analogy is used to illustrate frameshift mutations. Each three-letter word represents a codon (three bases). Inserting or deleting letters that are not multiples of 3 shifts the reading frame, just as inserting or deleting bases shifts the codon reading frame during protein synthesis.'),
  ('52a67af5-09a9-478e-b7ba-a0be848732e4','7479da0f-3d03-4b36-b551-fd0fcecba1f4',1,'What is the key difference between point mutations and frameshift mutations?','Point mutations change a single base pair, usually affecting only one amino acid (or sometimes none if silent). Frameshift mutations, caused by insertion or deletion of bases not divisible by 3, shift the entire reading frame, altering all amino acids from the mutation point onward, typically causing more severe effects.'),
  ('ef31a0a1-c68e-4cd1-8feb-2082658461f4','249636e8-826b-43ec-addc-a905a23768fa',1,'In the context of genetic mutations, what does ''reading frame'' refer to?','The reading frame refers to the way the sequence of bases in mRNA is grouped into consecutive non-overlapping triplets called codons. Each codon specifies one amino acid. A frameshift mutation disrupts this grouping, causing completely different amino acids to be read from the mutation point onward.'),
  ('27ea2822-b237-4248-873a-2c7338d71e0a','d47b1082-c84c-4af4-8e8a-92287cb27b29',1,'Why are frameshift mutations generally more harmful than point mutations?','Frameshift mutations are more harmful because shifting the reading frame changes every subsequent codon, producing a completely different protein sequence from the mutation point. They also often create premature stop codons, truncating the protein. Point mutations usually affect only one amino acid.'),
  ('05e4ed76-75f5-411a-9377-89d1f22b317b','54046998-ad3d-4aa5-b632-005917169da0',1,'If a deletion removes exactly three bases from the middle of a gene, what is the expected outcome?','Deletion of exactly three bases (one codon) removes exactly one amino acid from the protein product. Since 3 is divisible by 3, the reading frame remains preserved. All other codons downstream are read correctly, though one amino acid is missing. This is less disruptive than frameshift mutations.'),
  ('bade6b80-1e5a-4018-ad06-cb63f964f8e1','28b553c0-c785-4312-af44-b8def316dbf4',1,'What type of mutation occurs when a single base change results in a different amino acid being incorporated?','A missense mutation is a point mutation where a single base change results in a different amino acid being incorporated into the protein. The sickle cell anemia mutation (glutamate to valine) is a classic example of a missense mutation.'),
  ('a93f8030-1e4d-449f-8a8a-023a15f02f50','6437b41c-04a8-4784-9e0c-cd7c2d9fd221',1,'Which experimental method was instrumental in synthesizing RNA molecules with defined combinations of bases for genetic code studies?','The chemical method developed by Har Gobind Khorana was instrumental in synthesizing RNA molecules with defined combinations of bases (homopolymers and copolymers). This was crucial for deciphering the genetic code and understanding how codons specify amino acids.'),
  ('6fc3f428-7f4c-4af3-bf48-7c331620fab9','f1a2df53-e6b2-4bad-b76d-c56d65a1c080',1,'What process requires transfer of genetic information from a polymer of nucleotides to synthesise a polymer of amino acids?','Translation is the process that requires transfer of genetic information from a polymer of nucleotides (mRNA) to synthesise a polymer of amino acids (proteins).'),
  ('be7c3035-a77b-4f3b-83e8-fde1c15e3279','63760eb2-8795-4443-8ffb-8f63f5d9cf45',1,'Does any complementarity exist between nucleotides and amino acids?','No complementarity exists between nucleotides and amino acids, nor could any be drawn theoretically. This is why a genetic code was proposed.'),
  ('375a5ddb-c2fb-4123-bfda-062cdad5a4cc','43a81a0b-4ee1-41ee-ab81-24f20b333aa3',1,'Who argued that the genetic code should be made up of three nucleotides to code for 20 amino acids?','George Gamow, a physicist, argued that since there are only 4 bases and if they have to code for 20 amino acids, the code should constitute a combination of three nucleotides.'),
  ('4d3dfd99-b413-4636-a0ea-2eda806300c7','95d21ac3-8b70-4cdd-950d-9ac061476f9f',1,'How many codons are generated by a permutation combination of 4^3 (4 x 4 x 4)?','A permutation combination of 4^3 (4 x 4 x 4) generates 64 codons, which is more than the 20 amino acids needed.'),
  ('863a9b3f-cf28-4047-b963-e7aa91154e3d','313acf14-6e0b-4fe8-8050-a7ca2d58c9e8',1,'Which scientist developed the chemical method for synthesising RNA molecules with defined combinations of bases?','Har Gobind Khorana developed the chemical method instrumental in synthesising RNA molecules with defined combinations of bases (homopolymers and copolymers).'),
  ('5c7ed3bb-76d5-4c11-8791-61c22de8e5ef','bfc30ff6-7521-4337-82b5-298ccb4da7e1',1,'Whose cell-free system for protein synthesis helped decipher the genetic code?','Marshall Nirenberg''s cell-free system for protein synthesis finally helped the genetic code to be deciphered.'),
  ('327f063b-cf09-4f47-a3c0-3c5392c327aa','f1ee7aa6-d8ed-4d50-84c7-bc4eeb26ad1f',1,'Which enzyme was helpful in polymerising RNA with defined sequences in a template-independent manner?','Severo Ochoa enzyme (polynucleotide phosphorylase) was helpful in polymerising RNA with defined sequences in a template-independent manner (enzymatic synthesis of RNA).'),
  ('c8e4d2ea-0ea0-4efb-ac09-d1f37bc7a366','6afdea7c-159d-459d-a589-7bf0c4bbfb6b',1,'How many codons code for amino acids?','61 codons code for amino acids and 3 codons do not code for any amino acids, hence they function as stop codons.'),
  ('bf570f68-18ed-4533-a28b-1ee7c6d76b98','fa938d66-e573-45df-a6a0-df86f792442c',1,'What is the term for the property where some amino acids are coded by more than one codon?','Some amino acids are coded by more than one codon, hence the code is degenerate. This means multiple codons can specify the same amino acid.'),
  ('ed8ed656-0838-42be-a93e-43d07911fb09','39c0d6f5-4c3f-4e7d-9616-a86ef520103f',1,'How is the codon read in mRNA?','The codon is read in mRNA in a contiguous fashion. There are no punctuations between codons.'),
  ('647f05f4-afe2-485f-b397-0ae5c8f58c5f','5431d511-ca51-48ff-8ca2-8a26153fd8ac',1,'Which codon codes for Phenylalanine (Phe) from bacteria to humans?','UUU would code for Phenylalanine (phe) from bacteria to human, demonstrating the near-universal nature of the genetic code.'),
  ('0b6aea3c-63cd-42d4-aee0-6b7d1a9b057f','72299bda-bfd7-49f0-80a6-b67801461a9d',1,'Which codon has dual functions - coding for Methionine and acting as an initiator codon?','AUG has dual functions. It codes for Methionine (met), and it also acts as initiator codon.'),
  ('0f17935a-3ad3-4e6f-abf3-e31d1c33b8da','d8b24ac6-f6a7-4cd7-94c7-0d93d3c7b86f',1,'Which of the following are stop terminator codons?','UAA, UAG, and UGA are stop terminator codons that signal the end of protein synthesis.'),
  ('a79ca15d-8cec-4c8c-9688-a1ef8daa055c','75835569-e579-4035-a202-f682433465f2',1,'What was George Gamow''s profession?','George Gamow was a physicist who proposed the triplet nature of the genetic code.'),
  ('e7636b03-ad7a-4d35-9cf0-af8fd03301b2','4c82fe59-8a15-42ae-a463-7bf2d9534887',1,'Why was the proposition and deciphering of genetic code considered most challenging?','The proposition and deciphering of genetic code required involvement of scientists from several disciplines including physicists, organic chemists, biochemists and geneticists.'),
  ('24c9bc89-cbc1-4dd2-819c-80c72c88937f','0c831ded-ccbc-4dce-a2ed-1cf7249d8f08',1,'What type of RNA molecules did Khorana synthesise?','Har Gobind Khorana synthesised RNA molecules with defined combinations of bases including homopolymers and copolymers.'),
  ('bd47fde8-ee9d-49f7-b882-0028101f865a','76226982-e622-4d50-91ef-393afbcee4bf',1,'What does the term ''degenerate'' mean in the context of genetic code?','Degenerate means that some amino acids are coded by more than one codon, providing redundancy in the genetic code.'),
  ('ce5a0bda-129f-48be-9f08-0719c35d7d26','77a7ff3b-0121-466a-b3e6-32b6c275eb87',1,'What is the nature of the genetic code regarding its universality?','The code is nearly universal from bacteria to human, but some exceptions have been found in mitochondrial codons and in some protozoans.'),
  ('cc65f8e3-05d8-49df-b12f-a1eaded25afd','fc2e96de-a514-4dfe-8cf2-6f25b6249537',1,'How many stop codons are there in the genetic code?','There are 3 stop codons (UAA, UAG, UGA) that do not code for any amino acids.'),
  ('49900d2a-7ccd-4392-9d7b-a09e023288ee','be3b4fef-6897-4d1a-8c04-83de142c28cd',1,'What is the total number of possible codons in the genetic code?','The total number of possible codons is 64, generated by 4^3 permutation combination of the four bases.'),
  ('4974cf82-3a17-4642-9ee2-990e74c5f1e7','dcd0eee2-2bdf-4cbe-9777-b3f62ba0d768',1,'What does AUG code for?','AUG codes for Methionine (met) and also acts as the initiator codon.'),
  ('427d9321-a2bc-496b-8195-806ac8cd5d02','237ba042-c992-4569-b8d7-656e07ccab17',1,'Which of the following is NOT a feature of the genetic code?','The codon is read in a contiguous fashion with no punctuations. There are no punctuation marks between codons.'),
  ('7f009fe1-3186-4853-ab3e-827394395378','a805c594-9dbe-408e-9313-1e5b2d754aec',1,'What was the role of polynucleotide phosphorylase in deciphering the genetic code?','Polynucleotide phosphorylase (Severo Ochoa enzyme) polymerised RNA with defined sequences in a template-independent manner, aiding in enzymatic synthesis of RNA.'),
  ('e4d89e1e-6136-45c5-b9d4-406e376c5273','dbd8ad72-7e9c-4304-867b-2305be159d9f',1,'What does the term ''contiguous'' mean in the context of genetic code reading?','Contiguous means the codon is read continuously in mRNA without any gaps or punctuation marks between codons.'),
  ('93d2b641-0df7-4586-93eb-163a7f8981f7','b413f9f9-9d39-4f1f-ae34-89742cfcefc6',1,'When was the Human Genome Project launched?','The Human Genome Project was launched in the year 1990. It was a 13-year project coordinated by the U.S. Department of Energy and the National Institute of Health.'),
  ('c126c956-cd50-40d0-b52c-9876648dd82d','4091137a-0d57-4a5e-8522-7dfc91dccd5c',1,'What is the approximate size of the human genome in base pairs?','The human genome is said to have approximately 3 x 10^9 base pairs. This enormous size made the project a mega project requiring massive resources.'),
  ('9fdf774a-31fd-4d16-a0de-2ff767eca061','b0f0ef1f-7870-4481-8a50-ae4bcc5dcc8c',1,'Which organizations coordinated the Human Genome Project?','The Human Genome Project was coordinated by the U.S. Department of Energy and the National Institute of Health. The Wellcome Trust (U.K.) became a major partner during the early years.'),
  ('b213945e-412a-4c68-a8a2-2ada29bf26ca','90683d7d-6c3a-445a-8027-537d8bd63a17',1,'In which year was the Human Genome Project completed?','The Human Genome Project was completed in 2003. It was a 13-year project that started in 1990.'),
  ('27074d62-13f7-4f0d-9c82-3106e369eb1a','c18c958a-c4af-47cf-967c-9b136794050d',1,'What was the estimated cost of sequencing per base pair at the beginning of the project?','The estimated cost of sequencing at the beginning of the project was US $3 per base pair. With approximately 3 x 10^9 bp, the total estimated cost would be approximately 9 billion US dollars.'),
  ('9d8f0b93-726d-4999-9286-9b8dac17eb11','3a10050f-69b7-4115-a4b2-1b4479d55cd6',1,'Which of the following countries contributed to the Human Genome Project?','The Human Genome Project received contributions from multiple countries including the United States, United Kingdom (Wellcome Trust), Japan, France, Germany, and China.'),
  ('f378a71c-630f-41ac-8888-136d037f0f9c','59a12696-834e-4c44-acbf-562fc175ab37',1,'How many books would be required to store the DNA sequence from a single human cell if each page contained 1000 letters and each book contained 1000 pages?','If each page contained 1000 letters and each book contained 1000 pages, then 3300 such books would be required to store the information of DNA sequence from a single human cell. This illustrates the enormous amount of data generated by the project.'),
  ('24b5ae6a-4e72-4a87-aa63-4addfdd2aa47','b35eca7f-6986-4b50-83f5-19eb061e5f59',1,'What is one of the primary goals of the Human Genome Project?','One of the important goals of the Human Genome Project was to identify all the approximately 20,000-25,000 genes in human DNA. Other goals included determining the sequences of the 3 billion chemical base pairs and storing this information in databases.'),
  ('f3842d9e-bfb9-4338-bcc3-2535a35bdd0f','0a6ed879-dba1-42f3-834c-5308fa9c4337',1,'What does ELSI stand for in the context of the Human Genome Project?','ELSI stands for Ethical, Legal, and Social Issues. Addressing these issues was one of the goals of the Human Genome Project as genetic information raises important ethical and legal concerns.'),
  ('6f8c3020-1877-45f4-8453-4574503fb180','75c04582-4887-45f8-beae-5cfa732b0875',1,'What new area in biology developed rapidly in association with the Human Genome Project?','The Human Genome Project was closely associated with the rapid development of a new area in biology called Bioinformatics. The enormous amount of data necessitated the use of high speed computational devices for data storage, retrieval, and analysis.'),
  ('5c566dbb-607e-43b8-b6fb-abe85f2c122e','49eddec7-30c1-4973-84ad-a82fca2cf0d4',1,'What was the approximate number of base pairs in the human genome according to the Human Genome Project?','The human genome contains approximately 3 x 10^9 base pairs. This was one of the key figures mentioned in the Human Genome Project description.'),
  ('43b9ac2e-a26b-421a-905c-69eaea1f6db5','43ebcd3d-4000-4b38-885b-8b4c6aadb1be',1,'What was the estimated cost per base pair for sequencing at the beginning of the Human Genome Project?','The cost of sequencing was estimated at US $3 per base pair at the beginning of the Human Genome Project.'),
  ('d7a424f5-1873-4c0d-ad50-f52f02f2be65','c04debfb-34eb-4bee-b163-1a33fcb486ad',1,'What was the total estimated cost of the Human Genome Project?','With approximately 3 x 10^9 bp at $3 per bp, the total estimated cost was approximately 9 billion US dollars.'),
  ('4d7fa4fb-0da5-460b-ad8f-84139d6fb84e','f93cbeed-9075-4cab-830b-5de04342ae76',1,'How many books would be required to store the DNA sequence information from a single human cell if each book had 1000 pages with 1000 letters per page?','3300 books would be required to store the DNA sequence from a single human cell, given 3 x 10^9 base pairs and 1000 letters per page across 1000 pages per book.'),
  ('4250bfbb-bff4-4d9f-ad85-f52c011981bb','557dcb2a-9c0f-4be5-ac97-64cd66b5af5e',1,'Which new area in biology was closely associated with the Human Genome Project?','Bioinformatics was the new area in biology that developed rapidly in association with the Human Genome Project for data storage, retrieval, and analysis.'),
  ('8df11917-fc64-4c80-ad96-92be7ceb891d','86a62a62-53d4-457b-a69a-cb94f80ac637',1,'What was the approximate range of genes the Human Genome Project aimed to identify in human DNA?','The Human Genome Project aimed to identify approximately 20,000-25,000 genes in human DNA.'),
  ('8eb8edfc-e728-4abc-955b-d61e51ac33bd','b128f633-34c4-4ec7-9c49-6c95c719d542',1,'How many chemical base pairs make up human DNA according to the Human Genome Project goals?','The Human Genome Project aimed to determine the sequences of 3 billion chemical base pairs that make up human DNA.'),
  ('b1efc567-9356-41c1-8e1a-c806877f2209','35af140b-77d8-48a8-8e84-7e32f998a8ab',1,'Which of the following was NOT listed as a goal of the Human Genome Project?','Cloning human beings was not listed as a goal. The goals included identifying genes, storing information, improving tools, transferring technologies, and addressing ethical issues.'),
  ('ee20ef3c-57fb-4801-b2c3-7a667e0fe038','959f113c-7da3-4d2e-84fc-cefba31d3eef',1,'What does ELSI stand for in the context of Human Genome Project goals?','ELSI stands for Ethical, Legal, and Social Issues that may arise from the Human Genome Project.'),
  ('bc442605-d1f8-46e4-bc28-2d9333894e3c','b5e20078-0312-47ec-9726-6b8a3561310e',1,'How long was the Human Genome Project planned to run?','The Human Genome Project was a 13-year project coordinated by the U.S. Department of Energy and the National Institute of Health.'),
  ('c01a16dc-8f0e-490c-849d-d00b357d3446','8fcceda3-68c6-4605-9d71-03741390d6b5',1,'Which UK organization became a major partner during the early years of the Human Genome Project?','The Wellcome Trust (U.K.) became a major partner during the early years of the Human Genome Project.'),
  ('55c6a3fa-01fe-4588-a219-305ded934172','9b603210-3c17-400f-a8a9-930da28c9d94',1,'In which year was the Human Genome Project completed?','The Human Genome Project was completed in 2003.'),
  ('05799cc9-2c9a-4164-ac10-e239574fd3a6','18fff5a0-e69c-483e-b731-b41a16e7e6c4',1,'Which of the following countries contributed to the Human Genome Project?','Contributions came from the U.S., U.K. (Wellcome Trust), Japan, France, Germany, China and others.'),
  ('f5d99607-c799-4d8c-bcf1-c6685ad5de52','5121ea97-c87a-4ca2-b62e-763c96bee9d7',1,'What are Expressed Sequence Tags (ESTs) in the context of Human Genome Project methodologies?','ESTs are identified genes that are expressed as RNA. This was one of the two major approaches used in the Human Genome Project.'),
  ('7010cdbc-25a7-44f8-8c68-ead6eea4d4d8','244ad2bb-4069-48e7-8201-6fad9d4a316a',1,'What is Sequence Annotation in the context of Human Genome Project methodologies?','Sequence Annotation refers to assigning different regions in the sequence with functions after sequencing the whole genome.'),
  ('c7502c8f-306b-45e0-b084-30f2beccf705','d38a7f85-e076-4f16-b013-6d45a71d0b3c',1,'What were BAC and YAC used as in the Human Genome Project?','BAC (bacterial artificial chromosomes) and YAC (yeast artificial chromosomes) were used as vectors for cloning DNA fragments in suitable hosts.'),
  ('14905916-2593-4994-aafd-242677290669','9808f653-3458-4c21-9eec-a0cffb63971e',1,'Which organisms were commonly used as hosts for cloning DNA fragments in the Human Genome Project?','Bacteria and yeast were the commonly used hosts for cloning DNA fragments in the Human Genome Project.'),
  ('9f329a9f-0038-4f4b-a5c5-f841218366b2','bf1666b4-62a1-4714-ad11-9698dbd00c6f',1,'Who developed the method used by automated DNA sequencers in the Human Genome Project?','Frederick Sanger developed the method used by automated DNA sequencers. He is also credited for developing the method for determination of amino acid sequences in proteins.'),
  ('c57227e0-790f-43ab-bc46-eb27b91a6cb6','eaac773d-77fc-42a3-b150-0ebbdfdce966',1,'When was the sequence of chromosome 1 completed?','The sequence of chromosome 1 was completed in May 2006, which was the last of the 24 human chromosomes to be sequenced.'),
  ('a1f76dfc-e675-4385-8527-8bde5831d92d','8058dc55-4b60-4d19-b51a-42a39ec7fe76',1,'How many human chromosomes were sequenced in the Human Genome Project?','24 human chromosomes were sequenced: 22 autosomes and X and Y chromosomes.'),
  ('cbf47d1e-8396-48d7-852d-5097c80c2da7','3a8dc069-fa92-4b3e-acd6-7bfa82d6dcd8',1,'What were used to generate genetic and physical maps on the genome?','Genetic and physical maps were generated using information on polymorphism of restriction endonuclease recognition sites and repetitive DNA sequences known as microsatellites.'),
  ('0e673d13-1396-464d-bd47-9d81801557bf','acc0edb4-b20d-4086-bc90-7ff018167f7c',1,'What is a microsatellite?','Microsatellites are repetitive DNA sequences that were used in generating genetic and physical maps on the genome.'),
  ('6c98a1e1-7455-4293-a8e5-c16161eed10e','faf7b901-e13b-47db-838b-19582c01bc30',1,'What was the purpose of converting total DNA into random fragments of smaller sizes?','DNA was converted into random fragments of smaller sizes because there are technical limitations in sequencing very long pieces of DNA.'),
  ('1ce03d7e-9879-4a80-9c3e-f9b7849a919a','f7cd9621-c81d-4530-9b0f-f0d80da8bdd9',1,'Which of the following is a non-human model organism that was sequenced in the Human Genome Project?','Caenorhabditis elegans, a free living non-pathogenic nematode, was one of the non-human model organisms sequenced.'),
  ('08bf96e0-b051-48b9-ab47-b04c54ef65bb','4e8ba654-1091-48af-8f4c-ad365dccf739',1,'What was the main reason for calling the Human Genome Project a mega project?','The HGP was called a mega project because of its enormous magnitude, requirements, cost (approximately 9 billion dollars), and the need for high-speed computational devices for data management.'),
  ('6402c8f8-1e9c-4c9c-ace4-1a16c6eb3c72','e8de8d83-c6bb-4b80-b863-a0e45f9e8ab0',1,'What was the estimated cost of the Human Genome Project if sequencing cost $3 per base pair?','The Human Genome Project aimed to sequence approximately 3 billion base pairs. At $3 per base pair, the total estimated cost was approximately 9 billion US dollars.'),
  ('0132f3b1-996e-41aa-9f11-496eee050ec3','91ea451f-f549-473c-b365-6e7e4c5011e3',1,'Which two organizations coordinated the Human Genome Project?','The Human Genome Project was a 13-year project coordinated by the U.S. Department of Energy and the National Institute of Health.'),
  ('82af05d8-0ba3-49ea-99f4-e56df9f677fd','bf02c81f-424d-48b9-9963-c92f326491e6',1,'In which year was the Human Genome Project completed?','The Human Genome Project was completed in 2003.'),
  ('8b2924e5-6827-4207-bfd5-cd8c17aab79f','76b72647-ac29-4f5e-84d6-7646b39e7174',1,'What does EST stand for in the context of the Human Genome Project?','EST stands for Expressed Sequence Tags, which are RNA sequences that are expressed as genes. This was one of the two major approaches used in the Human Genome Project.'),
  ('e21e2265-1056-438c-9e26-3fbc7544d35b','fa23a19c-04a8-4faf-ad74-3d3cc34a1d4a',1,'Which method was used for DNA sequencing in the Human Genome Project?','The fragments were sequenced using automated DNA sequencers that worked on the principle of a method developed by Frederick Sanger.'),
  ('da0ed2c3-209a-46f5-a77f-44aa04da5286','65c642a2-69dc-4577-8657-3752e8748eed',1,'What are BAC and YAC used for in the Human Genome Project?','BAC (Bacterial Artificial Chromosomes) and YAC (Yeast Artificial Chromosomes) were used as vectors to clone DNA fragments in suitable hosts like bacteria and yeast.'),
  ('204f7b57-7eab-4947-ada0-c94380201191','ac85e135-6ac1-4ccf-8607-41bd66a9f676',1,'When was the sequence of chromosome 1 completed?','The sequence of chromosome 1 was completed in May 2006, which was the last of the 24 human chromosomes to be sequenced.'),
  ('a0a0582e-433f-43ff-9037-d786c214ef0b','0b6941c7-4065-48ee-8ed2-7ab3ae6acb4d',1,'How many human chromosomes were sequenced in the Human Genome Project?','The Human Genome Project sequenced 24 human chromosomes: 22 autosomes and the X and Y chromosomes.'),
  ('4bb32b48-94ab-4084-a91c-080ccad034c0','a894aa3d-740b-4437-9f45-b513321e6d65',1,'What is sequence annotation in the context of the Human Genome Project?','Sequence annotation refers to assigning different regions in the sequence with functions after sequencing the whole genome.'),
  ('89dda2b9-7c4f-4f36-b503-d9285c391efc','075eef55-e8b6-4366-91d4-8d4a850be119',1,'Which country became a major partner during the early years of the Human Genome Project?','During the early years of the HGP, the Wellcome Trust (U.K.) became a major partner.'),
  ('44aaa167-34b4-4c33-ac2d-6d129b5afe04','7865a272-5be1-45c4-8484-75981a05df92',1,'What is the purpose of generating overlapping DNA fragments in the Human Genome Project?','Overlapping fragments were generated so that the sequences could be arranged based on overlapping regions present in them. This alignment was done using specialized computer-based programs.'),
  ('a5abcba5-1b40-4569-ab7e-94d732d36d79','b414bacc-7e52-420d-8d30-d642c03b924d',1,'What are microsatellites used for in the Human Genome Project?','Microsatellites are repetitive DNA sequences used for assigning genetic and physical maps on the genome, along with restriction endonuclease recognition site polymorphism.'),
  ('114a25f7-4e89-4de8-9802-2693a80842da','7f79e344-da19-49e4-bfc1-8404394d1904',1,'Which of the following was NOT a goal of the Human Genome Project?','Creating genetically modified humans was not a goal of the Human Genome Project. The goals included identifying genes, determining base pair sequences, storing information, improving data analysis tools, and addressing ethical issues.'),
  ('a2f6fc76-5ebb-4330-8091-72d0523f69c4','bb6b22da-aa89-490d-9ede-84b57f99ec63',1,'How many books would be required to store the DNA sequence from a single human cell if each page contains 1000 letters and each book contains 1000 pages?','If each page contains 1000 letters and each book contains 1000 pages, then 3300 such books would be required to store the information of DNA sequence from a single human cell.'),
  ('1d9f31c6-a424-4128-9ea8-ede19c7ac7e0','6d3f1720-38af-4661-afc5-f1334cff9580',1,'What does ELSI stand for in the Human Genome Project?','ELSI stands for Ethical, Legal, and Social Issues, which the Human Genome Project aimed to address.'),
  ('23a2d067-aed6-4217-8c41-d792275b8ddc','88d19e5a-bcd4-4f79-9ffb-bdbaec87880c',1,'Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a non-human model organism sequenced in the Human Genome Project?','Elephant was not mentioned as a model organism. The text mentions bacteria, yeast, Caenorhabditis elegans, Drosophila, rice, and Arabidopsis as model organisms.'),
  ('c6229bfc-623d-47c7-9c60-1d51b136a427','a903ca4e-7084-4493-9451-4e82ea9ca594',1,'What was the primary reason for cloning DNA fragments before sequencing?','Cloning resulted in amplification of each DNA fragment so that it could subsequently be sequenced with ease.'),
  ('4e5b03ff-9fe1-4fb4-acc9-d4f0f48e59bc','e888e61f-7cb0-4bd9-a49c-04d18278e954',1,'What type of DNA sequences are microsatellites?','Microsatellites are repetitive DNA sequences that show polymorphism and are used for genetic mapping.'),
  ('fa1795a5-60f2-418c-b94f-faecd2553c39','b6c58ee0-99f8-40d1-a6ea-219a296b58c9',1,'What is the significance of the Human Genome Project for understanding human biology?','The Human Genome Project provides clues to understanding human biology and also helps in understanding non-human organisms for applications in healthcare, agriculture, energy production, and environmental remediation.'),
  ('a30cbc58-393a-4255-96a2-2f71f33f928f','5fa9fb8d-bd4a-49fb-86f2-ab936395d498',1,'Which technique was used to separate DNA fragments of different sizes in the Human Genome Project?','Electrophoresis was used to separate DNA fragments by size, which is a standard technique in DNA analysis.'),
  ('835e753c-af96-4326-8a00-55cf854ee79b','6cba3d7a-b3d5-416d-ac60-f30e82675dd3',1,'In Griffith''s experiment with Streptococcus pneumoniae, which strain was virulent and caused pneumonia in mice?','The S strain (smooth) bacteria had a mucous polysaccharide coat and were virulent, causing pneumonia in mice. The R strain (rough) lacked this coat and was non-virulent.'),
  ('5b714cd6-1b0f-4d80-a6b8-5c5521c1bef9','fee90623-04ad-4805-8067-55e51bbcd304',1,'What happened when heat-killed S strain bacteria were injected into mice?','Heat-killed S strain bacteria did not kill the mice because the bacteria were dead and could not cause infection.'),
  ('7fb5bdaa-ef87-4f46-a5ad-6a73432843c6','1cbc4445-eae4-4793-aafb-d154c762029a',1,'What was Griffith''s conclusion when mice died after being injected with a mixture of heat-killed S and live R bacteria?','Griffith concluded that some transforming principle was transferred from heat-killed S strain to live R strain, enabling R strain to synthesize a smooth polysaccharide coat and become virulent.'),
  ('bce6744c-e2de-4cee-bb92-cbccfdb56ae2','f1f71666-ce82-48a8-93ca-94b07a98c041',1,'Which enzymes did Avery, MacLeod, and McCarty use to prove that DNA is the transforming principle?','They used proteases (protein-digesting) and RNases (RNA-digesting) which did not affect transformation, but DNase (DNA-digesting) inhibited transformation, proving DNA is the genetic material.'),
  ('7f6e48ec-3e95-43fb-a59d-2206cda3cf34','c6c71590-f4ee-4a12-acf2-4f849b9ee935',1,'In the Hershey-Chase experiment, which radioactive isotope was used to label DNA?','Radioactive phosphorus (32P) was used to label DNA because DNA contains phosphorus in its phosphate groups. Protein does not contain phosphorus.'),
  ('9585446c-8258-4514-909d-1f7971982756','fa05ca92-802f-4fa1-a291-311cbd9974ca',1,'What was the key finding of the Hershey-Chase experiment after blending and centrifugation?','Bacteria infected with 32P-labeled DNA were radioactive, indicating DNA passed from virus to bacteria. Bacteria with 35S-labeled protein were not radioactive, showing proteins did not enter.'),
  ('ce18152d-d1e3-486a-8066-eed8a811ac45','97dc8490-3d12-445c-bf5b-3016940d891d',1,'What characteristic of the S strain made it appear smooth and shiny on culture plates?','The S strain bacteria produced smooth shiny colonies because they had a mucous polysaccharide coat. The R strain lacked this coat and appeared rough.'),
  ('8df9e58c-9c25-408b-aa1a-e566a2101d7c','62e2fd76-ebd6-46dc-a691-c67f14983bab',1,'Before Avery''s work, what was commonly believed to be the genetic material?','Prior to Avery, MacLeod, and McCarty''s work, proteins were thought to be the genetic material due to their complexity and variety.'),
  ('bd641a89-2782-48f3-a844-003f00499ca9','0584e7f1-4c22-4eec-9afa-6ec8dd019a01',1,'What effect did DNase have on the transforming principle in Avery''s experiment?','Digestion with DNase inhibited transformation, suggesting that DNA was the transforming substance. This was crucial evidence that DNA is the genetic material.'),
  ('897fb62c-a8c2-4318-bca5-c9a8729b7b00','75f716cb-454c-417c-8991-0059c47029c7',1,'What type of viruses did Hershey and Chase use in their experiment?','Hershey and Chase worked with bacteriophages, which are viruses that infect bacteria. These viruses attach to bacteria and inject their genetic material.'),
  ('54b81195-d2ee-4ce0-ae51-3f0d609f25a8','d8c79588-35dc-4304-bca0-915b06dc9439',1,'Why was radioactive sulfur (35S) used in the Hershey-Chase experiment?','Radioactive sulfur was used because sulfur is present in proteins (in amino acids like cysteine and methionine) but absent in DNA. This allowed specific labeling of protein.'),
  ('91675b48-943b-4934-92f7-df97fb28ba75','7281aca8-9cf9-4a0b-95ba-8cdab13b15d1',1,'What happened when live R strain bacteria were injected into mice?','Mice infected with the live R strain did not develop pneumonia and survived because the R strain lacked the protective mucous coat and was non-virulent.'),
  ('b1e750c3-7ed4-4444-aceb-23663b275740','2e41a04e-1933-4ab4-9d07-aae65e509696',1,'Which experiment provided the unequivocal proof that DNA is the genetic material?','Hershey and Chase''s 1952 experiment provided the unequivocal proof that DNA is the genetic material by showing that only DNA entered bacteria during viral infection.'),
  ('09115aae-7bd6-499b-9346-e72b222d2acf','78377700-fcd8-4a82-a353-eca735756fa0',1,'What was the purpose of using a blender in the Hershey-Chase experiment?','The blender was used to agitate and remove the viral protein coats from the surface of bacteria after infection, allowing separation of coats from bacterial cells.'),
  ('fb0ba6fd-0598-4f2e-9481-d8cdc2b9a46d','d4cd4ea0-250d-4d0b-b8e7-337dd321cc5a',1,'In the Hershey-Chase experiment, what did centrifugation achieve?','Centrifugation separated the heavier bacterial cells from the lighter virus particles, allowing researchers to determine which radioactive component entered the bacteria.'),
  ('b4da7dbc-ca43-4ef4-82a3-d2427956da25','0ef6c39d-b690-4318-a58b-8553c1327477',1,'Which property of DNA made it a better candidate for genetic material than protein?','DNA can generate its replica through replication and is chemically and structurally stable, making it suitable for storing genetic information.'),
  ('3ad0e8b7-7243-48a1-9ceb-0994796d714f','82779b21-866b-4e91-af2d-1465878725e6',1,'In bacteria, how many major types of RNA are needed for protein synthesis?','In bacteria, there are three major types of RNAs: mRNA (messenger RNA), tRNA (transfer RNA), and rRNA (ribosomal RNA). All three are essential for protein synthesis. The mRNA provides the template, tRNA brings amino acids and reads the genetic code, and rRNAs play structural and catalytic roles during translation.'),
  ('e400b472-cec4-4497-bc27-633bf922e6f2','2f8d18bc-079b-4c7b-894b-2846d833446b',1,'Which enzyme catalyzes transcription of all types of RNA in bacteria?','There is a single DNA-dependent RNA polymerase that catalyzes transcription of all types of RNA in bacteria. This enzyme binds to the promoter and initiates transcription, using nucleoside triphosphates as substrate.'),
  ('3312a4f9-433a-4183-b19a-c59b31ece1da','427a3997-1f6d-4549-82d2-d08963550511',1,'What does the sigma factor do during bacterial transcription?','The sigma factor is an initiation factor that associates transiently with RNA polymerase to help it recognize and bind to the promoter region. It alters the specificity of RNA polymerase to initiate transcription. After initiation, the sigma factor dissociates.'),
  ('2ddb07b6-85e8-460d-a927-1b3ad97f591c','b3c21e83-4822-4575-9aae-70fdee440e1c',1,'Which factor is responsible for termination of transcription in bacteria?','The Rho factor is a termination factor that associates with RNA polymerase to terminate transcription. When the polymerase reaches the terminator region, the nascent RNA falls off along with the RNA polymerase, resulting in termination.'),
  ('7f5091e1-19e9-43ef-93a5-109c699a8013','18fb7937-2cf9-4fa4-883e-a73264e264f8',1,'Why can transcription and translation be coupled in bacteria?','In bacteria, transcription and translation take place in the same compartment because there is no separation of cytosol and nucleus. The mRNA does not require processing to become active, so translation can begin before the mRNA is fully transcribed.'),
  ('9670f9da-8228-4405-8645-9aabfc847a60','c8d9b513-733c-4254-8f34-81b1e195d4a9',1,'Which RNA polymerase in eukaryotes transcribes rRNAs (28S, 18S, and 5.8S)?','In eukaryotes, RNA polymerase I transcribes rRNAs (28S, 18S, and 5.8S). RNA polymerase II transcribes precursor of mRNA (hnRNA), and RNA polymerase III is responsible for transcription of tRNA, 5S rRNA, and snRNAs.'),
  ('45ca09fe-926b-4bea-b72c-0ef5b264f951','6d87feea-a7b2-4ff4-88ae-4d9ae805ff49',1,'What is the process called when introns are removed and exons are joined in eukaryotic mRNA?','Splicing is the process where introns (intervening sequences) are removed and exons (expressed sequences) are joined in a defined order. This produces functional mRNA from the primary transcript that contains both exons and introns.'),
  ('979d4614-8d10-439e-a55d-bf9ef88bf283','b9584024-4c40-4695-8b73-b70cd6203ea7',1,'What is added to the 5'' end of hnRNA during capping in eukaryotes?','In capping, an unusual nucleotide called methyl guanosine triphosphate is added to the 5''-end of hnRNA. This cap protects the mRNA from degradation and helps in ribosome binding during translation.'),
  ('297c09a8-75be-4563-993a-b5f55fbfcae8','95fae31d-4764-423b-be79-ead2064e6e57',1,'How many adenylate residues are typically added during tailing of eukaryotic mRNA?','During tailing, adenylate residues (200-300) are added at the 3''-end of hnRNA in a template-independent manner. This poly-A tail helps in mRNA stability and export from the nucleus.'),
  ('f3d329ac-d528-41b7-aad1-a1c7ae5bc44e','983167f2-b2a8-4f1b-b3f0-e58bd5d92b8f',1,'What is the fully processed hnRNA called after capping, tailing, and splicing in eukaryotes?','The fully processed hnRNA, after undergoing capping, tailing, and splicing, is called mRNA (messenger RNA). This mature mRNA is then transported out of the nucleus for translation.'),
  ('cb26bc84-e693-470a-914b-fdad583d0859','a61fcb18-8fea-4d3e-808c-7e6f680d2097',1,'Who performed the experiment that proved DNA replicates semiconservatively in 1958?','Matthew Meselson and Franklin Stahl performed the experiment in 1958 that proved DNA replicates semiconservatively. They used E. coli grown in heavy nitrogen (15N) medium and then transferred to normal nitrogen (14N) medium.'),
  ('2c5c6cd9-abec-4afd-a315-722723c6a981','46a2a3cf-c92b-4729-ab02-c20dd7ef4392',1,'What isotope of nitrogen was used as the heavy nitrogen source in Meselson and Stahl''s experiment?','15N (heavy isotope of nitrogen) was used as the nitrogen source in the form of 15NH4Cl. This heavy nitrogen gets incorporated into newly synthesized DNA.'),
  ('3f459ab4-ef46-41ab-acd8-c9ac12821c49','d918aec6-a924-4c41-891c-3e4490c01f37',1,'How was the heavy DNA distinguished from normal DNA in the Meselson and Stahl experiment?','The heavy DNA molecule was distinguished from normal DNA by centrifugation in a cesium chloride (CsCl) density gradient. 15N is not radioactive, so it can only be separated based on density differences.'),
  ('32b2e331-baa6-4e15-b512-feb530959551','c298bc8f-fe65-45b1-b7d9-d327a26db69a',1,'What was the density of DNA extracted after one generation (20 minutes) when E. coli was transferred from 15N to 14N medium?','After one generation (20 minutes), the DNA extracted had a hybrid or intermediate density because it contained one strand of heavy DNA (15N) and one strand of light DNA (14N).'),
  ('0854ed76-99f5-4379-8f35-641cab7f6859','13a6138d-acc3-43c7-9efa-b6203bae41ef',1,'What was the composition of DNA after two generations (40 minutes) in the Meselson and Stahl experiment?','After two generations (40 minutes), the DNA was composed of equal amounts of hybrid DNA (one 15N strand and one 14N strand) and light DNA (both 14N strands).'),
  ('f598c48b-3d52-48ce-84c5-47c67f4132a0','c7ec3b8d-b24e-4f85-9e47-52ab16f23039',1,'Which plant was used by Taylor and colleagues in 1958 to prove semiconservative DNA replication in chromosomes?','Taylor and colleagues performed experiments on Vicia faba (faba beans) using radioactive thymidine to detect distribution of newly synthesized DNA in chromosomes, proving semiconservative replication.'),
  ('3bc5413f-2f3e-42ac-9275-b8be90df4603','8eb52ad7-4a3f-42dd-a620-867b8da1a317',1,'What technique did Taylor use to detect newly synthesized DNA in chromosomes?','Taylor and colleagues used radioactive thymidine to detect the distribution of newly synthesized DNA in chromosomes during their experiments on Vicia faba.'),
  ('93237a60-fe63-489a-b78b-0207115c64fa','797ff3f0-f941-4992-953b-ce62982ee484',1,'If E. coli was allowed to grow for 80 minutes in 14N medium after being in 15N medium, what would be the proportions of light and hybrid DNA?','After 80 minutes (4 generations), the proportion would be more light DNA than hybrid DNA. In each generation, the number of light DNA molecules doubles while hybrid DNA remains constant. After 4 generations: 14 light DNA and 2 hybrid DNA molecules.'),
  ('901be5d7-9a46-440c-9f03-8d7049500639','38a241aa-324a-47b6-bfee-ebea8824a10f',1,'Which direction does DNA-dependent DNA polymerase catalyse polymerisation?','DNA-dependent DNA polymerases catalyse polymerisation only in the 5'' to 3'' direction. This creates complications at the replication fork because the two template strands have opposite polarities.'),
  ('195f1ab8-d00f-4c51-81f5-c36aec2bc164','a6662751-3373-4c89-9f57-6f337b98e7a3',1,'On which template strand is DNA replication continuous?','On the template strand with 3'' to 5'' polarity, replication is continuous because the new strand can be synthesised in the 5'' to 3'' direction continuously. This is called the leading strand.'),
  ('993bdad7-7012-4eb0-93d4-c28f5e43cfd3','85520804-39f0-47e2-b475-d466d8191cd7',1,'What are the discontinuously synthesised DNA fragments called?','The discontinuously synthesised fragments on the lagging strand are called Okazaki fragments. They are later joined by DNA ligase to form a continuous strand.'),
  ('0b061f85-bc37-4313-93bb-e370cfceac41','543aacfb-5f8c-4457-8a45-cd0b3403b045',1,'What is the function of DNA ligase in replication?','DNA ligase joins the discontinuously synthesised Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand by forming phosphodiester bonds between them.'),
  ('6a95d51b-180f-4f59-955f-c436d2ef1051','2076c318-7422-4155-ab02-043eb2eaf978',1,'What is the specific region where replication originates called?','The origin of replication is a definite region in DNA where replication initiates. DNA polymerases cannot initiate replication randomly at any place.'),
  ('991c4aee-fe7a-46c4-8f7b-492670237920','ea5f3b4c-71ad-4390-826a-3ade2b45314b',1,'How long does E. coli take to complete DNA replication?','E. coli completes the process of replication within 18 minutes. It has 4.6 x 10^6 base pairs, requiring an average rate of approximately 2000 bp per second.'),
  ('fc92abdb-0dc4-4812-92c4-7287d0ea6d80','587178af-6d68-4ef8-8317-f0dbf6df87d0',1,'In which phase of the cell cycle does DNA replication occur in eukaryotes?','In eukaryotes, DNA replication takes place during the S-phase (synthesis phase) of the cell cycle. This is when the cell duplicates its DNA content.'),
  ('fb912c7f-bd81-4182-a10f-47dcab3eaa6e','5c02029c-f378-4741-b794-fcd982ba0f27',1,'What chromosomal anomaly results from failure in cell division after DNA replication?','A failure in cell division after DNA replication results in polyploidy, a chromosomal anomaly where cells contain multiple sets of chromosomes.'),
  ('f2b53099-0104-4eeb-881d-a9a513d8b063','01573d30-a547-4ea7-89af-769aa03609ee',1,'What dual purpose do deoxyribonucleoside triphosphates serve during replication?','Deoxyribonucleoside triphosphates serve dual purposes: they act as substrates for polymerisation and provide energy for the reaction through their two terminal high-energy phosphates.'),
  ('b74c6a2f-4922-4ced-b9f8-2296ccf35f9b','aa4f87b9-444c-47b5-9523-d177e51b0c80',1,'What is the small opening of the DNA helix where replication occurs called?','The replication fork is the small opening of the DNA helix where replication occurs. Since the two strands cannot be separated in their entire length due to high energy requirements, replication occurs within this opening.'),
  ('8fbd0242-0752-4ce3-9e32-0480305b9496','219605e1-5448-4061-8d2c-0bc9140935c2',1,'What is the process of copying genetic information from one strand of DNA into RNA called?','Transcription is the process of copying genetic information from one strand of DNA into RNA. During this process, the principle of complementarity governs, but adenosine pairs with uracil instead of thymine.'),
  ('baa78394-0c31-4f7a-88f3-5aa589424754','11802047-1881-44da-a19a-a39b07ac19ac',1,'In transcription, which nitrogenous base pairs with adenine instead of thymine?','During transcription, adenosine forms a base pair with uracil instead of thymine. This is a key difference between DNA replication and transcription.'),
  ('40fcc2c1-a007-44ad-87e3-11bc8d46df5e','773bb048-46e9-4a27-a642-ca6c8ceb8359',1,'Unlike replication, what happens to the DNA during transcription?','Unlike replication where the total DNA of an organism gets duplicated, in transcription only a segment of DNA and only one of the strands is copied into RNA. This requires defining boundaries for the region and strand to be transcribed.'),
  ('5ba07a2f-8411-4502-b6fd-3d007e64c281','2a3e3a7a-6856-49fe-801b-f647f24ea9a0',1,'Why are both strands of DNA not copied during transcription?','If both strands act as templates, they would code for RNA molecules with different sequences, leading to different proteins and complicating genetic information transfer. Also, the two complementary RNA molecules would form double-stranded RNA, preventing translation into protein.'),
  ('0e89beb9-d9a3-45ae-8af5-aadc51db469f','e6efb650-d434-4ab8-a9d3-9cc81bd67e32',1,'What are the three regions that define a transcription unit in DNA?','A transcription unit in DNA is defined by three primary regions: (i) A Promoter, (ii) The Structural gene, and (iii) A Terminator. These regions work together to control the transcription process.'),
  ('b31ef096-d393-4c30-a637-b41eeedf46bb','f5a5ee99-3b1e-4dde-8608-388dac43b560',1,'Which strand of DNA acts as the template strand during transcription?','The strand that has the polarity 3'' to 5'' acts as the template strand during transcription. RNA polymerase catalyzes polymerization in the 5'' to 3'' direction, so it reads the template strand from 3'' to 5''.'),
  ('a9a62ce0-6068-48aa-9bfc-bf2bffb9a075','3badd2c7-6882-462c-93c1-f96b09f1f2e0',1,'What is the coding strand also known as?','The coding strand has the polarity 5'' to 3'' and the sequence same as RNA (except thymine at the place of uracil). It is also referred to as the sense strand. The template strand is also called the antisense strand.'),
  ('2cf575d9-7854-4aef-b71e-40aab58a2c1c','d37077de-08de-4854-9ff6-694357de96f5',1,'Where is the promoter located in relation to the structural gene?','The promoter is located towards the 5'' end (upstream) of the structural gene, with reference to the polarity of the coding strand. It provides the binding site for RNA polymerase and defines the template and coding strands.'),
  ('806f6950-970c-4b6c-b86a-2d435843a344','2bb014a1-750e-4b7e-a186-326d205ca8c8',1,'What would happen if both DNA strands were transcribed simultaneously?','If both strands were transcribed, the two RNA molecules produced would be complementary to each other and would form double-stranded RNA. This would prevent RNA from being translated into protein, making transcription futile.'),
  ('15ff836b-d2bb-4687-a142-b791f94069f1','a0c6204e-009f-49ac-8236-bdd8f0113b18',1,'Which enzyme catalyzes polymerization during transcription?','DNA-dependent RNA polymerase catalyzes the polymerization of nucleotides during transcription. It reads the template strand and synthesizes RNA in the 5'' to 3'' direction.'),
  ('644b2bf5-506b-44bc-abd6-60b91aca855e','2aacdbb0-7eca-437b-98c1-d3017854ee50',1,'What is the direction of RNA polymerase activity during transcription?','DNA-dependent RNA polymerase catalyzes polymerization only in one direction, that is 5'' to 3''. This is why the template strand must be read from 3'' to 5'' for proper RNA synthesis.'),
  ('e9fec46d-3b09-4fd9-96d5-383c5c681007','48853793-2ac4-49d9-837b-19be4dadf881',1,'What happens to the coding strand during transcription?','The coding strand is displaced during transcription. It has the same sequence as RNA (except thymine instead of uracil) and does not code for anything directly, but serves as a reference point for defining the transcription unit.'),
  ('1d197b96-0898-43b1-b6f1-80cb19d6ca9b','9cd12423-f949-431e-9316-e12aa740d103',1,'Where is the terminator located in a transcription unit?','The terminator is located towards the 3'' end (downstream) of the coding strand and usually defines the end of the transcription process. It works together with the promoter to flank the structural gene.'),
  ('9aba38a0-4e48-4c4a-9b97-d0f355b447a8','c99e4af0-7717-4741-b7ba-51d66eb55a94',1,'What additional sequences may be present near the promoter in a transcription unit?','There are additional regulatory sequences that may be present further upstream or downstream to the promoter. These sequences play roles in regulating gene expression and will be discussed in the section on regulation of gene expression.'),
  ('3b199f75-638a-4419-8bbb-5be8f48e7bcd','0122dc69-1752-425e-8fb6-b1f3ffa2415c',1,'In the given example, what is the sequence of the template strand?','The template strand has the polarity 3'' to 5'' and the sequence 3''-ATGCATGCATGCATGCATGCATGC-5''. This strand is read by RNA polymerase to synthesize complementary RNA.'),
  ('0f1a6037-d2e1-4ede-ac1d-b363f080f6b6','e70b7ce1-8850-4aea-93f2-0eaef293e214',1,'What is the relationship between the coding strand and the RNA sequence?','The coding strand has the same sequence as the RNA produced during transcription, except that thymine (T) in DNA is replaced by uracil (U) in RNA. This is why it is called the coding strand.'),
  ('658bb984-c269-46f6-8eaa-d398b4d8bedb','9d6ce9c8-4a7c-456f-9e62-d23b9ea42b7b',1,'A transcription unit in DNA is defined by how many primary regions?','A transcription unit in DNA is defined primarily by three regions: (i) A Promoter, (ii) The Structural gene, and (iii) A Terminator.'),
  ('8578d62d-ab1e-4ba8-bfc8-d3d3e254c075','c4e55254-3bd1-4a5a-807d-a5f07021cb70',1,'Which strand of DNA acts as the template during transcription?','The strand that has the polarity 3''-5'' acts as a template and is referred to as the template strand. The DNA-dependent RNA polymerase catalyzes polymerization in the 5''-3'' direction, so it reads the template strand in the 3''-5'' direction.'),
  ('62e13441-6475-49cf-b0c2-9ae91cb72693','da7fe034-4d8b-49ad-a374-ff8cad6a7b80',1,'What is the coding strand also called during transcription?','The coding strand has the polarity 5''-3'' and the sequence same as RNA (except thymine at the place of uracil). It is also referred to as the sense strand. The template strand is sometimes called the antisense strand.'),
  ('825d4e2b-0b6a-48ad-a28e-c643f761be1f','6762120d-1d4e-4017-8ce8-21c22d5b67e7',1,'Where is the promoter located with respect to the structural gene?','The promoter is located towards the 5''-end (upstream) of the structural gene. The reference is made with respect to the polarity of the coding strand. The terminator is located towards the 3''-end (downstream).'),
  ('865d6490-0749-4879-8cf1-06616e6aef7e','9a6c9cd0-5d1b-439c-a309-458b89dd3971',1,'What happens if the promoter and terminator positions are switched in a transcription unit?','By switching the position of the promoter with the terminator, the definition of coding and template strands could be reversed. This is because the promoter defines which strand serves as the template for transcription.'),
  ('7c1cd179-bcbc-40e7-81d2-94335b5308c3','f141905e-9bad-473c-9754-0a66a9c50c14',1,'A cistron is defined as a segment of DNA coding for what?','A cistron is defined as a segment of DNA coding for a polypeptide. The structural gene in a transcription unit could be monocistronic (mostly in eukaryotes) or polycistronic (mostly in bacteria or prokaryotes).'),
  ('6f3b64d3-fec4-4e50-b49d-1af0ba163215','e48b0843-e534-4fce-8a5d-221cba394673',1,'Which type of structural gene arrangement is mostly found in eukaryotes?','Monocistronic structural genes are mostly found in eukaryotes. In bacteria or prokaryotes, polycistronic structural genes are more common. Monocistronic genes code for a single polypeptide.'),
  ('8f87765f-d2cb-4a78-9eff-be8adfa6970f','c6918064-759f-4d7b-8f6a-2cab2cfb2897',1,'What are the coding sequences in eukaryotic genes called?','The coding sequences or expressed sequences in eukaryotic genes are defined as exons. Exons are those sequences that appear in mature or processed RNA. They are interrupted by introns, which do not appear in mature RNA.'),
  ('6ae8febb-0e02-49a3-89f8-308ac8ab7b79','4ba81e76-b9c7-4916-a6cb-800f1a2a427d',1,'What are introns in eukaryotic genes?','Introns or intervening sequences are non-coding sequences that do not appear in mature or processed RNA. They interrupt the exons and are removed during the process of splicing to produce functional RNA.'),
  ('c36dc491-b9a5-471a-b60d-fee0d036f714','1000c7ad-91f0-4843-8c53-73cd351327ad',1,'The split-gene arrangement in eukaryotes complicates which definition?','The split-gene arrangement, where exons are interrupted by introns, further complicates the definition of a gene in terms of a DNA sequence. It makes it difficult to literally define a gene as a continuous DNA sequence.'),
  ('c641901e-3493-412d-9c5c-14c861ea59b1','ba7152a2-1881-490f-8178-9917a61a2eed',1,'Besides structural genes, which sequences also affect inheritance of a character?','Inheritance of a character is also affected by promoter and regulatory sequences of a structural gene. Hence, sometimes the regulatory sequences are loosely defined as regulatory genes, even though these sequences do not code for any RNA or protein.'),
  ('ab733664-8354-4e7b-a7e9-b04c4a7094d5','0511090e-9edb-49c7-b21f-69a7a539a34a',1,'Which strand is displaced during transcription and does NOT code for anything?','The coding strand, which has the polarity 5''-3'', is displaced during transcription. Strangely, this strand which does not code for anything is referred to as the coding strand. The template strand is the one that is actually read by RNA polymerase.'),
  ('8f06caab-1626-4365-90b7-a3403f55c204','973f2989-d9c4-402d-9e7a-5579e5827107',1,'According to the double helix model, how many hydrogen bonds are formed between Guanine and Cytosine?','Guanine forms three hydrogen bonds with Cytosine from the opposite strand, while Adenine forms only two hydrogen bonds with Thymine.'),
  ('82f6af13-f0d6-4c28-ac9c-7733444cbbe5','637c436b-482e-4b55-b53e-4793d779d005',1,'What is the approximate pitch of the DNA double helix?','The pitch of the DNA helix is 3.4 nanometres, with roughly 10 base pairs in each turn.'),
  ('989f3189-6916-49db-abc4-a1e277f8eea1','9aa583a9-4f01-4011-bb7e-c5ef4caaeb12',1,'How many base pairs are present approximately in each turn of the DNA double helix?','There are roughly 10 base pairs in each turn of the DNA double helix, with a distance of approximately 0.34 nm between consecutive base pairs.'),
  ('5d8e2d7a-1442-4fda-9113-a0b6887c6a12','005dc7bc-2e90-4aa1-ae59-5ba0fc116107',1,'What is the distance between two consecutive base pairs in the DNA helix?','The distance between consecutive base pairs in the DNA helix is approximately 0.34 nm (0.34 x 10^-9 m).'),
  ('3491def5-66b5-4baf-a91b-42c0d40608e0','d274830e-ab83-49cc-8634-8288d7d48a67',1,'The two chains of DNA are coiled in which fashion?','The two chains of DNA are coiled in a right-handed fashion to form the double helix structure.'),
  ('eddcab32-f3d0-428d-a44a-2da68d1f85a7','973952db-2bb9-4838-9127-b34f6643c6b2',1,'What additional factor, besides hydrogen bonds, confers stability to the helical structure of DNA?','The plane of one base pair stacks over the other in the double helix, which in addition to hydrogen bonds confers stability to the helical structure.'),
  ('de64a0c6-a43b-4a0d-82da-6009d610039c','6ea30391-48d6-45f2-9985-916762c3dc38',1,'According to the Central Dogma of molecular biology proposed by Francis Crick, what is the direction of genetic information flow?','The Central Dogma states that genetic information flows from DNA through transcription to mRNA, and then through translation to protein.'),
  ('b6685d27-fa7a-4642-ac19-424fdd8bc0be','8e171144-2778-43e0-8e2a-322f6fd47f92',1,'Which nitrogenous base pairs with Thymine through two hydrogen bonds?','Adenine forms two hydrogen bonds with Thymine from the opposite strand, following the complementary base pairing rule.'),
  ('941b28ff-a974-4093-94de-b2f3fbcc0ae9','ff842dbc-0020-40cb-9a67-9e72c0dc6c0e',1,'What is the polarity relationship between the two strands of DNA?','The two chains have anti-parallel polarity; if one chain has 5'' to 3'' polarity, the other has 3'' to 5'' polarity.'),
  ('da0fdc12-1f2d-4242-99fd-caad8eb58d97','cad5d779-b3cb-4d29-a772-98446a38e905',1,'Why does a purine always come opposite to a pyrimidine in DNA base pairing?','A purine always comes opposite to a pyrimidine to generate approximately uniform distance between the two strands of the helix, maintaining the constant diameter of the DNA molecule.'),
  ('ef800ca7-a361-4566-9f73-9bf8ac6473bc','187857e4-f312-4b16-8fc2-007c71c461a1',1,'What is the significance of complementary base pairing in DNA?','Because of complementary base pairing, if the sequence of bases in one strand is known, then the sequence in the other strand can be predicted.'),
  ('19d514e1-ae3c-47b5-ade9-dcb02ec0863e','78c2d277-a794-4ab9-a78d-0ace336e44b3',1,'Which of the following is NOT a salient feature of the double-helix structure of DNA?','The bases project inside the helix, not outside. The backbone is formed by sugar and phosphates, and the nitrogenous bases linked to sugar project from the backbone inward.'),
  ('f8021840-6f4f-42ac-bda7-ef5aeab5070f','c6e32535-e986-4204-8d6a-27ea0767ca7b',1,'In the DNA double helix, what constitutes the backbone of the polynucleotide chain?','The backbone of a polynucleotide chain is formed due to sugar and phosphates, while the nitrogenous bases project from this backbone.'),
  ('bc732d43-744d-4f77-a186-3e47911a615e','5a3c61a6-768d-4a86-b1d0-98b43689d453',1,'What process is represented by the circular arrow in the Central Dogma diagram?','The circular arrow in the Central Dogma diagram represents DNA replication, where DNA makes a copy of itself.'),
  ('892a289a-31a9-4d7c-b9ef-e7cc0c5df179','519d1a24-3ade-47a0-a93c-83ce65d351e4',1,'If one strand of DNA has the sequence 5''-ATGC-3'', what would be the sequence of the complementary strand in 5'' to 3'' direction?','The complementary strand would be 3''-TACG-5'', which when written in 5'' to 3'' direction becomes 5''-GCAT-3''.'),
  ('14a6f525-87c4-40fe-831c-7d23e7e89832','acde863d-44e5-41f6-a6e8-86a06b57d629',1,'What is the approximate length of DNA double helix in a typical mammalian cell if calculated by multiplying total base pairs by distance between consecutive base pairs?','The text states that multiplying 6.6 x 10^9 bp by 0.34 x 10^-9 m/bp gives approximately 2.2 meters. This length is far greater than the dimension of a typical nucleus (approximately 10^-6 m).'),
  ('c04f4f94-7ae1-4eac-9e49-1ac8f6691b49','e947266f-9b56-46bf-b9eb-b06f55f4a120',1,'How is DNA organized in prokaryotes such as E. coli?','In prokaryotes like E. coli, the DNA is not scattered throughout the cell. It is held with some proteins in a region termed as nucleoid, organized in large loops held by proteins.'),
  ('4310bfc8-6dd2-4998-a0db-e33cf7d9e0e7','c3b6d173-a35d-4e14-b66d-906533fa3599',1,'What are histones rich in?','Histones are rich in the basic amino acid residues lysine and arginine. Both these amino acid residues carry positive charges in their side chains.'),
  ('521c1751-115d-4d97-85c2-d70fa1a1f386','e2705ff9-ba2c-417a-85b0-05dc96680eb7',1,'What is a nucleosome composed of?','The negatively charged DNA is wrapped around the positively charged histone octamer to form a structure called nucleosome. A typical nucleosome contains 200 bp of DNA helix.'),
  ('0f11b46e-a5d0-4273-bae5-dd5a5ea1491b','4426f629-662a-4914-8c99-1c30d55d7f59',1,'What does the electron microscope show nucleosomes as?','The nucleosomes in chromatin are seen as beads-on-string structure when viewed under electron microscope.'),
  ('3276d806-5dd0-4de5-bac5-9fce7c9b4502','83cab588-ef81-4787-963b-ae3b8a0d8db7',1,'What is the repeating unit of chromatin structure in the nucleus?','Nucleosomes constitute the repeating unit of a structure in nucleus called chromatin.'),
  ('286e88ff-2686-4026-876c-e3ef0846a8f6','bf1e5994-7161-4410-83c7-1b2812fac8a4',1,'What additional proteins are required for packaging chromatin at higher levels?','The packaging of chromatin at higher level requires additional set of proteins that collectively are referred to as Non-histone Chromosomal (NHC) proteins.'),
  ('dc84df3d-e36f-4716-9b75-3f3523ccc104','f21dc46f-9102-40e1-b94f-328fe9634234',1,'Which type of chromatin is transcriptionally active?','Euchromatin is said to be transcriptionally active chromatin, whereas heterochromatin is more densely packed and stains dark.'),
  ('3dc57452-d355-408c-a1c1-ba6b08cd7cb2','6b442bef-06b1-40b6-8076-351e9ae4f570',1,'What is the diameter of a typical nucleus?','The text mentions that a typical nucleus has dimensions of approximately 10^-6 m, while the DNA length is 2.2 meters.'),
  ('0c758557-c3b9-4e47-8696-fdc2a7e4bcf3','3cac296c-75e3-44ce-a5d0-9686f7896b6c',1,'How many histone molecules form a histone octamer?','Histones are organized to form a unit of eight molecules called histone octamer.'),
  ('596503ba-1246-4c45-8f49-76ce2b0e1bbe','a00b5f6e-5240-4fd3-b80b-f27c18829fcd',1,'What is the approximate distance between two consecutive base pairs in DNA?','The text states that the distance between two consecutive base pairs is taken as 0.34 nm (0.34 x 10^-9 m).'),
  ('a3e26b59-f79d-4a63-ac09-d05cac13f62f','2553f01e-ff86-49df-be3d-5add0ff44e0b',1,'What charge does DNA carry?','DNA being negatively charged is held with some proteins that have positive charges.'),
  ('4327f7e7-5c0e-409a-92b7-7cbb71ea6857','dbc616ce-dccb-42a3-918d-ea4049eee89b',1,'What is the length of E. coli DNA?','The text states that the length of E. coli DNA is 1.36 mm.'),
  ('41e24d42-5e2f-4c39-8a91-77e4bdb0b915','c9c90435-39eb-4658-bcd4-ea061cc7b2b2',1,'What is chromatin?','Chromatin is described as threadlike stained (coloured) bodies seen in nucleus. The nucleosomes in chromatin are seen as beads-on-string structure.'),
  ('b4c87884-1fc8-4978-a872-68c06ab1b40a','a9289a46-10bf-4725-a1bb-7e18a729cf78',1,'Which region of chromatin stains light?','Some region of chromatin are loosely packed and stains light, referred to as euchromatin. The chromatin that is more densely packed and stains dark are called heterochromatin.'),
  ('8d52dfe4-efa5-47da-85a3-bd4d8fee0144','cba6351a-1156-48ea-a966-3d7bd59e97cc',1,'Which of the following is NOT a criterion for a molecule to act as genetic material?','The criteria for genetic material include: (i) ability to replicate, (ii) chemical and structural stability, (iii) scope for slow changes (mutations) required for evolution, and (iv) ability to express as Mendelian characters. Rapid changes are not required; rather, slow changes are needed.'),
  ('4fdb50e4-4051-4823-88f5-1183c40b8848','19b26f61-1452-48c8-8d80-6ea6ac2c6725',1,'Why is DNA chemically less reactive compared to RNA?','The 2''-OH group present at every nucleotide in RNA is a reactive group that makes RNA labile and easily degradable. RNA is also catalytic and hence reactive. DNA lacks this 2''-OH group, making it chemically less reactive and structurally more stable.'),
  ('cd06827f-e886-440c-82b5-fd14a9b3df8e','36af9fd5-ab46-4f54-a7df-edf0cbd69e13',1,'What additional stability does thymine confer to DNA compared to uracil in RNA?','Thymine (5-methyl uracil) is essentially a methylated form of uracil. The presence of thymine at the place of uracil confers additional stability to DNA. This is related to DNA repair processes.'),
  ('f1c8408c-59c3-4be5-b490-08454c6f59e8','58a071b8-dfb8-4e83-aaff-c05acb82aa62',1,'Which statement about RNA viruses is correct?','RNA being unstable mutates at a faster rate. Consequently, viruses having RNA genome and having shorter life span mutate and evolve faster.'),
  ('89fee658-c7f2-463f-929a-0682ab466a73','cf550abc-a270-4edc-87a4-6626a04292d9',1,'Which molecule can directly code for the synthesis of proteins?','RNA can directly code for the synthesis of proteins, hence can easily express the characters. DNA, however, is dependent on RNA for synthesis of proteins. The protein synthesising machinery has evolved around RNA.'),
  ('622f92a1-aac2-49fe-a4f1-d0a55f453999','c720f992-cab6-4a13-854e-7f59daff8115',1,'Why is DNA preferred for storage of genetic information over RNA?','Both RNA and DNA can function as genetic material, but DNA being more stable is preferred for storage of genetic information. For the transmission of genetic information, RNA is better suited.'),
  ('22ccbf52-5b83-42b0-8817-1076f12bc7b3','e10b5ae8-a69b-4adc-a509-28c738fe03fc',1,'What is the significance of the RNA World hypothesis?','RNA was the first genetic material. Essential life processes such as metabolism, translation, and splicing evolved around RNA. RNA used to act as a genetic material as well as a catalyst.'),
  ('ccc0780f-ceb9-4ef6-9461-b7dad5e65135','655c5564-d78e-4902-a3b7-1aee387ffa23',1,'Which of the following is a key difference between DNA and RNA as genetic material?','Because of the 2''-OH group present in RNA, it is more reactive and labile. DNA lacks this group and is therefore chemically less reactive and structurally more stable, making it a better genetic material for storage.'),
  ('ffd7076f-5143-4f07-86c5-989812ab9c56','148f8139-5335-4db2-9429-7fce2eee3f03',1,'What does the Central Dogma of molecular biology state?','Francis Crick proposed the Central Dogma which states that genetic information flows from DNA to RNA to protein. The process is: DNA replicates, transcribes to mRNA, and translates to protein.'),
  ('098963ab-4115-4bde-a016-b9ff37140531','d878558c-d54b-4af0-9577-8cbbf18bb2fd',1,'In some viruses, genetic information flows from RNA to DNA. What is this process called?','In some viruses (retroviruses), the flow of information is in reverse direction from RNA to DNA. This process is called reverse transcription and is catalyzed by the enzyme reverse transcriptase.'),
  ('cf29849f-f6db-4b84-924b-2840676a3687','2b23456f-8fe7-435f-a4d5-9939b9f96455',1,'Which enzyme is responsible for joining discontinuously synthesized DNA fragments during replication?','The discontinuously synthesised fragments (Okazaki fragments) on the lagging strand are later joined by the enzyme DNA ligase. This creates a continuous DNA strand.'),
  ('a31a9de9-eb71-49aa-9371-a32c0af4c3f4','69f4d2d6-3dd3-4e81-932c-ecd6b70e02e5',1,'What is the direction of DNA polymerase catalyzed polymerization?','DNA-dependent DNA polymerases catalyse polymerisation only in one direction, that is 5'' to 3''. This creates complications at the replication fork, requiring continuous and discontinuous synthesis on the two strands.'),
  ('32f797dc-304c-4571-9082-a1fb15486ab0','9989bb5c-2cea-4624-924d-0cbbc6b04b9e',1,'Where is the promoter located in a transcription unit?','The promoter is located towards the 5''-end (upstream) of the structural gene. The reference is made with respect to the polarity of the coding strand.'),
  ('8db12e6e-effa-4b19-b17c-aa327da9cc48','aa43800a-ccdb-4936-9312-9ac2928cc42a',1,'What is the function of the promoter in a transcription unit?','The promoter is a DNA sequence that provides a binding site for RNA polymerase. Its presence in a transcription unit also defines the template and coding strands.'),
  ('ef341f5f-4b62-441c-b8ea-2d9adc001c9a','a4d8da1d-cb22-4be6-815c-ba03d6b7404c',1,'Where is the terminator located in a transcription unit?','The terminator is located towards the 3''-end (downstream) of the coding strand and it usually defines the end of the process of transcription.'),
  ('f3b293f2-f2e8-4eb9-af3b-5fcd263a56f5','861cbc09-c159-4857-ab73-711036cbe0bc',1,'What is a gene defined as?','A gene is defined as the functional unit of inheritance. Genes are located on DNA, and the DNA sequence coding for tRNA or rRNA molecules also defines a gene.'),
  ('433fe2e9-965d-4889-83af-f2ef6aada800','8583e465-3825-42bc-bd54-a3d73aac7687',1,'What is a cistron defined as?','A cistron is defined as a segment of DNA coding for a polypeptide. The structural gene in a transcription unit could be monocistronic (mostly in eukaryotes) or polycistronic (mostly in bacteria or prokaryotes).'),
  ('0ba78e5c-3097-4558-80a8-5ed94b437069','31ef6663-87af-4a17-9e82-e5425719463d',1,'What are exons?','Exons are defined as coding sequences or expressed sequences that appear in mature or processed RNA. They are the parts of the gene that are expressed after introns are removed.'),
  ('6d4543d3-f77c-48c5-b688-b2a6f9c62b33','785fc33c-ec2b-43c7-9a7a-ac15a689db60',1,'What are introns?','Introns or intervening sequences are non-coding sequences that do not appear in mature or processed RNA. They interrupt the exons in eukaryotic genes.'),
  ('6e8d7ff9-fe22-4c1e-a3ba-0e1c16860d29','593466ce-59de-455b-b55c-3e565b3336e2',1,'How many major types of RNA are found in bacteria?','In bacteria, there are three major types of RNAs: mRNA (messenger RNA), tRNA (transfer RNA), and rRNA (ribosomal RNA). All three are needed to synthesize a protein in a cell.'),
  ('974f02b2-d767-4fcf-832c-9f8e20023103','a094d164-b4e2-45b2-a557-64f69e5e8e48',1,'What role does mRNA play during protein synthesis?','The mRNA provides the template for protein synthesis. It carries the genetic information from DNA to the ribosome where translation occurs.'),
  ('2998d95b-3c2a-4362-a466-d7cc897f4e3a','0d2c08f4-5825-4cf1-a3c2-9f830dd6afd6',1,'What is the function of tRNA during translation?','tRNA brings amino acids and reads the genetic code during translation. It acts as an adapter molecule that links the mRNA codon to the corresponding amino acid.'),
  ('5cac4aa7-f634-4258-af4d-129b765a926f','6aa38dbd-2dcd-4e39-94dd-dba8680d02f7',1,'What role do rRNAs play during translation?','rRNAs play structural and catalytic roles during translation. One of the rRNAs acts as a catalyst for peptide bond formation, which is an example of an RNA enzyme (ribozyme).'),
  ('4af563fd-16bd-4ab8-8632-f490fc0bdd1b','c1a02ec5-b54d-4de4-88fe-5f3047e9831a',1,'How many RNA polymerases are present in the nucleus of eukaryotes?','In eukaryotes, there are at least three RNA polymerases in the nucleus (in addition to the RNA polymerase found in organelles). RNA polymerase I transcribes rRNAs, RNA polymerase II transcribes hnRNA (precursor of mRNA), and RNA polymerase III transcribes tRNA, 5s rRNA, and snRNAs.'),
  ('2bed17b3-20ee-4396-90f1-8c2342cf4257','34024a86-e56e-43fa-86ab-19db798d77f7',1,'Which RNA polymerase transcribes the precursor of mRNA (hnRNA) in eukaryotes?','RNA polymerase II transcribes the precursor of mRNA, which is called heterogeneous nuclear RNA (hnRNA). This is then processed to form mature mRNA.'),
  ('e59a631b-0876-4835-b8ab-00e9a160e42d','132e369d-6ec7-4899-b483-201888a92264',1,'What process removes introns and joins exons in eukaryotic gene expression?','Splicing is the process where introns are removed and exons are joined in a defined order. The primary transcripts contain both exons and introns and are non-functional until splicing occurs.'),
  ('7117721b-e795-4d27-8f21-c09285022bc7','8b944997-1c15-4bc9-8170-b406de1511ed',1,'What is added to the 5''-end of hnRNA during capping?','In capping, an unusual nucleotide called methyl guanosine triphosphate is added to the 5''-end of hnRNA. This protects the mRNA and helps in translation initiation.'),
  ('7c974257-59dd-44c3-bfb7-0fceca9a3af8','dea723bb-02bd-453e-bfea-ccfbd507e936',1,'How many adenylate residues are added during tailing of hnRNA?','In tailing, adenylate residues (200-300) are added at the 3''-end of hnRNA in a template-independent manner. This poly-A tail is important for mRNA stability and export from the nucleus.'),
  ('b461f3f0-3933-46b5-b3f3-aafc7e3640a4','cdaab7f7-f4f8-422a-838d-c4d1ed3aa7eb',1,'What is the fully processed hnRNA called?','The fully processed hnRNA, after capping, tailing, and splicing, is called mRNA. It is then transported out of the nucleus for translation.'),
  ('c2d0c13b-05e1-4ef1-8fa2-0f89184a3899','bea153f0-adb4-45f8-b2a6-1f9758edcdae',1,'Why can transcription and translation be coupled in bacteria?','In bacteria, transcription and translation can be coupled because there is no separation of cytosol and nucleus. The mRNA does not require processing and translation can begin before transcription is complete.'),
  ('10ed06c5-6d41-46eb-979c-76657bd58b75','b123034d-45da-4ce4-a7ae-1e9323fd07f7',1,'Which factor associates with RNA polymerase for initiation of transcription in bacteria?','The RNA polymerase associates transiently with the initiation factor (sigma factor) to initiate transcription. The sigma factor helps the RNA polymerase recognize the promoter region.'),
  ('ce647c94-3504-431a-9b8e-4eb04655ecd0','ac1e1b79-a68a-4e66-b4a3-7f06f2adc162',1,'Which factor is involved in termination of transcription in bacteria?','The RNA polymerase associates with the termination factor (rho factor) to terminate transcription. When the polymerase reaches the terminator region, the nascent RNA falls off along with the RNA polymerase.'),
  ('840a9ef2-814f-4a93-9065-83a8a7c5ff7c','b2d24ae1-8831-4024-96e9-430977cf5a68',1,'What does RNA polymerase use as substrate during transcription?','RNA polymerase uses nucleoside triphosphates as substrate and polymerizes in a template-dependent fashion following the rule of complementarity.'),
  ('8bd5b8db-9cbb-428b-bf9c-ea58d392067b','5dc24b24-d4ea-475a-aab9-1fc2554a932f',1,'What is the significance of split-gene arrangements in eukaryotes?','The split-gene arrangements represent probably an ancient feature of the genome. The presence of introns is reminiscent of antiquity, and the process of splicing represents the dominance of the RNA-world.'),
  ('ce1d2d97-c696-43fd-acb9-08fcee19203c','55ffef97-e3c6-400f-bcf7-624a71df08aa',1,'What is the main topic of Chapter 5 in the uploaded biology textbook?','The chapter title clearly states ''MOLECULAR BASIS OF INHERITANCE'' as the main topic.'),
  ('f0254745-b6d2-4611-9de5-441b375ad8cc','36b127e1-4408-413a-a9af-592868edf348',1,'Which two types of nucleic acids are found in living systems?','The text explicitly states that ''Deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) and ribonucleic acid (RNA) are the two types of nucleic acids found in living systems.'''),
  ('21c4d326-1005-4c26-9564-51d5b99f530a','3a0081f1-e943-4983-96d2-6c210d81211a',1,'What does DNA primarily act as in most organisms?','The text states ''DNA acts as the genetic material in most of the organisms.'''),
  ('44aeb112-b16a-4735-b93d-9a8718ab4dc2','57108acf-57ad-430c-94d8-d67664c0c2be',1,'What additional role does RNA have besides acting as a messenger?','The text mentions that RNA ''mostly functions as a messenger'' and has ''additional roles as well. It functions as adapter, structural, and in some cases as a catalytic molecule.'''),
  ('db06f1c7-e4e0-45f7-a44a-5414eaebc8ae','5886777f-c667-40aa-a2b4-5fdbbfdc2374',1,'What is the full form of DNA?','The text defines DNA as ''deoxyribonucleic acid''.'),
  ('b185668f-15c4-4769-bfb2-5a97fe237caf','db03d086-6726-4e9e-8079-895494130942',1,'According to the chapter, what was the nature of Mendel''s ''factors'' at his time?','The text states ''At the time of Mendel, the nature of those factors regulating the pattern of inheritance was not clear.'''),
  ('6f0d41c7-f1c1-4ccf-8424-f2859c3fecba','70b91e5c-4df1-4728-9c7d-85723c2132f8',1,'Which of the following is NOT listed as a section in Chapter 5?','The chapter sections listed are: 5.1 The DNA, 5.2 The Search for Genetic Material, 5.3 RNA World, 5.4 Replication, 5.5 Transcription, 5.6 Genetic Code, 5.7 Translation, 5.8 Regulation of Gene Expression, 5.9 Human Genome Project, and 5.10 DNA Fingerprinting. Photosynthesis is not mentioned.'),
  ('0cb82136-3a5e-4057-a13a-14a7b04cc306','1928340d-4061-4f98-ac46-6ab42de04328',1,'What did the realization over the next hundred years after Mendel culminate in?','The text states that investigation culminated ''in the realisation that DNA - deoxyribonucleic acid - is the genetic material, at least for the majority of organisms.'''),
  ('434e4fdf-5718-48d9-a4b2-76e1dee204e4','2577e7f2-e7f7-4b7d-a16f-8069cd7a82e6',1,'In which class did the student previously learn about the structures of nucleotides?','The text mentions ''In Class XI you have already learnt the structures of nucleotides and the way these monomer units are linked to form nucleic acid polymers.'''),
  ('05881eec-c693-4ab9-b33f-bf6d2a333ab6','0b4f31ee-f77f-43d3-8427-f46a5433db75',1,'What are the main processes discussed in this chapter?','The text states the chapter will discuss ''the structure of DNA, its replication, the process of making RNA from DNA (transcription), the genetic code that determines the sequences of amino acids in proteins, the process of protein synthesis (translation) and elementary basis of their regulation.'''),
  ('e74cb88b-6890-4be9-a069-a748e7509c50','a80be87a-508d-40a7-a438-8549060263d2',1,'What new era was set in by the determination of complete nucleotide sequence of human genome?','The text states ''The determination of complete nucleotide sequence of human genome during last decade has set in a new era of genomics.'''),
  ('33e91703-8d70-4d20-b669-5c8b29e8dedd','e48e3dca-da29-41eb-a586-eff5eed07302',1,'What does the chapter state about RNA acting as genetic material?','The text states ''RNA though it also acts as a genetic material in some viruses, mostly functions as a messenger.'''),
  ('b2a299c2-3ad1-4dda-a811-1e4e604da29b','9bcfcf85-6fec-4387-abc6-1b53ca9d790b',1,'Which image/model is shown on the first page of the chapter?','The first page contains an image labeled ''Watson-Crick model for Semiconservative DNA replication'' showing a DNA double helix.'),
  ('5f296492-b7b3-4ae6-8f26-992c6b7433ae','c0ceea85-bc92-4754-ab51-b40a21a0bdd1',1,'What is the relationship between DNA and RNA as described on page 1?','The text states ''we will understand that why it is the most abundant genetic material, and what its relationship is with RNA.'''),
  ('e591e819-2e33-4d0b-aaec-aa9d9607de26','3d804df9-8a87-4bcc-b8cb-43de20a6fadd',1,'What is the reprint year mentioned at the bottom of page 1?','The footer of page 1 clearly shows ''Reprint 2026-27''.'),
  ('592d855b-05db-40a3-ad04-5a5563a3136f','6b737f54-4c0f-45e9-8866-849c38152b65',1,'Which section number corresponds to ''RNA World''?','The section list shows 5.3 as ''RNA World''.'),
  ('4672a394-17df-4d76-aff3-a58136b1b3a9','5ee24758-8e71-4b89-aa7b-a5dd5a2db37c',1,'What is the section number for ''DNA Fingerprinting''?','The section list shows 5.10 as ''DNA Fingerprinting''.'),
  ('c7a02cc5-17bf-4b56-bbd3-ff57a37532ef','8deb0941-ce01-4f0f-bf07-79d3dcc70593',1,'What does the chapter say about the inheritance patterns learned in the previous chapter?','The text states ''In the previous chapter, you have learnt the inheritance patterns and the genetic basis of such patterns. Over the next hundred years, the nature of the putative genetic material was investigated.'''),
  ('95f6b2d9-0d0a-479b-9fa2-6d44b642ee43','06450e40-2cc0-4367-be11-5f5666403945',1,'What is the section number for ''Regulation of Gene Expression''?','The section list shows 5.8 as ''Regulation of Gene Expression''.'),
  ('46867552-17a8-4074-a49c-880194eeb5e5','6a3ae931-9112-4070-bf83-c6f5dab0f2e6',1,'What is the section number for ''Human Genome Project''?','The section list shows 5.9 as ''Human Genome Project''.'),
  ('f796c94c-5ab3-4394-b3ac-ab31a0ddbecb','0e8e46c3-09f6-465f-9fcc-237ff5faa9ea',1,'What is the full form of DNA?','DNA stands for Deoxyribonucleic acid, which is a long polymer of deoxyribonucleotides.'),
  ('be694db9-74ea-4fa7-ac1c-7a7c7f146d0b','521780f5-bfd3-4714-b164-d220c1697eba',1,'How is the length of DNA usually defined?','The length of DNA is usually defined as the number of nucleotides (or a pair of nucleotides referred to as base pairs) present in it.'),
  ('d7f061bf-11ed-44b0-9fc9-c68f028c6aa1','2d15af8a-88f5-4b5d-89dc-53c481803f64',1,'How many nucleotides does the bacteriophage phi X 174 contain?','The bacteriophage known as phi X 174 has 5386 nucleotides.'),
  ('cb72b1b7-5dd0-4233-a6f5-e16351f9c0cf','f6085a29-c90d-47b8-a1d3-794fc1bfe0e1',1,'How many base pairs does Bacteriophage lambda have?','Bacteriophage lambda has 48502 base pairs (bp).'),
  ('5935d60d-befd-423a-9735-73fa7d81f8e7','1cd6db79-8ce2-4be2-b9a0-170c562933c3',1,'What is the haploid content of human DNA in base pairs?','The haploid content of human DNA is 3.3 x 10^9 base pairs.'),
  ('e67040f0-bc76-4680-aa23-94c73d94d0f0','1fe7b6b0-1706-4b2c-810f-2d1bb01cb19d',1,'A nucleotide has how many components?','A nucleotide has three components: a nitrogenous base, a pentose sugar, and a phosphate group.'),
  ('8d2f6d7a-5c2f-40e3-bca2-8220e3b85c8c','4369a241-67fb-48a6-a7a4-dff13d62c92a',1,'Which sugar is present in RNA?','RNA contains ribose as its pentose sugar, while DNA contains deoxyribose.'),
  ('7d6ec7bf-415f-4c14-8dcb-330d3794d6bc','8398612e-c02c-435d-ad87-e0684be0faef',1,'Which sugar is present in DNA?','DNA contains deoxyribose as its pentose sugar, while RNA contains ribose.'),
  ('6bdb14f2-c456-4819-aeac-9b27cdfbdcea','8a308f9b-0bcf-4815-a79c-d9533195e2dc',1,'Which of the following are purines?','Purines are Adenine and Guanine. Pyrimidines are Cytosine, Uracil, and Thymine.'),
  ('45a23367-95bf-4027-8e7d-af94b6b82933','238f5abf-23f1-40fc-b91b-7be49c3bf03f',1,'Which of the following are pyrimidines?','Pyrimidines are Cytosine, Uracil, and Thymine. Purines are Adenine and Guanine.'),
  ('b68a7bee-b763-49b4-875b-4a9e5bb123be','a6ec968b-ec85-4278-b4d5-2d60d35f9605',1,'Which nitrogenous base is common for both DNA and RNA?','Cytosine is common for both DNA and RNA. Thymine is present in DNA, and Uracil is present in RNA at the place of Thymine.'),
  ('0c11c7eb-5d23-4e85-88e2-fc669ae65711','dc775008-ee30-4e17-8644-92c1e7dfd496',1,'Which base is present in DNA but not in RNA?','Thymine is present in DNA, while Uracil is present in RNA at the place of Thymine.'),
  ('46bb656f-4833-4a8f-9a2b-251163eb6e0a','42154041-b34e-4b96-95fa-486328ccf5ed',1,'Which base is present in RNA at the place of Thymine?','Uracil is present in RNA at the place of Thymine. Thymine is also known as 5-methyl uracil.'),
  ('eb546c94-17ee-4d0f-adc2-1105b85d5e5a','a5afaf4b-8c7c-4d31-b84f-d9b728040799',1,'What type of linkage is formed between a nitrogenous base and the OH of 1''C pentose sugar?','A nitrogenous base is linked to the OH of 1''C pentose sugar through a N-glycosidic linkage to form a nucleoside.'),
  ('77cf2e9d-09fd-40c2-a2b4-54b8de65b7a7','c1a1901a-fbdc-4597-b10c-74168abd07d5',1,'What is formed when a nitrogenous base is linked to the OH of 1''C pentose sugar?','When a nitrogenous base is linked to the OH of 1''C pentose sugar through N-glycosidic linkage, a nucleoside is formed.'),
  ('3da51a15-fa31-41a8-b0e7-d20f992080ba','eda6f887-7e9d-41b3-a3b9-3becf55fca45',1,'What type of linkage connects a phosphate group to the OH of 5''C of a nucleoside?','When a phosphate group is linked to OH of 5''C of a nucleoside through phosphoester linkage, a nucleotide is formed.'),
  ('d3fc5b87-7cf3-4168-9e93-488aa1d25e83','9022fc11-7d9c-4044-b11b-37dcd693838b',1,'What is formed when a phosphate group is linked to the OH of 5''C of a nucleoside?','When a phosphate group is linked to OH of 5''C of a nucleoside through phosphoester linkage, a corresponding nucleotide is formed.'),
  ('47645d61-adc1-482a-b850-1d77c9ca0459','1c854d0a-b88a-40cc-9054-4e45288af778',1,'What type of linkage joins two nucleotides together?','Two nucleotides are linked through 3''-5'' phosphodiester linkage to form a dinucleotide.'),
  ('eb839561-9ccf-437c-a467-99c4062a8e03','94b5b8a6-fff1-4d8e-9d83-9752659539a0',1,'What is the end of the polynucleotide chain that has a free phosphate moiety at 5''-end of sugar called?','The polymer has at one end a free phosphate moiety at 5''-end of sugar, which is referred to as 5''-end of polynucleotide chain.'),
  ('d6cfe1f7-28d2-4f59-a53e-ebcc9861b782','53fb4bb5-6c86-4097-8551-563a5e955c05',1,'What is the end of the polynucleotide chain that has a free OH of 3''C group called?','At the other end of the polymer, the sugar has a free OH of 3''C group which is referred to as 3''-end of the polynucleotide chain.'),
  ('d95e0728-bc4e-42af-bafe-1bd0d81c7d85','5c135cd7-838b-4ef1-bc28-4bc7c7c3eee0',1,'What forms the backbone of a polynucleotide chain?','The backbone of a polynucleotide chain is formed due to sugar and phosphates. The nitrogenous bases linked to sugar moiety project from the backbone.'),
  ('9bfee900-8205-4b64-b6aa-8c22cca6843c','a623954e-7750-4115-9dce-b3f9bdc2effc',1,'In RNA, what additional group is present at the 2''-position in the ribose?','In RNA, every nucleotide residue has an additional OH group present at 2''-position in the ribose.'),
  ('ca1453e7-bc6c-4dee-9c50-3fda3955fc98','2a74baba-6779-4301-8fe6-9b6871443139',1,'What is another chemical name for thymine?','Thymine is also known as 5-methyl uracil.'),
  ('31b24447-388a-4522-84d8-e3bd78fa5f21','bf276d55-bb54-4d1d-97de-b93d16f9f105',1,'How many base pairs does Escherichia coli have?','Escherichia coli has 4.6 x 10^6 base pairs.'),
  ('3f96f432-03fc-41fb-8682-26a965c4471c','79b80f1b-fa92-407e-8706-97ac36f79fee',1,'What are the examples of nucleosides mentioned in the text?','The text mentions nucleosides such as adenosine or deoxyadenosine, guanosine or deoxyguanosine, cytidine or deoxycytidine, and uridine or deoxythymidine.'),
  ('e9aa5f4a-fcb2-462f-b243-1767530772cf','6c5d0af9-1715-42ec-a172-7d206f6d751d',1,'What is the characteristic of an organism related to DNA length?','The length of DNA is usually defined as number of nucleotides or base pairs present in it, and this is a characteristic of an organism.'),
  ('d054a223-bf13-4e7b-b335-fdfaf556d9ce','e2f4c6d6-0dd4-448a-a391-557b8385d764',1,'Which of the following is NOT a component of a nucleotide?','A nucleotide has three components: a nitrogenous base, a pentose sugar, and a phosphate group. Amino acids are not part of nucleotides.'),
  ('c7525900-70fc-46d5-a589-83db564410c4','27952714-87a3-4c9e-ae85-39070ee18bf9',1,'What is formed when more nucleotides are joined through phosphodiester linkages?','More nucleotides can be joined through 3''-5'' phosphodiester linkages to form a polynucleotide chain.'),
  ('c9b70a30-c960-4c80-a93f-5f8a984da179','809fd121-a87f-43b0-b41a-e4388cc521ec',1,'In the polynucleotide chain diagram, which base is shown at the 5'' end?','In the polynucleotide chain figure (Figure 5.1), Adenine (A) is shown at the 5'' phosphate end.'),
  ('15ee65d7-6470-4104-8ef4-a921713a97b8','0caac389-fd52-4367-8890-826e6204c479',1,'Where do the nitrogenous bases project from in a polynucleotide chain?','The nitrogenous bases linked to sugar moiety project from the backbone of the polynucleotide chain.'),
  ('c7cc1be4-2f68-4c17-98c4-aabd252e7f12','d6fbe2b6-e8b8-498a-a586-d1479ef6ede3',1,'Who first identified DNA as an acidic substance present in the nucleus in 1869?','Friedrich Meischer first identified DNA as an acidic substance present in the nucleus in 1869 and named it as Nuclein.'),
  ('f3c20364-35c3-4707-98ce-fd9dc1c1ad4c','bd06ea79-5d5f-4522-89ad-4aace32a470a',1,'In which year did James Watson and Francis Crick propose the Double Helix model for the structure of DNA?','James Watson and Francis Crick proposed the Double Helix model for the structure of DNA in 1953, based on X-ray diffraction data produced by Maurice Wilkins and Rosalind Franklin.'),
  ('c0b63580-c919-4be6-849d-cbe11ba03611','257433a0-9974-4167-b8a1-60a18e41fffe',1,'According to Erwin Chargaffs observation, what is the ratio between Adenine and Thymine in a double stranded DNA?','Erwin Chargaff observed that for a double stranded DNA, the ratios between Adenine and Thymine and between Guanine and Cytosine are constant and equal to one.'),
  ('7b63b834-6505-4272-a102-3188e1789384','8fe2e981-36bf-4681-aed0-d4078df53f6b',1,'How many hydrogen bonds are formed between Guanine and Cytosine in DNA base pairing?','In DNA base pairing, Guanine is bonded with Cytosine through three hydrogen bonds, while Adenine forms two hydrogen bonds with Thymine.'),
  ('81edded9-4854-419a-a6ef-740898443572','1bf17209-8a2d-4d5f-a949-42a88c089b7a',1,'What type of polarity do the two chains of DNA double helix exhibit?','The two chains of DNA double helix have anti-parallel polarity, meaning if one chain has the polarity 5 prime to 3 prime, the other has 3 prime to 5 prime.'),
  ('b15ac221-7914-42f6-b9af-26da9c2c6623','13709328-3cbd-4a3f-8ea2-e1143c3c5380',1,'What is the approximate pitch of the DNA double helix?','The pitch of the DNA double helix is 3.4 nm (nanometre), and there are roughly 10 base pairs in each turn.'),
  ('74faea4f-0bee-40dd-a437-bd020bcaab79','5951d65a-aa5e-4d6b-8672-8f4224a94aa2',1,'Why does a purine always come opposite to a pyrimidine in DNA base pairing?','A purine always comes opposite to a pyrimidine in DNA base pairing to generate approximately uniform distance between the two strands of the helix, maintaining the constant diameter of the double helix.'),
  ('6d4c3dc6-5a73-4387-8bc6-0d7e835035f2','aef626f2-2866-4c20-adbb-cbf3c5178050',1,'What additional stability is conferred to the DNA double helix structure besides hydrogen bonds?','Besides hydrogen bonds, the plane of one base pair stacks over the other in the double helix, which confers additional stability to the helical structure.'),
  ('757f134e-a608-4730-8838-7d364c4cdd7b','e4461e19-c4f3-48c1-ae38-c7d7cd142619',1,'What is the approximate distance between two consecutive base pairs in the DNA double helix?','The distance between two consecutive base pairs in the DNA double helix is approximately 0.34 nm (0.34 x 10^-9 m).'),
  ('abccf047-413a-4d81-a8f3-9ba0bc86adcc','da66510e-3a74-4291-85dd-1bc3aecb213e',1,'What is the significance of base pairing in DNA for genetic implications?','Because of base pairing, the two strands are complementary to each other. If each strand from a parental DNA acts as a template for synthesis of a new strand, the two daughter DNA molecules produced would be identical to the parental DNA molecule.'),
  ('6fbee0c2-e138-4c9a-a27d-e193ed70cfa5','e8a89df4-8d0a-4abf-86ae-83bd0a1fdfa7',1,'Which bacterium did Frederick Griffith use in his transformation experiments?','Frederick Griffith used Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococcus), the bacterium responsible for pneumonia, in his famous transformation experiments in 1928.'),
  ('dd55045f-6451-4bdd-afce-ff16a9ac3cfc','4a061637-2ade-4ccb-9470-ffe56eeb8fc8',1,'What characteristic distinguishes the S strain of Streptococcus pneumoniae from the R strain?','The S strain has a mucous (polysaccharide) coat and produces smooth shiny colonies, making it virulent. The R strain lacks this coat and produces rough colonies, and is non-virulent.'),
  ('c6efd843-6c11-4749-8d24-e7311328fd0d','76ea2dd5-2543-4fa1-9ed5-2d63b0423292',1,'What was the outcome when Griffith injected heat-killed S strain bacteria into mice?','When heat-killed S strain bacteria were injected into mice, the mice survived because the heat had killed the bacteria, rendering them non-virulent.'),
  ('ab342147-62b6-4619-8ca4-7c17c2b7eef0','3bb8cb5f-adcf-457d-b76e-102e3336b618',1,'What happened when Griffith injected a mixture of heat-killed S strain and live R strain bacteria into mice?','The mice died, and Griffith recovered living S strain bacteria from the dead mice. This indicated that the R strain had been transformed by some ''transforming principle'' from the heat-killed S strain.'),
  ('2508a988-c30f-454b-8452-9993054c2fbf','a2934dda-bccb-439a-9a18-74c04f052630',1,'What did Griffith conclude from his experiment with heat-killed S and live R strains?','Griffith concluded that the R strain bacteria had been transformed by a ''transforming principle'' transferred from the heat-killed S strain, which enabled the R strain to synthesize a smooth polysaccharide coat and become virulent.'),
  ('3fc44855-d5e2-40b4-b41b-e03df5828375','a2554cb7-30ed-4466-bda8-fd971769f5af',1,'Before the work of Avery, MacLeod, and McCarty, what was the prevailing belief about the nature of genetic material?','Prior to the work of Avery, MacLeod, and McCarty (1933-44), the genetic material was thought to be a protein.'),
  ('3b48645a-ad1c-4229-b108-4d07332a0a10','61c02551-4b06-40f4-9f8b-f2e96b0546cc',1,'Which enzyme digestion did NOT affect the transformation of R strain bacteria in Avery''s experiments?','Proteases and RNases did not affect transformation, indicating that the transforming substance was neither protein nor RNA. Only DNase inhibited transformation.'),
  ('bdecfadb-f3e4-444d-8914-9b19cbd739cc','fa910b4c-6c08-42b4-881a-d2b641f0993c',1,'What was the key conclusion of Avery, MacLeod, and McCarty''s experiments regarding the transforming principle?','They concluded that DNA is the hereditary material, as DNA alone from S bacteria caused R bacteria to become transformed, and DNase digestion inhibited this transformation.'),
  ('c0f9cb83-7714-4eba-96b1-97b89d66467d','d5cae8f2-8df1-48c8-99f9-ff254bb05ea3',1,'Which radioactive isotope did Hershey and Chase use to label the DNA of bacteriophages?','Hershey and Chase used radioactive phosphorus (32P) to label DNA because DNA contains phosphorus but proteins do not. This allowed them to track whether DNA entered the bacterial cells during infection.'),
  ('a19da3f6-01a9-49bf-a208-b72c5ea8500e','7199afac-87f5-4347-81a0-47501f41704a',1,'What was the purpose of using radioactive sulfur (35S) in the Hershey-Chase experiment?','Radioactive sulfur (35S) was used to label the protein coat of bacteriophages because sulfur is present in proteins (in amino acids like cysteine and methionine) but not in DNA. This helped distinguish whether protein or DNA entered the bacteria.'),
  ('72368fb8-f38f-4b43-bfa3-81700f9d91dd','09ddf251-5e8c-459e-9db1-f6d16ea3af1a',1,'In the Hershey-Chase experiment, what happened when bacteriophages with radioactive DNA infected E. coli?','When bacteriophages with radioactive DNA (32P-labeled) infected E. coli, the bacteria became radioactive because the DNA entered the bacterial cells. The supernatant (containing empty phage coats) was not radioactive, proving that DNA was the genetic material.'),
  ('4690d131-acf8-4835-a89b-68f9d50209c2','97b5b083-87f9-4cfd-bc06-1443b4f98d52',1,'What instrument was used by Hershey and Chase to separate the virus particles from the bacteria after infection?','A centrifuge was used to separate the virus particles from the bacteria after infection. The blender was used to agitate and remove the viral coats from the bacteria, and then centrifugation separated the heavier bacterial cells from the lighter virus particles.'),
  ('1e9840a4-a8d9-4e83-a5a7-f0a33cfa1787','cc78e706-30aa-471e-8781-7957d342fcd5',1,'What was the result when bacteria were infected with bacteriophages containing radioactive protein (35S)?','When bacteria were infected with 35S-labeled bacteriophages, the bacteria were not radioactive because the protein coat did not enter the bacterial cells. The supernatant containing the empty phage coats was radioactive. This proved that protein was not the genetic material.'),
  ('8d88557e-173a-4139-85f8-3783b8f49e3f','53c28bca-a907-48d7-b445-778d702ad937',1,'What does the term ''bacteriophage'' literally mean?','Bacteriophage literally means ''bacteria eater'' (from Greek ''bacteria'' meaning bacteria and ''phagein'' meaning to eat). These are viruses that infect and replicate within bacteria.'),
  ('193244d9-04d9-4cce-923e-23e96ac4b9a7','26cb6744-0ae0-482e-a42f-08ba1dc56c84',1,'Why does DNA contain radioactive phosphorus (32P) when grown in a medium with radioactive phosphorus?','DNA contains phosphorus in its phosphate groups (the backbone of DNA consists of sugar-phosphate linkages). When bacteria are grown in a medium with radioactive phosphorus, this isotope gets incorporated into the DNA during synthesis.'),
  ('548cd407-c540-489b-9a38-0ad067ce7ee5','6642b11d-5359-45b7-9a40-b2dfc9968a6c',1,'What was the key conclusion of the Hershey-Chase experiment?','The Hershey-Chase experiment conclusively proved that DNA is the genetic material that passes from virus to bacteria during infection. The radioactive DNA entered the bacterial cells and directed the production of new virus particles, while the protein coat remained outside.'),
  ('c9bc7780-b005-475f-86ec-41e7131b258d','a1eb3898-89d7-4acb-b5ca-b0609ea76ecb',1,'In the Hershey-Chase experiment, what was the role of the blender?','The blender was used to agitate the bacteria after infection, which helped remove the viral protein coats that were attached to the outside of the bacterial cells. This allowed researchers to separate the external protein coats from the internal bacterial contents.'),
  ('1b09fe5b-41ed-4db5-9e55-5fc77db4f67c','d85a757c-f36c-465a-bffb-682e6032a13f',1,'What type of organism does a bacteriophage infect?','A bacteriophage is a virus that specifically infects bacteria. The term ''bacteriophage'' means ''bacteria eater'' and these viruses attach to bacterial cells and inject their genetic material into them.'),
  ('98b3e8aa-0700-46f3-a55b-9e63311b8399','b2b56f09-bf24-4154-ba34-fc1a8c84a7dc',1,'Which component of the bacteriophage enters the bacterial cell during infection?','During infection, only the DNA of the bacteriophage enters the bacterial cell. The protein coat remains outside attached to the bacterial surface. This was demonstrated by the Hershey-Chase experiment using radioactive labeling.'),
  ('7e89a384-ba49-4271-8250-dd0559375b23','b716d928-bf24-49aa-813f-dcfc5c0cec87',1,'What does the bacterial cell do with the viral genetic material after it enters?','The bacterial cell treats the viral genetic material as if it was its own and subsequently manufactures more virus particles. The viral DNA hijacks the bacterial machinery to replicate itself and produce new phages.'),
  ('276559fb-7990-4050-8fb6-7d9fca87ea37','d160925d-cb14-4a8d-9f2b-d93bb9fe7bab',1,'What is the primary process described in Section 5.7 Translation?','Translation is the process of polymerization of amino acids to form a polypeptide. The sequence of amino acids is determined by the sequence of bases in mRNA.'),
  ('8863b475-58b6-45c6-a710-859fc96ff612','867ad5f1-2de6-4ab4-bfaf-6ef006b7d81d',1,'What type of bond joins amino acids during translation?','Amino acids are joined by a peptide bond during translation. This bond formation requires energy.'),
  ('a63678a3-5032-4a30-b013-1c8f5d21776c','2a1431fc-79c1-4693-a8f4-ed4bf8bcd76d',1,'What is the first step in translation where amino acids are activated and linked to tRNA?','In the first phase of translation, amino acids are activated in the presence of ATP and linked to their cognate tRNA. This process is called charging of tRNA or aminoacylation of tRNA.'),
  ('d3c737f0-1c1d-4bf3-926d-29917353484d','5a61955b-0d04-43f4-9336-c99a82df5b7b',1,'Which cellular structure serves as the site of translation?','Ribosomes are the site of translation (protein synthesis). They bind to mRNA and provide a platform for joining of amino acids.'),
  ('e3e7047f-ef67-4388-9f93-eaf600883088','4e1b7bcd-53f5-43e1-b11c-323f122a6175',1,'Which rRNA acts as a catalyst for peptide bond formation during translation?','One of the rRNAs acts as a catalyst for peptide bond formation. This is an example of a ribozyme (RNA enzyme). In prokaryotes, 23S rRNA and in eukaryotes, 28S rRNA catalyzes this reaction.'),
  ('a924b34b-4252-47af-9b6f-8bd009faa119','14c3a05d-8192-4353-98ae-d68293a73b39',1,'In which direction is mRNA read during translation?','During translation, mRNA is read in the 5 prime to 3 prime direction. The ribosome moves along the mRNA from the 5 prime end to the 3 prime end.'),
  ('05c091df-63d5-45d0-ae8e-d5de9e476c12','909600fc-4fba-48d5-98fe-7d825055f912',1,'Which codon serves as the start codon during translation?','AUG is the start codon that codes for methionine (Met) and also acts as the initiator codon. Translation begins at this codon.'),
  ('64110762-e9b2-4a19-9710-666c5e04c287','801addd8-b35f-40ed-8217-7538dacbcb4a',1,'Which of the following are stop codons that terminate translation?','UAA, UAG, and UGA are stop codons (terminator codons) that do not code for any amino acid. They signal the termination of translation.'),
  ('3569b349-82d8-4ab9-ba6d-f1152b0ef7b7','30daa386-e9ea-46cb-97e8-e49c3e3e2137',1,'What are polysomes in the context of translation?','Polysomes (polyribosomes) are structures where multiple ribosomes translate a single mRNA molecule simultaneously. This allows efficient protein synthesis.'),
  ('81d820c0-64d8-4803-93f8-30eea651796a','d2fd4752-8069-4a6a-b250-6d9d1e9050b4',1,'What happens to the newly synthesized polypeptide after translation?','After translation, the newly synthesized polypeptide undergoes post-translational modifications such as folding, cleavage, or addition of chemical groups to become a functional protein.'),
  ('93a71007-b060-4594-af19-26a55627099a','20dcb21e-c4a1-42aa-a7d5-8035b105a4fb',1,'What energy source is required for the charging of tRNA during translation?','ATP provides the energy required for activating amino acids and linking them to their cognate tRNA during the charging process.'),
  ('9df64c8e-4753-4218-b3eb-604aa8196bae','c81c5882-c085-4c5c-b381-e4d7b2c995ba',1,'Which statement best describes the relationship between mRNA sequence and amino acid sequence?','The order and sequence of amino acids in the polypeptide are defined by the sequence of bases in the mRNA. The genetic code in mRNA is read in triplets to specify each amino acid.'),
  ('b5b85537-2016-4753-b541-f3bfda824cf4','4552e61e-6176-42ce-96be-60035b3e1a36',1,'What is Evolutionary Biology defined as?','Evolutionary Biology is defined as the study of history of life forms on earth.'),
  ('41bd9b2e-4d11-4c08-822d-3ce44b61a195','838f2067-19ec-47cf-ad3b-e1c9f729896d',1,'To understand evolution, what must we understand first?','To understand changes in flora and fauna, we must understand the context of origin of life, evolution of earth, stars and universe.'),
  ('5520bcd3-09fb-41f1-ad3c-2b10d2799691','ca0f1bde-4ec4-4ad1-822f-222be8ae0a84',1,'What are stellar distances measured in?','Stellar distances are measured in light years.'),
  ('c9004d5c-4c54-4424-aaf5-2512e121e057','72fe684f-1f01-4f3d-ab72-6cb125121415',1,'When we look at stars on a clear night sky, what are we actually doing?','When we look at stars, we are looking back in time because the light started its journey millions of years ago.'),
  ('811e0bf8-097b-4d62-877a-28cdb76bd1dc','9d4a0f25-7cc4-4b71-95da-ffd801066767',1,'How old is the universe approximately?','The universe is approximately 13.8 billion years old.'),
  ('a493e1e6-d328-4673-9e9d-eae40267d8d5','b94b9b02-f6aa-4054-8c56-01bca0fc9fbe',1,'What does the Big Bang theory explain?','The Big Bang theory attempts to explain the origin of the universe.'),
  ('4ebad54e-df74-48f1-a4f7-0e8f2ffa03e9','63a3d9b2-ce5c-41ef-969f-255646a2a19c',1,'What were the first elements formed after the Big Bang?','Hydrogen and Helium formed after the Big Bang when the universe expanded and temperature came down.'),
  ('3295adf7-6b5c-43c4-89dc-522b14fa9c2d','9f0d2c06-6b27-4b79-ad0c-b2cfbf19d308',1,'When was earth formed according to the text?','Earth was formed about 4.5 billion years back in the solar system of the Milky Way galaxy.'),
  ('b8f0775e-4cb7-48c4-b756-1ff3df7331e8','03516d05-32b3-467c-bc1a-33afa6a79d55',1,'What gases covered the surface of early earth?','Water vapor, methane, carbon dioxide and ammonia released from molten mass covered the surface of early earth.'),
  ('7a92ca94-f974-40f0-bce0-5433715ed7d7','9709d027-240d-4f6c-8dc9-cea4976b3735',1,'What broke up water into Hydrogen and Oxygen on early earth?','The UV rays from the sun broke up water into Hydrogen and Oxygen.'),
  ('2837ace6-0caa-45e6-8e5c-f49fa544710e','1168bcb9-746d-47d9-b368-5dc1d9ba5ee7',1,'What happened to the lighter Hydrogen gas after water was broken up?','The lighter H2 escaped after water was broken up by UV rays.'),
  ('71abc378-76c9-4aff-8484-68edf555f511','49bccde0-f81b-469e-ae29-8821680de7be',1,'What did oxygen combine with to form water and CO2?','Oxygen combined with ammonia and methane to form water, CO2 and others.'),
  ('ce6f837b-06ac-4de7-8d01-8a5ea1dd5b1c','013aa540-cc4b-4104-9382-7b6d2bde31c8',1,'When did life appear on earth after its formation?','Life appeared 500 million years after the formation of earth, i.e., almost four billion years ago.'),
  ('3eaffcd0-7801-4702-9cd4-bb61716d149e','358ba089-abb0-434c-b432-2dc8525f581f',1,'What does the text describe evolution as?','The text describes evolution as the longest of all the construed and conjectured stories.'),
  ('1856409b-e918-4001-a2c1-f65e814c2efb','db8bc87d-2cf9-41ae-8ecc-52b487994744',1,'What formed when the gases condensed under gravitation?','The gases condensed under gravitation and formed the galaxies of the present day universe.'),
  ('01ec767d-46bf-4256-a7f5-dd50e2661d7f','44fea629-58c7-432f-b66b-d38495295dcf',1,'What can one find out in a given population according to the Hardy-Weinberg principle?','In a given population one can find out the frequency of occurrence of alleles of a gene or a locus.'),
  ('e06f7d11-ac9e-48d2-b84c-cd39aecb3368','2b0e3928-30c2-4849-9226-dc6660934f2f',1,'What does the Hardy-Weinberg principle state about allele frequencies?','The principle says that allele frequencies in a population are stable and constant from generation to generation.'),
  ('7962e665-09af-44ce-9ac1-4c3582152c34','dacc3b2c-b06a-459c-9269-4bc4c89928a7',1,'What is the gene pool?','The gene pool is defined as total genes and their alleles in a population.'),
  ('991cf497-0a83-4338-b4c6-3130df8c9664','37b036d8-f7eb-409b-8974-9a75102b73b8',1,'What is genetic equilibrium?','Genetic equilibrium is when the gene pool remains a constant.'),
  ('bddf4cbd-5a97-4cec-9245-e25c532cbfb7','ed9bee4d-8683-48e0-b7b3-b3f4d4182df3',1,'What is the sum total of all allelic frequencies?','The sum total of all the allelic frequencies is 1.'),
  ('3e1d3b22-d0a6-4fef-8faf-7d6a5c5333a2','42253634-e18d-4e17-9e2c-9dc126c4e004',1,'In a diploid, what do p and q represent?','In a diploid, p and q represent the frequency of allele A and allele a.'),
  ('1868275e-2471-4347-8d5d-c81e9ff979c6','20f60368-4161-4792-a643-654b01c8ced5',1,'What is the frequency of AA individuals in a population?','The frequency of AA individuals in a population is p^2.'),
  ('fd854d62-2dee-4ac8-86e6-451632a5ec0a','d22f358d-9c5c-456b-8369-e60e2a4cf90e',1,'What is the frequency of aa individuals in a population?','The frequency of aa individuals is q^2.'),
  ('374d43c4-119c-4134-934d-2e5e1b19c381','e4f1a736-010f-48c4-bfcf-bd887baad3fc',1,'What is the frequency of Aa individuals in a population?','The frequency of Aa individuals is 2pq.'),
  ('268af0bf-6061-478f-b8a3-c080d9371c62','66e78c16-36c1-4803-857e-f6e348ba33a2',1,'What is the Hardy-Weinberg equation?','The Hardy-Weinberg equation is p^2 + 2pq + q^2 = 1. This is a binomial expansion of (p + q)^2.'),
  ('f0f417b6-3336-476f-bc44-1678fa76225d','d6b518bb-ae55-41a0-9832-4f455f0b2d1b',1,'What does a difference between measured frequency and expected values indicate?','When frequency measured differs from expected values, the difference indicates the extent of evolutionary change.'),
  ('29fe3e63-8eb4-4a6c-8962-a30fd8ec675c','c104091f-a54b-426f-a2ee-9a4d2b798390',1,'What is a disturbance in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium interpreted as?','Change of frequency of alleles in a population is interpreted as resulting in evolution.'),
  ('9814fbeb-e8df-4856-ba3a-ac464e56e3bd','a429fcd9-c952-4462-b71f-1a559de0e40a',1,'How many factors are known to affect Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium?','Five factors are known to affect Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium.'),
  ('24d7f45b-35cd-47ee-a3e9-b1ac9635d00e','5fad44fc-47ba-401d-8dc7-0f049ea729f4',1,'Which of the following is NOT a factor affecting Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium?','The five factors are gene migration, genetic drift, mutation, genetic recombination and natural selection. Climate change is not listed.')
INSERT INTO question_translations (id, question_id, lang_id, question_text, explanation) VALUES
  ('7501633c-c379-492e-bfba-f9377737b5fc','1b4058d4-1468-4e02-80a7-f9a8763e6be1',1,'What happens when migration of a section of population occurs?','When migration occurs, gene frequencies change in the original as well as in the new population.'),
  ('30721401-4c94-4a70-8499-3f73cb88ce3a','a81a5300-1d39-4594-820a-492f3d3368cc',1,'What is gene flow?','Gene flow occurs when gene migration happens multiple times.'),
  ('64821ffe-df53-481b-b730-45ea2a33b5f2','fe62e59c-6e1f-45a2-9fb6-c44c8da1b92d',1,'What is genetic drift?','Genetic drift is when the same change occurs by chance in allele frequency.'),
  ('b016a7cb-db61-4599-a320-136b2953faac','96b9e8f6-48ed-45e3-a5c1-a0164c9a0420',1,'What is the founder effect?','The founder effect occurs when the original drifted population becomes founders.'),
  ('b645a8a0-41d7-41c8-981b-3a8de8d0c73c','583c8e90-c78b-4d56-b348-815cc80a3d58',1,'What do microbial experiments show about advantageous mutations?','Pre-existing advantageous mutations when selected result in new phenotypes and over generations lead to speciation.'),
  ('1485f707-32f9-4098-b162-0bc902264625','1e7e7e78-0f74-4a1e-bc1e-ff477042f977',1,'What is natural selection?','Natural selection is a process in which heritable variations enabling better survival are enabled to reproduce and leave greater number of progeny.'),
  ('d98acbf0-95af-482c-8173-dd13ba6b7ae1','613629f8-b581-4239-a69d-b1930e3bdf02',1,'What causes changed frequency of genes and alleles in future generations?','Variation due to mutation, recombination during gametogenesis, gene flow, or genetic drift results in changed frequency of genes and alleles.'),
  ('8bac06df-e31e-42c3-87d2-ab3d58b168d4','a21eae9d-86f5-44bc-a366-9eae1b5b0c5e',1,'What is stabilisation in natural selection?','Stabilisation is when more individuals acquire mean character value.'),
  ('314daf6f-d1ac-4558-8b8f-616257071b93','b1bc4383-d507-4cf7-88ed-b76f02735a6f',1,'What is directional change in natural selection?','Directional change is when more individuals acquire value other than the mean character value.'),
  ('fa0d72fa-d34f-4c8c-ac64-b5ec0f0f31e8','78fdf7c2-d222-4e1d-9fc3-a87cc766f9db',1,'What is disruption in natural selection?','Disruption is when more individuals acquire peripheral character value at both ends of the distribution curve.'),
  ('e31a3fb9-a66a-4db5-b5ee-7247c08be2c6','2191bdd1-75c3-4270-bb41-f6bfcc2dec1d',1,'In a diploid, what do p and q represent?','In a diploid, p and q represent the frequency of allele A and allele a respectively.'),
  ('478e079a-fdb5-48d2-9681-d9dac90a70e5','79bc5422-db1f-4a28-8e20-95c0621766ae',1,'What is the frequency of AA individuals in a population?','The frequency of AA individuals in a population is p^2.'),
  ('e31055d2-7ab2-4f93-95c5-de9e3d5f45d6','e1014f7c-ec7e-42a2-837d-51baed1ca1fe',1,'What is the frequency of aa individuals in a population?','The frequency of aa individuals in a population is q^2.'),
  ('36b9292d-d978-4bb1-bd0f-a1def536ada9','e3e4c304-6251-4141-873e-5824fb7b7eb8',1,'What is the frequency of Aa individuals in a population?','The frequency of Aa individuals in a population is 2pq.'),
  ('05ba4975-ee71-4fd6-a05f-f447ee100633','ba15d0e7-8033-4c7e-86d4-42588c2ac2f5',1,'What is the Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium equation?','The Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium equation is p^2 + 2pq + q^2 = 1, which is a binomial expansion of (p + q)^2.'),
  ('071857af-deac-4181-8db4-2becf64785a3','9b0d5d73-5dc1-4f96-b348-e499f09d5030',1,'What does the difference between measured frequency and expected values indicate?','When frequency measured differs from expected values, the difference indicates the extent of evolutionary change.'),
  ('18e75392-2043-4ddb-9454-9d40e84e6480','f41fb976-3249-4d37-87b5-01f79dad9bd3',1,'What is another name for genetic equilibrium?','Genetic equilibrium is also called Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium.'),
  ('ec85a835-7e9c-4dfd-bf54-cc26fb3b821c','5337a2c2-afcf-4f1b-8388-3158021423c1',1,'How many factors are known to affect Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium?','Five factors are known to affect Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium.'),
  ('2f2a795c-9df1-4ee6-a8a0-e3ec1e470f0c','f9dab5c1-411b-4994-a868-28f216313dca',1,'Which of the following is NOT a factor affecting Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium?','Photosynthesis is not a factor affecting Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. The five factors are gene migration, genetic drift, mutation, genetic recombination and natural selection.'),
  ('82c32314-0177-4cc7-bbf0-93d93fab0bf3','db398525-6efc-40e3-b7f4-c06ad2c6747b',1,'What happens when migration of a section of population occurs to another place?','When migration occurs, gene frequencies change in the original as well as in the new population.'),
  ('27a23264-c86d-4317-b764-30fc587d3c0f','579fbd8d-4630-467f-a674-3e2d8a5073e5',1,'What is added to the new population during gene migration?','New genes or alleles are added to the new population during gene migration.'),
  ('419eed14-0221-4239-a573-23a292303d75','f2058f35-e52b-46d9-b976-30464bc051b3',1,'What is gene flow?','Gene flow occurs when gene migration happens multiple times.'),
  ('71ea3e3c-6578-4925-b108-43d2c99d4690','375b9e3c-6e53-4e35-aea0-a9b2c7ddfbbf',1,'What is genetic drift?','Genetic drift is the change in allele frequency that occurs by chance.'),
  ('c7ef6c8f-5955-418b-94cf-489398129bd9','bd97ccf8-11b7-43c3-beb4-681f7b2038dc',1,'What is the founder effect?','The founder effect occurs when the original drifted population becomes founders and the change in allele frequency is so different that they become a different species.'),
  ('ac75b4cc-fde1-4ee3-9118-6148c9f43370','56f086e7-3ca0-438f-8250-f6d8a80539a9',1,'What do microbial experiments show about advantageous mutations?','Microbial experiments show that pre-existing advantageous mutations when selected will result in observation of new phenotypes.'),
  ('51878f8f-6e80-4a1e-b866-16e9b08382c5','4fd053ee-5143-4494-8655-4900c18cbccf',1,'What is the result of advantageous mutations over a few generations?','Over a few generations, advantageous mutations result in speciation.'),
  ('1b15b3be-c1c1-4914-be5a-5140e2331f90','f2c0796b-ef3a-40c5-a2a1-10f4a5c79bd4',1,'What is natural selection?','Natural selection is a process in which heritable variations enabling better survival are enabled to reproduce and leave greater number of progeny.'),
  ('b05dda8e-6b2f-4a36-ae65-4efcfb1480b6','748f2851-8bd1-4d82-8c0d-523ea6ad6d6d',1,'What causes variation in future generations?','Variation in future generations can result from mutation, recombination during gametogenesis, gene flow or genetic drift.'),
  ('1f8a1caf-1650-450e-8fe1-6226e7389ed0','82ec15f7-1ba9-481e-921f-d6a98ce7deb8',1,'What is stabilising selection?','Stabilising selection is when more individuals acquire mean character value.'),
  ('2107cef2-0cd1-4d70-9a85-d93295958116','00cad44f-4b45-41e2-aecc-3456dbf5ce3d',1,'What is directional selection?','Directional selection is when more individuals acquire value other than the mean character value.'),
  ('1e1863eb-62bb-423c-a175-0b6fc265acb4','fb3fb48b-cd40-4da3-badb-4e87f091bd58',1,'What is disruptive selection?','Disruptive selection is when more individuals acquire peripheral character value at both ends of the distribution curve.'),
  ('83ebe276-5b74-493a-90bf-1c637cb0e64f','31fa8ec9-d589-4323-b82c-2d139734dc4d',1,'When did the first cellular forms of life appear on earth?','The first cellular forms of life appeared on earth about 2000 million years ago.'),
  ('5826281e-a295-4ffe-a5cc-7aec940e4f95','517c1ba0-3a24-4e86-93a9-c831b416d58a',1,'What ability did some of the early cells have?','Some of the early cells had the ability to release O2.'),
